{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23914, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23914", "issue_numbers": ["12201"], "base_commit": "29241a3aff4ad41bf79ed5782ea4059cb74d0b4d", "patch": "diff --git a/docs/v3/how-to-guides/deployment_infra/manage-work-pools.mdx b/docs/v3/how-to-guides/deployment_infra/manage-work-pools.mdx\nindex eff87a4c7a88..cc3a60b50c3c 100644\n--- a/docs/v3/how-to-guides/deployment_infra/manage-work-pools.mdx\n+++ b/docs/v3/how-to-guides/deployment_infra/manage-work-pools.mdx\n@@ -110,7 +110,54 @@ View default base job template:\n prefect work-pool get-default-base-job-template --type process\n ```\n \n-Example `prefect.yaml`:\n+#### Create a work pool with custom defaults\n+\n+A base job template contains `job_configuration`, which references job variables, and `variables`, which defines their JSON schema.\n+Set pool-wide defaults in `variables.properties.<variable_name>.default`.\n+For example, the Docker worker's `image_pull_policy` default belongs in `variables.properties.image_pull_policy.default`.\n+\n+Connect the CLI to Prefect Cloud or a running Prefect server before creating or updating a work pool.\n+The following example creates a Docker work pool that uses images already available on the Docker host and removes containers after completion.\n+\n+1. Save the default template for the Docker work pool type:\n+\n+   ```bash\n+   prefect work-pool get-default-base-job-template --type docker --file base-job-template.json\n+   ```\n+\n+2. Run this Python script in the same directory to change two defaults while preserving the rest of the generated template:\n+\n+   {/* pmd-metadata: notest */}\n+   ```python\n+   import json\n+   from pathlib import Path\n+\n+   path = Path(\"base-job-template.json\")\n+   template = json.loads(path.read_text())\n+   properties = template[\"variables\"][\"properties\"]\n+   properties[\"image_pull_policy\"][\"default\"] = \"Never\"\n+   properties[\"auto_remove\"][\"default\"] = True\n+   path.write_text(json.dumps(template, indent=2))\n+   print(f\"Saved work pool defaults to {path}\")\n+   ```\n+\n+   This script uses the file saved in the preceding step.\n+   Keep the existing `job_configuration` references, such as `\"image_pull_policy\": \"{{ image_pull_policy }}\"`, so the worker receives the configured values.\n+\n+3. Create the work pool with the customized template:\n+\n+   ```bash\n+   prefect work-pool create docker-pool --type docker --base-job-template base-job-template.json\n+   ```\n+\n+To apply the file to an existing pool, use:\n+\n+```bash\n+prefect work-pool update docker-pool --base-job-template base-job-template.json\n+```\n+\n+Deployment-level `job_variables` override the work pool defaults.\n+For example, use the following in `prefect.yaml`:\n \n ```yaml\n deployments:\n@@ -155,4 +202,4 @@ import { API } from \"/snippets/resource-management/api.mdx\"\n ## Further reading\n \n - [Work pools](/v3/concepts/work-pools) concept page\n-- [Customize job variables](/v3/deploy/infrastructure-concepts/customize) page\n\\ No newline at end of file\n+- [Customize job variables](/v3/deploy/infrastructure-concepts/customize) page\n", "test_patch": "", "problem_statement": "Better documention for work pool 'base-job-template'\n### First check\r\n\r\n- [X] I added a descriptive title to this issue.\r\n- [X] I used GitHub search to find a similar request and didn't find it \ud83d\ude07\r\n\r\n### Describe the issue\r\n\r\nAttempting to create a base job template to use with the cli `prefect work-pool create` command is not difficult, as there is no documentation for this, only for using `.deploy` and `prefect.yml`.\r\n\r\nThis is for a Docker work pool, for context.\r\n\r\nBased on the schema in a newly created work pool in the UI I tried creating a file for my base job template like this\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"variables\": { \"image_pull_policy\": \"Never\", \"auto_remove\": true },\r\n  \"job_configuration\": {\r\n    \"image_pull_policy\": \"{{ image_pull_policy }}\",\r\n    \"auto_remove\": \"{{ auto_remove }}\"\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nBut this fails with\r\n\r\n```txt\r\nResponse: {'exception_message': 'Invalid request received.', 'exception_detail': [{'loc': ['body', 'base_job_template'], 'msg': 'The variables specified in the job configuration template must be present as properties in the variables schema. Your job configuration uses the following undeclared variable(s): auto_remove ,image_pull_policy.', 'type': 'value_error'}], 'request_body': {'name': 'test', 'type': 'docker', 'base_job_template': {'variables': {'image_pull_policy': 'Never', 'auto_remove': True}, 'job_configuration': {'image_pull_policy': '{{ image_pull_policy }}', 'auto_remove': '{{ auto_remove }}'}}, 'is_paused': False}}\r\n```\r\n\r\nI eventually realized it needs to be declared with `{{ variables.auto_remove }}`, for example.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"variables\": { \"image_pull_policy\": \"Never\", \"auto_remove\": true },\r\n  \"job_configuration\": {\r\n    \"image_pull_policy\": \"{{ variables.image_pull_policy }}\",\r\n    \"auto_remove\": \"{{ variables.auto_remove }}\"\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd this works but shows this in the UI when you try to edit it:\r\n\r\n![image](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/assets/8505845/097b7ea7-8cfa-417a-8cf2-4e06b5e236a1)\r\n\r\nSo the actual way to do this is to copy the entire default from the UI Advanced tab and then add defaults to the schema definitions, which is not clear or, in my opinion, a very clean way to do this. Perhaps there's a simpler way that I'm missing.\r\n\r\n### Describe the proposed change\r\n\r\nI would recommend detailing that the way to set defaults on a work pool using the `--base-job-template` argument of the CLI be included in the documentation, explaining that the best way to do this is to copy the values from the Advanced tab of the UI into a file and then adding defaults where desired in the schema definition.\r\n\r\nAlthough honestly I think the better option would be to find a better way to set defaults, especially because I have a feeling this method is going to override any default schema changes made in later versions of Prefect, and that won't be obvious until there is a desired feature/value missing from that schema.\r\n\r\n### Additional context\r\n\r\n_No response_\n", "hints_text": "hi @NodeJSmith - thank you for the issue!\r\n\r\nI agree that the ideas behind the base job template could use some more explanation in the docs - we'll add this to the docs backlog\nI am trying to switch to using the Python client instead of the cli - I've finally gotten defaults working in the work pool create client call, but it is a lot of code, a lot more than I'd expected. Is the below the right way to do this, or am I missing something simpler?\r\n\r\n```python\r\n\r\noverride_kwargs = {\"image\": \"test-image:latest\", \"image_pull_policy\": \"Never\", \"auto_remove\": True}\r\n\r\njob_config = DockerWorkerJobConfiguration()\r\n\r\njob_config_schema = json.loads(job_config.schema_json())\r\njob_config_schema.pop(\"title\")\r\n\r\nfor k, v in override_kwargs.items():\r\n    job_config_schema[\"properties\"][k][\"default\"] = v\r\n\r\njob_config_schema = {\"variables\": job_config_schema}\r\n\r\njob_config_schema[\"job_configuration\"] = job_config.json_template()\r\nwp_create = WorkPoolCreate(\r\n    name=\"test_pool\",\r\n    description=\"Work pool for test\",\r\n    type=\"docker\",\r\n    base_job_template=job_config_schema,\r\n)\r\n\r\nwp_update = WorkPoolUpdate(base_job_template=job_config_schema)\r\n```\n+1 to improving the documentation on this section.\r\n\r\nI've so far spent several days just trying to set my ECS worker to have default values for the `vpc_id` and other infrastructure parameters, and even with the help of Nate (https://prefect-community.slack.com/archives/CL09KU1K7/p1724127210012189) and Bianca Hoch (https://prefect-community.slack.com/archives/CL09KU1K7/p1723717205484319) we still haven't got anything working properly. The only solution we have which partially works right now is a literal string of sed commands on the base template:\r\n\r\n![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/2f70598b-381d-44e6-a8fe-8ecf8202eae7)\r\n", "created_at": "2026-11-14T11:03:35Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23912, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23912", "issue_numbers": ["23859"], "base_commit": "29241a3aff4ad41bf79ed5782ea4059cb74d0b4d", "patch": "diff --git a/ui-v2/src/components/flow-runs/flow-run-details-page/flow-run-subflows.tsx b/ui-v2/src/components/flow-runs/flow-run-details-page/flow-run-subflows.tsx\nindex d2311fe3e19e..6bc1d13eb8f7 100644\n--- a/ui-v2/src/components/flow-runs/flow-run-details-page/flow-run-subflows.tsx\n+++ b/ui-v2/src/components/flow-runs/flow-run-details-page/flow-run-subflows.tsx\n@@ -39,6 +39,8 @@ export const FlowRunSubflows = ({ parentFlowRunId }: FlowRunSubflowsProps) => {\n \n \tconst debouncedSearch = useDebounce(search, 300);\n \n+\t// Unfiltered count, used to tell \"no subflows at all\" apart from\n+\t// \"no subflows match the current filters\".\n \tconst countFilter: FlowRunsCountFilter = useMemo(() => {\n \t\treturn {\n \t\t\tflow_runs: {\n@@ -156,6 +158,11 @@ export const FlowRunSubflows = ({ parentFlowRunId }: FlowRunSubflowsProps) => {\n \n \tconst hasFilters = search || stateFilter.size > 0;\n \n+\t// The heading must reflect the active state and name filters, so derive it\n+\t// from the paginated response (same filter as the list) instead of the\n+\t// unfiltered total. Falls back to the total until the first page resolves.\n+\tconst filteredCount = paginatedData?.count ?? countData;\n+\n \tif (countData === 0) {\n \t\treturn (\n \t\t\t<div className=\"flex items-center justify-center h-48 border rounded-lg bg-muted/50\">\n@@ -171,7 +178,7 @@ export const FlowRunSubflows = ({ parentFlowRunId }: FlowRunSubflowsProps) => {\n \t\t\t<div className=\"grid grid-cols-12 items-center gap-2\">\n \t\t\t\t<div className=\"flex flex-col gap-1 xl:col-span-3 md:col-span-12 col-span-6 md:order-0 order-2\">\n \t\t\t\t\t<p className=\"text-sm text-muted-foreground\">\n-\t\t\t\t\t\t{countData} Subflow run{countData !== 1 ? \"s\" : \"\"}\n+\t\t\t\t\t\t{filteredCount} Subflow run{filteredCount !== 1 ? \"s\" : \"\"}\n \t\t\t\t\t</p>\n \t\t\t\t</div>\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/ui-v2/src/components/flow-runs/flow-run-details-page/flow-run-subflows.test.tsx b/ui-v2/src/components/flow-runs/flow-run-details-page/flow-run-subflows.test.tsx\nindex 7f8b956b0cd0..8ef8b2dde2b0 100644\n--- a/ui-v2/src/components/flow-runs/flow-run-details-page/flow-run-subflows.test.tsx\n+++ b/ui-v2/src/components/flow-runs/flow-run-details-page/flow-run-subflows.test.tsx\n@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@ import {\n \tRouterProvider,\n } from \"@tanstack/react-router\";\n import { render, screen, waitFor } from \"@testing-library/react\";\n+import userEvent from \"@testing-library/user-event\";\n import { buildApiUrl, server } from \"@tests/utils\";\n import { HttpResponse, http } from \"msw\";\n import { createContext, type ReactNode, Suspense, useContext } from \"react\";\n@@ -265,4 +266,66 @@ describe(\"FlowRunSubflows\", () => {\n \t\t\tlimit: 10,\n \t\t});\n \t});\n+\n+\tit(\"updates the displayed count when a state filter narrows the list\", async () => {\n+\t\tconst hasRunningStateFilter = (body: unknown): boolean => {\n+\t\t\tconst state = (\n+\t\t\t\tbody as { flow_runs?: { state?: { name?: { any_?: string[] } } } }\n+\t\t\t)?.flow_runs?.state;\n+\t\t\treturn state?.name?.any_?.includes(\"Running\") ?? false;\n+\t\t};\n+\n+\t\tserver.use(\n+\t\t\thttp.post(buildApiUrl(\"/flow_runs/count\"), async ({ request }) => {\n+\t\t\t\tconst body = await request.json();\n+\t\t\t\treturn HttpResponse.json(hasRunningStateFilter(body) ? 1 : 3);\n+\t\t\t}),\n+\t\t\thttp.post(buildApiUrl(\"/flow_runs/paginate\"), async ({ request }) => {\n+\t\t\t\tconst body = await request.json();\n+\t\t\t\tif (!hasRunningStateFilter(body)) {\n+\t\t\t\t\treturn HttpResponse.json({\n+\t\t\t\t\t\tresults: mockSubflowRuns,\n+\t\t\t\t\t\tcount: 3,\n+\t\t\t\t\t\tpages: 1,\n+\t\t\t\t\t\tpage: 1,\n+\t\t\t\t\t\tlimit: 10,\n+\t\t\t\t\t});\n+\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t\treturn HttpResponse.json({\n+\t\t\t\t\tresults: [mockSubflowRuns[1]],\n+\t\t\t\t\tcount: 1,\n+\t\t\t\t\tpages: 1,\n+\t\t\t\t\tpage: 1,\n+\t\t\t\t\tlimit: 10,\n+\t\t\t\t});\n+\t\t\t}),\n+\t\t\thttp.post(buildApiUrl(\"/flows/filter\"), () =>\n+\t\t\t\tHttpResponse.json(mockFlows),\n+\t\t\t),\n+\t\t\thttp.get(buildApiUrl(\"/observed-states\"), () => HttpResponse.json([])),\n+\t\t\thttp.post(buildApiUrl(\"/ui/flow_runs/count-task-runs\"), () =>\n+\t\t\t\tHttpResponse.json({}),\n+\t\t\t),\n+\t\t);\n+\n+\t\tconst user = userEvent.setup();\n+\n+\t\tawait renderWithProviders(\n+\t\t\t<Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\n+\t\t\t\t<FlowRunSubflows parentFlowRunId=\"test-parent-flow-run-id\" />\n+\t\t\t</Suspense>,\n+\t\t);\n+\n+\t\tawait waitFor(() => {\n+\t\t\texpect(screen.getByText(\"3 Subflow runs\")).toBeInTheDocument();\n+\t\t});\n+\n+\t\tawait user.click(screen.getByRole(\"button\", { name: /All run states/ }));\n+\t\tconst runningOption = await screen.findAllByLabelText(\"Running\");\n+\t\tawait user.click(runningOption[0]);\n+\n+\t\tawait waitFor(() => {\n+\t\t\texpect(screen.getByText(\"1 Subflow run\")).toBeInTheDocument();\n+\t\t});\n+\t});\n });\n", "problem_statement": "UI v2: filtering flow runs by run states does not change the count\n### Bug summary\n\nOn Prefect 3.8.5, UI v2, filtering flow runs by any run states i.e. Running status does not change the displayed count. The list can be filtered, but the count stays at the unfiltered number.\n\n### Reproduction\n\n1. Open UI v2 on a self-hosted Prefect 3.8.5 server with flow runs in more than one state.\n2. Open the flow runs view.\n3. Note the count.\n4. Filter by Running.\n\n### Expected\n\nThe count matches the runs that are Running.\n\n### Actual\n\nThe count does not change when the Running filter is applied.\n\n### Version\n\nPrefect 3.8.5, UI v2, self-hosted.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.8.5\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.11.16\nBuilt:                Thu, Sep 03, 2026 09:29 PM\nOS/Arch:              linux/x86_64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          ephemeral\nPydantic version:     2.13.5\nServer:\n  Database:           Postgres\n  SQLite version:     17\nIntegrations:\n  prefect-kubernetes: 0.7.12\n  prefect-redis:      0.2.14\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\nSelf-hosted Prefect server, UI v2. The runs list itself updates when filtered to Running, but the count shown with that filter stays at the unfiltered total.\n\nWe have a large flow-run history, so a count that ignores the state filter is easy to mistake for the number of running runs.\n\nChecked the 3.8.6 through 3.9.2 release notes and did not find a fix for this. Happy to add a screenshot of the count before and after the Running filter.\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-14T10:09:57Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23911, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23911", "issue_numbers": ["13268"], "base_commit": "29241a3aff4ad41bf79ed5782ea4059cb74d0b4d", "patch": "diff --git a/src/integrations/prefect-aws/prefect_aws/workers/ecs_worker.py b/src/integrations/prefect-aws/prefect_aws/workers/ecs_worker.py\nindex c5b20ae47e24..a0e7f226b27b 100644\n--- a/src/integrations/prefect-aws/prefect_aws/workers/ecs_worker.py\n+++ b/src/integrations/prefect-aws/prefect_aws/workers/ecs_worker.py\n@@ -653,9 +653,13 @@ class ECSVariables(BaseVariables):\n     network_configuration: Dict[str, Any] = Field(\n         default_factory=dict,\n         description=(\n-            \"When `network_configuration` is supplied it will override ECS Worker's\"\n-            \"awsvpcConfiguration that defined in the ECS task executing your workload. \"\n-            \"See the [AWS documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/aws-properties-ecs-service-awsvpcconfiguration.html)\"  # noqa\n+            \"Override the ECS worker's `awsvpcConfiguration`. Use the case-sensitive\"\n+            \" boto3 keys `subnets`, `securityGroups`, and `assignPublicIp`, for example\"\n+            ' `{\"subnets\": [\"subnet-...\"], \"securityGroups\": [\"sg-...\"],'\n+            ' \"assignPublicIp\": \"DISABLED\"}`. Supply these keys directly, without a'\n+            \" surrounding `networkConfiguration` or `awsvpcConfiguration` object.\"\n+            \" Requires `vpc_id`. See the\"\n+            \" [boto3 documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/boto3/latest/reference/services/ecs/client/run_task.html)\"\n             \" for available options.\"\n         ),\n     )\n", "test_patch": "", "problem_statement": "Enhance documentation clarity for JSON field inputs for the ECS work pool and ideally across other work pool types\n### First check\r\n\r\n- [X] I added a descriptive title to this issue.\r\n- [X] I used GitHub search to find a similar request and didn't find it \ud83d\ude07\r\n\r\n### Describe the issue\r\n\r\nWhen a user goes in to add network connection information to their ECS worker, the UX is like:\r\n![image](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/assets/68969861/dfac0980-6ffb-47b8-84a9-3d38bee8398d)\r\n\r\nWhen they click on the [link to aws docs](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/aws-properties-ecs-service-awsvpcconfiguration.html) they are given the following JSON example:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"AssignPublicIp\" : String,\r\n  \"SecurityGroups\" : [ String, ... ],\r\n  \"Subnets\" : [ String, ... ]\r\n}\r\n\r\n```\r\n^Notice a capital **`S`** for `Subnets`.\r\n\r\nHowever, further research into AWS docs led me to \r\n\r\n>If you are defining ECS services in a CloudFormation template, use \"Subnets\". If you are using the Boto3 client, use \"subnets\".\r\n\r\nWith respect to the Prefect ECS work pool, we employ boto3. The request that is happening under the hood is [RunTask](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECS/latest/APIReference/API_RunTask.html), so a lowercase `s` is used.\r\n ```\r\n   \"networkConfiguration\": { \r\n      \"awsvpcConfiguration\": { \r\n         \"assignPublicIp\": \"string\",\r\n         \"securityGroups\": [ \"string\" ],\r\n         \"subnets\": [ \"string\" ]\r\n      }\r\n   },\r\n```\r\nThe following worked for the user after extensive trial and error:\r\n\r\n```\r\n{\"awsvpcConfiguration\":{\"subnets\":[\"subnet-xx\",\"subnet-xx\"],\"assignPublicIp\":\"DISABLED\"}}\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhile adding the following addition to the base job template:\r\n```\r\n      \"launchType\": \"{{ launch_type }}\",\r\n      \"taskDefinition\": \"{{ task_definition_arn }}\",\r\n      \"networkConfiguration\": \"{{ network_configuration }}\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n### Describe the proposed change\r\n\r\nIdeally the in-UI and external documentation makes it clearer what should be included in each text/JSON block. We recommend enhancing documentation clarity for JSON field inputs across all work pool types by providing precise examples that reflect the required API or framework and aligning field names with their actual API counterparts, ensuring correct casing and format. This will help ensure accuracy and improve user experience.\r\n\r\n### Additional context\r\n\r\n_No response_\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-14T08:38:11Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23905, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23905", "issue_numbers": ["23904"], "base_commit": "29241a3aff4ad41bf79ed5782ea4059cb74d0b4d", "patch": "diff --git a/src/integrations/prefect-email/prefect_email/message.py b/src/integrations/prefect-email/prefect_email/message.py\nindex 9f4859b9f6db..4eea54d1c700 100644\n--- a/src/integrations/prefect-email/prefect_email/message.py\n+++ b/src/integrations/prefect-email/prefect_email/message.py\n@@ -113,10 +113,9 @@ def example_email_send_message_flow():\n \n         encoders.encode_base64(part)\n         filename = os.path.basename(filepath)\n-        part.add_header(\n-            \"Content-Disposition\",\n-            f\"attachment; filename= {filename}\",\n-        )\n+        # pass filename as a parameter so it is quoted, and RFC 2231 encoded\n+        # when it is not ASCII\n+        part.add_header(\"Content-Disposition\", \"attachment\", filename=filename)\n         message.attach(part)\n \n     for cid, filepath in (inline_images or {}).items():\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/src/integrations/prefect-email/tests/test_message.py b/src/integrations/prefect-email/tests/test_message.py\nindex df6f7126b91d..b96986a803ac 100644\n--- a/src/integrations/prefect-email/tests/test_message.py\n+++ b/src/integrations/prefect-email/tests/test_message.py\n@@ -1,4 +1,6 @@\n import base64\n+import email\n+import email.policy\n import pathlib\n \n import pytest\n@@ -83,3 +85,27 @@ async def test_flow():\n         if isinstance(val, list):\n             val = \", \".join(val)\n         assert message[key] == val\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"filename\", [\"Q3 report.txt\", \"r\u00e9sum\u00e9.txt\"])\n+async def test_email_send_message_attachment_filename(\n+    filename, tmp_path, email_server_credentials\n+):\n+    attachment = tmp_path / filename\n+    attachment.write_bytes(b\"attachment contents\")\n+\n+    @flow\n+    async def test_flow():\n+        return await email_send_message(\n+            email_server_credentials=email_server_credentials,\n+            subject=\"Example Flow Notification\",\n+            msg=\"<h1>Report attached</h1>\",\n+            attachments=[str(attachment)],\n+            email_to=\"someone@email.com\",\n+        )\n+\n+    message = await test_flow()\n+\n+    # parse the message as a mail client would receive it\n+    received = email.message_from_bytes(message.as_bytes(), policy=email.policy.default)\n+    assert [part.get_filename() for part in received.iter_attachments()] == [filename]\n", "problem_statement": "prefect-email: attachment filenames with spaces or non-ASCII characters are garbled\n### Bug summary\n\n`email_send_message` in prefect-email sets the attachment header as `attachment; filename= <name>`, with the name unquoted. Mail clients that parse the header per RFC 2183 cut the filename at the first space, `;` or `,`, and a non-ASCII name makes the email package encode the whole header as one encoded word, so the name comes back mangled:\n\n```python\nimport email\nimport email.policy\nfrom email.mime.base import MIMEBase\n\nfor name in [\"Q3 report.pdf\", \"r\u00e9sum\u00e9.pdf\"]:\n    part = MIMEBase(\"application\", \"octet-stream\")\n    # what email_send_message does in prefect_email/message.py\n    part.add_header(\"Content-Disposition\", f\"attachment; filename= {name}\")\n    parsed = email.message_from_bytes(part.as_bytes(), policy=email.policy.default)\n    print(repr(name), \"->\", repr(parsed.get_filename()))\n```\n\n```\n'Q3 report.pdf' -> 'Q3'\n'r\u00e9sum\u00e9.pdf' -> 'r\u00e9sum\u00e9. pdf'\n```\n\nPassing the name as a header parameter (`part.add_header(\"Content-Disposition\", \"attachment\", filename=filename)`) quotes it and uses RFC 2231 encoding when needed.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nprefect: 3.9.3 (main at 29241a3af)\nprefect-email: main at 29241a3af\nPython: 3.12.13\nOS: Windows-11-10.0.26200-SP0\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\nThe header is built in `src/integrations/prefect-email/prefect_email/message.py`, in the attachments loop of `email_send_message`.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-13T23:51:31Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23897, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23897", "issue_numbers": ["23896"], "base_commit": "69d45c6146cbb8b991b67485fb66ae3a4dbc9336", "patch": "diff --git a/.github/workflows/codspeed-benchmarks.yaml b/.github/workflows/codspeed-benchmarks.yaml\nindex f16e2ff60c49..e7fbab9a5224 100644\n--- a/.github/workflows/codspeed-benchmarks.yaml\n+++ b/.github/workflows/codspeed-benchmarks.yaml\n@@ -71,9 +71,17 @@ jobs:\n           # reference prefect.* warning classes, which would otherwise\n           # trigger a prefect import during pytest startup.\n           #\n+          # --timeout=600 raises the global 90s pytest-timeout for the import\n+          # benchmarks: cold imports run far slower under simulation mode and\n+          # the heavier ones (e.g. prefect.server.api.server) exceed 90s.\n+          #\n           # pytest-codspeed writes each invocation's results to a unique file\n           # (keyed by PID), so the CodSpeed action collects all of them.\n+          #\n+          # Failures are tracked in `status` so every benchmark still runs,\n+          # but any failing invocation fails the job.\n           run: |\n+            status=0\n             for module in \\\n               prefect \\\n               prefect.client.schemas.objects \\\n@@ -84,10 +92,10 @@ jobs:\n               prefect.task_runners \\\n             ; do\n               uv run pytest \"benches/bench_import.py::bench_import_module[$module]\" \\\n-                --codspeed -vv -o 'filterwarnings='\n+                --codspeed -vv -o 'filterwarnings=' --timeout=600 || status=1\n             done\n             uv run pytest benches/bench_import.py::bench_import_prefect_flow \\\n-              --codspeed -vv -o 'filterwarnings='\n+              --codspeed -vv -o 'filterwarnings=' --timeout=600 || status=1\n             uv run pytest \\\n               benches/bench_tasks.py \\\n               benches/bench_flows.py::bench_flow_decorator \\\n@@ -95,5 +103,6 @@ jobs:\n               --codspeed \\\n               --numprocesses auto \\\n               --dist worksteal \\\n-              -vv\n+              -vv || status=1\n+            exit $status\n           token: ${{ secrets.CODSPEED_TOKEN }}\n", "test_patch": "", "problem_statement": "CodSpeed: `bench_import_module[prefect.server.api.server]` hits the 90 s pytest-timeout on every run, and the job still passes\n### Bug summary\n\nIn the CodSpeed Benchmarks workflow, the cold-import benchmark for `prefect.server.api.server` fails on every run on `main` with `Failed: Timeout (>90.0s) from pytest-timeout`. The limit comes from the global `timeout = 90` in [`pyproject.toml`](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/69d45c6146cbb8b991b67485fb66ae3a4dbc9336/pyproject.toml#L303), and under CodSpeed's simulation mode this import takes longer than that. The `for module in ...` loop in [`codspeed-benchmarks.yaml`](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/69d45c6146cbb8b991b67485fb66ae3a4dbc9336/.github/workflows/codspeed-benchmarks.yaml#L76-L88) has no `set -e`, so the script's exit status is that of the last `pytest` call: the job passes and this benchmark never produces a result.\n\nTo see it, open the \"Run benchmarks\" step of the latest run on `main`, [run 43491690878](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/actions/runs/43491690878/job/132114234766) (2026-11-13, green):\n\n```\nbenches/bench_import.py::bench_import_module[prefect.server.api.server] FAILED [100%]\n...\nFAILED benches/bench_import.py::bench_import_module[prefect.server.api.server] - Failed: Timeout (>90.0s) from pytest-timeout.\n=================== 1 failed, 6 warnings in 95.44s (0:01:35) ===================\n```\n\nThe same failure is in every green run on `main` I checked between 09-01 and 11-13: [33902417460](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/actions/runs/33902417460), [36280193815](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/actions/runs/36280193815), [38960623072](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/actions/runs/38960623072), [41223453614](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/actions/runs/41223453614), [41914629697](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/actions/runs/41914629697), [43317793280](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/actions/runs/43317793280) and the one above. Logs from before September have expired.\n\n[`benches/bench_import.py`](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/69d45c6146cbb8b991b67485fb66ae3a4dbc9336/benches/bench_import.py#L40-L52) says each entry \"is the deepest leaf in its import chain so that a regression in any parent module (e.g. prefect.server.schemas) is still caught transitively\". So at the moment a slowdown in the server import chain would not show up. The CodSpeed PR reports also show `1 skipped benchmark ... the baseline result was used instead` (for example on #23679). I couldn't check from outside CodSpeed which benchmark that is, but this one would appear that way.\n\nA possible fix, in the workflow's `run:` script:\n- give the import benchmarks a timeout that fits simulation mode, for example `--timeout=0` or `-o timeout=600` on the `uv run pytest` call inside the loop (the job's `timeout-minutes: 20` still bounds it), or `@pytest.mark.timeout(...)` on `bench_import_module`;\n- start the script with `set -e`, so a failing benchmark fails the job.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nNot version-specific: CI on main at 69d45c6 (pytest-codspeed 5.0.3, CodSpeedHQ/action v5.6.3, mode: simulation).\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\nFound and written up with Claude Code (an AI coding assistant) while reading benchmark job logs. The log lines above are copied from the linked runs.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-13T20:19:28Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23886, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23886", "issue_numbers": ["23373"], "base_commit": "7ec97d9bd4a941a7ace4f10e4325bf597902e31e", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/flow_engine.py b/src/prefect/flow_engine.py\nindex 25c213de0b58..0cba9aa991b6 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/flow_engine.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/flow_engine.py\n@@ -2273,10 +2273,12 @@ async def run_generator_flow_async(\n                         # link the current state to the result for dependency tracking\n                         link_state_to_flow_run_result(engine.state, gen_result)\n                         yield gen_result\n-                except (StopAsyncIteration, GeneratorExit) as exc:\n+                except StopAsyncIteration:\n                     await engine.handle_success(None)\n-                    if isinstance(exc, GeneratorExit):\n-                        gen.throw(exc)\n+                except GeneratorExit:\n+                    await engine.handle_success(None)\n+                    await gen.aclose()\n+                    raise\n \n     # async generators can't return, but we can raise failures here\n     if engine.state.is_failed():\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/test_flow_engine.py b/tests/test_flow_engine.py\nindex e76c3af56a9a..150e9364c34c 100644\n--- a/tests/test_flow_engine.py\n+++ b/tests/test_flow_engine.py\n@@ -5072,6 +5072,43 @@ async def g(required: str, model: TheModel = {\"x\": [1, 2, 3]}):  # type: ignore\n \n \n class TestAsyncGenerators:\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"retries\", [0, 1])\n+    async def test_generator_flow_closed_early_is_completed(\n+        self, prefect_client: PrefectClient, retries: int\n+    ):\n+        starts: list[str] = []\n+        hooks: list[str] = []\n+        finally_ran_in_context: list[bool] = []\n+\n+        @flow(\n+            retries=retries,\n+            on_completion=[lambda *args: hooks.append(\"completion\")],\n+            on_failure=[lambda *args: hooks.append(\"failure\")],\n+        )\n+        async def g() -> AsyncGenerator[UUID, None]:\n+            starts.append(\"start\")\n+            try:\n+                yield get_run_context().flow_run.id\n+            finally:\n+                finally_ran_in_context.append(FlowRunContext.get() is not None)\n+\n+        gen = g()\n+        async for fr_id in gen:\n+            break\n+        await gen.aclose()\n+\n+        run = await prefect_client.read_flow_run(fr_id)\n+        assert run.state.is_completed()\n+        history = await prefect_client.read_flow_run_states(fr_id)\n+        assert [state.type for state in history] == [\n+            StateType.PENDING,\n+            StateType.RUNNING,\n+            StateType.COMPLETED,\n+        ]\n+        assert starts == [\"start\"]\n+        assert hooks == [\"completion\"]\n+        assert finally_ran_in_context == [True]\n+\n     async def test_generator_flow(self):\n         \"\"\"\n         Test for generator behavior including StopIteration\n", "problem_statement": "Breaking out of an async generator flow marks the run Failed after Completed\n### Bug summary\n\nWhen I stop consuming an async generator flow early (`break` and then `aclose()`), the flow run is first set to `Completed` and then to `Failed` with `AttributeError: 'async_generator' object has no attribute 'throw'`. Both the `on_completion` and the `on_failure` hooks run for the same flow run, and the generator's own `finally` block does not run while the flow run context is open. The same pattern with a sync generator flow ends `Completed` as expected.\n\nRepro (ephemeral server):\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Breaking out of an async generator flow ends the run Failed after Completed.\"\"\"\n\nimport asyncio\n\nfrom prefect import flow\nfrom prefect.client.orchestration import get_client\nfrom prefect.context import FlowRunContext\n\nhooks: list[tuple[str, str]] = []\nfinally_ran: list[tuple[str, bool]] = []\nrun_ids: dict[str, object] = {}\n\n\ndef on_completion(flow, flow_run, state):\n    hooks.append((flow.name, \"completion\"))\n\n\ndef on_failure(flow, flow_run, state):\n    hooks.append((flow.name, \"failure\"))\n\n\n@flow(name=\"sgen\", on_completion=[on_completion], on_failure=[on_failure])\ndef sync_gen():\n    run_ids[\"sgen\"] = FlowRunContext.get().flow_run.id\n    try:\n        for i in range(5):\n            yield i\n    finally:\n        finally_ran.append((\"sgen\", FlowRunContext.get() is not None))\n\n\n@flow(name=\"agen\", on_completion=[on_completion], on_failure=[on_failure])\nasync def async_gen():\n    run_ids[\"agen\"] = FlowRunContext.get().flow_run.id\n    try:\n        for i in range(5):\n            yield i\n    finally:\n        finally_ran.append((\"agen\", FlowRunContext.get() is not None))\n\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    for i in sync_gen():\n        if i == 1:\n            break\n\n    g = async_gen()\n    async for i in g:\n        if i == 1:\n            break\n    await g.aclose()\n\n    async with get_client() as client:\n        for name in (\"sgen\", \"agen\"):\n            run = await client.read_flow_run(run_ids[name])\n            history = await client.read_flow_run_states(run_ids[name])\n            print(f\"{name}: final state {run.state.type.value}, history {[s.name for s in history]}\")\n    print(\"hooks fired:\", hooks)\n    print(\"generator finally blocks that have run (name, FlowRunContext present):\", finally_ran)\n    print(\"expected: agen ends COMPLETED with history ['Pending', 'Running', 'Completed'],\"\n          \" only the completion hook fires, and its finally runs inside the flow run context\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    asyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nOutput (run with `PREFECT_LOGGING_LEVEL=WARNING`):\n\n```\n05:57:14.638 | ERROR   | Flow run 'bold-asp' - Encountered exception during execution: AttributeError(\"'async_generator' object has no attribute 'throw'\")\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \".../site-packages/prefect/flow_engine.py\", line 2213, in run_generator_flow_async\n    yield gen_result\nGeneratorExit\n\nDuring handling of the above exception, another exception occurred:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \".../site-packages/prefect/flow_engine.py\", line 2073, in run_context\n    yield self\n  File \".../site-packages/prefect/flow_engine.py\", line 2217, in run_generator_flow_async\n    gen.throw(exc)\n    ^^^^^^^^^\nAttributeError: 'async_generator' object has no attribute 'throw'. Did you mean: 'athrow'?\n05:57:14.654 | ERROR   | Flow run 'bold-asp' - Finished in state Failed(\"Flow run encountered an exception: AttributeError: 'async_generator' object has no attribute 'throw'\")\nsgen: final state COMPLETED, history ['Pending', 'Running', 'Completed']\nagen: final state FAILED, history ['Pending', 'Running', 'Completed', 'Failed']\nhooks fired: [('sgen', 'completion'), ('agen', 'completion'), ('agen', 'failure')]\ngenerator finally blocks that have run (name, FlowRunContext present): [('sgen', True)]\nexpected: agen ends COMPLETED with history ['Pending', 'Running', 'Completed'], only the completion hook fires, and its finally runs inside the flow run context\n```\n\nExpected: the async flow run ends `Completed` like the sync one, only `on_completion` fires, and the generator receives `GeneratorExit` (so its `finally` runs) while the flow run context is still active.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.8.8\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.12.3\nGit commit:           b73b09dc\nBuilt:                Tue, Oct 06, 2026 04:33 PM\nOS/Arch:              linux/x86_64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          ephemeral\nPydantic version:     2.14.0\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.45.1\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\nCause, at `e9bc082` on `main`: in `run_generator_flow_async` (`src/prefect/flow_engine.py` lines 2214-2217), the `except (StopAsyncIteration, GeneratorExit)` branch awaits `engine.handle_success(None)`, which records `Completed`, and then calls `gen.throw(exc)` on the user's async generator. Async generator objects only have `athrow`, so this raises `AttributeError`, which the `except Exception` in `run_context` (around line 2076) turns into `handle_exception` and a `Failed` state on top of `Completed`. The sync version at lines 2174-2176 uses `gen.throw` on a sync generator, which is valid, so the two paths differ only here. `TestAsyncGenerators` in `tests/test_flow_engine.py` only covers exhausting the generator or letting it raise.\n\nProposed fix: close the user generator with `await gen.aclose()` (or `await gen.athrow(exc)`, handling the `StopAsyncIteration` or `GeneratorExit` it re-raises) and then re-raise `GeneratorExit`, as the sync path does. A `TestAsyncGenerators` case that breaks early and asserts the run ends `Completed` with only the completion hook run would cover it.\n\nI'm happy to open a PR for this.\n\n", "hints_text": "Hi, I'd like to work on this.\n\nI checked current main, and the bug is still there. `run_generator_flow_async` in `src/prefect/flow_engine.py` and `run_generator_task_async` in `src/prefect/task_engine.py` both call `gen.throw(exc)` when they catch `GeneratorExit`. Async generators do not have `throw`, so that raises `AttributeError`. The run is first marked Completed, then Failed, both `on_completion` and `on_failure` fire, and the generator's `finally` does not run while the flow or task run context is still open. The sync path already uses `gen.throw` correctly.\n\nThere is a stale bot PR, #23375, that tried the right idea (`await gen.aclose()` and re-raise `GeneratorExit`) and was closed by the stale bot without a maintainer review. I would not revive that branch. I would open a fresh PR that:\n\n- In both async helpers, on `GeneratorExit` calls `await gen.aclose()` and re-raises, and keeps `StopAsyncIteration` on its own branch.\n- Leaves the sync helpers unchanged.\n- Adds regression tests that follow the issue's repro: break out of a sync generator flow (still Completed), break out of an async generator flow and task then `aclose()`, and assert the final state is Completed, the history never includes Failed, only the completion hook runs, and the generator `finally` runs with the run context still open.\n\nEdge cases I would cover: early close after the first yield, close before any yield, a `finally` that itself fails, and the same cases for a generator task.\n\nHappy to open the PR once this direction looks right. Could you assign me?\n", "created_at": "2026-11-13T17:11:05Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23856, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23856", "issue_numbers": ["18701"], "base_commit": "7ec97d9bd4a941a7ace4f10e4325bf597902e31e", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/server/api/deployments.py b/src/prefect/server/api/deployments.py\nindex 4176a728f8d7..47ad12459dbe 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/server/api/deployments.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/server/api/deployments.py\n@@ -4,12 +4,13 @@\n \n import datetime\n import logging\n-from typing import List, Optional\n+from typing import List, Optional, Sequence\n from uuid import UUID\n \n import jsonschema.exceptions\n import sqlalchemy as sa\n from fastapi import Body, Depends, HTTPException, Path, Response\n+from sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import AsyncSession\n \n import prefect.server.api.dependencies as dependencies\n import prefect.server.models as models\n@@ -62,6 +63,50 @@ def _multiple_schedules_error(deployment_id) -> HTTPException:\n     )\n \n \n+async def _hydrate_schedule_parameters(\n+    session: AsyncSession,\n+    schedules: Sequence[\n+        schemas.actions.DeploymentScheduleCreate\n+        | schemas.actions.DeploymentScheduleUpdate\n+    ],\n+) -> None:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Hydrate the parameter overrides of `schedules` in place, the same way\n+    deployment parameters are hydrated, so that scheduled runs receive values\n+    rather than the dehydrated form the UI sends.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    schedules_with_parameters = [\n+        schedule for schedule in schedules if schedule.parameters\n+    ]\n+    if not schedules_with_parameters:\n+        return\n+\n+    try:\n+        ctx = await HydrationContext.build(\n+            session=session,\n+            raise_on_error=True,\n+            render_jinja=True,\n+            render_workspace_variables=True,\n+        )\n+        for schedule in schedules_with_parameters:\n+            parameters = hydrate(schedule.parameters, ctx)\n+            # A wrapper around all the overrides can hydrate to any JSON value\n+            if not isinstance(parameters, dict):  # pyright: ignore[reportUnnecessaryIsInstance]\n+                raise HTTPException(\n+                    status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST,\n+                    detail=(\n+                        \"Error hydrating schedule parameters: Parameters must be a\"\n+                        \" JSON object\"\n+                    ),\n+                )\n+            schedule.parameters = parameters\n+    except HydrationError as exc:\n+        raise HTTPException(\n+            status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST,\n+            detail=f\"Error hydrating schedule parameters: {exc}\",\n+        )\n+\n+\n @router.post(\"/\")\n async def create_deployment(\n     deployment: schemas.actions.DeploymentCreate,\n@@ -106,6 +151,8 @@ async def create_deployment(\n                 deployment,\n             )\n \n+        await _hydrate_schedule_parameters(session, deployment.schedules)\n+\n         # hydrate the input model into a full model\n         deployment_dict: dict = deployment.model_dump(\n             exclude={\"work_pool_name\"},\n@@ -225,6 +272,8 @@ async def update_deployment(\n                 status.HTTP_404_NOT_FOUND, detail=\"Deployment not found.\"\n             )\n \n+        await _hydrate_schedule_parameters(session, deployment.schedules)\n+\n         # Checking how we should handle schedule updates\n         # If not all existing schedules have slugs then we'll fall back to the existing logic where are schedules are recreated to match the request.\n         # If the existing schedules have slugs, but not all provided schedules have slugs, then we'll return a 422 to avoid accidentally blowing away schedules.\n@@ -1283,6 +1332,8 @@ async def create_deployment_schedules(\n                 status.HTTP_404_NOT_FOUND, detail=\"Deployment not found.\"\n             )\n \n+        await _hydrate_schedule_parameters(session, schedules)\n+\n         try:\n             created = await models.deployments.create_deployment_schedules(\n                 session=session,\n@@ -1320,6 +1371,8 @@ async def update_deployment_schedule(\n                 status.HTTP_404_NOT_FOUND, detail=\"Deployment not found.\"\n             )\n \n+        await _hydrate_schedule_parameters(session, [schedule])\n+\n         updated = await models.deployments.update_deployment_schedule(\n             session=session,\n             deployment_id=deployment_id,\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/utilities/schema_tools/hydration.py b/src/prefect/utilities/schema_tools/hydration.py\nindex 3909b2b1cef6..ca5fbafee041 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/utilities/schema_tools/hydration.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/utilities/schema_tools/hydration.py\n@@ -140,6 +140,15 @@ def message(self) -> str:\n         return message\n \n \n+class InvalidPrefectKind(HydrationError):\n+    @property\n+    def message(self) -> str:\n+        message = \"Invalid __prefect_kind\"\n+        if self.detail:\n+            message += f\": {self.detail}\"\n+        return message\n+\n+\n class WorkspaceVariableNotFound(HydrationError):\n     @property\n     def variable_name(self) -> str:\n@@ -178,10 +187,15 @@ def decorator(func: Handler) -> Handler:\n \n \n def call_handler(kind: PrefectKind, obj: dict[str, Any], ctx: HydrationContext) -> Any:\n-    if kind not in _handlers:\n+    # Unhashable kinds can't be looked up in `_handlers`; other unrecognized\n+    # kinds pass `value` through below\n+    if isinstance(kind, (dict, list)):\n+        res = InvalidPrefectKind(detail=f\"expected a string, got {type(kind).__name__}\")\n+    elif kind not in _handlers:\n         return obj.get(\"value\", None)\n+    else:\n+        res = _handlers[kind](obj, ctx)\n \n-    res = _handlers[kind](obj, ctx)\n     if ctx.raise_on_error and isinstance(res, HydrationError):\n         raise res\n     return res\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployment_schedules.py b/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployment_schedules.py\nindex 0329cc60c584..e8bc79ee42ab 100644\n--- a/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployment_schedules.py\n+++ b/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployment_schedules.py\n@@ -1,5 +1,5 @@\n from datetime import timedelta\n-from typing import Callable, Optional\n+from typing import Any, Callable, Optional\n from uuid import UUID, uuid4\n \n import pytest\n@@ -8,7 +8,7 @@\n from httpx import AsyncClient\n \n from prefect.server import models, schemas\n-from prefect.server.database import PrefectDBInterface\n+from prefect.server.database import PrefectDBInterface, orm_models\n from prefect.types._datetime import now\n from tests.server import AsyncSessionGetter\n \n@@ -174,6 +174,33 @@ async def test_schedule_slug_must_be_unique_within_deployment(\n \n         assert response.status_code == status.HTTP_409_CONFLICT\n \n+    async def test_create_hydrates_parameters(\n+        self,\n+        client: AsyncClient,\n+        schedules_url: Callable[..., str],\n+        deployment: orm_models.Deployment,\n+    ):\n+        # The UI sends parameters edited in JSON mode in their dehydrated form\n+        response = await client.post(\n+            schedules_url(deployment.id),\n+            json=[\n+                {\n+                    \"schedule\": {\"interval\": 3600},\n+                    \"parameters\": {\n+                        \"tables_to_fetch\": {\n+                            \"__prefect_kind\": \"json\",\n+                            \"value\": '[{\"prop\": \"value\", \"some_arr\": []}]',\n+                        }\n+                    },\n+                }\n+            ],\n+        )\n+\n+        assert response.status_code == status.HTTP_201_CREATED\n+        assert response.json()[0][\"parameters\"] == {\n+            \"tables_to_fetch\": [{\"prop\": \"value\", \"some_arr\": []}]\n+        }\n+\n     async def test_404_non_existent_deployment(\n         self,\n         client: AsyncClient,\n@@ -333,6 +360,82 @@ async def test_update_keeps_parameters_it_does_not_set(\n \n         assert the_schedule.parameters == {\"foo\": \"bar\"}\n \n+    async def test_update_hydrates_parameters(\n+        self,\n+        get_server_session: AsyncSessionGetter,\n+        client: AsyncClient,\n+        deployment_with_schedules: orm_models.Deployment,\n+        schedules_url: Callable[..., str],\n+        schedule_to_update: schemas.core.DeploymentSchedule,\n+    ):\n+        url = schedules_url(\n+            deployment_with_schedules.id, schedule_id=schedule_to_update.id\n+        )\n+        response = await client.patch(\n+            url,\n+            json={\n+                \"parameters\": {\n+                    \"tables_to_fetch\": {\n+                        \"__prefect_kind\": \"json\",\n+                        \"value\": '[{\"prop\": \"value\", \"some_arr\": []}]',\n+                    }\n+                }\n+            },\n+        )\n+\n+        assert response.status_code == status.HTTP_204_NO_CONTENT\n+\n+        async with get_server_session() as session:\n+            schedules = await models.deployments.read_deployment_schedules(\n+                session=session,\n+                deployment_id=deployment_with_schedules.id,\n+            )\n+\n+        the_schedule = next(\n+            schedule for schedule in schedules if schedule.id == schedule_to_update.id\n+        )\n+\n+        assert the_schedule.parameters == {\n+            \"tables_to_fetch\": [{\"prop\": \"value\", \"some_arr\": []}]\n+        }\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"parameters, error\",\n+        [\n+            (\n+                {\"x\": {\"__prefect_kind\": \"json\", \"value\": '{\"invalid\": json}'}},\n+                \"Invalid JSON: Expecting value:\",\n+            ),\n+            (\n+                {\"__prefect_kind\": \"json\", \"value\": \"[1, 2]\"},\n+                \"Parameters must be a JSON object\",\n+            ),\n+            (\n+                {\"x\": {\"__prefect_kind\": []}},\n+                \"Invalid __prefect_kind: expected a string, got list\",\n+            ),\n+        ],\n+        ids=[\"invalid-json\", \"not-an-object\", \"list-kind\"],\n+    )\n+    async def test_update_with_parameters_that_fail_to_hydrate(\n+        self,\n+        client: AsyncClient,\n+        deployment_with_schedules: orm_models.Deployment,\n+        schedules_url: Callable[..., str],\n+        schedule_to_update: schemas.core.DeploymentSchedule,\n+        parameters: dict[str, Any],\n+        error: str,\n+    ):\n+        url = schedules_url(\n+            deployment_with_schedules.id, schedule_id=schedule_to_update.id\n+        )\n+        response = await client.patch(url, json={\"parameters\": parameters})\n+\n+        assert response.status_code == status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST\n+        assert (\n+            f\"Error hydrating schedule parameters: {error}\" in response.json()[\"detail\"]\n+        )\n+\n     async def test_404_non_existent_deployment(\n         self,\n         client: AsyncClient,\ndiff --git a/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployments.py b/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployments.py\nindex b09cdc9c83ad..e3d5e5a73e4e 100644\n--- a/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployments.py\n+++ b/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployments.py\n@@ -280,6 +280,29 @@ async def test_create_deployment_with_multiple_schedules_populates_legacy_schedu\n         assert len(schedules) == 2\n         assert schedules[0].id == first_schedule.id\n \n+    async def test_create_deployment_hydrates_schedule_parameters(\n+        self,\n+        client: AsyncClient,\n+        flow: Flow,\n+    ):\n+        response = await client.post(\n+            \"/deployments/\",\n+            json={\n+                \"name\": \"My Deployment\",\n+                \"flow_id\": str(flow.id),\n+                \"schedules\": [\n+                    {\n+                        \"schedule\": {\"interval\": 3600},\n+                        \"parameters\": {\n+                            \"x\": {\"__prefect_kind\": \"json\", \"value\": '\"str_of_json\"'}\n+                        },\n+                    }\n+                ],\n+            },\n+        )\n+        assert response.status_code == 201\n+        assert response.json()[\"schedules\"][0][\"parameters\"] == {\"x\": \"str_of_json\"}\n+\n     async def test_default_work_queue_name_is_none(self, session, client, flow):\n         data = DeploymentCreate(name=\"My Deployment\", flow_id=flow.id).model_dump(\n             mode=\"json\"\n@@ -2091,6 +2114,57 @@ async def test_update_deployment_hydrates_workspace_variable_kind_parameters(\n         response = await client.get(f\"/deployments/{deployment.id}\")\n         assert response.json()[\"parameters\"] == {\"x\": value}\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"existing_slug\",\n+        [None, \"test-schedule\"],\n+        ids=[\"replaces-schedules\", \"updates-schedules-by-slug\"],\n+    )\n+    async def test_update_deployment_hydrates_schedule_parameters(\n+        self,\n+        client: AsyncClient,\n+        flow: Flow,\n+        existing_slug: str | None,\n+    ):\n+        response = await client.post(\n+            \"/deployments/\",\n+            json={\n+                \"name\": \"My Deployment\",\n+                \"flow_id\": str(flow.id),\n+                \"schedules\": [{\"schedule\": {\"interval\": 3600}, \"slug\": existing_slug}],\n+            },\n+        )\n+        assert response.status_code == 201\n+        deployment_id = response.json()[\"id\"]\n+\n+        parameters = {\"x\": {\"__prefect_kind\": \"json\", \"value\": '\"str_of_json\"'}}\n+        response = await client.patch(\n+            f\"/deployments/{deployment_id}\",\n+            json={\n+                \"schedules\": [\n+                    {\n+                        \"schedule\": {\"interval\": 3600},\n+                        \"slug\": \"test-schedule\",\n+                        \"parameters\": parameters,\n+                    },\n+                    {\n+                        \"schedule\": {\"interval\": 7200},\n+                        \"slug\": \"new-schedule\",\n+                        \"parameters\": parameters,\n+                    },\n+                ]\n+            },\n+        )\n+        assert response.status_code == 204\n+\n+        response = await client.get(f\"/deployments/{deployment_id}\")\n+        assert {\n+            schedule[\"slug\"]: schedule[\"parameters\"]\n+            for schedule in response.json()[\"schedules\"]\n+        } == {\n+            \"test-schedule\": {\"x\": \"str_of_json\"},\n+            \"new-schedule\": {\"x\": \"str_of_json\"},\n+        }\n+\n     async def test_update_deployment_can_update_pull_steps(\n         self,\n         client,\ndiff --git a/tests/utilities/schema_tools/test_hydration.py b/tests/utilities/schema_tools/test_hydration.py\nindex cbddde6f7bb0..784cfadfd28f 100644\n--- a/tests/utilities/schema_tools/test_hydration.py\n+++ b/tests/utilities/schema_tools/test_hydration.py\n@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@\n     HydrationContext,\n     InvalidJinja,\n     InvalidJSON,\n+    InvalidPrefectKind,\n     TemplateNotFound,\n     ValidJinja,\n     ValueNotFound,\n@@ -77,6 +78,38 @@ async def test_dont_raise_if_no_error(self):\n         hydrate(values, ctx=HydrationContext(raise_on_error=False))\n \n \n+class TestHydrateWithNonStringPrefectKind:\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"kind, detail\",\n+        [([], \"expected a string, got list\"), ({}, \"expected a string, got dict\")],\n+        ids=[\"list\", \"dict\"],\n+    )\n+    def test_hydrate_returns_error(self, kind: object, detail: str):\n+        assert hydrate({\"param\": {\"__prefect_kind\": kind, \"value\": \"hello\"}}) == {\n+            \"param\": InvalidPrefectKind(detail=detail)\n+        }\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"kind, detail\",\n+        [([], \"expected a string, got list\"), ({}, \"expected a string, got dict\")],\n+        ids=[\"list\", \"dict\"],\n+    )\n+    def test_hydrate_raises_error(self, kind: object, detail: str):\n+        with pytest.raises(InvalidPrefectKind) as exc:\n+            hydrate(\n+                {\"param\": {\"__prefect_kind\": kind, \"value\": \"hello\"}},\n+                ctx=HydrationContext(raise_on_error=True),\n+            )\n+        assert str(exc.value) == f\"Invalid __prefect_kind: {detail}\"\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"kind\", [1, None], ids=[\"int\", \"none\"])\n+    def test_hydrate_passes_value_through(self, kind: object):\n+        \"\"\"Like unrecognized string kinds, these pass `value` through.\"\"\"\n+        assert hydrate({\"param\": {\"__prefect_kind\": kind, \"value\": \"hello\"}}) == {\n+            \"param\": \"hello\"\n+        }\n+\n+\n class TestHydrateWithNonePrefectKind:\n     @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n         \"input_object, expected_output\",\n", "problem_statement": "flow parameters altered after setting schedule & overriding in ui\n### Bug summary\n\nHi all,\n\nI've got a deployed flow that I reuse with different params. \n\nWhen i run the flow once with input params, they get accepted 1:1\n(value in the succesfully ran Web UI \"Parameters\" tab):\n```\njson\n{\n  \"write_dir\": \"\\\\\\\\some\\\\dir\",\n  \"tables_to_fetch\": [\n    {\n      \"prop\": \"value\",\n      \"some_arr\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n\nHowever, when i create a schedule for the deployed flow, and override the params there, the job fails and the parameters tab holds this:\n```\njson\n{\n  \"write_dir\": \"\\\\\\\\some\\\\dir\",\n  \"tables_to_fetch\": {\n    \"value\": \"[\\n            {\\n                \\\"prop\\\": \\\"value\\\",\\n                \\\"some_arr\\\": []\\n            }\\n        ]\",\n    \"__prefect_kind\": \"json\"\n  }\n}\n```\nThe input json gets stringified and gains value and __prefect_kind properties..\n\nI create both the one-time run and the scheduled one in the prefect frontend. \n\nOn a side note, I noticed that variables can't be selected when scheduling a flow, whereas they can be if I select quick run\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:             3.4.11\nAPI version:         0.8.4\nPython version:      3.13.6\nGit commit:          3c98aa7f\nBuilt:               Thu, Jul 31, 2025 08:45 PM\nOS/Arch:             win32/AMD64\nProfile:             local\nServer type:         server\nPydantic version:    2.11.7\nIntegrations:\n  prefect-github:    0.3.1\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_\n", "hints_text": "Experiencing same issue with prefect==3.4.17. Can't schedule any flows on the UI because of this issue.\nthis is still happening in the new year\nI do have the same issue, anyone found a way fix this ? UI is pretty much unusable at this point. Arguments are not getting passed correctly, only yaml works.\n```\nasync def classify_articles(limit: int = 10, model: Optional[str] = None) -> list[str]:\n```\n\n\n```\nEngine execution exited with unexpected exception\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/app/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/prefect/flow_engine.py\", line 1762, in run_flow\n    parameters=_flow_parameters(\n               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/app/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/prefect/flow_engine.py\", line 1802, in _flow_parameters\n    call_args, call_kwargs = parameters_to_args_kwargs(flow.fn, parameters)\n                             ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/app/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/prefect/utilities/callables.py\", line 192, in parameters_to_args_kwargs\n    raise SignatureMismatchError.from_bad_params(\nprefect.exceptions.SignatureMismatchError: Function expects parameters ['limit', 'model'] but was provided with parameters ['value', '__prefect_kind']\n```", "created_at": "2026-11-12T15:20:15Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23853, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23853", "issue_numbers": ["23197"], "base_commit": "a47c471ae4bf5fb83e9475d30aec0db160a8da36", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/server/events/storage/database.py b/src/prefect/server/events/storage/database.py\nindex c2f27ad099aa..79ef879cc6d3 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/server/events/storage/database.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/server/events/storage/database.py\n@@ -197,6 +197,37 @@ async def read_events(\n     return select_events_query_result.scalars().unique().all()\n \n \n+def _replace_null_bytes(value: Any) -> Any:\n+    \"\"\"Replace null bytes with U+FFFD in every string in a JSON-compatible value.\"\"\"\n+    if isinstance(value, str):\n+        return value.replace(\"\\x00\", \"\\ufffd\")\n+    if isinstance(value, dict):\n+        return {\n+            _replace_null_bytes(key): _replace_null_bytes(item)\n+            for key, item in value.items()\n+        }\n+    if isinstance(value, list):\n+        return [_replace_null_bytes(item) for item in value]\n+    return value\n+\n+\n+def _sanitize_event_strings(event: ReceivedEvent) -> ReceivedEvent:\n+    \"\"\"Replace null bytes in an event's strings with U+FFFD.\n+\n+    PostgreSQL rejects null bytes (0x00) in `text` columns with\n+    `CharacterNotInRepertoireError` and in `jsonb` columns with\n+    `UntranslatableCharacterError`. Events are inserted in batches, so one such\n+    event would fail every event written with it. Unlike log messages, the null\n+    bytes are replaced rather than removed, so that a label like\n+    `prefect.resource.id` can't become empty.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    if \"\\\\u0000\" not in event.model_dump_json():\n+        return event\n+    return ReceivedEvent.model_validate(\n+        _replace_null_bytes(event.model_dump(mode=\"json\"))\n+    )\n+\n+\n async def write_events(session: AsyncSession, events: list[ReceivedEvent]) -> None:\n     \"\"\"\n     Write events to the database.\n@@ -206,6 +237,7 @@ async def write_events(session: AsyncSession, events: list[ReceivedEvent]) -> No\n         events: the events to insert\n     \"\"\"\n     if events:\n+        events = [_sanitize_event_strings(event) for event in events]\n         dialect = get_dialect(PREFECT_API_DATABASE_CONNECTION_URL.value())\n         if dialect.name == \"postgresql\":\n             await _write_postgres_events(session, events)\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/events/server/storage/test_database.py b/tests/events/server/storage/test_database.py\nindex 9f7da09c7e81..3082c247d45f 100644\n--- a/tests/events/server/storage/test_database.py\n+++ b/tests/events/server/storage/test_database.py\n@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@\n     _write_postgres_events,\n     get_number_of_event_fields,\n     get_number_of_resource_fields,\n+    query_events,\n     read_events,\n     write_events,\n )\n@@ -209,6 +210,93 @@ async def test_write_events_writes_in_chunks(\n                 assert len(list(results)) == len(event.related) + 1\n \n \n+class TestWriteEventsWithNullBytes:\n+    async def test_write_events_replaces_null_bytes(\n+        self, session: AsyncSession, db: PrefectDBInterface\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"PostgreSQL rejects null bytes (0x00) in `text` and `jsonb` columns, so\n+        they are replaced with U+FFFD before the event is written.\"\"\"\n+        event = ReceivedEvent(\n+            occurred=now(\"UTC\"),\n+            event=\"hello\\x00\",\n+            resource={\"prefect.resource.id\": \"my.id\\x00\", \"label\\x00\": \"value\\x00\"},\n+            related=[\n+                {\n+                    \"prefect.resource.id\": \"related-1\",\n+                    \"prefect.resource.role\": \"role\\x00\",\n+                }\n+            ],\n+            payload={\"stdout\": \"binary\\x00output\", \"nested\\x00\": [{\"key\": \"a\\x00b\"}]},\n+            id=uuid4(),\n+        )\n+\n+        async with session as session:\n+            await write_events(session=session, events=[event])\n+            await session.commit()\n+\n+        async with session as session:\n+            stored = await session.get(db.Event, event.id)\n+            assert stored\n+            assert stored.event == \"hello\\ufffd\"\n+            assert stored.resource == {\n+                \"prefect.resource.id\": \"my.id\\ufffd\",\n+                \"label\\ufffd\": \"value\\ufffd\",\n+            }\n+            assert stored.related == [\n+                {\n+                    \"prefect.resource.id\": \"related-1\",\n+                    \"prefect.resource.role\": \"role\\ufffd\",\n+                }\n+            ]\n+            assert stored.payload == {\n+                \"stdout\": \"binary\\ufffdoutput\",\n+                \"nested\\ufffd\": [{\"key\": \"a\\ufffdb\"}],\n+            }\n+\n+            rows = await session.scalars(\n+                sa.select(db.EventResource)\n+                .where(db.EventResource.event_id == event.id)\n+                .order_by(db.EventResource.resource_role)\n+            )\n+            assert [(r.resource_id, r.resource_role, r.resource) for r in rows] == [\n+                (\"my.id\\ufffd\", \"\", {\"label\\ufffd\": \"value\\ufffd\"}),\n+                (\"related-1\", \"role\\ufffd\", {}),\n+            ]\n+\n+    async def test_write_events_keeps_null_byte_only_labels_valid(\n+        self, session: AsyncSession\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"Removing the null bytes, as is done for logs, would leave these labels\n+        empty, which resource validation rejects.\"\"\"\n+        event = ReceivedEvent(\n+            occurred=now(\"UTC\"),\n+            event=\"hello\",\n+            resource={\"prefect.resource.id\": \"\\x00\"},\n+            related=[\n+                {\"prefect.resource.id\": \"related-1\", \"prefect.resource.role\": \"\\x00\"}\n+            ],\n+            id=uuid4(),\n+        )\n+\n+        async with session as session:\n+            await write_events(session=session, events=[event])\n+            await session.commit()\n+\n+        async with session as session:\n+            events, _, _ = await query_events(\n+                session=session,\n+                filter=EventFilter(\n+                    id=EventIDFilter(id=[event.id]),\n+                    occurred=EventOccurredFilter(\n+                        since=now(\"UTC\") - datetime.timedelta(days=1)\n+                    ),\n+                ),\n+            )\n+            assert len(events) == 1\n+            assert events[0].resource.id == \"\\ufffd\"\n+            assert events[0].related[0].role == \"\\ufffd\"\n+\n+\n class TestReadEvents:\n     @pytest.fixture\n     async def event_1(self, session: AsyncSession) -> ReceivedEvent:\ndiff --git a/tests/events/server/storage/test_event_persister.py b/tests/events/server/storage/test_event_persister.py\nindex 8caeef9ecdc0..d26bbd6fb881 100644\n--- a/tests/events/server/storage/test_event_persister.py\n+++ b/tests/events/server/storage/test_event_persister.py\n@@ -536,6 +536,34 @@ async def sometimes_failing_write_events(session, events):\n     assert (await get_event_count(session)) >= 1\n \n \n+async def test_event_with_null_bytes_does_not_drop_its_batch(\n+    event: ReceivedEvent,\n+    session: AsyncSession,\n+):\n+    \"\"\"PostgreSQL rejects null bytes, and events are written in batches, so one\n+    event with a null byte must not keep the rest of its batch from being stored.\"\"\"\n+    async with event_persister.create_handler(\n+        batch_size=10,\n+        flush_every=timedelta(days=100),\n+    ) as handler:\n+        for _ in range(9):\n+            event.id = uuid4()\n+            message = CapturedMessage(\n+                data=event.model_dump_json().encode(), attributes={}\n+            )\n+            await handler(message)\n+\n+        event.id = uuid4()\n+        event.payload = {\"stdout\": \"binary\\x00output\"}\n+        message = CapturedMessage(data=event.model_dump_json().encode(), attributes={})\n+        await handler(message)\n+\n+    assert (await get_event_count(session)) == 10\n+    stored_event = await get_event(event.id)\n+    assert stored_event\n+    assert stored_event.payload == {\"stdout\": \"binary\\ufffdoutput\"}\n+\n+\n async def test_logs_warning_at_high_queue_capacity(\n     event: ReceivedEvent,\n     caplog: pytest.LogCaptureFixture,\n", "problem_statement": "Event persister drops a whole batch when one event contains a null byte (PostgreSQL)\n\n### Bug summary\n\nOn PostgreSQL, one event with a null byte (`\\x00`) in its name, a resource label, or a payload string makes the event persister drop every event flushed alongside it.\n\n`write_events` inserts a batch in one statement, and PostgreSQL rejects null bytes in `text` columns (`CharacterNotInRepertoireError`) and `jsonb` columns (`UntranslatableCharacterError: unsupported Unicode escape sequence`). The persister requeues the batch, fails again `max_flush_retries` times, then logs `Max flush retries (5) reached, dropping N events` and discards them all. The messages are already acked, so nothing redelivers them, and the client that emitted them sees no error.\n\nA payload carrying captured process output is enough to trigger it. This is the events counterpart of #19292, which #20677 fixed for logs.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom datetime import timedelta\nfrom uuid import uuid4\n\nimport sqlalchemy as sa\n\nfrom prefect.server.database import provide_database_interface\nfrom prefect.server.events.schemas.events import ReceivedEvent\nfrom prefect.server.events.services.event_persister import create_handler\nfrom prefect.server.utilities.messaging.memory import MemoryMessage\nfrom prefect.types._datetime import now\n\n\ndef event(name, payload=None):\n    return ReceivedEvent(\n        occurred=now(\"UTC\"),\n        event=name,\n        resource={\"prefect.resource.id\": \"repro.nul\"},\n        payload=payload or {},\n        id=uuid4(),\n    )\n\n\nasync def main():\n    db = provide_database_interface()\n    await db.create_db()\n    events = [event(\"repro.good\") for _ in range(9)]\n    events.insert(5, event(\"repro.bad\", {\"stdout\": \"binary\\x00output\"}))\n    async with create_handler(\n        batch_size=100, flush_every=timedelta(seconds=0.2)\n    ) as handler:\n        for e in events:\n            await handler(\n                MemoryMessage(data=e.model_dump_json().encode(), attributes={\"id\": str(e.id)})\n            )\n        await asyncio.sleep(2)\n    async with db.session_context() as session:\n        stored = await session.scalar(\n            sa.select(sa.func.count()).where(db.Event.id.in_([e.id for e in events]))\n        )\n    print(f\"stored {stored} of {len(events)}\")\n\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nRun with `PREFECT_API_DATABASE_CONNECTION_URL` pointing at PostgreSQL:\n\n```\nERROR | prefect.server.events.services.event_persister - Max flush retries (5) reached, dropping 10 events\nasyncpg.exceptions.UntranslatableCharacterError: unsupported Unicode escape sequence\nstored 0 of 10\n```\n\nThe same happens end to end: `prefect server start` on PostgreSQL, 10 events sent with `emit_event`, 0 recorded.\n\nExpected: the 9 valid events are stored, and the bad one is stored with the null bytes removed, as logs are since #20677.\n\n### Version info\n\n```\nPrefect main @ 148e99781 (3.8.7.dev6), Python 3.13, PostgreSQL 14.19, asyncpg\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\nI have a fix ready that follows #20677 and will open a PR for it.\n\n", "hints_text": "I'll take this. Plan: mirror the logs fix from #20677 for the event persister \u2014 sanitize null bytes out of the event name, resource labels, and payload strings before the batch insert, so the offending event is stored cleaned instead of the whole batch being dropped after 5 retries. I'll add a regression test pushing a null-byte payload through the persister.\nHi @developer-rpai, thanks for the interest. There's already a PR open for this one: #23198. It follows #20677 as you describe, with a couple of extra cases that came up in review (null-byte-only labels, keys that differ only by a null byte, tuples in payloads).\n", "created_at": "2026-11-12T14:02:11Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23851, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23851", "issue_numbers": ["23850"], "base_commit": "5cf5adc41e7d21f6c32f49a1443ac0441c6fd44c", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/utilities/collections.py b/src/prefect/utilities/collections.py\nindex 11a52f4d36df..0c3a20228fec 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/utilities/collections.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/utilities/collections.py\n@@ -317,7 +317,7 @@ def visit_collection(\n     max_depth: int = -1,\n     context: Optional[dict[str, VT]] = None,\n     remove_annotations: bool = False,\n-    _seen: Optional[dict[int, Any]] = None,\n+    _seen: Optional[dict[tuple[int, Optional[type[Any]]], Any]] = None,\n ) -> Optional[Any]:\n     \"\"\"\n     Visits and potentially transforms every element of an arbitrary Python collection.\n@@ -434,17 +434,19 @@ def visit_expression(expr: Any) -> Any:\n     # If we have reached the maximum depth or we have already visited this object,\n     # return the result if we are returning data, otherwise return None\n     obj_id = id(expr)\n+    annotation = context.get(\"annotation\") if context is not None else None\n+    seen_key = (obj_id, type(annotation) if annotation is not None else None)\n     if max_depth == 0:\n         return result if return_data else None\n-    elif obj_id in _seen:\n+    elif seen_key in _seen:\n         # Return the cached transformed result\n-        return _seen[obj_id][1] if return_data else None\n+        return _seen[seen_key][1] if return_data else None\n \n     # Mark this object as being processed to handle circular references\n     # We'll update with the actual result later. The object is stored next to\n     # its result so it stays alive and its id cannot be reused by a different\n     # object during this traversal.\n-    _seen[obj_id] = (expr, expr)\n+    _seen[seen_key] = (expr, expr)\n \n     # Then visit every item in the expression if it is a collection\n \n@@ -573,7 +575,7 @@ def visit_expression(expr: Any) -> Any:\n \n     # Update the cache with the final transformed result\n     if return_data:\n-        _seen[obj_id] = (expr, result)\n+        _seen[seen_key] = (expr, result)\n \n     if return_data:\n         return result\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/utilities/test_collections.py b/tests/utilities/test_collections.py\nindex cfc00b6f8981..224690c26920 100644\n--- a/tests/utilities/test_collections.py\n+++ b/tests/utilities/test_collections.py\n@@ -10,7 +10,14 @@\n import pydantic\n import pytest\n \n-from prefect.utilities.annotations import BaseAnnotation, opaque, quote\n+from prefect.exceptions import UpstreamTaskError\n+from prefect.states import Failed\n+from prefect.utilities.annotations import (\n+    BaseAnnotation,\n+    allow_failure,\n+    opaque,\n+    quote,\n+)\n from prefect.utilities.collections import (\n     AutoEnum,\n     StopVisiting,\n@@ -25,6 +32,7 @@\n     set_in_dict,\n     visit_collection,\n )\n+from prefect.utilities.engine import resolve_inputs, resolve_inputs_sync\n \n \n class ExampleAnnotation(BaseAnnotation):\n@@ -1036,3 +1044,29 @@ class TestDeepMergeDicts:\n     )\n     def test_deep_merge_dicts(self, dicts, expected):\n         assert deep_merge_dicts(*dicts) == expected\n+\n+\n+class TestVisitCollectionAnnotationCache:\n+    def test_aliased_nested_input_does_not_reuse_allow_failure_resolution_sync(self):\n+        failed = Failed(data=ValueError(\"upstream failed\"))\n+        shared = [failed]\n+        with pytest.raises(UpstreamTaskError):\n+            resolve_inputs_sync({\"items\": [allow_failure(shared), shared]})\n+\n+    async def test_aliased_nested_input_does_not_reuse_allow_failure_resolution_async(\n+        self,\n+    ):\n+        failed = Failed(data=ValueError(\"upstream failed\"))\n+        shared = [failed]\n+        with pytest.raises(UpstreamTaskError):\n+            await resolve_inputs({\"items\": [allow_failure(shared), shared]})\n+\n+    def test_aliased_nested_input_same_annotation_context_reuses_result(self):\n+        err = ValueError(\"upstream failed\")\n+        failed = Failed(data=err)\n+        shared = [failed]\n+        resolved = resolve_inputs_sync(\n+            {\"items\": [allow_failure(shared), allow_failure(shared)]}\n+        )\n+        assert resolved[\"items\"][0] == [err]\n+        assert resolved[\"items\"][1] is resolved[\"items\"][0]\n", "problem_statement": "Aliased nested inputs can reuse allow_failure resolution for an unwrapped failed state\nBoth input resolvers can accept a plain failed dependency when the same nested list was already visited under `allow_failure`.\n\nReproduced from current source `bc0ff4b8c9e44cc474482ae69005945541fdcfe2` on macOS arm64 / Python 3.11.15, with normal Prefect runtime dependencies. This uses real in-memory Prefect states; no flow/server or external provider was needed.\n\n```python\nfrom prefect.states import Failed\nfrom prefect.utilities.annotations import allow_failure\nfrom prefect.utilities.engine import resolve_inputs_sync\n\nfailed = Failed(data=ValueError(\"upstream failed\"))\nshared = [failed]\nresult = resolve_inputs_sync({\"items\": [allow_failure(shared), shared]})\nprint(result)\n```\n\nExpected `UpstreamTaskError` for the second, unwrapped occurrence. Actual result contains the exception twice:\n\n```text\n{'items': [[ValueError('upstream failed')], [ValueError('upstream failed')]]}\n```\n\nThe two output lists are also the same object. `await resolve_inputs(...)` behaves the same way. Plain-only input, plain-first order, and two separate equal nested lists correctly raise; allow-failure-only correctly returns the exception. I verified all five cases through each resolver.\n\n`visit_collection` caches the transformed nested list by object identity while sharing that cache across annotation contexts. The later plain occurrence reuses the earlier result without visiting its failed child under the plain context. Current shared-reference tests do not combine this alias shape with different annotations.\n\nProposed scope: separate traversal/result reuse where effective annotation semantics differ, while retaining ordinary alias reuse, circular-reference termination, object-lifetime retention, no-copy behavior, depth limits, `quote`, `opaque` and annotation removal. Add resolver regressions for both sync/async paths and preserve the existing collection identity/cycle tests. I would keep this focused on the shared traversal boundary rather than changing `allow_failure` or copying every input.\n\nBecause this touches shared traversal semantics, could a maintainer confirm the approach and whether I should take the fix before I start changing the implementation? I have not prepared or opened a PR.\n\nInvestigation and draft prepared with Codex assistance; the behavior above was reproduced locally against the pinned source. No production incidence or full flow-execution result is claimed.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-12T07:31:22Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23847, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23847", "issue_numbers": ["14645"], "base_commit": "bc0ff4b8c9e44cc474482ae69005945541fdcfe2", "patch": "diff --git a/src/integrations/prefect-sqlalchemy/prefect_sqlalchemy/database.py b/src/integrations/prefect-sqlalchemy/prefect_sqlalchemy/database.py\nindex e69f6a629920..d4725afae5a0 100644\n--- a/src/integrations/prefect-sqlalchemy/prefect_sqlalchemy/database.py\n+++ b/src/integrations/prefect-sqlalchemy/prefect_sqlalchemy/database.py\n@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@\n from contextlib import AsyncExitStack, ExitStack, asynccontextmanager, contextmanager\n from typing import Annotated, Any, Dict, List, Optional, Tuple, Union\n \n+import cloudpickle\n from pydantic import AfterValidator, ConfigDict, Field\n from sqlalchemy import __version__ as SQLALCHEMY_VERSION\n from sqlalchemy.engine import Connection, Engine, create_engine\n@@ -13,7 +14,7 @@\n from typing_extensions import Literal\n \n from prefect.blocks.abstract import CredentialsBlock, DatabaseBlock\n-from prefect.utilities.hashing import hash_objects\n+from prefect.utilities.hashing import stable_hash\n from prefect_sqlalchemy.credentials import (\n     AsyncDriver,\n     ConnectionComponents,\n@@ -293,29 +294,34 @@ def _execute(\n         return result_set\n \n     def _get_result_set(\n-        self, *execute_args: Tuple[Any], **execute_kwargs: Any\n+        self, operation: str, *execute_args: Tuple[Any], **execute_kwargs: Any\n     ) -> CursorResult:\n         \"\"\"\n         Returns a new or existing result set.\n \n         Args:\n+            operation: The SQL query or other operation to be executed.\n             *execute_args: Args to pass to execute.\n             **execute_kwargs: Keyword args to pass to execute.\n \n         Returns:\n             The result set from the operation.\n         \"\"\"\n-        input_hash = hash_objects(*execute_args, **execute_kwargs)\n-        assert input_hash is not None, (\n-            \"We were not able to hash your inputs, \"\n-            \"which resulted in an unexpected data return; \"\n-            \"please open an issue with a reproducible example.\"\n+        # SQLAlchemy initializes shared type caches during execution, so hashing\n+        # a TextClause can produce different keys for the same query over time.\n+        # Keep pickle's parameter type distinctions (e.g. tuple versus list).\n+        input_hash = stable_hash(\n+            cloudpickle.dumps(((operation, *execute_args), execute_kwargs))\n         )\n \n         if input_hash not in self._unique_results.keys():\n             connection = self._exit_stack.enter_context(self.get_connection())\n             result_set = self._execute(\n-                connection, *execute_args, execute_commit=False, **execute_kwargs\n+                connection,\n+                text(operation),\n+                *execute_args,\n+                execute_commit=False,\n+                **execute_kwargs,\n             )\n             self._unique_results[input_hash] = result_set\n         else:\n@@ -406,7 +412,7 @@ def fetch_one(\n             ```\n         \"\"\"\n         result_set = self._get_result_set(\n-            text(operation), parameters, execution_options=execution_options\n+            operation, parameters, execution_options=execution_options\n         )\n         self.logger.debug(\"Preparing to fetch one row.\")\n         row = result_set.fetchone()\n@@ -462,7 +468,7 @@ def fetch_many(\n             ```\n         \"\"\"\n         result_set = self._get_result_set(\n-            text(operation), parameters, execution_options=execution_options\n+            operation, parameters, execution_options=execution_options\n         )\n         size = size or self.fetch_size\n         self.logger.debug(f\"Preparing to fetch {size} rows.\")\n@@ -516,7 +522,7 @@ def fetch_all(\n             ```\n         \"\"\"\n         result_set = self._get_result_set(\n-            text(operation), parameters, execution_options=execution_options\n+            operation, parameters, execution_options=execution_options\n         )\n         self.logger.debug(\"Preparing to fetch all rows.\")\n         rows = result_set.fetchall()\n@@ -930,23 +936,24 @@ async def _execute(\n         return result_set\n \n     async def _get_result_set(\n-        self, *execute_args: Tuple[Any], **execute_kwargs: Any\n+        self, operation: str, *execute_args: Tuple[Any], **execute_kwargs: Any\n     ) -> CursorResult:\n         \"\"\"\n         Returns a new or existing result set.\n \n         Args:\n+            operation: The SQL query or other operation to be executed.\n             *execute_args: Args to pass to execute.\n             **execute_kwargs: Keyword args to pass to execute.\n \n         Returns:\n             The result set from the operation.\n         \"\"\"\n-        input_hash = hash_objects(*execute_args, **execute_kwargs)\n-        assert input_hash is not None, (\n-            \"We were not able to hash your inputs, \"\n-            \"which resulted in an unexpected data return; \"\n-            \"please open an issue with a reproducible example.\"\n+        # SQLAlchemy initializes shared type caches during execution, so hashing\n+        # a TextClause can produce different keys for the same query over time.\n+        # Keep pickle's parameter type distinctions (e.g. tuple versus list).\n+        input_hash = stable_hash(\n+            cloudpickle.dumps(((operation, *execute_args), execute_kwargs))\n         )\n \n         if input_hash not in self._unique_results.keys():\n@@ -954,7 +961,11 @@ async def _get_result_set(\n                 self.get_connection()\n             )\n             result_set = await self._execute(\n-                connection, *execute_args, execute_commit=False, **execute_kwargs\n+                connection,\n+                text(operation),\n+                *execute_args,\n+                execute_commit=False,\n+                **execute_kwargs,\n             )\n             self._unique_results[input_hash] = result_set\n         else:\n@@ -1053,7 +1064,7 @@ async def example_run():\n             ```\n         \"\"\"\n         result_set = await self._get_result_set(\n-            text(operation), parameters, execution_options=execution_options\n+            operation, parameters, execution_options=execution_options\n         )\n         self.logger.debug(\"Preparing to fetch one row.\")\n         row = result_set.fetchone()\n@@ -1113,7 +1124,7 @@ async def example_run():\n             ```\n         \"\"\"\n         result_set = await self._get_result_set(\n-            text(operation), parameters, execution_options=execution_options\n+            operation, parameters, execution_options=execution_options\n         )\n         size = size or self.fetch_size\n         self.logger.debug(f\"Preparing to fetch {size} rows.\")\n@@ -1171,7 +1182,7 @@ async def example_run():\n             ```\n         \"\"\"\n         result_set = await self._get_result_set(\n-            text(operation), parameters, execution_options=execution_options\n+            operation, parameters, execution_options=execution_options\n         )\n         self.logger.debug(\"Preparing to fetch all rows.\")\n         rows = result_set.fetchall()\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/src/integrations/prefect-sqlalchemy/tests/test_cursor_reuse.py b/src/integrations/prefect-sqlalchemy/tests/test_cursor_reuse.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 000000000000..e9c474fe6d98\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/src/integrations/prefect-sqlalchemy/tests/test_cursor_reuse.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,120 @@\n+import json\n+import subprocess\n+import sys\n+from textwrap import dedent\n+\n+import pytest\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"is_async\", [False, True], ids=[\"sync\", \"async\"])\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+    \"method, expected\",\n+    [\n+        (\"fetch_one\", [[1], [2], None]),\n+        (\"fetch_many\", [[[1]], [[2]], []]),\n+        (\"fetch_all\", [[[1], [2]], [], []]),\n+    ],\n+)\n+def test_first_parameterized_fetch_reuses_cursor(is_async, method, expected):\n+    # A fresh process matters: previous queries can initialize SQLAlchemy's\n+    # shared type caches and hide the first-query regression.\n+    script = dedent(\n+        \"\"\"\n+        import asyncio\n+        import json\n+        import sys\n+\n+        from prefect_sqlalchemy import AsyncSqlAlchemyConnector, SqlAlchemyConnector\n+\n+        method = sys.argv[1]\n+        query = \"SELECT :value UNION ALL SELECT :value + 1 ORDER BY 1\"\n+        kwargs = {\"parameters\": {\"value\": 1}}\n+        if method == \"fetch_many\":\n+            kwargs[\"size\"] = 1\n+\n+        def normalize(result):\n+            if method == \"fetch_one\":\n+                return None if result is None else tuple(result)\n+            return [tuple(row) for row in result]\n+\n+        async def run_async():\n+            async with AsyncSqlAlchemyConnector(\n+                connection_info=\"sqlite+aiosqlite:///:memory:\"\n+            ) as connector:\n+                fetch = getattr(connector, method)\n+                return [normalize(await fetch(query, **kwargs)) for _ in range(3)]\n+\n+        def run_sync():\n+            with SqlAlchemyConnector(\n+                connection_info=\"sqlite+pysqlite:///:memory:\"\n+            ) as connector:\n+                fetch = getattr(connector, method)\n+                return [normalize(fetch(query, **kwargs)) for _ in range(3)]\n+\n+        result = asyncio.run(run_async()) if sys.argv[2] == \"async\" else run_sync()\n+        print(json.dumps(result))\n+        \"\"\"\n+    )\n+    result = subprocess.run(\n+        [sys.executable, \"-c\", script, method, \"async\" if is_async else \"sync\"],\n+        check=False,\n+        capture_output=True,\n+        text=True,\n+        timeout=60,\n+    )\n+    assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr\n+    assert json.loads(result.stdout) == expected\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"is_async\", [False, True], ids=[\"sync\", \"async\"])\n+def test_fetch_cache_distinguishes_adapted_parameter_types(is_async):\n+    # SQLite adapters are process-global, so isolate them from the rest of the\n+    # suite. Both values are valid inputs but have different database meanings.\n+    script = dedent(\n+        \"\"\"\n+        import asyncio\n+        import json\n+        import sqlite3\n+        import sys\n+\n+        from prefect_sqlalchemy import AsyncSqlAlchemyConnector, SqlAlchemyConnector\n+\n+        sqlite3.register_adapter(tuple, lambda value: \"tuple\")\n+        sqlite3.register_adapter(list, lambda value: \"list\")\n+        values = [(1, 2), [1, 2]]\n+\n+        async def run_async():\n+            async with AsyncSqlAlchemyConnector(\n+                connection_info=\"sqlite+aiosqlite:///:memory:\"\n+            ) as connector:\n+                return [\n+                    [tuple(row) for row in await connector.fetch_all(\n+                        \"SELECT :value\", parameters={\"value\": value}\n+                    )]\n+                    for value in values\n+                ]\n+\n+        def run_sync():\n+            with SqlAlchemyConnector(\n+                connection_info=\"sqlite+pysqlite:///:memory:\"\n+            ) as connector:\n+                return [\n+                    [tuple(row) for row in connector.fetch_all(\n+                        \"SELECT :value\", parameters={\"value\": value}\n+                    )]\n+                    for value in values\n+                ]\n+\n+        result = asyncio.run(run_async()) if sys.argv[1] == \"async\" else run_sync()\n+        print(json.dumps(result))\n+        \"\"\"\n+    )\n+    result = subprocess.run(\n+        [sys.executable, \"-c\", script, \"async\" if is_async else \"sync\"],\n+        check=False,\n+        capture_output=True,\n+        text=True,\n+        timeout=60,\n+    )\n+    assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr\n+    assert json.loads(result.stdout) == [[[\"tuple\"]], [[\"list\"]]]\n", "problem_statement": "prefect_sqlalchemy SyncDriver.POSTGRESQL_PSYCOPG2 returns duplicate results on second fetch_many execution\n### First check\n\n- [X] I added a descriptive title to this issue.\n- [X] I used the GitHub search to find a similar issue and didn't find it.\n- [X] I searched the Prefect documentation for this issue.\n- [X] I checked that this issue is related to Prefect and not one of its dependencies.\n\n### Bug summary\n\nprefect_sqlalchemy 0.4.3 SyncDriver.POSTGRESQL_PSYCOPG2 returns duplicate results on second fetch_many execution\r\n\r\nRun the flow below against a docker postgres database. Firstly, run it with the data population code uncommented so there is some data in the dataase. Then run it again with the data population code commented out. \r\n\r\nOn the second run, the first two `fetch_many` queries give the following logs:\r\n```\r\n08:40:24.548 | INFO    | Flow run 'deft-mustang' - Created a new engine.\r\n08:40:24.582 | INFO    | Flow run 'deft-mustang' - Created a new connection.\r\n[('1', 'A')]\r\n08:40:24.598 | INFO    | Flow run 'deft-mustang' - Created a new connection.\r\n08:40:24.606 | INFO    | Flow run 'deft-mustang' - Reset opened connections and their results.\r\n08:40:24.608 | INFO    | Flow run 'deft-mustang' - Disposed the engine.\r\n[('1', 'A')]\r\n```\r\n\r\n\n\n### Reproduction\n\n```python3\nfrom prefect import flow\r\nfrom prefect_sqlalchemy import SqlAlchemyConnector, ConnectionComponents, SyncDriver\r\nfrom pydantic.v1 import SecretStr\r\n\r\n\r\n# docker run --name some-postgres -p 5432:5432 -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=pwd123 -d postgres\r\n@flow\r\ndef run_sqlalchemy_query():\r\n    connection_info = ConnectionComponents(host=\"localhost\", port=\"5432\",\r\n                                           driver=SyncDriver.POSTGRESQL_PSYCOPG2, database=\"postgres\",\r\n                                           username=\"postgres\", password=SecretStr(\"pwd123\"))\r\n\r\n    # with SqlAlchemyConnector(connection_info=connection_info) as database:\r\n    #     database.execute(\"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS customers (name varchar, address varchar);\")\r\n    #     database.execute_many(\r\n    #         \"INSERT INTO customers (name, address) VALUES (:name, :address);\",\r\n    #         seq_of_parameters=[\r\n    #             {\"name\": \"1\", \"address\": \"A\"},\r\n    #             {\"name\": \"2\", \"address\": \"B\"},\r\n    #             {\"name\": \"3\", \"address\": \"C\"},\r\n    #         ],\r\n    #     )\r\n\r\n    with SqlAlchemyConnector(connection_info=connection_info) as database:\r\n        results = database.fetch_many(\"SELECT * FROM customers where name = :name;\",\r\n                                      parameters={'name': '1'}, size=2)\r\n        print(results)\r\n        results = database.fetch_many(\"SELECT * FROM customers where name = :name;\",\r\n                                      parameters={'name': '1'}, size=2)\r\n        print(results)\r\n        results = database.fetch_many(\"SELECT * FROM customers where name = :name;\",\r\n                                      parameters={'name': '1'}, size=2)\r\n        print(results)\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nif __name__ == '__main__':\r\n    run_sqlalchemy_query()\n```\n\n\n### Error\n\n```python3\n08:40:24.035 | INFO    | prefect.engine - Created flow run 'deft-mustang' for flow 'run-sqlalchemy-query'\r\n08:40:24.044 | INFO    | Flow run 'deft-mustang' - View at https://app.prefect.cloud/account/f11f03de-8f9c-4660-bbba-f00d85087da4/workspace/00985c36-8720-46ae-a667-258da45f0916/flow-runs/flow-run/0e839d07-8d08-45ba-89f1-8e2c1c6c1eb0\r\n08:40:24.548 | INFO    | Flow run 'deft-mustang' - Created a new engine.\r\n08:40:24.582 | INFO    | Flow run 'deft-mustang' - Created a new connection.\r\n[('1', 'A')]\r\n08:40:24.598 | INFO    | Flow run 'deft-mustang' - Created a new connection.\r\n08:40:24.606 | INFO    | Flow run 'deft-mustang' - Reset opened connections and their results.\r\n08:40:24.608 | INFO    | Flow run 'deft-mustang' - Disposed the engine.\r\n[('1', 'A')]\r\n[]\r\n08:40:24.884 | INFO    | Flow run 'deft-mustang' - Finished in state Completed()\n```\n\n\n### Versions (`prefect version` output)\n\n```Text\nalakazam-vR_Oc5bv-py3.10 \u276f prefect version\r\nVersion:             2.19.7\r\nAPI version:         0.8.4\r\nPython version:      3.10.3\r\nGit commit:          60f05122\r\nBuilt:               Fri, Jun 28, 2024 11:27 AM\r\nOS/Arch:             darwin/arm64\r\nProfile:             dev\r\nServer type:         cloud\n```\n\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_\n", "hints_text": "Thanks for the issue @markns! ", "created_at": "2026-11-11T11:54:50Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23839, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23839", "issue_numbers": ["23838"], "base_commit": "d6e625a3fe70ea824ec5515417318aa40dfe151e", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/server/orchestration/core_policy.py b/src/prefect/server/orchestration/core_policy.py\nindex a9202a37c1e7..d895bedcec40 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/server/orchestration/core_policy.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/server/orchestration/core_policy.py\n@@ -1538,7 +1538,12 @@ async def after_transition(\n             updated_policy[\"resuming\"] = False\n             updated_policy[\"pause_keys\"] = set()\n             updated_policy[\"retry_type\"] = \"reschedule\"\n-        else:\n+        elif validated_state is not None and validated_state.is_scheduled():\n+            # The run resumes from the top in a new process; the flag tells\n+            # `IncrementFlowRunCount` that its next `Running` state continues\n+            # the current attempt. A blocking pause resumed with `Running` is\n+            # handled there directly, and leaving the flag set on any other\n+            # state would make the next attempt skip its run count increment.\n             updated_policy[\"resuming\"] = True\n \n         context.run.empirical_policy = core.FlowRunPolicy(**updated_policy)\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/server/orchestration/global_policy.py b/src/prefect/server/orchestration/global_policy.py\nindex 6188555dc490..bf1fab5243c7 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/server/orchestration/global_policy.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/server/orchestration/global_policy.py\n@@ -319,7 +319,9 @@ class IncrementFlowRunCount(\n     FlowRunUniversalTransform[orm_models.FlowRun, core.FlowRunPolicy]\n ):\n     \"\"\"\n-    Records the number of times a run enters a running state. For use with retries.\n+    Records the number of times a run starts an attempt by entering a running\n+    state. Resuming a paused run continues its current attempt. For use with\n+    retries.\n     \"\"\"\n \n     async def before_transition(\n@@ -337,6 +339,17 @@ async def before_transition(\n                     if context.run.empirical_policy.resuming:\n                         return\n \n+                    # a blocking pause resumes in the same process with\n+                    # `Running`, continuing the attempt that paused\n+                    initial_state = context.initial_state\n+                    if (\n+                        initial_state is not None\n+                        and initial_state.is_paused()\n+                        and not initial_state.state_details.pause_reschedule\n+                        and context.run.run_count > 0\n+                    ):\n+                        return\n+\n                 # increment the run count\n                 context.run.run_count += 1\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_flow_runs.py b/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_flow_runs.py\nindex 4c0cf60afb89..5d389c2f1918 100644\n--- a/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_flow_runs.py\n+++ b/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_flow_runs.py\n@@ -1574,6 +1574,155 @@ async def test_resuming_blocking_pauses(\n         )\n         assert resumed_run.state.type == \"RUNNING\"\n \n+    async def test_resuming_blocking_pause_keeps_run_count_and_retries(\n+        self, flow: Flow, client: AsyncClient, session: AsyncSession\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"Resuming a blocking pause continues the current attempt, so it must\n+        not count as a new run or use up a retry.\"\"\"\n+        flow_run = await models.flow_runs.create_flow_run(\n+            session=session,\n+            flow_run=schemas.core.FlowRun(\n+                flow_id=flow.id,\n+                empirical_policy=schemas.core.FlowRunPolicy(retries=1, retry_delay=0),\n+            ),\n+        )\n+        await session.commit()\n+        flow_run_id = flow_run.id\n+\n+        async def set_state(type: StateType, **kwargs: Any) -> OrchestrationResult:\n+            response = await client.post(\n+                f\"/flow_runs/{flow_run_id}/set_state\",\n+                json=dict(state=dict(type=type.value, **kwargs)),\n+            )\n+            assert response.status_code == 201, response.text\n+            return OrchestrationResult.model_validate(response.json())\n+\n+        async def read_run_count() -> int:\n+            session.expire_all()\n+            run = await models.flow_runs.read_flow_run(\n+                session=session, flow_run_id=flow_run_id\n+            )\n+            assert run is not None\n+            return run.run_count\n+\n+        await set_state(StateType.PENDING)\n+        await set_state(StateType.RUNNING)\n+        await set_state(\n+            StateType.PAUSED,\n+            name=\"Paused\",\n+            state_details=dict(\n+                pause_key=\"1\",\n+                pause_reschedule=False,\n+                pause_timeout=(now(\"UTC\") + datetime.timedelta(hours=1)).isoformat(),\n+            ),\n+        )\n+        assert await read_run_count() == 1\n+\n+        response = await client.post(f\"/flow_runs/{flow_run_id}/resume\")\n+        assert response.status_code == 201, response.text\n+        assert response.json()[\"status\"] == \"ACCEPT\"\n+        assert await read_run_count() == 1\n+\n+        # The first failure after the pause still has its retry available.\n+        result = await set_state(StateType.FAILED)\n+        assert result.status == responses.SetStateStatus.REJECT\n+        assert result.state is not None\n+        assert result.state.name == \"AwaitingRetry\"\n+\n+    async def test_attempt_after_resuming_blocking_pause_is_counted(\n+        self, flow: Flow, client: AsyncClient, session: AsyncSession\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"A run rescheduled after a blocking pause was resumed (for example on\n+        SIGTERM) starts a new attempt, which must be counted.\"\"\"\n+        flow_run = await models.flow_runs.create_flow_run(\n+            session=session, flow_run=schemas.core.FlowRun(flow_id=flow.id)\n+        )\n+        await session.commit()\n+        flow_run_id = flow_run.id\n+\n+        async def set_state(type: StateType, **kwargs: Any) -> OrchestrationResult:\n+            response = await client.post(\n+                f\"/flow_runs/{flow_run_id}/set_state\",\n+                json=dict(state=dict(type=type.value, **kwargs)),\n+            )\n+            assert response.status_code == 201, response.text\n+            return OrchestrationResult.model_validate(response.json())\n+\n+        await set_state(StateType.PENDING)\n+        await set_state(StateType.RUNNING)\n+        await set_state(\n+            StateType.PAUSED,\n+            name=\"Paused\",\n+            state_details=dict(pause_key=\"1\", pause_reschedule=False),\n+        )\n+        response = await client.post(f\"/flow_runs/{flow_run_id}/resume\")\n+        assert response.json()[\"status\"] == \"ACCEPT\"\n+\n+        await set_state(StateType.SCHEDULED, name=\"AwaitingRetry\")\n+        await set_state(StateType.PENDING)\n+        await set_state(StateType.RUNNING)\n+\n+        session.expire_all()\n+        run = await models.flow_runs.read_flow_run(\n+            session=session, flow_run_id=flow_run_id\n+        )\n+        assert run is not None\n+        assert run.run_count == 2\n+        assert not run.empirical_policy.resuming\n+\n+    async def test_resuming_blocking_pause_before_first_run_counts_the_run(\n+        self, flow: Flow, client: AsyncClient, session: AsyncSession\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"A run forced into a blocking pause before it ever ran has no attempt\n+        to continue, so resuming it starts the first one.\"\"\"\n+        flow_run = await models.flow_runs.create_flow_run(\n+            session=session, flow_run=schemas.core.FlowRun(flow_id=flow.id)\n+        )\n+        await session.commit()\n+        flow_run_id = flow_run.id\n+\n+        response = await client.post(\n+            f\"/flow_runs/{flow_run_id}/set_state\",\n+            json=dict(\n+                state=dict(\n+                    type=StateType.PAUSED.value,\n+                    name=\"Paused\",\n+                    state_details=dict(pause_key=\"1\", pause_reschedule=False),\n+                ),\n+                force=True,\n+            ),\n+        )\n+        assert response.status_code == 201, response.text\n+\n+        response = await client.post(f\"/flow_runs/{flow_run_id}/resume\")\n+        assert response.json()[\"status\"] == \"ACCEPT\"\n+\n+        session.expire_all()\n+        run = await models.flow_runs.read_flow_run(\n+            session=session, flow_run_id=flow_run_id\n+        )\n+        assert run is not None\n+        assert run.run_count == 1\n+        assert not run.empirical_policy.resuming\n+\n+        # a later attempt is still counted\n+        for state in (\n+            dict(type=StateType.SCHEDULED.value, name=\"AwaitingRetry\"),\n+            dict(type=StateType.PENDING.value),\n+            dict(type=StateType.RUNNING.value),\n+        ):\n+            response = await client.post(\n+                f\"/flow_runs/{flow_run_id}/set_state\", json=dict(state=state)\n+            )\n+            assert response.status_code == 201, response.text\n+\n+        session.expire_all()\n+        run = await models.flow_runs.read_flow_run(\n+            session=session, flow_run_id=flow_run_id\n+        )\n+        assert run is not None\n+        assert run.run_count == 2\n+\n     async def test_resuming_nonblocking_pauses(\n         self, nonblocking_paused_flow_run, client, session\n     ):\n@@ -2026,7 +2175,9 @@ def paused(pause_key: str) -> dict[str, Any]:\n             session=session, flow_run_id=flow_run_id\n         )\n         assert run is not None\n-        assert run.run_count == 3\n+        # resuming the first pause continued the first attempt; only the\n+        # rescheduled attempt counts as a new run\n+        assert run.run_count == 2\n \n     async def test_input_pauses_can_be_replayed_after_blocking_pause_is_rescheduled(\n         self,\ndiff --git a/tests/server/orchestration/test_core_policy.py b/tests/server/orchestration/test_core_policy.py\nindex 4bb2ae4aa95f..53a64d41cf45 100644\n--- a/tests/server/orchestration/test_core_policy.py\n+++ b/tests/server/orchestration/test_core_policy.py\n@@ -3419,16 +3419,19 @@ async def test_marks_flow_run_as_resuming_upon_leaving_paused_state(\n         initialize_orchestration,\n     ):\n         initial_state_type = states.StateType.PAUSED\n-        proposed_state_type = states.StateType.RUNNING\n+        proposed_state_type = states.StateType.SCHEDULED\n         intended_transition = (initial_state_type, proposed_state_type)\n         ctx = await initialize_orchestration(\n             session,\n             \"flow\",\n             *intended_transition,\n+            proposed_state_name=\"Resuming\",\n         )\n         ctx.run.deployment_id = deployment.id\n         the_future = now(\"UTC\") + timedelta(minutes=5)\n-        ctx.initial_state.state_details = states.StateDetails(pause_timeout=the_future)\n+        ctx.initial_state.state_details = states.StateDetails(\n+            pause_timeout=the_future, pause_reschedule=True\n+        )\n \n         state_protection = HandleResumingPausedFlows(ctx, *intended_transition)\n \n@@ -3438,6 +3441,28 @@ async def test_marks_flow_run_as_resuming_upon_leaving_paused_state(\n         assert ctx.response_status == SetStateStatus.ACCEPT\n         assert ctx.run.empirical_policy.resuming\n \n+    async def test_does_not_mark_flow_run_as_resuming_when_blocking_pause_resumes(\n+        self,\n+        session,\n+        initialize_orchestration,\n+    ):\n+        # The resumed run is already running; a resuming indicator left on it\n+        # would make its next attempt skip the run count increment.\n+        intended_transition = (states.StateType.PAUSED, states.StateType.RUNNING)\n+        ctx = await initialize_orchestration(\n+            session,\n+            \"flow\",\n+            *intended_transition,\n+        )\n+        the_future = now(\"UTC\") + timedelta(minutes=5)\n+        ctx.initial_state.state_details = states.StateDetails(pause_timeout=the_future)\n+\n+        async with HandleResumingPausedFlows(ctx, *intended_transition) as ctx:\n+            await ctx.validate_proposed_state()\n+\n+        assert ctx.response_status == SetStateStatus.ACCEPT\n+        assert not ctx.run.empirical_policy.resuming\n+\n     async def test_resets_pause_metadata_when_blocking_pause_is_rescheduled(\n         self,\n         session: AsyncSession,\ndiff --git a/tests/server/orchestration/test_global_policy.py b/tests/server/orchestration/test_global_policy.py\nindex 185cc7ffcf53..762f08e5c0be 100644\n--- a/tests/server/orchestration/test_global_policy.py\n+++ b/tests/server/orchestration/test_global_policy.py\n@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@\n import datetime\n \n import pytest\n+from packaging.version import Version\n \n from prefect.server import models\n from prefect.server.orchestration.global_policy import (\n@@ -558,3 +559,87 @@ async def test_running_resuming_flow_does_not_increment_run_count(\n             await ctx.validate_proposed_state()\n \n         assert ctx.run.run_count == 42\n+\n+    async def test_resuming_blocking_pause_does_not_increment_run_count(\n+        self,\n+        session,\n+        initialize_orchestration,\n+    ):\n+        # A blocking pause resumes in the same process with `Running`, so the\n+        # run continues its current attempt instead of starting a new one.\n+        intended_transition = (states.StateType.PAUSED, states.StateType.RUNNING)\n+        ctx = await initialize_orchestration(\n+            session,\n+            \"flow\",\n+            *intended_transition,\n+            initial_details={\"pause_reschedule\": False, \"pause_key\": \"1\"},\n+            flow_run_count=1,\n+        )\n+\n+        assert not ctx.run.empirical_policy.resuming\n+\n+        async with IncrementFlowRunCount(ctx, *intended_transition) as ctx:\n+            await ctx.validate_proposed_state()\n+\n+        assert ctx.run.run_count == 1\n+\n+    async def test_resuming_blocking_pause_before_first_run_increments_run_count(\n+        self,\n+        session,\n+        initialize_orchestration,\n+    ):\n+        # A run forced into a blocking pause before it ever ran has no attempt\n+        # to continue.\n+        intended_transition = (states.StateType.PAUSED, states.StateType.RUNNING)\n+        ctx = await initialize_orchestration(\n+            session,\n+            \"flow\",\n+            *intended_transition,\n+            initial_details={\"pause_reschedule\": False, \"pause_key\": \"1\"},\n+        )\n+\n+        async with IncrementFlowRunCount(ctx, *intended_transition) as ctx:\n+            await ctx.validate_proposed_state()\n+\n+        assert ctx.run.run_count == 1\n+\n+    async def test_running_from_reschedule_pause_increments_run_count(\n+        self,\n+        session,\n+        initialize_orchestration,\n+    ):\n+        # A reschedule-style pause exits the process, so entering `Running`\n+        # without the resuming indicator starts a new attempt.\n+        intended_transition = (states.StateType.PAUSED, states.StateType.RUNNING)\n+        ctx = await initialize_orchestration(\n+            session,\n+            \"flow\",\n+            *intended_transition,\n+            initial_details={\"pause_reschedule\": True, \"pause_key\": \"1\"},\n+            flow_run_count=1,\n+        )\n+\n+        async with IncrementFlowRunCount(ctx, *intended_transition) as ctx:\n+            await ctx.validate_proposed_state()\n+\n+        assert ctx.run.run_count == 2\n+\n+    async def test_resuming_blocking_pause_increments_run_count_for_old_clients(\n+        self,\n+        session,\n+        initialize_orchestration,\n+    ):\n+        intended_transition = (states.StateType.PAUSED, states.StateType.RUNNING)\n+        ctx = await initialize_orchestration(\n+            session,\n+            \"flow\",\n+            *intended_transition,\n+            initial_details={\"pause_reschedule\": False, \"pause_key\": \"1\"},\n+            flow_run_count=1,\n+        )\n+        ctx.parameters[\"api-version\"] = Version(\"0.8.3\")\n+\n+        async with IncrementFlowRunCount(ctx, *intended_transition) as ctx:\n+            await ctx.validate_proposed_state()\n+\n+        assert ctx.run.run_count == 2\n", "problem_statement": "Resuming a blocking pause counts as a new run, so a flow with retries that paused once is not retried\n### Bug summary\n\nA flow with `retries=1` that calls `pause_flow_run()` (or `wait_for_input`) and then fails after being resumed is not retried. Resuming the pause increments `flow_run.run_count`, so when the flow fails `RetryFailedFlows` sees `run_count (2) > retries (1)` and accepts `Failed` instead of moving the run to `AwaitingRetry`.\n\nMinimal reproduction (ephemeral server, no deployment needed):\n\n```python\nimport threading\nimport time\n\nfrom prefect import flow\nfrom prefect.client.orchestration import get_client\nfrom prefect.context import get_run_context\nfrom prefect.flow_runs import pause_flow_run, resume_flow_run\n\n\ndef resume_soon(flow_run_id):\n    time.sleep(5)\n    resume_flow_run(flow_run_id)\n\n\n@flow(retries=1, retry_delay_seconds=0, log_prints=True)\ndef approve_then_fail():\n    flow_run = get_run_context().flow_run\n    print(\"starting an attempt\")\n    threading.Thread(target=resume_soon, args=(flow_run.id,), daemon=True).start()\n    pause_flow_run(timeout=60)\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed after approval\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    state = approve_then_fail(return_state=True)\n    with get_client(sync_client=True) as client:\n        run = client.read_flow_run(state.state_details.flow_run_id)\n    print(f\"final state: {state.type.value}, run_count: {run.run_count}\")\n```\n\nOn current `main` this prints \"starting an attempt\" once, then `final state: FAILED, run_count: 2`. Expected: the failure is retried once (\"Received non-final state 'AwaitingRetry'...\", a second \"starting an attempt\"), and `run_count` is 1 while the first attempt is paused and resumed.\n\nWhere it comes from: a blocking pause is resumed by proposing `Running()` directly from `Paused` (`POST /flow_runs/{id}/resume`). `IncrementFlowRunCount` (global policy, `before_transition`) only skips the increment when `empirical_policy.resuming` is already set, but for a blocking pause `HandleResumingPausedFlows` sets `resuming=True` in its `after_transition`, after the increment already happened. For a reschedule pause (`suspend_flow_run`) the flag is set on `Paused -> Scheduled(\"Resuming\")` and consumed on the later `Running`, so that path counts correctly. The flag set after a blocking resume then stays on the `Running` run until some later transition clears it.\n\nProposed fix: in `IncrementFlowRunCount`, don't count `Running` when the initial state is a blocking pause (`pause_reschedule=False`) and the run has already started an attempt (`run_count > 0`), under the same API-version gate as the existing `resuming` check. In `HandleResumingPausedFlows.after_transition`, set `resuming=True` only when the run leaves the pause for a scheduled state (the `suspend_flow_run` resume path that actually needs it), so the flag no longer stays on a `Running` run and makes its next attempt skip the increment. One existing assertion changes: `test_pauses_fire_again_after_blocking_pause_is_rescheduled` (from #23399) expects `run_count == 3` after two attempts; it becomes 2. Happy to open a PR with this if the approach looks right.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:             3.9.2+5.g941c57c524\nAPI version:         0.8.4\nPython version:      3.13.14\nGit commit:          941c57c5\nOS/Arch:             darwin/arm64\nServer type:         ephemeral\nDatabase:            sqlite\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\nRelated: #23399 (reset pause metadata when a blocking pause is rescheduled) documents that a blocking pause always resumes in-process with `Running()`, which is the case that should not count as a new attempt.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-10T17:01:01Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23836, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23836", "issue_numbers": ["23835"], "base_commit": "d0d23e0d668fec252fb35f9c6365a304dbbfbcff", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/workers/base.py b/src/prefect/workers/base.py\nindex 0dda557773d4..b9619726aaf7 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/workers/base.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/workers/base.py\n@@ -1593,25 +1593,12 @@ async def _submit_run(self, flow_run: \"FlowRun\") -> None:\n         \"\"\"\n         run_logger = self.get_flow_run_logger(flow_run)\n \n-        if flow_run.deployment_id:\n-            try:\n-                await self.client.read_deployment(flow_run.deployment_id)\n-            except ObjectNotFound:\n-                self._logger.exception(\n-                    f\"Deployment {flow_run.deployment_id} no longer exists. \"\n-                    f\"Flow run {flow_run.id} will not be submitted for\"\n-                    \" execution\"\n-                )\n-                self._submitting_flow_run_ids.remove(flow_run.id)\n-                if self._cancelling_observer is not None:\n-                    self._cancelling_observer.remove_in_flight_flow_run_id(flow_run.id)\n-                await self._mark_flow_run_as_cancelled(\n-                    flow_run,\n-                    state_updates=dict(\n-                        message=f\"Deployment {flow_run.deployment_id} no longer exists, cancelled run.\"\n-                    ),\n-                )\n-                return\n+        if not await self._check_deployment_before_submission(flow_run, run_logger):\n+            self._release_limit_slot(flow_run.id)\n+            self._submitting_flow_run_ids.remove(flow_run.id)\n+            if self._cancelling_observer is not None:\n+                self._cancelling_observer.remove_in_flight_flow_run_id(flow_run.id)\n+            return\n \n         ready_to_submit = await self._propose_pending_state(flow_run)\n         self._logger.debug(f\"Ready to submit {flow_run.id}: {ready_to_submit}\")\n@@ -1648,6 +1635,50 @@ async def _submit_run(self, flow_run: \"FlowRun\") -> None:\n         if self._cancelling_observer is not None:\n             self._cancelling_observer.remove_in_flight_flow_run_id(flow_run.id)\n \n+    async def _check_deployment_before_submission(\n+        self, flow_run: \"FlowRun\", run_logger: PrefectLogAdapter\n+    ) -> bool:\n+        \"\"\"\n+        Returns whether the flow run's deployment can be read, so the run can be\n+        submitted.\n+\n+        Runs whose deployment no longer exists are cancelled. Any other error\n+        leaves the run scheduled so a later poll can retry it; letting it escape\n+        would crash the worker's task group.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        if not flow_run.deployment_id:\n+            return True\n+\n+        try:\n+            await self.client.read_deployment(flow_run.deployment_id)\n+            return True\n+        except ObjectNotFound:\n+            self._logger.exception(\n+                f\"Deployment {flow_run.deployment_id} no longer exists. \"\n+                f\"Flow run {flow_run.id} will not be submitted for\"\n+                \" execution\"\n+            )\n+        except Exception:\n+            run_logger.exception(\n+                f\"Failed to read deployment {flow_run.deployment_id} for flow run\"\n+                f\" '{flow_run.id}'. The flow run will be retried on the next poll.\"\n+            )\n+            return False\n+\n+        try:\n+            await self._mark_flow_run_as_cancelled(\n+                flow_run,\n+                state_updates=dict(\n+                    message=f\"Deployment {flow_run.deployment_id} no longer exists, cancelled run.\"\n+                ),\n+            )\n+        except Exception:\n+            run_logger.exception(\n+                f\"Failed to cancel flow run '{flow_run.id}'. The flow run will be\"\n+                \" retried on the next poll.\"\n+            )\n+        return False\n+\n     async def _submit_run_and_capture_errors(\n         self,\n         flow_run: \"FlowRun\",\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/workers/test_base_worker.py b/tests/workers/test_base_worker.py\nindex b905c15f7c7e..f15ec903e6e1 100644\n--- a/tests/workers/test_base_worker.py\n+++ b/tests/workers/test_base_worker.py\n@@ -797,6 +797,157 @@ def create_run_with_deployment(state: State):\n     assert updated_flow_run.state.is_crashed()\n \n \n+def _http_status_error(status_code: int) -> httpx.HTTPStatusError:\n+    request = httpx.Request(\"GET\", \"http://test/api/deployments/123\")\n+    response = httpx.Response(status_code, request=request)\n+    return httpx.HTTPStatusError(\n+        f\"HTTP {status_code}\", request=request, response=response\n+    )\n+\n+\n+def _bad_gateway_error() -> httpx.HTTPStatusError:\n+    return _http_status_error(status.HTTP_502_BAD_GATEWAY)\n+\n+\n+async def test_worker_survives_error_reading_deployment_before_submission(\n+    prefect_client: PrefectClient,\n+    worker_deployment_wq1: WorkQueue,\n+    work_pool: WorkPool,\n+):\n+    flow_run = await prefect_client.create_flow_run_from_deployment(\n+        worker_deployment_wq1.id,\n+        state=Scheduled(scheduled_time=now_fn(\"UTC\") - timedelta(days=1)),\n+    )\n+\n+    run_mock = AsyncMock()\n+\n+    # A non-404 error must not escape `_submit_run` and tear down the worker's\n+    # task group; exiting the context re-raises anything that escaped.\n+    async with WorkerTestImpl(work_pool_name=work_pool.name, limit=1) as worker:\n+        worker.run = run_mock\n+        worker.client.read_deployment = AsyncMock(side_effect=_bad_gateway_error())\n+\n+        submitted_flow_runs = await worker.get_and_submit_flow_runs()\n+        assert [run.id for run in submitted_flow_runs] == [flow_run.id]\n+\n+    run_mock.assert_not_called()\n+    assert worker.limiter.borrowed_tokens == 0\n+    assert flow_run.id not in worker._submitting_flow_run_ids\n+\n+    # The run is left scheduled so a later poll can pick it up again\n+    updated_flow_run = await prefect_client.read_flow_run(flow_run.id)\n+    assert updated_flow_run.state is not None\n+    assert updated_flow_run.state.is_scheduled()\n+\n+\n+async def test_worker_resubmits_run_after_error_reading_deployment(\n+    prefect_client: PrefectClient,\n+    worker_deployment_wq1: WorkQueue,\n+    work_pool: WorkPool,\n+):\n+    flow_run = await prefect_client.create_flow_run_from_deployment(\n+        worker_deployment_wq1.id,\n+        state=Scheduled(scheduled_time=now_fn(\"UTC\") - timedelta(days=1)),\n+    )\n+\n+    async with WorkerTestImpl(work_pool_name=work_pool.name, limit=1) as worker:\n+        worker._propose_pending_state = AsyncMock(return_value=False)\n+        read_deployment = worker.client.read_deployment\n+        worker.client.read_deployment = AsyncMock(side_effect=_bad_gateway_error())\n+\n+        await worker.get_and_submit_flow_runs()\n+        async for attempt in retry_asserts(max_attempts=20, delay=0.1):\n+            with attempt:\n+                assert flow_run.id not in worker._submitting_flow_run_ids\n+\n+        worker.client.read_deployment = read_deployment\n+        submitted_flow_runs = await worker.get_and_submit_flow_runs()\n+        assert [run.id for run in submitted_flow_runs] == [flow_run.id]\n+\n+    worker._propose_pending_state.assert_awaited_once()\n+\n+\n+async def test_worker_releases_limit_slot_when_deployment_was_deleted(\n+    prefect_client: PrefectClient,\n+    worker_deployment_wq1: WorkQueue,\n+    work_pool: WorkPool,\n+):\n+    orphaned_flow_run = await prefect_client.create_flow_run_from_deployment(\n+        worker_deployment_wq1.id,\n+        state=Scheduled(scheduled_time=now_fn(\"UTC\") - timedelta(days=2)),\n+    )\n+\n+    async with WorkerTestImpl(work_pool_name=work_pool.name, limit=1) as worker:\n+        worker._propose_pending_state = AsyncMock(return_value=False)\n+        read_deployment = worker.client.read_deployment\n+        worker.client.read_deployment = AsyncMock(\n+            side_effect=ObjectNotFound(\n+                http_exc=_http_status_error(status.HTTP_404_NOT_FOUND)\n+            )\n+        )\n+\n+        submitted_flow_runs = await worker.get_and_submit_flow_runs()\n+        assert [run.id for run in submitted_flow_runs] == [orphaned_flow_run.id]\n+        async for attempt in retry_asserts(max_attempts=20, delay=0.1):\n+            with attempt:\n+                assert orphaned_flow_run.id not in worker._submitting_flow_run_ids\n+\n+        assert worker.limiter.borrowed_tokens == 0\n+\n+        # With `limit=1`, a leaked slot would block every later run\n+        worker.client.read_deployment = read_deployment\n+        next_flow_run = await prefect_client.create_flow_run_from_deployment(\n+            worker_deployment_wq1.id,\n+            state=Scheduled(scheduled_time=now_fn(\"UTC\") - timedelta(days=1)),\n+        )\n+        submitted_flow_runs = await worker.get_and_submit_flow_runs()\n+        assert [run.id for run in submitted_flow_runs] == [next_flow_run.id]\n+\n+    updated_flow_run = await prefect_client.read_flow_run(orphaned_flow_run.id)\n+    assert updated_flow_run.state is not None\n+    assert updated_flow_run.state.is_cancelled()\n+\n+\n+async def test_worker_survives_error_cancelling_run_of_deleted_deployment(\n+    prefect_client: PrefectClient,\n+    worker_deployment_wq1: WorkQueue,\n+    work_pool: WorkPool,\n+):\n+    flow_run = await prefect_client.create_flow_run_from_deployment(\n+        worker_deployment_wq1.id,\n+        state=Scheduled(scheduled_time=now_fn(\"UTC\") - timedelta(days=1)),\n+    )\n+\n+    async with WorkerTestImpl(work_pool_name=work_pool.name, limit=1) as worker:\n+        worker.client.read_deployment = AsyncMock(\n+            side_effect=ObjectNotFound(\n+                http_exc=_http_status_error(status.HTTP_404_NOT_FOUND)\n+            )\n+        )\n+        set_flow_run_state = worker.client.set_flow_run_state\n+        worker.client.set_flow_run_state = AsyncMock(side_effect=_bad_gateway_error())\n+\n+        await worker.get_and_submit_flow_runs()\n+        async for attempt in retry_asserts(max_attempts=20, delay=0.1):\n+            with attempt:\n+                assert flow_run.id not in worker._submitting_flow_run_ids\n+\n+        assert worker.limiter.borrowed_tokens == 0\n+\n+        # Cancelling failed, so the run stays scheduled for the next poll\n+        updated_flow_run = await prefect_client.read_flow_run(flow_run.id)\n+        assert updated_flow_run.state is not None\n+        assert updated_flow_run.state.is_scheduled()\n+\n+        # The next poll cancels it once the API recovers\n+        worker.client.set_flow_run_state = set_flow_run_state\n+        await worker.get_and_submit_flow_runs()\n+\n+    updated_flow_run = await prefect_client.read_flow_run(flow_run.id)\n+    assert updated_flow_run.state is not None\n+    assert updated_flow_run.state.is_cancelled()\n+\n+\n async def test_worker_skips_duplicate_submission_of_run_holding_limit_slot(\n     prefect_client: PrefectClient,\n     worker_deployment_wq1: WorkQueue,\n", "problem_statement": "Worker crashes when reading the deployment fails with a non-404 error before submitting a run\n### Bug summary\n\nBefore submitting a flow run, `BaseWorker._submit_run` calls `client.read_deployment(flow_run.deployment_id)` and only handles `ObjectNotFound`. Any other error from that call (a 502/504 from a load balancer, a 401, a timeout that outlives the client retries) escapes the task that `_submit_run` runs in. That task lives in the worker's `_runs_task_group`, so the whole task group is cancelled and the worker process exits with the HTTP error. Other runs that worker was supervising are cancelled with it, and the run being submitted keeps its limit slot until the process dies.\n\nThis is the `_submit_run -> read_deployment` stack from #16977. The inline `read_deployment` call was added in #17313 to cancel runs whose deployment was deleted, and it only catches `ObjectNotFound`.\n\nThere is a second, related problem in that `ObjectNotFound` branch: it cancels the run and returns without calling `_release_limit_slot`. With `prefect worker start --limit N`, each run whose deployment was deleted permanently takes one slot. After N of them the worker keeps polling but never submits anything again; the only trace is the debug log \"Flow run limit reached\".\n\nMinimal reproduction (ephemeral server, no infrastructure needed):\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom typing import Any\nfrom unittest.mock import AsyncMock\n\nimport httpx\n\nfrom prefect import flow\nfrom prefect.client.orchestration import get_client\nfrom prefect.client.schemas.actions import WorkPoolCreate\nfrom prefect.exceptions import ObjectNotFound\nfrom prefect.states import Scheduled\nfrom prefect.testing.utilities import prefect_test_harness\nfrom prefect.workers.base import BaseJobConfiguration, BaseWorker, BaseWorkerResult\n\n\nclass DemoWorker(BaseWorker[BaseJobConfiguration, Any, BaseWorkerResult]):\n    type = \"demo\"\n    job_configuration = BaseJobConfiguration\n\n    async def run(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> BaseWorkerResult:\n        return BaseWorkerResult(status_code=0, identifier=\"demo\")\n\n\n@flow\ndef hello() -> None:\n    pass\n\n\ndef bad_gateway() -> httpx.HTTPStatusError:\n    request = httpx.Request(\"GET\", \"http://server/api/deployments/x\")\n    response = httpx.Response(502, request=request)\n    return httpx.HTTPStatusError(\"502 Bad Gateway\", request=request, response=response)\n\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    async with get_client() as client:\n        pool = await client.create_work_pool(WorkPoolCreate(name=\"demo\", type=\"demo\"))\n        flow_id = await client.create_flow(hello)\n        deployment_id = await client.create_deployment(\n            flow_id=flow_id, name=\"demo\", work_pool_name=pool.name\n        )\n\n        # 1. A 502 from read_deployment crashes the worker\n        await client.create_flow_run_from_deployment(deployment_id, state=Scheduled())\n        try:\n            async with DemoWorker(work_pool_name=pool.name, limit=2) as worker:\n                worker.client.read_deployment = AsyncMock(side_effect=bad_gateway())\n                await worker.get_and_submit_flow_runs()\n            print(\"1. worker survived the 502\")\n        except httpx.HTTPStatusError as exc:\n            print(f\"1. worker crashed: {exc!r}\")\n\n        # 2. With limit=1, a run whose deployment is gone leaks the only slot\n        async with DemoWorker(work_pool_name=pool.name, limit=1) as worker:\n            real_read_deployment = worker.client.read_deployment\n            worker.client.read_deployment = AsyncMock(\n                side_effect=ObjectNotFound(http_exc=bad_gateway())\n            )\n            await worker.get_and_submit_flow_runs()\n            await asyncio.sleep(1)\n            print(f\"2. slots in use after the run was cancelled: {worker.limiter.borrowed_tokens}\")\n\n            worker.client.read_deployment = real_read_deployment\n            healthy = await client.create_flow_run_from_deployment(\n                deployment_id, state=Scheduled()\n            )\n            submitted = await worker.get_and_submit_flow_runs()\n            print(f\"2. next healthy run submitted: {healthy.id in [r.id for r in submitted]}\")\n\n\nwith prefect_test_harness():\n    asyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nOutput on `main`:\n\n```\n1. worker crashed: HTTPStatusError('502 Bad Gateway')\n2. slots in use after the run was cancelled: 1\n2. next healthy run submitted: False\n```\n\nThe mocks only stand in for a flaky API and a deleted deployment; in production the first case is any non-retried error on that request, and the second is a run that was scheduled before its deployment was deleted.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n1. An error reading the deployment is logged against the flow run, the limit slot is released and the run stays `Scheduled`, so the next poll tries again. This is what the worker already does when proposing the `Pending` state fails (`_propose_pending_state`).\n2. Cancelling a run because its deployment no longer exists releases its limit slot.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nmain @ 941c57c524 (2026-11-09); the code path is unchanged since #17313 (March 2025).\nPython 3.13, macOS; any worker type that inherits from BaseWorker is affected.\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\n`_cancel_run` has a similar shape (`read_flow_run` there only handles `ObjectNotFound`), but it is a separate code path and not part of this report.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-10T16:26:01Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23818, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23818", "issue_numbers": ["23817"], "base_commit": "bad8ed43ae93fe3a817e29ae6736fa02dfbae71d", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/utilities/AGENTS.md b/src/prefect/utilities/AGENTS.md\nindex 85686691d4d3..3a89e25117ea 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/utilities/AGENTS.md\n+++ b/src/prefect/utilities/AGENTS.md\n@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@ Each subpackage owns its own `AGENTS.md` with entry points and pitfalls specific\n - `templating/` \u2192 Placeholder detection and value application for Prefect's `{{ }}` templating (see `templating/AGENTS.md`)\n - `engine/` \u2192 Result-to-state linking, SIGTERM bridge management, and control-intent coordination (see `engine/AGENTS.md`)\n - `filesystem/` \u2192 File filtering, path normalization, `tmpchdir` (see `filesystem/AGENTS.md`)\n+- `hashing/` \u2192 Stable hashing (`stable_hash`, `file_hash`, `hash_objects`) and cache-key/checksum stability contracts (see `hashing/AGENTS.md`)\n \n ## Flat modules\n \n@@ -33,7 +34,6 @@ These modules have no dedicated intent node yet. Promote any one of them to a su\n - `dispatch.py` \u2014 Dynamic type dispatch registry\n - `importtools.py` \u2014 Dynamic imports, aliased module loading, script-to-module conversion\n - `pydantic.py` \u2014 Pydantic v1/v2 compatibility shims, custom serializers, type dispatch integration\n-- `hashing.py` \u2014 Stable hashing (`stable_hash`, `file_hash`, `hash_objects`)\n - `dockerutils.py` \u2014 Docker image building, Python version detection, Docker client helpers\n - `timeout.py` \u2014 Timeout context managers for async/sync code\n - `services.py` \u2014 Client metrics server and resilient service loop with backoff\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/utilities/hashing.py b/src/prefect/utilities/hashing.py\ndeleted file mode 100644\nindex f131e4898314..000000000000\n--- a/src/prefect/utilities/hashing.py\n+++ /dev/null\n@@ -1,91 +0,0 @@\n-import hashlib\n-from functools import partial\n-from pathlib import Path\n-from typing import Any, Callable, Optional, Union\n-\n-import cloudpickle  # type: ignore  # no stubs available\n-\n-from prefect.exceptions import HashError\n-from prefect.serializers import JSONSerializer\n-\n-_md5 = partial(hashlib.md5, usedforsecurity=False)\n-\n-\n-def stable_hash(*args: Union[str, bytes], hash_algo: Callable[..., Any] = _md5) -> str:\n-    \"\"\"Given some arguments, produces a stable 64-bit hash of their contents.\n-\n-    Supports bytes and strings. Strings will be UTF-8 encoded.\n-\n-    Args:\n-        *args: Items to include in the hash.\n-        hash_algo: Hash algorithm from hashlib to use.\n-\n-    Returns:\n-        A hex hash.\n-    \"\"\"\n-    h = hash_algo()\n-    for a in args:\n-        if isinstance(a, str):\n-            a = a.encode()\n-        h.update(a)\n-    return h.hexdigest()\n-\n-\n-def file_hash(path: str, hash_algo: Callable[..., Any] = _md5) -> str:\n-    \"\"\"Given a path to a file, produces a stable hash of the file contents.\n-\n-    Args:\n-        path (str): the path to a file\n-        hash_algo: Hash algorithm from hashlib to use.\n-\n-    Returns:\n-        str: a hash of the file contents\n-    \"\"\"\n-    contents = Path(path).read_bytes()\n-    return stable_hash(contents, hash_algo=hash_algo)\n-\n-\n-def hash_objects(\n-    *args: Any,\n-    hash_algo: Callable[..., Any] = _md5,\n-    raise_on_failure: bool = False,\n-    **kwargs: Any,\n-) -> Optional[str]:\n-    \"\"\"\n-    Attempt to hash objects by dumping to JSON or serializing with cloudpickle.\n-\n-    Args:\n-        *args: Positional arguments to hash\n-        hash_algo: Hash algorithm to use\n-        raise_on_failure: If True, raise exceptions instead of returning None\n-        **kwargs: Keyword arguments to hash\n-\n-    Returns:\n-        A hash string or None if hashing failed\n-\n-    Raises:\n-        HashError: If objects cannot be hashed and raise_on_failure is True\n-    \"\"\"\n-    json_error = None\n-    pickle_error = None\n-\n-    try:\n-        serializer = JSONSerializer(dumps_kwargs={\"sort_keys\": True})\n-        return stable_hash(serializer.dumps((args, kwargs)), hash_algo=hash_algo)\n-    except Exception as e:\n-        json_error = str(e)\n-\n-    try:\n-        return stable_hash(cloudpickle.dumps((args, kwargs)), hash_algo=hash_algo)  # type: ignore[reportUnknownMemberType]\n-    except Exception as e:\n-        pickle_error = str(e)\n-\n-    if raise_on_failure:\n-        msg = (\n-            \"Unable to create hash - objects could not be serialized.\\n\"\n-            f\"  JSON error: {json_error}\\n\"\n-            f\"  Pickle error: {pickle_error}\"\n-        )\n-        raise HashError(msg)\n-\n-    return None\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/utilities/hashing/AGENTS.md b/src/prefect/utilities/hashing/AGENTS.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 000000000000..10bafac265e3\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/src/prefect/utilities/hashing/AGENTS.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,17 @@\n+# hashing\n+\n+Stable hashing of strings, files, and arbitrary Python objects.\n+\n+## Purpose & Scope\n+\n+`stable_hash`, `file_hash`, and `hash_objects`. `hash_objects` produces the cache keys for `task_input_hash` and the `Inputs`/`FlowParameters` cache policies, and the checksums for block schemas (`Block._calculate_schema_checksum`).\n+\n+## Entry Points\n+\n+- `hash_objects(*args, hash_algo=..., raise_on_failure=False, **kwargs)` \u2014 hash via JSON (`JSONSerializer`, `sort_keys=True`), falling back to cloudpickle of the original objects when JSON fails.\n+\n+## Contracts\n+\n+- **Hashes of JSON-native inputs must not change.** Block schema checksums are persisted server-side and compared against client-computed values, so inputs made only of str-keyed dicts, lists, and scalars must keep serializing to the same JSON. When no tuple or non-str-key dict is found, `hash_objects` hashes the untagged `(args, kwargs)` exactly as before; this includes str-keyed dicts that happen to use the `__tuple__`/`__dict__` tag names.\n+- **JSON-lossy types are tagged before serializing.** When a tuple or non-str-key dict is present, tuples become `{\"__tuple__\": [...]}` and dicts with non-str keys (`int`, `float`, `bool`, `None`) become `{\"__dict__\": [[key, value], ...]}`, sorted by each key's JSON encoding. The tagged JSON is hashed with a `\\x00` prefix, which JSON output can never start with, so tagged hashes can't collide with untagged ones. Within a tagged payload, a str-keyed dict whose only key is `__tuple__` or `__dict__` is escaped into the `__dict__` form so real values can't collide with tags. Other key types raise `TypeError` to preserve the cloudpickle fallback.\n+- Tagging only walks dicts, lists, and tuples. Containers nested inside objects handled by `prefect_json_object_encoder` (Pydantic models, dataclasses) are serialized by Pydantic in JSON mode and are not tagged.\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/utilities/hashing/__init__.py b/src/prefect/utilities/hashing/__init__.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 000000000000..2c31ef6f9178\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/src/prefect/utilities/hashing/__init__.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,151 @@\n+import hashlib\n+import json\n+from functools import partial\n+from pathlib import Path\n+from typing import Any, Callable, Optional, Union\n+\n+import cloudpickle  # type: ignore  # no stubs available\n+\n+from prefect.exceptions import HashError\n+from prefect.serializers import JSONSerializer\n+\n+_md5 = partial(hashlib.md5, usedforsecurity=False)\n+\n+_TUPLE_TAG = \"__tuple__\"\n+_DICT_TAG = \"__dict__\"\n+_JSON_KEY_TYPES = (str, int, float, bool, type(None))\n+# JSON payloads always start with `[`, so prefixed tagged payloads can never\n+# collide with untagged ones\n+_TAGGED_PAYLOAD_PREFIX = b\"\\x00\"\n+\n+\n+def _tag_json_lossy_types(obj: Any) -> tuple[Any, bool]:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Recursively tag tuples and dicts with non-string keys so that values JSON would\n+    otherwise conflate (e.g. `(1, 2)` and `[1, 2]`, `{1: \"a\"}` and `{\"1\": \"a\"}`)\n+    serialize differently.\n+\n+    Returns the tagged value and whether any tuple or non-string-key dict was found.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    if isinstance(obj, tuple):\n+        items = [_tag_json_lossy_types(item) for item in obj]\n+        return {_TUPLE_TAG: [item for item, _ in items]}, True\n+    if isinstance(obj, list):\n+        items = [_tag_json_lossy_types(item) for item in obj]\n+        return [item for item, _ in items], any(lossy for _, lossy in items)\n+    if isinstance(obj, dict):\n+        if all(isinstance(key, str) for key in obj):\n+            tagged = {key: _tag_json_lossy_types(value) for key, value in obj.items()}\n+            lossy = any(lossy for _, lossy in tagged.values())\n+            if len(obj) == 1 and next(iter(obj)) in (_TUPLE_TAG, _DICT_TAG):\n+                # Escape dicts that look like tags so they can't collide with them\n+                return {_DICT_TAG: [[key, v] for key, (v, _) in tagged.items()]}, lossy\n+            return {key: v for key, (v, _) in tagged.items()}, lossy\n+        for key in obj:\n+            if not isinstance(key, _JSON_KEY_TYPES):\n+                raise TypeError(\n+                    f\"keys must be str, int, float, bool or None, not {type(key).__name__}\"\n+                )\n+        # Sorted by each key's JSON encoding so the result is independent of\n+        # insertion order, matching `sort_keys=True` for string-keyed dicts\n+        items = sorted(obj.items(), key=lambda item: json.dumps(item[0]))\n+        return {\n+            _DICT_TAG: [[key, _tag_json_lossy_types(value)[0]] for key, value in items]\n+        }, True\n+    return obj, False\n+\n+\n+def stable_hash(*args: Union[str, bytes], hash_algo: Callable[..., Any] = _md5) -> str:\n+    \"\"\"Given some arguments, produces a stable 64-bit hash of their contents.\n+\n+    Supports bytes and strings. Strings will be UTF-8 encoded.\n+\n+    Args:\n+        *args: Items to include in the hash.\n+        hash_algo: Hash algorithm from hashlib to use.\n+\n+    Returns:\n+        A hex hash.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    h = hash_algo()\n+    for a in args:\n+        if isinstance(a, str):\n+            a = a.encode()\n+        h.update(a)\n+    return h.hexdigest()\n+\n+\n+def file_hash(path: str, hash_algo: Callable[..., Any] = _md5) -> str:\n+    \"\"\"Given a path to a file, produces a stable hash of the file contents.\n+\n+    Args:\n+        path (str): the path to a file\n+        hash_algo: Hash algorithm from hashlib to use.\n+\n+    Returns:\n+        str: a hash of the file contents\n+    \"\"\"\n+    contents = Path(path).read_bytes()\n+    return stable_hash(contents, hash_algo=hash_algo)\n+\n+\n+def hash_objects(\n+    *args: Any,\n+    hash_algo: Callable[..., Any] = _md5,\n+    raise_on_failure: bool = False,\n+    **kwargs: Any,\n+) -> Optional[str]:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Attempt to hash objects by dumping to JSON or serializing with cloudpickle.\n+\n+    Args:\n+        *args: Positional arguments to hash\n+        hash_algo: Hash algorithm to use\n+        raise_on_failure: If True, raise exceptions instead of returning None\n+        **kwargs: Keyword arguments to hash\n+\n+    Returns:\n+        A hash string or None if hashing failed\n+\n+    Raises:\n+        HashError: If objects cannot be hashed and raise_on_failure is True\n+    \"\"\"\n+    json_error = None\n+    pickle_error = None\n+\n+    try:\n+        serializer = JSONSerializer(dumps_kwargs={\"sort_keys\": True})\n+        tagged_args = [_tag_json_lossy_types(arg) for arg in args]\n+        tagged_kwargs = {\n+            key: _tag_json_lossy_types(value) for key, value in kwargs.items()\n+        }\n+        lossy = any(lossy for _, lossy in tagged_args) or any(\n+            lossy for _, lossy in tagged_kwargs.values()\n+        )\n+        if not lossy:\n+            # Keep hashes for JSON-native inputs identical to previous releases\n+            return stable_hash(serializer.dumps((args, kwargs)), hash_algo=hash_algo)\n+        payload = (\n+            [arg for arg, _ in tagged_args],\n+            {key: value for key, (value, _) in tagged_kwargs.items()},\n+        )\n+        return stable_hash(\n+            _TAGGED_PAYLOAD_PREFIX, serializer.dumps(payload), hash_algo=hash_algo\n+        )\n+    except Exception as e:\n+        json_error = str(e)\n+\n+    try:\n+        return stable_hash(cloudpickle.dumps((args, kwargs)), hash_algo=hash_algo)  # type: ignore[reportUnknownMemberType]\n+    except Exception as e:\n+        pickle_error = str(e)\n+\n+    if raise_on_failure:\n+        msg = (\n+            \"Unable to create hash - objects could not be serialized.\\n\"\n+            f\"  JSON error: {json_error}\\n\"\n+            f\"  Pickle error: {pickle_error}\"\n+        )\n+        raise HashError(msg)\n+\n+    return None\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/test_tasks.py b/tests/test_tasks.py\nindex 45e918e7d02e..29c4f8b9a1cf 100644\n--- a/tests/test_tasks.py\n+++ b/tests/test_tasks.py\n@@ -2446,6 +2446,24 @@ def bar():\n         assert await first_state.result() == await third_state.result()\n         assert await first_state.result() == 1\n \n+    async def test_task_input_hash_distinguishes_tuples_from_lists(self):\n+        @task(cache_key_fn=task_input_hash, persist_result=True)\n+        def foo(x):\n+            return type(x).__name__\n+\n+        @flow\n+        def bar():\n+            return (\n+                foo((1, 2, 3), return_state=True),\n+                foo([1, 2, 3], return_state=True),\n+            )\n+\n+        tuple_state, list_state = bar()\n+        assert tuple_state.name == \"Completed\"\n+        assert list_state.name == \"Completed\"\n+        assert await tuple_state.result() == \"tuple\"\n+        assert await list_state.result() == \"list\"\n+\n     async def test_task_input_hash_between_flows(\n         self,\n     ):\ndiff --git a/tests/utilities/test_hashing.py b/tests/utilities/test_hashing.py\nindex ac1e1547d386..45821fd89fad 100644\n--- a/tests/utilities/test_hashing.py\n+++ b/tests/utilities/test_hashing.py\n@@ -83,3 +83,56 @@ def test_hash_objects_raises_with_helpful_message(self):\n         assert \"Unable to create hash\" in error_msg\n         assert \"JSON error\" in error_msg\n         assert \"Pickle error\" in error_msg\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"a,b\",\n+        [\n+            ((1, 2, 3), [1, 2, 3]),\n+            ({\"x\": (1, 2, 3)}, {\"x\": [1, 2, 3]}),\n+            ({\"x\": [(1, 2)]}, {\"x\": [[1, 2]]}),\n+            ({1: \"a\", 2: \"b\"}, {\"1\": \"a\", \"2\": \"b\"}),\n+            ({True: \"a\"}, {\"true\": \"a\"}),\n+            ({None: \"a\"}, {\"null\": \"a\"}),\n+            ({1: \"a\"}, {True: \"a\"}),\n+            ({\"x\": {1: \"a\"}}, {\"x\": {\"1\": \"a\"}}),\n+            ((1, 2), {\"__tuple__\": [1, 2]}),\n+            ({1: \"a\"}, {\"__dict__\": [[1, \"a\"]]}),\n+            (((1, 2),), ({\"__tuple__\": [1, 2]},)),\n+            ({1: {\"__dict__\": [[1, \"a\"]]}}, {1: {1: \"a\"}}),\n+        ],\n+    )\n+    def test_hash_objects_distinguishes_types_json_conflates(self, a, b):\n+        assert hash_objects(a) != hash_objects(b)\n+        assert hash_objects(x=a) != hash_objects(x=b)\n+\n+    def test_hash_objects_non_string_key_dicts_ignore_insertion_order(self):\n+        assert hash_objects({1: \"a\", \"b\": 2, None: 3}) == hash_objects(\n+            {None: 3, \"b\": 2, 1: \"a\"}\n+        )\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"value,expected\",\n+        [\n+            ({\"__tuple__\": [1, 2]}, \"0ec37ee9aedc75d89ae93290c56c0bfa\"),\n+            ({\"a\": {\"__dict__\": [[1, \"a\"]]}}, \"27b3f9b96b0f98ea554f86eb4f8555ca\"),\n+        ],\n+    )\n+    def test_hash_objects_is_unchanged_for_json_native_inputs_using_tag_keys(\n+        self, value, expected\n+    ):\n+        assert hash_objects(value) == expected\n+\n+    def test_hash_objects_is_unchanged_for_json_native_inputs(self):\n+        assert (\n+            hash_objects({\"x\": [1, 2, 3], \"y\": {\"z\": None}}, k={\"q\": [1]})\n+            == \"545bbf5dffb19812091387db64bb8755\"\n+        )\n+\n+    def test_hash_objects_falls_back_to_pickle_for_unsupported_keys(self):\n+        assert hash_objects({(1, 2): \"a\"}) == hash_objects({(1, 2): \"a\"})\n+        assert hash_objects({(1, 2): \"a\"}) != hash_objects({(1, 3): \"a\"})\n+\n+    def test_hash_objects_handles_self_referential_containers(self):\n+        data: list[object] = [1]\n+        data.append(data)\n+        assert hash_objects(data) is not None\n", "problem_statement": "hash_objects erases tuple/list and int/str-key distinctions, so task_input_hash serves stale cached results\n## Description\n\n`hash_objects` (`src/prefect/utilities/hashing.py`) tries JSON first and only falls back to cloudpickle when JSON raises. JSON erases two type distinctions:\n\n- tuples serialize exactly like lists (`(1, 2, 3)` and `[1, 2, 3]` both become `[1, 2, 3]`)\n- int dict keys are coerced to strings (`{1: \"a\"}` and `{\"1\": \"a\"}` become identical)\n\nSo two different task inputs hash identically, and the second call is served the first call\u2019s cached result. This is a silent wrong result in the documented caching feature: any task whose behavior depends on the input container type (mutation, `isinstance` dispatch, tuple unpacking) is affected.\n\n## Minimal reproduction\n\nAgainst `main` (`b0479c0`, 3.9.2):\n\n```python\nfrom prefect.utilities.hashing import hash_objects\n\nassert hash_objects({\"x\": (1, 2, 3)}) == hash_objects({\"x\": [1, 2, 3]})\n# identical hash: 61ac92672f9dcab46768dbded544c49d\nassert hash_objects({1: \"a\", 2: \"b\"}) == hash_objects({\"1\": \"a\", \"2\": \"b\"})\n# identical hash: bf1832988873b502c915c271d46e6618\nassert hash_objects({\"x\": [(1, 2)]}) == hash_objects({\"x\": [[1, 2]]})\n```\n\nEnd to end, a task returning `type(x).__name__` with `cache_key_fn=task_input_hash` and `cache_expiration=timedelta(days=1)`, called in one flow with `(1, 2, 3)` then `[1, 2, 3]`:\n\n- tuple call returns `tuple` (Completed)\n- list call returns `tuple` with state `Cached(type=COMPLETED)` \u2014 stale; expected `list`.\n\n## Scope\n\nThe opt-in caching paths that hash inputs with `hash_objects`: `task_input_hash` and the `Inputs` cache policy. A plain `@task` with no cache args is not affected (verified: the default policy does not engage the cache lookup without `cache_expiration` / `persist_result`).\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nMake the JSON path of `hash_objects` type-preserving, e.g. tag tuples distinctly from lists and preserve int-key types before serializing, so inputs that differ only by container/key type hash differently. Note this changes the hash values themselves, invalidating previously cached keys; cache keys are not a stable API, but it is worth calling out.\n\nRelated observation, same root cause, not part of this fix: `JSONSerializer` round-trips `{1: \"a\"}` to `{\"1\": \"a\"}` and `(1, 2, 3)` to `[1, 2, 3]` for opt-in `result_serializer=\"json\"` users. Its docstring says inputs must be stdlib-JSON-compatible, so that reads as a documented limitation rather than a bug; mentioning it here only because the root cause is shared.\n\n## Environment\n\nPrefect 3.9.2, Python 3.12.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-09T05:14:12Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23816, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23816", "issue_numbers": ["23815"], "base_commit": "b0479c0c26045b06321191bbad0cdfbb334d7380", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/filesystems.py b/src/prefect/filesystems.py\nindex 789d22de959b..d8095164133d 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/filesystems.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/filesystems.py\n@@ -515,6 +515,17 @@ def get_directory(\n \n         return self.filesystem.get(from_path, local_path, recursive=True)\n \n+    def _put_file(self, local_path: str, remote_path: str, overwrite: bool) -> None:\n+        if not overwrite and self.filesystem.exists(remote_path):\n+            raise FileExistsError(f\"File {remote_path!r} already exists.\")\n+\n+        # fsspec's SMB implementation forwards extra keyword arguments to\n+        # `smbclient.open_file`, which rejects `overwrite`\n+        if overwrite and not self.basepath.startswith(\"smb://\"):\n+            self.filesystem.put_file(local_path, remote_path, overwrite=True)\n+        else:\n+            self.filesystem.put_file(local_path, remote_path)\n+\n     async def aput_directory(\n         self,\n         local_path: Optional[str] = None,\n@@ -526,6 +537,10 @@ async def aput_directory(\n         Uploads a directory from a given local path to a remote directory.\n \n         Defaults to uploading the entire contents of the current working directory to the block's basepath.\n+        If `overwrite` is `False`, a `FileExistsError` is raised when a file already exists at the destination.\n+        This check is best-effort: it is not atomic, so concurrent writers to the same destination may still\n+        overwrite each other. Files are uploaded one at a time, so files uploaded before a conflicting file\n+        is found are left in place.\n         \"\"\"\n         if to_path is None:\n             to_path = str(self.basepath)\n@@ -561,13 +576,9 @@ async def aput_directory(\n             if f.is_dir():\n                 pass\n             else:\n-                f = f.as_posix()\n-                if overwrite:\n-                    await run_sync_in_worker_thread(\n-                        self.filesystem.put_file, f, fpath, overwrite=True\n-                    )\n-                else:\n-                    await run_sync_in_worker_thread(self.filesystem.put_file, f, fpath)\n+                await run_sync_in_worker_thread(\n+                    self._put_file, f.as_posix(), fpath, overwrite\n+                )\n \n                 counter += 1\n \n@@ -585,6 +596,10 @@ def put_directory(\n         Uploads a directory from a given local path to a remote directory.\n \n         Defaults to uploading the entire contents of the current working directory to the block's basepath.\n+        If `overwrite` is `False`, a `FileExistsError` is raised when a file already exists at the destination.\n+        This check is best-effort: it is not atomic, so concurrent writers to the same destination may still\n+        overwrite each other. Files are uploaded one at a time, so files uploaded before a conflicting file\n+        is found are left in place.\n         \"\"\"\n         if to_path is None:\n             to_path = str(self.basepath)\n@@ -620,11 +635,7 @@ def put_directory(\n             if f.is_dir():\n                 pass\n             else:\n-                f = f.as_posix()\n-                if overwrite:\n-                    self.filesystem.put_file(f, fpath, overwrite=True)\n-                else:\n-                    self.filesystem.put_file(f, fpath)\n+                self._put_file(f.as_posix(), fpath, overwrite)\n \n                 counter += 1\n \n@@ -790,7 +801,6 @@ async def aput_directory(\n             local_path=local_path,\n             to_path=to_path,\n             ignore_file=ignore_file,\n-            overwrite=False,\n         )\n \n     @async_dispatch(aput_directory)\n@@ -808,7 +818,6 @@ def put_directory(\n             local_path=local_path,\n             to_path=to_path,\n             ignore_file=ignore_file,\n-            overwrite=False,\n         )\n \n     async def aread_path(self, path: str) -> bytes:\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/test_filesystems.py b/tests/test_filesystems.py\nindex f6494f1640dd..ccc2e409f773 100644\n--- a/tests/test_filesystems.py\n+++ b/tests/test_filesystems.py\n@@ -885,6 +885,76 @@ async def test_async_put_directory_works(self, tmp_path):\n         content = await fs.read_path(\"uploaded/test.txt\")\n         assert content == b\"hello aput\"\n \n+    def test_sync_put_directory_without_overwrite_raises_for_existing_file(\n+        self, tmp_path: Path\n+    ):\n+        fs = RemoteFileSystem(basepath=\"memory://test-put-dir-no-overwrite/\")\n+\n+        local_dir = tmp_path / \"local\"\n+        local_dir.mkdir()\n+        local_file = local_dir / \"test.txt\"\n+        local_file.write_bytes(b\"original\")\n+        fs.put_directory(local_path=str(local_dir), to_path=\"uploaded/\")\n+\n+        local_file.write_bytes(b\"replacement\")\n+        with pytest.raises(FileExistsError, match=\"test.txt\"):\n+            fs.put_directory(\n+                local_path=str(local_dir), to_path=\"uploaded/\", overwrite=False\n+            )\n+\n+        assert fs.read_path(\"uploaded/test.txt\") == b\"original\"\n+\n+    def test_sync_put_directory_without_overwrite_uploads_new_files(\n+        self, tmp_path: Path\n+    ):\n+        fs = RemoteFileSystem(basepath=\"memory://test-put-dir-no-overwrite-new/\")\n+\n+        local_dir = tmp_path / \"local\"\n+        local_dir.mkdir()\n+        (local_dir / \"test.txt\").write_bytes(b\"hello\")\n+\n+        count = fs.put_directory(\n+            local_path=str(local_dir), to_path=\"uploaded/\", overwrite=False\n+        )\n+\n+        assert count == 1\n+        assert fs.read_path(\"uploaded/test.txt\") == b\"hello\"\n+\n+    def test_sync_put_directory_overwrites_existing_file_by_default(\n+        self, tmp_path: Path\n+    ):\n+        fs = RemoteFileSystem(basepath=\"memory://test-put-dir-overwrite/\")\n+\n+        local_dir = tmp_path / \"local\"\n+        local_dir.mkdir()\n+        local_file = local_dir / \"test.txt\"\n+        local_file.write_bytes(b\"original\")\n+        fs.put_directory(local_path=str(local_dir), to_path=\"uploaded/\")\n+\n+        local_file.write_bytes(b\"replacement\")\n+        fs.put_directory(local_path=str(local_dir), to_path=\"uploaded/\")\n+\n+        assert fs.read_path(\"uploaded/test.txt\") == b\"replacement\"\n+\n+    async def test_async_put_directory_without_overwrite_raises_for_existing_file(\n+        self, tmp_path: Path\n+    ):\n+        fs = RemoteFileSystem(basepath=\"memory://test-aput-dir-no-overwrite/\")\n+\n+        local_dir = tmp_path / \"local\"\n+        local_dir.mkdir()\n+        local_file = local_dir / \"test.txt\"\n+        local_file.write_bytes(b\"original\")\n+        await fs.aput_directory(local_path=str(local_dir), to_path=\"uploaded/\")\n+\n+        local_file.write_bytes(b\"replacement\")\n+        with pytest.raises(FileExistsError, match=\"test.txt\"):\n+            await fs.aput_directory(\n+                local_path=str(local_dir), to_path=\"uploaded/\", overwrite=False\n+            )\n+\n+        assert await fs.aread_path(\"uploaded/test.txt\") == b\"original\"\n+\n \n try:\n     import fsspec.implementations.smb  # noqa: F401\n@@ -941,3 +1011,19 @@ async def test_write_path_constructs_good_unc_path(self, mock_smbclient, smb_blo\n             \"The host name should not be duplicated\"\n         )\n         assert unc_path == r\"\\\\my.smb.server.edu\\prefect\\storage\\test-dir\"\n+\n+    @patch(\"fsspec.implementations.smb.smbclient\")\n+    async def test_put_directory_overwrites_existing_files(\n+        self, mock_smbclient: MagicMock, smb_block: SMB, tmp_path: Path\n+    ):\n+        mock_smbclient.register_session.return_value = None\n+        mock_smbclient.open_file.return_value = MagicMock()\n+        (tmp_path / \"flow.py\").write_text(\"print('hello')\")\n+\n+        count = await smb_block.aput_directory(local_path=str(tmp_path))\n+\n+        assert count == 1\n+        mock_smbclient.open_file.assert_called_once()\n+        call_args, call_kwargs = mock_smbclient.open_file.call_args\n+        assert call_args[0] == r\"\\\\my.smb.server.edu\\prefect\\storage\\flow.py\"\n+        assert \"overwrite\" not in call_kwargs\n", "problem_statement": "RemoteFileSystem.put_directory(overwrite=False) silently overwrites existing files\n### Bug summary\n\n## Bug\n\n`RemoteFileSystem.put_directory(overwrite=False)` is supposed to prevent overwriting existing files,\nbut it silently overwrites them anyway.\n\n## Root cause\n\nIn `src/prefect/filesystems.py`, both `put_directory` and `aput_directory` have this logic:\n\n```python\nif overwrite:\n    self.filesystem.put_file(f, fpath, overwrite=True)\nelse:\n    self.filesystem.put_file(f, fpath)  # overwrite=False is dropped here\n```\n\nWhen `overwrite=False`, the code calls `put_file` with no mode argument. However, fsspec's\n`AbstractFileSystem.put_file` uses `mode='overwrite'` as its default \u2014 not an `overwrite=` boolean.\nSo omitting the argument always overwrites, regardless of what the caller passed.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```python\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom tempfile import TemporaryDirectory\nfrom prefect.filesystems import RemoteFileSystem\n\nwith TemporaryDirectory() as folder:\n    source = Path(folder) / \"orders.csv\"\n    remote = RemoteFileSystem(basepath=\"memory://overwrite-test\")\n\n    source.write_text(\"original\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    remote.put_directory(local_path=folder)\n\n    source.write_text(\"replacement\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    remote.put_directory(local_path=folder, overwrite=False)\n\n    print(remote.read_path(\"orders.csv\"))  # prints b'replacement' \u2014 should raise or skip\n```\n\n## Expected behaviour\n\nWhen `overwrite=False`, existing files should not be overwritten. fsspec supports\n`mode='error'` to raise on conflict, or `mode='overwrite'` to allow it.\n\n## Fix\n\n```python\nself.filesystem.put_file(f, fpath, mode='overwrite' if overwrite else 'error')\n```\n\nThis applies to both `put_directory` and `aput_directory`.\n\n## Environment\n\n- prefect version: (paste output of `python -c \"import prefect; print(prefect.__version__)\"`)\n- Python version: 3.x\n- OS: Windows / Linux / macOS\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\n- Prefect version: 3.9.2\n- API version: 0.8.4\n- Python version: 3.12.10\n- Git commit: b0479c0c\n- OS/Arch: win32/AMD64\n- Pydantic version: 2.14.1\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-09T01:31:26Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23814, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23814", "issue_numbers": ["23813"], "base_commit": "d42ca5255780cbb16d851bba76a6d41d87d0069c", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/runner/storage.py b/src/prefect/runner/storage.py\nindex 17895935096b..427515350279 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/runner/storage.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/runner/storage.py\n@@ -672,11 +672,13 @@ async def _clone_repo(self):\n             raise RuntimeError(error_message) from exc_chain\n \n         if self._commit_sha:\n-            # Fetch the commit\n-            await run_process(\n-                [\"git\", \"fetch\", \"origin\", self._commit_sha],\n-                cwd=self.destination,\n-            )\n+            # The clone usually already contains the commit. Only fetch it when\n+            # missing, since git can't fetch a short SHA from a remote.\n+            if not await self._has_commit():\n+                await run_process(\n+                    [\"git\", \"fetch\", \"origin\", self._commit_sha],\n+                    cwd=self.destination,\n+                )\n             await self._checkout_commit(self._commit_sha)\n \n         # Once repository is cloned and the repo is in sparse-checkout mode then grow the working directory\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/runner/test_storage.py b/tests/runner/test_storage.py\nindex 0f5f7592be52..f62c6b011b1a 100644\n--- a/tests/runner/test_storage.py\n+++ b/tests/runner/test_storage.py\n@@ -1345,11 +1345,18 @@ def test_to_pull_step_with_custom_name_and_directories(self):\n             result = repo.to_pull_step()\n             assert result == expected_output\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"has_commit\", [True, False])\n     async def test_clone_repo_with_commit_sha(\n-        self, mock_run_process: AsyncMock, monkeypatch\n+        self,\n+        mock_run_process: AsyncMock,\n+        monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch,\n+        has_commit: bool,\n     ):\n         # pretend the repo doesn't exist\n         monkeypatch.setattr(\"pathlib.Path.exists\", lambda x: False)\n+        monkeypatch.setattr(\n+            GitRepository, \"_has_commit\", AsyncMock(return_value=has_commit)\n+        )\n \n         repo = GitRepository(\n             url=\"https://github.com/org/repo.git\", commit_sha=\"1234567890\"\n@@ -1357,7 +1364,17 @@ async def test_clone_repo_with_commit_sha(\n \n         await repo.pull_code()\n \n-        # Verify the expected git commands were called in order\n+        # Only fetch the commit when the clone doesn't already contain it\n+        fetch_calls = (\n+            []\n+            if has_commit\n+            else [\n+                call(\n+                    [\"git\", \"fetch\", \"origin\", \"1234567890\"],\n+                    cwd=Path.cwd() / \"repo\",\n+                )\n+            ]\n+        )\n         expected_calls = [\n             call(\n                 [\n@@ -1369,10 +1386,7 @@ async def test_clone_repo_with_commit_sha(\n                     str(Path.cwd() / \"repo\"),\n                 ]\n             ),\n-            call(\n-                [\"git\", \"fetch\", \"origin\", \"1234567890\"],\n-                cwd=Path.cwd() / \"repo\",\n-            ),\n+            *fetch_calls,\n             call(\n                 [\"git\", \"checkout\", \"1234567890\"],\n                 cwd=Path.cwd() / \"repo\",\n@@ -1786,6 +1800,13 @@ async def test_pull_code_checks_out_commit_on_other_branch_in_shallow_checkout(\n         assert _git(\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\", cwd=repo.destination) == sha_c\n         assert (repo.destination / \"f.txt\").read_text() == \"C\"\n \n+    async def test_pull_code_clones_and_checks_out_short_sha(\n+        self, upstream: _Upstream, base_path: Path\n+    ):\n+        repo = await self._pull(upstream.url, base_path, upstream.sha_a[:7])\n+        assert _git(\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\", cwd=repo.destination) == upstream.sha_a\n+        assert (repo.destination / \"f.txt\").read_text() == \"A\"\n+\n     async def test_pull_code_checks_out_local_commit_without_fetching(\n         self, upstream: _Upstream, base_path: Path\n     ):\n", "problem_statement": "git fetch origin <short-sha> fails with exit code 128 in GitRepository._clone_repo\n### Bug summary\n\n## Bug\n\n`GitRepository._clone_repo` unconditionally runs `git fetch origin <commit_sha>` after cloning. This fails with exit code 128 when a short SHA is provided.\n\nGit cannot fetch by short SHA from a remote \u2014 it only accepts full SHAs, branch names, or tags.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom prefect.runner.storage import GitRepository\n\nrepo = GitRepository(\n    url=\"https://github.com/org/repo.git\",\n    commit_sha=\"1234567\"  # short SHA\n)\nawait repo.pull_code()\n# Raises: RuntimeError \u2014 git fetch exits with code 128\n```\n\n## Expected behaviour\n\nAfter cloning with `--no-checkout`, the commit is usually already present in the local object store. Prefect should check first and only fetch if the commit is genuinely missing.\n\n## Environment\n\n- Prefect version: 3.x\n- Python: 3.12\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.9.2\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.12.10\nGit commit:           b0479c0c\nBuilt:                Sun, Nov 08, 2026 09:39 PM\nOS/Arch:              win32/AMD64\nProfile:              local\nServer type:          server\nPydantic version:     2.14.1\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.47.1\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-09T01:16:54Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23809, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23809", "issue_numbers": ["23808"], "base_commit": "2433f8690e4c5864e51e9a2aa5e37e048f2998fe", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/_internal/pydantic/v2_schema.py b/src/prefect/_internal/pydantic/v2_schema.py\nindex 1785e1215a5a..acfd1d0a55e2 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/_internal/pydantic/v2_schema.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/_internal/pydantic/v2_schema.py\n@@ -48,6 +48,23 @@ def has_v2_type_as_param(signature: inspect.Signature) -> bool:\n     return False\n \n \n+def _annotated_field_description(annotation: t.Any) -> str | None:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Return the description of the `Field`s in an `Annotated` type, if any.\n+\n+    The `Field`s are merged the way pydantic merges them, so a later `Field`\n+    that sets `description=None` or `\"\"` replaces an earlier description.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    if typing.get_origin(annotation) is not typing.Annotated:\n+        return None\n+    try:\n+        return FieldInfo.from_annotation(annotation).description\n+    except TypeError:\n+        # pydantic can't merge these `Field`s, e.g. `default` with\n+        # `default_factory`; `generate_parameter_schema` falls back to `Any`\n+        return None\n+\n+\n def process_v2_params(\n     param: inspect.Parameter,\n     *,\n@@ -72,10 +89,6 @@ def process_v2_params(\n \n     existing_field = param.default if isinstance(param.default, FieldInfo) else None\n     default_value = existing_field.default if existing_field else param.default\n-    if existing_field and existing_field.description:\n-        description = existing_field.description\n-    else:\n-        description = docstrings.get(param.name)\n \n     extra: dict[str, typing.Any] = {}\n     if existing_field and isinstance(existing_field.json_schema_extra, dict):\n@@ -93,6 +106,13 @@ def process_v2_params(\n         type_ = dict[str, type_]\n         default_value = {}\n \n+    # after the `*args`/`**kwargs` wrapping, so a `Field` in their annotation\n+    # keeps describing each value\n+    if existing_field and existing_field.description:\n+        description = existing_field.description\n+    else:\n+        description = _annotated_field_description(type_) or docstrings.get(param.name)\n+\n     field = pydantic.Field(\n         default=... if default_value is param.empty else default_value,\n         title=param.name,\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/_internal/schemas/test_v2_schema.py b/tests/_internal/schemas/test_v2_schema.py\nindex 103ce8bcac8d..afc2f73c55b1 100644\n--- a/tests/_internal/schemas/test_v2_schema.py\n+++ b/tests/_internal/schemas/test_v2_schema.py\n@@ -1,5 +1,7 @@\n import inspect\n+from typing import Annotated\n \n+import pytest\n from pydantic import Field\n from pydantic_core import PydanticUndefined\n \n@@ -113,3 +115,70 @@ def test_process_v2_params_with_existing_fieldinfo_no_json_schema_extra(self):\n \n         assert name == \"name\"\n         assert field.json_schema_extra == {\"position\": 5}\n+\n+    def test_process_v2_params_with_annotated_fieldinfo(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test parameter processing with a FieldInfo in an Annotated type.\"\"\"\n+        param = inspect.Parameter(\n+            \"name\",\n+            inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD,\n+            default=\"default_name\",\n+            annotation=Annotated[str, Field(description=\"Annotated field description\")],\n+        )\n+\n+        name, type_, field = process_v2_params(\n+            param, position=0, docstrings={\"name\": \"Docstring description\"}, aliases={}\n+        )\n+\n+        assert name == \"name\"\n+        assert field.default == \"default_name\"\n+        assert field.title == \"name\"\n+        assert field.description == \"Annotated field description\"\n+\n+    def test_process_v2_params_with_annotated_fieldinfo_uses_last_description(self):\n+        \"\"\"Test parameter processing with several FieldInfo objects in Annotated.\"\"\"\n+        param = inspect.Parameter(\n+            \"name\",\n+            inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD,\n+            annotation=Annotated[\n+                str,\n+                Field(description=\"First description\"),\n+                Field(description=\"Second description\"),\n+                Field(max_length=10),\n+            ],\n+        )\n+\n+        name, type_, field = process_v2_params(\n+            param, position=0, docstrings={}, aliases={}\n+        )\n+\n+        assert name == \"name\"\n+        assert field.description == \"Second description\"\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"cleared_description\", [None, \"\"])\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"docstrings, expected_description\",\n+        [({}, None), ({\"name\": \"Docstring description\"}, \"Docstring description\")],\n+    )\n+    def test_process_v2_params_with_annotated_fieldinfo_later_field_clears_description(\n+        self,\n+        cleared_description: str | None,\n+        docstrings: dict[str, str],\n+        expected_description: str | None,\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"Test that a later FieldInfo in Annotated can clear an earlier description.\"\"\"\n+        param = inspect.Parameter(\n+            \"name\",\n+            inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD,\n+            annotation=Annotated[\n+                str,\n+                Field(description=\"Earlier description\"),\n+                Field(description=cleared_description),\n+            ],\n+        )\n+\n+        name, type_, field = process_v2_params(\n+            param, position=0, docstrings=docstrings, aliases={}\n+        )\n+\n+        assert name == \"name\"\n+        assert field.description == expected_description\ndiff --git a/tests/utilities/test_callables.py b/tests/utilities/test_callables.py\nindex 34eded190cbb..c5eca11ede89 100644\n--- a/tests/utilities/test_callables.py\n+++ b/tests/utilities/test_callables.py\n@@ -5,7 +5,7 @@\n from enum import Enum\n from pathlib import Path\n from textwrap import dedent\n-from typing import Any, Callable, Dict, List, Optional, Tuple, Union\n+from typing import Annotated, Any, Callable, Dict, List, Optional, Tuple, Union\n \n import pytest\n from pydantic import BaseModel, Field, SecretStr\n@@ -821,6 +821,84 @@ def f(x, y):\n             \"definitions\": {},\n         }\n \n+    def test_flow_with_annotated_field_description(self):\n+        def f(\n+            x: Annotated[int, Field(description=\"x from Field\")],\n+            y: Annotated[int, Field(ge=0)] = 0,\n+            z: Annotated[int, Field(description=\"z from Field\")] = Field(\n+                default=1, description=\"z from default Field\"\n+            ),\n+            w: Annotated[\n+                int, Field(description=\"w from Field\"), Field(description=None)\n+            ] = 2,\n+        ):\n+            \"\"\"Function f.\n+\n+            Args:\n+                y: y from docstring\n+                w: w from docstring\n+            \"\"\"\n+\n+        schema = callables.parameter_schema(f)\n+        assert schema.model_dump_for_openapi() == {\n+            \"title\": \"Parameters\",\n+            \"type\": \"object\",\n+            \"properties\": {\n+                \"x\": {\n+                    \"title\": \"x\",\n+                    \"description\": \"x from Field\",\n+                    \"position\": 0,\n+                    \"type\": \"integer\",\n+                },\n+                \"y\": {\n+                    \"title\": \"y\",\n+                    \"description\": \"y from docstring\",\n+                    \"default\": 0,\n+                    \"minimum\": 0,\n+                    \"position\": 1,\n+                    \"type\": \"integer\",\n+                },\n+                \"z\": {\n+                    \"title\": \"z\",\n+                    \"description\": \"z from default Field\",\n+                    \"default\": 1,\n+                    \"position\": 2,\n+                    \"type\": \"integer\",\n+                },\n+                \"w\": {\n+                    \"title\": \"w\",\n+                    \"description\": \"w from docstring\",\n+                    \"default\": 2,\n+                    \"position\": 3,\n+                    \"type\": \"integer\",\n+                },\n+            },\n+            \"required\": [\"x\"],\n+            \"definitions\": {},\n+        }\n+\n+    def test_flow_with_annotated_fields_pydantic_cannot_merge(self):\n+        def f(\n+            x: Annotated[\n+                tuple[int, ...], Field(default=(1,)), Field(default_factory=tuple)\n+            ] = (),\n+        ):\n+            \"\"\"Function f.\n+\n+            Args:\n+                x: x from docstring\n+            \"\"\"\n+\n+        schema = callables.parameter_schema(f)\n+        assert schema.model_dump_for_openapi()[\"properties\"] == {\n+            \"x\": {\n+                \"title\": \"x\",\n+                \"description\": \"x from docstring\",\n+                \"default\": [],\n+                \"position\": 0,\n+            },\n+        }\n+\n \n class TestGetCallParameters:\n     def test_raises_parameter_bind_with_no_kwargs(self):\n", "problem_statement": "`Annotated[T, Field(description=...)]` descriptions are missing from flow parameter schemas\n### Bug summary\n\nA flow parameter declared as `Annotated[T, Field(description=...)]` ends up without a description in the flow's parameter schema, so the run form in the UI shows none. The same description given as a default, `x: int = Field(1, description=...)`, works since #19281 (for #13912).\n\nWe hit this in production: our flows declared their parameters as `Annotated[int, Field(description=...)]`, the Custom Run form showed no descriptions, and we moved every description to docstrings to get them back.\n\nMinimal repro, no server needed:\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Annotated\n\nfrom pydantic import Field\n\nfrom prefect import flow\n\n\n@flow\ndef with_default(batch_size: int = Field(40, description=\"Files per batch.\")):\n    pass\n\n\n@flow\ndef with_annotated(batch_size: Annotated[int, Field(description=\"Files per batch.\")] = 40):\n    pass\n\n\nfor f in (with_default, with_annotated):\n    print(f\"{f.name}: {f.parameters.properties['batch_size'].get('description')!r}\")\n```\n\nOn 3.9.2 it prints:\n\n```\nwith-default: 'Files per batch.'\nwith-annotated: None\n```\n\nExpected: `'Files per batch.'` for both.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.9.2\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.12.16\nGit commit:           b0479c0c\nBuilt:                Fri, Nov 06, 2026 09:21 PM\nOS/Arch:              linux/x86_64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          ephemeral\nPydantic version:     2.14.3\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.46.1\n```\n\nSame on `main` at b0479c0c26045b06321191bbad0cdfbb334d7380 (the 3.9.2 tag).\n\n### Additional context\n\nI think the root cause is in `process_v2_params`: the description only comes from a `Field` default or the docstring, so it's `None` here (https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/b0479c0c26045b06321191bbad0cdfbb334d7380/src/prefect/_internal/pydantic/v2_schema.py#L73-L78), and the field is built with that explicit `description=None` (https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/b0479c0c26045b06321191bbad0cdfbb334d7380/src/prefect/_internal/pydantic/v2_schema.py#L96-L102). When pydantic merges it with the `Field` in `Annotated`, the explicit `None` wins.\n\nA few related things I checked:\n- with a docstring entry for the parameter, the docstring wins over the `Annotated` description, while a `Field` default wins over the docstring;\n- other `Field` settings in `Annotated` (`ge`, `le`, `examples`, `json_schema_extra`) already reach the schema, since Prefect doesn't pass them explicitly; only `description` is lost (`title` is always the parameter name, with either form);\n- `prefect deploy`, `flow.serve()`/`flow.deploy()` and `Flow.parameters` all build the schema through this function, so deployments get the same schema.\n\nProposed fix: in `process_v2_params`, take the description from a `Field` in `Annotated` when there's no `Field` default with one, before falling back to the docstring, so both forms get the same precedence. I plan to open a pull request for this.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-08T01:21:32Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23807, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23807", "issue_numbers": ["23806"], "base_commit": "2433f8690e4c5864e51e9a2aa5e37e048f2998fe", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_commands.py b/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_commands.py\nindex 4f9e61e52d61..a68f8133bac7 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_commands.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_commands.py\n@@ -19,9 +19,11 @@\n import prefect.cli._app as _cli\n from prefect.cli._utilities import (\n     exit_with_error,\n+    print_verbatim,\n     with_cli_exception_handling,\n )\n from prefect.client.schemas.objects import ConcurrencyLimitConfig\n+from prefect.exceptions import ScriptError, exception_traceback\n \n deploy_app: cyclopts.App = cyclopts.App(\n     name=\"deploy\",\n@@ -488,5 +490,8 @@ async def deploy(\n                 console=_cli.console,\n                 is_interactive=_cli.is_interactive,\n             )\n+    except ScriptError as exc:\n+        print_verbatim(exception_traceback(exc.user_exc))\n+        exit_with_error(str(exc))\n     except ValueError as exc:\n         exit_with_error(str(exc))\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/cli/flow.py b/src/prefect/cli/flow.py\nindex d3eae5561a86..2ed7ac30face 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/cli/flow.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/cli/flow.py\n@@ -21,7 +21,7 @@\n from prefect.client.schemas.schedules import construct_schedule\n from prefect.client.schemas.sorting import FlowSort\n from prefect.deployments.runner import RunnerDeployment\n-from prefect.exceptions import MissingFlowError\n+from prefect.exceptions import MissingFlowError, ScriptError, exception_traceback\n from prefect.runner import Runner\n from prefect.utilities import urls\n \n@@ -225,6 +225,9 @@ async def serve(\n             version=version,\n             concurrency_limit=global_limit,\n         )\n+    except ScriptError as exc:\n+        print_verbatim(exception_traceback(exc.user_exc))\n+        exit_with_error(str(exc))\n     except (MissingFlowError, ValueError) as exc:\n         exit_with_error(str(exc))\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/cli/test_deploy.py b/tests/cli/test_deploy.py\nindex b2294b6ebbd4..a8c2755f9365 100644\n--- a/tests/cli/test_deploy.py\n+++ b/tests/cli/test_deploy.py\n@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@\n import warnings\n from datetime import timedelta\n from pathlib import Path\n+from textwrap import dedent\n from typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Any, Optional\n from unittest import mock\n from unittest.mock import AsyncMock\n@@ -492,6 +493,38 @@ async def test_deploy_with_missing_imports(\n         assert deployment.name == \"test-name\"\n         assert deployment.work_pool_name == work_pool.name\n \n+    async def test_deploy_reports_script_error_for_non_def_entrypoint(\n+        self, project_dir: Path, work_pool: WorkPool\n+    ):\n+        (project_dir / \"flows\" / \"etl.py\").write_text(\n+            dedent(\n+                \"\"\"\n+                import not_a_module\n+                from prefect import flow\n+\n+                @flow\n+                def etl():\n+                    pass\n+\n+                etl_daily = etl.with_options(name=\"etl-daily\")\n+                \"\"\"\n+            )\n+        )\n+\n+        await run_sync_in_worker_thread(\n+            invoke_and_assert,\n+            command=(\n+                f\"deploy ./flows/etl.py:etl_daily -n test-name -p {work_pool.name}\"\n+            ),\n+            expected_code=1,\n+            expected_output_contains=[\n+                \"import not_a_module\",\n+                \"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'not_a_module'\",\n+                \"encountered an exception: ModuleNotFoundError\",\n+            ],\n+            expected_output_does_not_contain=[\"load_flow_from_entrypoint\"],\n+        )\n+\n     async def test_project_deploy_with_default_work_pool(\n         self, project_dir: Path, prefect_client: PrefectClient\n     ):\ndiff --git a/tests/cli/test_flow.py b/tests/cli/test_flow.py\nindex a415a4f87d6a..0118c0328ec2 100644\n--- a/tests/cli/test_flow.py\n+++ b/tests/cli/test_flow.py\n@@ -1,5 +1,7 @@\n import datetime\n import json\n+from pathlib import Path\n+from textwrap import dedent\n from typing import Any\n from unittest.mock import AsyncMock\n \n@@ -39,6 +41,36 @@ def test_flow_serve_cli_requires_entrypoint(self):\n             expected_output_contains=\"requires an argument\",\n         )\n \n+    def test_flow_serve_cli_reports_script_error_for_non_def_entrypoint(\n+        self, tmp_path: Path\n+    ):\n+        flow_file = tmp_path / \"etl.py\"\n+        flow_file.write_text(\n+            dedent(\n+                \"\"\"\n+                import not_a_module\n+                from prefect import flow\n+\n+                @flow\n+                def etl():\n+                    pass\n+\n+                etl_daily = etl.with_options(name=\"etl-daily\")\n+                \"\"\"\n+            )\n+        )\n+\n+        invoke_and_assert(\n+            command=[\"flow\", \"serve\", f\"{flow_file}:etl_daily\", \"--name\", \"test\"],\n+            expected_code=1,\n+            expected_output_contains=[\n+                \"import not_a_module\",\n+                \"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'not_a_module'\",\n+                \"encountered an exception: ModuleNotFoundError\",\n+            ],\n+            expected_output_does_not_contain=[\"load_flow_from_entrypoint\"],\n+        )\n+\n     async def test_flow_serve_cli_creates_deployment(\n         self, prefect_client: PrefectClient, mock_runner_start: AsyncMock\n     ):\n", "problem_statement": "`prefect deploy` and `prefect flow serve` report flow script errors as `An exception occurred.`\n### Bug summary\n\nFollow-up to #23769. Since #23773 (in 3.9.2), a script that fails to import behind a non-`def` entrypoint raises its `ScriptError` instead of `Could not find object`, so the real error is no longer lost. But `prefect deploy` and `prefect flow serve` don't handle `ScriptError`, so it falls through to the CLI's catch-all handler: the output is the chained traceback, mostly Prefect's own frames, and the last line is `An exception occurred.`, which reads like Prefect crashed rather than the user's script failing to import.\n\nA syntax error in the flow file gets the same output with a regular `def` entrypoint, on 3.9.1 too (where it also prints the fallback's own `SyntaxError` from `ast.parse`, three tracebacks in total).\n\n`prefect block register` already handles a `ScriptError` by printing the script's traceback and its own error message.\n\nMinimal repro, same files as #23769, with a local server running (`prefect server start`):\n\n`flows.py`:\n\n```python\nimport some_dependency_not_installed  # e.g. a package that is only in the flow's image\n\nfrom prefect import flow\n\n\n@flow\ndef etl():\n    pass\n\n\netl_daily = etl.with_options(name=\"etl-daily\", retries=2)\n```\n\n`prefect.yaml`:\n\n```yaml\ndeployments:\n  - name: daily\n    entrypoint: flows.py:etl_daily\n    work_pool:\n      name: local\n```\n\n```bash\nprefect work-pool create local --type process\nprefect deploy --name daily\nprefect flow serve flows.py:etl_daily --name daily\n```\n\nOutput of `prefect deploy --name daily` on 3.9.2 (site-packages paths shortened); `prefect flow serve` prints the same, with `cli/flow.py` and `deployments/runner.py` in place of the two deploy frames:\n\n```\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap_external>\", line 999, in exec_module\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 488, in _call_with_frames_removed\n  File \"/tmp/project/flows.py\", line 1, in <module>\n    import some_dependency_not_installed  # e.g. a package that is only in the flow's image\n    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'some_dependency_not_installed'\n\nThe above exception was the direct cause of the following exception:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \".../site-packages/prefect/cli/_utilities.py\", line 55, in async_wrapper\n    return await fn(*args, **kwargs)\n           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \".../site-packages/prefect/cli/deploy/_commands.py\", line 483, in deploy\n    await _run_single_deploy(\n  File \".../site-packages/prefect/cli/deploy/_core.py\", line 116, in _run_single_deploy\n    flow = load_flow_from_entrypoint(deploy_config[\"entrypoint\"])\n           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \".../site-packages/prefect/flows.py\", line 3026, in load_flow_from_entrypoint\n    flow: Flow[P, Any] = import_object(entrypoint)  # pyright: ignore[reportRedeclaration]\n                         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \".../site-packages/prefect/utilities/importtools.py\", line 171, in import_object\n    module = load_script_as_module(script_path)\n             ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \".../site-packages/prefect/utilities/importtools.py\", line 136, in load_script_as_module\n    raise ScriptError(user_exc=exc, path=path) from exc\nprefect.exceptions.ScriptError: Script at 'flows.py' encountered an exception: ModuleNotFoundError(\"No module named 'some_dependency_not_installed'\")\nAn exception occurred.\n```\n\nExpected: the script's traceback and the error, as `prefect block register` does for a script that fails to load:\n\n```\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap_external>\", line 999, in exec_module\n  File \"<frozen importlib._bootstrap>\", line 488, in _call_with_frames_removed\n  File \"/tmp/project/flows.py\", line 1, in <module>\n    import some_dependency_not_installed  # e.g. a package that is only in the flow's image\n    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'some_dependency_not_installed'\n\nScript at 'flows.py' encountered an exception: ModuleNotFoundError(\"No module named 'some_dependency_not_installed'\")\n```\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.9.2\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.12.16\nGit commit:           b0479c0c\nBuilt:                Fri, Nov 06, 2026 09:21 PM\nOS/Arch:              linux/x86_64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          server\nPydantic version:     2.14.3\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.46.1\n```\n\nSame on `main` at b0479c0c26045b06321191bbad0cdfbb334d7380 (the 3.9.2 tag).\n\n### Additional context\n\nWhere it happens:\n- `prefect deploy` turns only `ValueError` into a message: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/b0479c0c26045b06321191bbad0cdfbb334d7380/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_commands.py#L491-L492\n- `prefect flow serve` handles only `MissingFlowError` and `ValueError`: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/b0479c0c26045b06321191bbad0cdfbb334d7380/src/prefect/cli/flow.py#L228-L229\n- anything else reaches `with_cli_exception_handling`, which prints the traceback and `An exception occurred.`: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/b0479c0c26045b06321191bbad0cdfbb334d7380/src/prefect/cli/_utilities.py#L60-L66\n- `prefect block register` catches `ScriptError` and prints `exception_traceback(exc.user_exc)`: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/b0479c0c26045b06321191bbad0cdfbb334d7380/src/prefect/cli/block.py#L228-L234\n\nProposed fix: catch `ScriptError` in both commands, print the script's traceback with `print_verbatim` (it holds user source, which `console.print` would parse as Rich markup), and exit with `exit_with_error(str(exc))`. I plan to open a pull request for this.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-08T00:49:37Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23794, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23794", "issue_numbers": ["23793"], "base_commit": "802fc9435423bed0d16fe30862de25a8df62493f", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/utilities/collections.py b/src/prefect/utilities/collections.py\nindex b0a68b865ac3..11a52f4d36df 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/utilities/collections.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/utilities/collections.py\n@@ -5,7 +5,7 @@\n import io\n import itertools\n import types\n-from collections import OrderedDict\n+from collections import Counter, OrderedDict, defaultdict, deque\n from collections.abc import (\n     Callable,\n     Collection,\n@@ -340,8 +340,10 @@ def visit_collection(\n     Supported types:\n     - List (including iterators)\n     - Tuple\n-    - Set\n-    - Dict (note: keys are also visited recursively)\n+    - Set and frozenset\n+    - Deque\n+    - Dict, including `defaultdict` and `Counter` (note: keys are also visited\n+      recursively)\n     - Dataclass\n     - Pydantic model\n     - Prefect annotations\n@@ -489,8 +491,10 @@ def visit_expression(expr: Any) -> Any:\n \n     # --- Sequences\n \n-    elif isinstance(expr, (list, tuple, set)):\n-        seq = cast(Union[list[Any], tuple[Any], set[Any]], expr)\n+    elif isinstance(expr, (list, tuple, set, frozenset, deque)):\n+        seq = cast(\n+            Union[list[Any], tuple[Any], set[Any], frozenset[Any], deque[Any]], expr\n+        )\n         items = [visit_nested(o) for o in seq]\n         if return_data:\n             modified = any(item is not orig for item, orig in zip(items, seq))\n@@ -498,6 +502,8 @@ def visit_expression(expr: Any) -> Any:\n                 # Use _make for NamedTuples\n                 if hasattr(type(seq), \"_make\"):\n                     result = type(seq)._make(items)\n+                elif isinstance(seq, deque):\n+                    result = type(seq)(items, maxlen=seq.maxlen)\n                 else:\n                     result = type(seq)(items)\n \n@@ -512,7 +518,13 @@ def visit_expression(expr: Any) -> Any:\n                 for (k1, v1), (k2, v2) in zip(items, mapping.items())\n             )\n             if modified:\n-                result = type(mapping)(items)\n+                if isinstance(mapping, defaultdict):\n+                    result = type(mapping)(mapping.default_factory, items)\n+                elif isinstance(mapping, Counter):\n+                    # `Counter(iterable)` would count the pairs themselves\n+                    result = type(mapping)(dict(items))\n+                else:\n+                    result = type(mapping)(items)\n \n     # --- Dataclasses\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/test_tasks.py b/tests/test_tasks.py\nindex a6899e9a9cae..2af54c7cb44a 100644\n--- a/tests/test_tasks.py\n+++ b/tests/test_tasks.py\n@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@\n import time\n import weakref\n from asyncio import Event, sleep\n+from collections import defaultdict, deque\n from functools import partial, wraps\n from pathlib import Path\n from typing import Any, Dict, List, Optional\n@@ -3082,6 +3083,41 @@ def test_flow():\n             },\n         )\n \n+    async def test_task_inputs_populated_with_upstream_futures_in_stdlib_containers(\n+        self, prefect_client, events_pipeline\n+    ):\n+        @task\n+        def foo(x):\n+            return x\n+\n+        @task\n+        def bar(x, y):\n+            return x, y\n+\n+        @flow\n+        def test_flow():\n+            a = foo.submit(1)\n+            b = foo.submit(2)\n+            d = bar(defaultdict(list, {\"a\": [a]}), deque([b]), return_state=True)\n+            return a, b, d\n+\n+        flow_state = test_flow(return_state=True)\n+\n+        a, b, d = await flow_state.result()\n+        x, y = await d.result()\n+\n+        assert x == defaultdict(list, {\"a\": [1]})\n+        assert y == deque([2])\n+\n+        await events_pipeline.process_events()\n+\n+        task_run = await prefect_client.read_task_run(d.state_details.task_run_id)\n+\n+        assert task_run.task_inputs == dict(\n+            x=[TaskRunResult(id=a.state_details.task_run_id)],\n+            y=[TaskRunResult(id=b.state_details.task_run_id)],\n+        )\n+\n     async def test_task_inputs_populated_with_subflow_upstream(\n         self, prefect_client, events_pipeline\n     ):\ndiff --git a/tests/utilities/test_collections.py b/tests/utilities/test_collections.py\nindex 8aa5d0431980..cfc00b6f8981 100644\n--- a/tests/utilities/test_collections.py\n+++ b/tests/utilities/test_collections.py\n@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@\n import io\n import json\n import uuid\n+from collections import Counter, defaultdict, deque\n from dataclasses import dataclass\n from typing import Any, NamedTuple\n \n@@ -243,6 +244,10 @@ class TestVisitCollection:\n             ({3: 4, 6: 7}, {3: -4, -6: 7}),\n             ({3: [4, {6: 7}]}, {3: [-4, {-6: 7}]}),\n             ({3, 4, 5}, {3, -4, 5}),\n+            (frozenset([3, 4, 5]), frozenset([3, -4, 5])),\n+            (deque([3, 4]), deque([3, -4])),\n+            (defaultdict(list, {3: [4]}), defaultdict(list, {3: [-4]})),\n+            (Counter({\"a\": 2, \"b\": 3}), Counter({\"a\": -2, \"b\": 3})),\n             (SimpleDataclass(x=1, y=2), SimpleDataclass(x=1, y=-2)),\n             (SimplePydantic(x=1, y=2), SimplePydantic(x=1, y=-2)),\n             (ExtraPydantic(x=1, y=2, z=3), ExtraPydantic(x=1, y=-2, z=3)),\n@@ -264,6 +269,10 @@ def test_visit_collection_and_transform_data(self, inp, expected):\n             ({3: 4, 6: 7}, {4, 6}),\n             ({3: [4, {6: 7}]}, {4, 6}),\n             ({3, 4, 5}, {4}),\n+            (frozenset([3, 4, 6]), {4, 6}),\n+            (deque([3, 4]), {4}),\n+            (defaultdict(list, {3: [4]}), {4}),\n+            (Counter({\"a\": 2, \"b\": 3}), {2}),\n             (SimpleDataclass(x=1, y=2), {2}),\n             (SimplePydantic(x=1, y=2), {2}),\n             (ExtraPydantic(x=1, y=2, z=4), {2, 4}),\n@@ -820,6 +829,45 @@ def double_ints(x):\n         assert result.done == {2, 4}\n         assert result.not_done == {6, 8}\n \n+    def test_visit_collection_defaultdict_preserves_default_factory(self):\n+        result = visit_collection(\n+            defaultdict(list, {\"a\": [2]}), negative_even_numbers, return_data=True\n+        )\n+\n+        assert type(result) is defaultdict\n+        assert result.default_factory is list\n+        assert result == {\"a\": [-2]}\n+\n+    def test_visit_collection_counter_preserves_counts(self):\n+        result = visit_collection(\n+            Counter({\"a\": 2, \"b\": 3}), negative_even_numbers, return_data=True\n+        )\n+\n+        assert type(result) is Counter\n+        assert dict(result) == {\"a\": -2, \"b\": 3}\n+\n+    def test_visit_collection_deque_preserves_maxlen(self):\n+        result = visit_collection(\n+            deque([3, 4], maxlen=5), negative_even_numbers, return_data=True\n+        )\n+\n+        assert type(result) is deque\n+        assert result.maxlen == 5\n+        assert list(result) == [3, -4]\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"val\",\n+        [\n+            frozenset([1, 3]),\n+            deque([1, 3]),\n+            defaultdict(list, {1: [3]}),\n+            Counter({\"a\": 1}),\n+        ],\n+    )\n+    def test_visit_collection_stdlib_containers_identity_when_unchanged(self, val):\n+        result = visit_collection(val, negative_even_numbers, return_data=True)\n+        assert result is val\n+\n \n class TestRemoveKeys:\n     def test_remove_single_key(self):\n", "problem_statement": "visit_collection raises on defaultdict, corrupts Counter, and skips deque/frozenset\n### Bug summary\n\n`prefect.utilities.collections.visit_collection` (used when resolving task arguments / collecting upstream task inputs) mishandles several stdlib container types.\n\n**1. `defaultdict` raises when any nested value is transformed**\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nfrom prefect.utilities.collections import visit_collection\n\ndef neg_even(x):\n    return -x if isinstance(x, int) and x % 2 == 0 else x\n\nvisit_collection(defaultdict(list, {\"a\": [2]}), neg_even, return_data=True)\n# TypeError: first argument must be callable or None\n```\n\nRoot cause: after visiting children it rebuilds with `type(mapping)(items)`. For `defaultdict` the first constructor argument is the default factory, so the list of pairs is treated as the factory.\n\n**2. `Counter` silently corrupts data**\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom prefect.utilities.collections import visit_collection\n\ndef neg_even(x):\n    return -x if isinstance(x, int) and x % 2 == 0 else x\n\nprint(visit_collection(Counter({\"a\": 2, \"b\": 3}), neg_even, return_data=True))\n# Counter({('a', -2): 1, ('b', 3): 1})   # wrong \u2014 counts the (key, value) pairs\n# expected: Counter({'a': -2, 'b': 3})\n```\n\nSame rebuild path: `Counter(items)` counts each `(key, value)` tuple instead of storing the mapping.\n\n**3. `deque` and `frozenset` are never descended into**\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\nfrom prefect.utilities.collections import visit_collection\n\nseen = set()\ndef visit_even(x):\n    if isinstance(x, int) and x % 2 == 0:\n        seen.add(x)\n    return x\n\nvisit_collection(deque([3, 4]), visit_even, return_data=False)\nprint(seen)  # set() \u2014 4 was never visited\n\nvisit_collection(frozenset([3, 4, 6]), visit_even, return_data=False)\n# likewise never visits 4 or 6\n```\n\nSequence handling only covers `list` / `tuple` / `set`, so futures nested in a `deque` or `frozenset` task argument are not resolved and are not recorded as upstream task inputs.\n\nI hit (1) and (3) while passing a `defaultdict(list, ...)` of task futures and a `deque` of futures into a downstream task; the flow either raises during argument resolution or silently drops the upstream dependency edges.\n\n### Version info\n\n```text\nReproduced on PrefectHQ/prefect main @ 802fc943\nPython version:       3.14.10\nOS/Arch:              linux/x86_64\n```\n\n(Also reproduces on the current PyPI 3.x line \u2014 the rebuild logic for mappings/sequences has been this way for a long time.)\n\n### Additional context\n\nProposed direction (happy to open a PR after maintainer confirmation):\n\n- Rebuild mappings through a small helper: `defaultdict(default_factory, items)`, `Counter(dict(items))`, otherwise `type(mapping)(items)`.\n- Treat `deque` / `frozenset` like the other sequences, preserving `deque.maxlen`.\n- Keep identity when nothing changed (existing behaviour).\n\nI already have a failing-before / passing-after unit suite for the cases above plus a task-input regression that asserts futures inside `defaultdict` / `deque` are resolved and show up in `task_run.task_inputs`.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-06T15:12:10Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23792, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23792", "issue_numbers": ["23791"], "base_commit": "802fc9435423bed0d16fe30862de25a8df62493f", "patch": "diff --git a/src/integrations/prefect-redis/prefect_redis/messaging.py b/src/integrations/prefect-redis/prefect_redis/messaging.py\nindex 754f06276ec1..0cc703c4d23f 100644\n--- a/src/integrations/prefect-redis/prefect_redis/messaging.py\n+++ b/src/integrations/prefect-redis/prefect_redis/messaging.py\n@@ -271,6 +271,7 @@ def __init__(\n     async def __aenter__(self) -> Self:\n         self._client = get_async_redis_client()\n         self._batch: list[RedisStreamsMessage] = []\n+        self._flush_lock = asyncio.Lock()\n \n         if self.publish_every is not None:\n             interval = self.publish_every.total_seconds()\n@@ -311,31 +312,38 @@ async def publish_data(self, data: bytes, attributes: dict[str, Any]):\n             await asyncio.shield(self._publish_current_batch())\n \n     async def _publish_current_batch(self) -> None:\n-        if not self._batch:\n-            return\n-\n-        if self.deduplicate_by:\n-            to_publish = await self.cache.without_duplicates(\n-                self.deduplicate_by, self._batch\n-            )\n-        else:\n-            to_publish = list(self._batch)\n+        async with self._flush_lock:\n+            if not self._batch:\n+                return\n \n-        self._batch.clear()\n+            # Take ownership of the batch before awaiting so that messages\n+            # published while this flush is in flight land in a fresh batch.\n+            batch, self._batch = self._batch, []\n \n-        try:\n-            for message in to_publish:\n-                await self._client.xadd(\n-                    self.stream,\n-                    {\n-                        \"data\": message.data,\n-                        \"attributes\": orjson.dumps(message.attributes),\n-                    },\n-                )\n-        except Exception:\n             if self.deduplicate_by:\n-                await self.cache.forget_duplicates(self.deduplicate_by, to_publish)\n-            raise\n+                try:\n+                    to_publish = await self.cache.without_duplicates(\n+                        self.deduplicate_by, batch\n+                    )\n+                except BaseException:\n+                    self._batch[:0] = batch\n+                    raise\n+            else:\n+                to_publish = batch\n+\n+            try:\n+                for message in to_publish:\n+                    await self._client.xadd(\n+                        self.stream,\n+                        {\n+                            \"data\": message.data,\n+                            \"attributes\": orjson.dumps(message.attributes),\n+                        },\n+                    )\n+            except Exception:\n+                if self.deduplicate_by:\n+                    await self.cache.forget_duplicates(self.deduplicate_by, to_publish)\n+                raise\n \n \n class Consumer(_Consumer):\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/src/integrations/prefect-redis/tests/test_messaging.py b/src/integrations/prefect-redis/tests/test_messaging.py\nindex 7adc1be2f6e6..01cd41c86242 100644\n--- a/src/integrations/prefect-redis/tests/test_messaging.py\n+++ b/src/integrations/prefect-redis/tests/test_messaging.py\n@@ -356,6 +356,70 @@ async def handler(message: Message):\n     assert not remaining_message\n \n \n+async def test_publisher_keeps_messages_published_while_a_flush_awaits_deduplication(\n+    redis: Redis, cache: Cache, monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch\n+):\n+    \"\"\"Regression test for https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/issues/23791\"\"\"\n+    original_without_duplicates = cache.without_duplicates\n+    flush_in_flight = asyncio.Event()\n+    release_flush = asyncio.Event()\n+\n+    async def without_duplicates_then_pause(\n+        attribute: str, messages: list[RedisStreamsMessage]\n+    ) -> list[RedisStreamsMessage]:\n+        # Pause after the dedup check has read the batch, while the flush is\n+        # still awaiting Redis\n+        result = await original_without_duplicates(attribute, messages)\n+        flush_in_flight.set()\n+        await release_flush.wait()\n+        return result\n+\n+    monkeypatch.setattr(cache, \"without_duplicates\", without_duplicates_then_pause)\n+\n+    async with Publisher(\n+        \"message-tests\",\n+        cache=cache,\n+        deduplicate_by=\"id\",\n+        batch_size=100,\n+        publish_every=timedelta(milliseconds=10),\n+    ) as p:\n+        await p.publish_data(b\"first\", {\"id\": \"1\"})\n+        await asyncio.wait_for(flush_in_flight.wait(), timeout=5)\n+        await p.publish_data(b\"second\", {\"id\": \"2\"})\n+        release_flush.set()\n+\n+    entries = await redis.xrange(_stream_key(\"message-tests\"))\n+    assert [fields[\"data\"] for _, fields in entries] == [\"first\", \"second\"]\n+\n+\n+async def test_publisher_retains_batch_when_deduplication_fails(\n+    redis: Redis, cache: Cache, monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch\n+):\n+    original_without_duplicates = cache.without_duplicates\n+    calls = 0\n+\n+    async def fail_once(\n+        attribute: str, messages: list[RedisStreamsMessage]\n+    ) -> list[RedisStreamsMessage]:\n+        nonlocal calls\n+        calls += 1\n+        if calls == 1:\n+            raise RedisConnectionError(\"boom\")\n+        return await original_without_duplicates(attribute, messages)\n+\n+    monkeypatch.setattr(cache, \"without_duplicates\", fail_once)\n+\n+    async with Publisher(\n+        \"message-tests\", cache=cache, deduplicate_by=\"id\", batch_size=1\n+    ) as p:\n+        with pytest.raises(RedisConnectionError):\n+            await p.publish_data(b\"first\", {\"id\": \"1\"})\n+        await p.publish_data(b\"second\", {\"id\": \"2\"})\n+\n+    entries = await redis.xrange(_stream_key(\"message-tests\"))\n+    assert [fields[\"data\"] for _, fields in entries] == [\"first\", \"second\"]\n+\n+\n async def test_repeatedly_failed_message_is_moved_to_dead_letter_queue(\n     redis: Redis,\n     deduplicating_publisher: Publisher,\n", "problem_statement": "prefect-redis: Publisher silently drops a message published while a flush awaits the dedup check\n### Bug summary\n\n`prefect_redis.messaging.Publisher._publish_current_batch` loses any message that `publish_data` appends while a flush is waiting on the deduplication round-trip. The flush passes `self._batch` to `Cache.without_duplicates`, which builds its `SET NX` pipeline from the messages present at that moment and then awaits `p.execute()`. When it returns, `self._batch.clear()` also discards the message appended in the meantime. Nothing is logged and nothing raises.\n\nhttps://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/fe0aa235f2c5a2fe6387e412aea67e57738f0ae3/src/integrations/prefect-redis/prefect_redis/messaging.py#L313-L338\n\n`create_event_publisher()` always deduplicates by `id`, so this applies to every event received on `/api/events/in`. Redis does not need to be slow or unhealthy. A normal round-trip is enough if an event arrives during it.\n\n#### Reproduction\n\nNeeds Redis on `localhost:6379`. The patch only delays the dedup pipeline (the only pipeline on the publish path), which makes the interleaving deterministic.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nimport uuid\nfrom datetime import timedelta\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\n\nfrom redis.asyncio.client import Pipeline\n\nfrom prefect_redis.client import get_async_redis_client\nfrom prefect_redis.messaging import Cache, Publisher\n\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    # Open a pooled connection up front so connection setup doesn't shift the timing.\n    await get_async_redis_client().ping()\n\n    release = asyncio.Event()\n    original_execute = Pipeline.execute\n\n    async def slow_execute(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        # The only pipeline on the publish path is the SET NX in\n        # Cache.without_duplicates. Holding it open stands in for a slow\n        # round-trip between reading the batch and self._batch.clear().\n        await release.wait()\n        return await original_execute(self, *args, **kwargs)\n\n    publisher = Publisher(\n        f\"race-repro-{uuid.uuid4().hex[:8]}\",\n        cache=Cache(),\n        deduplicate_by=\"id\",  # same as create_event_publisher()\n        batch_size=100,  # leave flushing to the periodic task\n        publish_every=timedelta(milliseconds=100),\n    )\n\n    with patch.object(Pipeline, \"execute\", slow_execute):\n        async with publisher as p:\n            await p.publish_data(b\"first\", {\"id\": uuid.uuid4().hex})\n            await asyncio.sleep(0.3)  # periodic flush is now waiting on Redis\n            await p.publish_data(b\"second\", {\"id\": uuid.uuid4().hex})\n            release.set()\n            await asyncio.sleep(0.3)\n\n    entries = await get_async_redis_client().xrange(p.stream)\n    print(\n        f\"published 2 messages, stream has {len(entries)}:\",\n        [fields[\"data\"] for _, fields in entries],\n    )\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    asyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nExpected `2` entries. Actual: `published 2 messages, stream has 1: ['first']`. `second` is never written to the stream. This happened on every run with prefect-redis 0.3.0, prefect 3.9.1, redis-py 8.1.0 and Redis 7.4.\n\n#### What we saw in production\n\nRedis was healthy and there were no errors anywhere. A flow emitted three events within about 8 ms at the end of a task. The first and third were persisted. The middle one was a custom event that a compound automation depends on, and it never reached Redis. The websocket stayed open and the client treated the event as delivered.\n\nTimestamps from the `events` table, UTC:\n\n| Event | received | recorded |\n|---|---|---|\n| first client event on the connection (flow-run heartbeat) | 04:11:24.492 | 04:11:34.521 |\n| task-run Pending / Running | 24.755 / 24.757 | 34.521 |\n| task-run Completed | 44.460 | 44.811 |\n| custom event | ~44.462 | never |\n| flow-run heartbeat | 44.465 | 44.811 |\n\n- The first three events waited about 10 s before being recorded. They were held until the connection's periodic flush (`publish_every` defaults to 10 s). Server-emitted events in the same window were recorded within 0.2 to 0.7 s.\n- The connection opened between 24.431 and 24.492, so the second periodic tick fell between 44.43 and 44.50. The lost event arrived inside that window. Task Completed was already in the batch the flush passed to `without_duplicates`. The custom event was appended during `await p.execute()`, and `self._batch.clear()` then discarded it. The heartbeat arrived after the clear and was published when the connection closed.\n- An identical run the day before had its end-of-run burst about 2 s away from any tick, and it lost nothing.\n\nRuled out:\n- API pod logs had no `Refusing to publish` warning and no ASGI exception.\n- The client's `emit_event` logged no error.\n- Redis had no evictions and no failover. Stream trimming isn't involved because the event never reached the stream.\n\nMost events that hit this window are heartbeats or task-run state events, so losing one usually goes unnoticed. It becomes visible when an automation waits for the dropped event.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nServer:            3.8.0, prefecthq/prefect:3.8.0-python3.14 image with the prefect[redis] extra\nprefect-redis:     0.2.13 / 0.2.14\nClients:           prefect 3.6.18, emitting over /api/events/in\nBroker and cache:  prefect_redis.messaging\nPublisher:         default settings (batch_size 5, publish_every 10 s)\n```\n\n`_publish_current_batch` is unchanged in prefect-redis 0.2.15, 0.3.0 and current `main`.\n\n### Additional context\n\nI first described this in a comment on #23015. That PR fixes the race, but it was closed as stale on 2026-11-02 together with its socket-timeout work for #22478, which is still open. I'm filing this separately because it loses events during normal operation, not only during a Redis outage.\n\n**Suggested fix:** swap the batch out before the first `await` (`batch, self._batch = self._batch, []`). Also serialize flushes with a lock, so a flush triggered by `batch_size` can't interleave with the periodic one. #23015 does both. With only the swap applied, the reproduction above publishes both messages. A regression test has to pause inside the Redis call, as the reproduction above does. `without_duplicates` receives the batch by reference, so pausing before the call lets the later message into the batch it reads, and the test passes against the current code.\n\n**Workaround we're using:** `PREFECT_REDIS_MESSAGING_PUBLISHER_BATCH_SIZE=1`. Each `publish_data` call then flushes its own message before any other `await`, so the message is always in the batch that its flush reads. The periodic flush almost always finds an empty batch. This costs one dedup round-trip per event, and we haven't confirmed it closes every path.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-06T14:48:18Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23786, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23786", "issue_numbers": ["23785"], "base_commit": "802fc9435423bed0d16fe30862de25a8df62493f", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/filesystems.py b/src/prefect/filesystems.py\nindex 14beedb14de5..789d22de959b 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/filesystems.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/filesystems.py\n@@ -547,7 +547,10 @@ async def aput_directory(\n         counter = 0\n         for f in Path(local_path).rglob(\"*\"):\n             relative_path = f.relative_to(local_path)\n-            if included_files and relative_path.as_posix() not in included_files:\n+            if (\n+                included_files is not None\n+                and relative_path.as_posix() not in included_files\n+            ):\n                 continue\n \n             if to_path.endswith(\"/\"):\n@@ -603,7 +606,10 @@ def put_directory(\n         counter = 0\n         for f in Path(local_path).rglob(\"*\"):\n             relative_path = f.relative_to(local_path)\n-            if included_files and relative_path.as_posix() not in included_files:\n+            if (\n+                included_files is not None\n+                and relative_path.as_posix() not in included_files\n+            ):\n                 continue\n \n             if to_path.endswith(\"/\"):\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/test_filesystems.py b/tests/test_filesystems.py\nindex 9459905cfefd..f6494f1640dd 100644\n--- a/tests/test_filesystems.py\n+++ b/tests/test_filesystems.py\n@@ -91,6 +91,32 @@ async def test_nested_ignored_files(tmp_path: Path, storage: str, sync: bool):\n         assert not (downloaded / \"a\" / \"b\" / \"ignored.txt\").exists()\n \n \n+@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"sync\", [False, True])\n+async def test_remote_put_directory_uploads_nothing_when_ignore_file_excludes_everything(\n+    tmp_path: Path, sync: bool\n+):\n+    source = tmp_path / \"source\"\n+    (source / \"nested\").mkdir(parents=True)\n+    (source / \"demo.txt\").write_text(\"demo\")\n+    (source / \"nested\" / \"config.txt\").write_text(\"config\")\n+    ignore_file = tmp_path / \".prefectignore\"\n+    ignore_file.write_text(\"*\\n\")\n+\n+    fs = RemoteFileSystem(basepath=f\"memory://{tmp_path.name}/ignore-all-{sync}\")\n+\n+    if sync:\n+        count = fs.put_directory(\n+            local_path=str(source), ignore_file=str(ignore_file), _sync=True\n+        )\n+    else:\n+        count = await fs.aput_directory(\n+            local_path=str(source), ignore_file=str(ignore_file)\n+        )\n+\n+    assert count == 0\n+    assert fs.filesystem.find(f\"/{tmp_path.name}/ignore-all-{sync}\") == []\n+\n+\n class TestLocalFileSystem:\n     async def test_read_write_roundtrip(self, tmp_path):\n         fs = LocalFileSystem(basepath=str(tmp_path))\n", "problem_statement": "RemoteFileSystem uploads all files when ignore patterns exclude every entry\n### Bug summary\n\n`RemoteFileSystem.put_directory()` and `aput_directory()` upload every source file when an explicitly supplied ignore file excludes every entry. They should upload zero files.\n\nBoth methods test the truthiness of `included_files`. An empty set from `filter_files()` therefore disables the exclusion check, just like `None` when no ignore file is supplied.\n\n### Reproduction\n\nThis uses synthetic files and fsspec's in-memory filesystem; no server or external storage is required.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom tempfile import TemporaryDirectory\n\nfrom prefect.filesystems import RemoteFileSystem\n\n\nasync def main():\n    with TemporaryDirectory() as directory:\n        root = Path(directory)\n        source = root / \"source\"\n        (source / \"nested\").mkdir(parents=True)\n        (source / \"demo.txt\").write_text(\"synthetic demo\")\n        (source / \".env\").write_text(\"SYNTHETIC_VALUE=example\")\n        (source / \"nested\" / \"config.txt\").write_text(\"synthetic config\")\n        ignore_file = root / \".prefectignore\"\n        ignore_file.write_text(\"*\\n\")\n\n        for sync in (True, False):\n            name = f\"ignore-all-{sync}\"\n            storage = RemoteFileSystem(basepath=f\"memory://{name}\")\n            kwargs = {\n                \"local_path\": str(source),\n                \"ignore_file\": str(ignore_file),\n            }\n            if sync:\n                count = storage.put_directory(**kwargs, _sync=True)\n            else:\n                count = await storage.aput_directory(**kwargs)\n            print(sync, count, storage.filesystem.find(f\"/{name}\"))\n\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n**Expected:** Both calls return `0`, and both destination file lists are empty.\n\n**Actual:** Both calls upload all three files and return `3`.\n\n### Version info\n\nReproduced against unchanged source at `1d50f74d953cabd5ebed03f06d8e779d1c1fad19`, using Python 3.13.13 on macOS, fsspec 2026.7.0, and pathspec 1.0.4. Affected released-version boundaries have not been established.\n\n### Additional context\n\nPartial exclusion, an empty ignore file, no ignore file, and a missing ignore-file path were checked in both modes and behaved as expected. The defect occurs when the inclusion set is empty.\n\nThe affected guards are in [the async upload method](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/1d50f74d953cabd5ebed03f06d8e779d1c1fad19/src/prefect/filesystems.py#L550) and [the sync upload method](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/1d50f74d953cabd5ebed03f06d8e779d1c1fad19/src/prefect/filesystems.py#L606). Both should distinguish `included_files is None` from an empty set, with regression coverage for both modes.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-05T19:45:46Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23784, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23784", "issue_numbers": ["21932"], "base_commit": "7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/flow_engine.py b/src/prefect/flow_engine.py\nindex fc0131411c14..25c213de0b58 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/flow_engine.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/flow_engine.py\n@@ -11,7 +11,7 @@\n import sys\n import threading\n import time\n-from collections.abc import Mapping\n+from collections.abc import Callable, Mapping\n from contextlib import (\n     AsyncExitStack,\n     ExitStack,\n@@ -334,28 +334,40 @@ def _send_heartbeats(\n     resource, related = engine._build_heartbeat_event_template()\n \n     stop_event = threading.Event()\n+    # The heartbeat thread checks for a stop and emits while holding this lock,\n+    # so once `stop_heartbeats` returns, no heartbeat is mid-emit and none will\n+    # be emitted.\n+    emit_lock = threading.Lock()\n \n-    def heartbeat_loop() -> None:\n-        while not stop_event.is_set():\n-            # Check state before emitting - don't emit if final\n-            if (\n-                engine.flow_run\n-                and engine.flow_run.state\n-                and engine.flow_run.state.is_final()\n-            ):\n-                engine.logger.debug(\"Flow run in terminal state, stopping heartbeat\")\n-                return\n-\n-            try:\n-                engine._emit_flow_run_heartbeat(resource, related)\n-            except Exception:\n-                engine.logger.debug(\"Failed to emit heartbeat\", exc_info=True)\n+    def stop_heartbeats() -> None:\n+        with emit_lock:\n+            stop_event.set()\n \n-            # Sleep in increments to allow quick shutdown\n-            for _ in range(heartbeat_seconds):\n+    def heartbeat_loop() -> None:\n+        while True:\n+            with emit_lock:\n                 if stop_event.is_set():\n                     return\n-                time.sleep(1)\n+\n+                # Check state before emitting - don't emit if final\n+                if (\n+                    engine.flow_run\n+                    and engine.flow_run.state\n+                    and engine.flow_run.state.is_final()\n+                ):\n+                    engine.logger.debug(\n+                        \"Flow run in terminal state, stopping heartbeat\"\n+                    )\n+                    return\n+\n+                try:\n+                    engine._emit_flow_run_heartbeat(resource, related)\n+                except Exception:\n+                    engine.logger.debug(\"Failed to emit heartbeat\", exc_info=True)\n+\n+            # Waiting on the event wakes the thread as soon as heartbeats stop\n+            if stop_event.wait(heartbeat_seconds):\n+                return\n \n     # Copy the current context so the heartbeat thread sees the same\n     # `SettingsContext` (and therefore the same `PREFECT_API_URL`) as the\n@@ -370,13 +382,14 @@ def heartbeat_loop() -> None:\n     thread = threading.Thread(\n         target=heartbeat_ctx.run, args=(heartbeat_loop,), daemon=True\n     )\n+    engine._stop_heartbeats = stop_heartbeats\n     thread.start()\n     engine.logger.debug(\"Started flow run heartbeat context\")\n \n     try:\n         yield\n     finally:\n-        stop_event.set()\n+        stop_heartbeats()\n         if join_on_exit:\n             thread.join(timeout=2)\n         engine.logger.debug(\"Stopped flow run heartbeat context\")\n@@ -428,6 +441,15 @@ class BaseFlowRunEngine(Generic[P, R]):\n         default_factory=FlowRunSuspensionRequest\n     )\n     _attempt_conclusion: EngineOutcomeReceipt | None = field(default=None, init=False)\n+    # Stops the heartbeat thread started by the most recent `_send_heartbeats`.\n+    # `set_state` calls it before proposing a terminal state: the server\n+    # timestamps that state when it accepts it, but the heartbeat thread sees\n+    # it only after the response arrives.  A heartbeat sent in that gap occurs\n+    # after the terminal state, and an automation that expects every heartbeat\n+    # to be followed by another event then fires on the finished run.\n+    _stop_heartbeats: Callable[[], None] | None = field(\n+        default=None, init=False, repr=False\n+    )\n \n     def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n         if self.flow is None and self.flow_run_id is None:\n@@ -761,6 +783,9 @@ def set_state(self, state: State, force: bool = False) -> State:\n         if self.short_circuit:\n             return self.state\n \n+        if state.is_final() and self._stop_heartbeats is not None:\n+            self._stop_heartbeats()\n+\n         state = propose_state_sync(\n             self.client, state, flow_run_id=self.flow_run.id, force=force\n         )  # type: ignore\n@@ -1500,6 +1525,9 @@ async def set_state(self, state: State, force: bool = False) -> State:\n         if self.short_circuit:\n             return self.state\n \n+        if state.is_final() and self._stop_heartbeats is not None:\n+            self._stop_heartbeats()\n+\n         state = await propose_state(\n             self.client, state, flow_run_id=self.flow_run.id, force=force\n         )  # type: ignore\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/test_flow_engine.py b/tests/test_flow_engine.py\nindex d6bce080f813..e76c3af56a9a 100644\n--- a/tests/test_flow_engine.py\n+++ b/tests/test_flow_engine.py\n@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@\n import uuid\n from collections.abc import AsyncGenerator, Awaitable, Callable, Generator\n from contextlib import asynccontextmanager, contextmanager, nullcontext\n+from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n from textwrap import dedent\n from types import SimpleNamespace\n from typing import Any, Literal, Optional\n@@ -29,7 +30,7 @@\n from prefect.cache_policies import INPUTS\n from prefect.client.orchestration import PrefectClient, SyncPrefectClient, get_client\n from prefect.client.schemas.filters import FlowFilter, FlowFilterName, FlowRunFilter\n-from prefect.client.schemas.objects import FlowRun, StateType\n+from prefect.client.schemas.objects import FlowRun, State, StateType\n from prefect.client.schemas.sorting import FlowRunSort\n from prefect.concurrency.asyncio import concurrency as aconcurrency\n from prefect.concurrency.sync import concurrency\n@@ -40,6 +41,8 @@\n     get_run_context,\n )\n from prefect.engine import _drive_run_flow_result, handle_engine_signals\n+from prefect.events.schemas.events import Event\n+from prefect.events.worker import EventsWorker\n from prefect.exceptions import (\n     Abort,\n     CrashedRun,\n@@ -51,6 +54,7 @@\n from prefect.flow_engine import (\n     MINIMUM_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL,\n     AsyncFlowRunEngine,\n+    BaseFlowRunEngine,\n     FlowRunEngine,\n     NotSet,\n     _load_flow_from_runtime_entrypoint,\n@@ -6657,3 +6661,101 @@ def mock_emit_event(**kwargs: object) -> None:\n                 time.sleep(3)\n \n         assert heartbeat_count >= 1\n+\n+\n+@dataclass\n+class _HeartbeatSpy:\n+    events: list[Event] = field(default_factory=list)\n+    emitted: threading.Event = field(default_factory=threading.Event)\n+\n+\n+class TestHeartbeatsAroundTerminalState:\n+    \"\"\"Regression tests for https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/issues/21932\n+\n+    The server timestamps a terminal state when it accepts it, and the engine\n+    records that state only when the response arrives.  A heartbeat emitted\n+    in between occurs after the terminal state, and a proactive trigger with\n+    `after: prefect.flow-run.heartbeat` then opens a window that no later\n+    event closes, so it fires on a run that completed normally.\n+    \"\"\"\n+\n+    @pytest.fixture\n+    def heartbeats(\n+        self,\n+        monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch,\n+        asserting_events_worker: EventsWorker,\n+    ) -> _HeartbeatSpy:\n+        \"\"\"Emit a heartbeat every 50 milliseconds and record each one.\"\"\"\n+        monkeypatch.setattr(flow_engine_module, \"MINIMUM_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL\", 0.05)\n+        monkeypatch.setattr(BaseFlowRunEngine, \"heartbeat_seconds\", 0.05)\n+\n+        spy = _HeartbeatSpy()\n+        real_emit_event = flow_engine_module.emit_event\n+\n+        def recording_emit_event(**kwargs: Any) -> Event | None:\n+            event = real_emit_event(**kwargs)\n+            if event is not None and event.event == \"prefect.flow-run.heartbeat\":\n+                spy.events.append(event)\n+                spy.emitted.set()\n+            return event\n+\n+        monkeypatch.setattr(flow_engine_module, \"emit_event\", recording_emit_event)\n+        return spy\n+\n+    @pytest.fixture\n+    def slow_terminal_state_responses(\n+        self, monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch, heartbeats: _HeartbeatSpy\n+    ) -> None:\n+        \"\"\"After the server accepts a terminal state, hold its response until the\n+        heartbeat thread emits, or for five heartbeat intervals if it doesn't.\"\"\"\n+\n+        def propose_with_slow_response(*args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> State:\n+            state = propose_state_sync(*args, **kwargs)\n+            if state.is_final():\n+                heartbeats.emitted.clear()\n+                heartbeats.emitted.wait(timeout=0.25)\n+            return state\n+\n+        async def apropose_with_slow_response(*args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> State:\n+            state = await propose_state(*args, **kwargs)\n+            if state.is_final():\n+                heartbeats.emitted.clear()\n+                await asyncio.to_thread(heartbeats.emitted.wait, 0.25)\n+            return state\n+\n+        monkeypatch.setattr(\n+            flow_engine_module, \"propose_state_sync\", propose_with_slow_response\n+        )\n+        monkeypatch.setattr(\n+            flow_engine_module, \"propose_state\", apropose_with_slow_response\n+        )\n+\n+    def test_no_heartbeat_occurs_after_terminal_state_sync(\n+        self, heartbeats: _HeartbeatSpy, slow_terminal_state_responses: None\n+    ):\n+        @flow\n+        def my_flow() -> int:\n+            heartbeats.emitted.wait(timeout=5)\n+            return 42\n+\n+        state = my_flow(return_state=True)\n+\n+        assert state.is_completed()\n+        occurred = [heartbeat.occurred for heartbeat in heartbeats.events]\n+        assert occurred\n+        assert max(occurred) < state.timestamp\n+\n+    async def test_no_heartbeat_occurs_after_terminal_state_async(\n+        self, heartbeats: _HeartbeatSpy, slow_terminal_state_responses: None\n+    ):\n+        @flow\n+        async def my_flow() -> int:\n+            await asyncio.to_thread(heartbeats.emitted.wait, 5)\n+            return 42\n+\n+        state = await my_flow(return_state=True)\n+\n+        assert state.is_completed()\n+        occurred = [heartbeat.occurred for heartbeat in heartbeats.events]\n+        assert occurred\n+        assert max(occurred) < state.timestamp\n", "problem_statement": "Zombie-detection automation false-positives on healthy completed flows\n### Bug summary\n\n## Description\n\nSome `prefect.flow-run.*` events appear in the event log but are not delivered to the automation trigger evaluation pipeline. Most notably, `prefect.flow-run.Completed` events are not seen by automation triggers, causing Proactive triggers that `expect: prefect.flow-run.*` to fire on healthy flows after they complete.\n\nThis breaks the canonical zombie-detection automation pattern documented at <https://docs.prefect.io/v3/advanced/detect-zombie-flows>.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n### Step 1: Create the zombie-detection automation from the docs\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import timedelta\nfrom prefect.automations import Automation\nfrom prefect.client.schemas.objects import StateType\nfrom prefect.events.actions import ChangeFlowRunState\nfrom prefect.events.schemas.automations import EventTrigger, Posture\nfrom prefect.events.schemas.events import ResourceSpecification\n\nautomation = Automation(\n    name=\"Crash zombie flows\",\n    trigger=EventTrigger(\n        after={\"prefect.flow-run.heartbeat\"},\n        expect={\"prefect.flow-run.*\"},\n        match=ResourceSpecification(\n            {\"[prefect.resource.id](http://prefect.resource.id/)\": [\"prefect.flow-run.*\"]}\n        ),\n        for_each={\"[prefect.resource.id](http://prefect.resource.id/)\"},\n        posture=Posture.Proactive,\n        threshold=1,\n        within=timedelta(seconds=600),\n    ),\n    actions=[\n        ChangeFlowRunState(\n            state=StateType.CRASHED,\n            message=\"Flow run marked as crashed due to missing heartbeats.\",\n        )\n    ],\n)\nautomation.create()\n```\n\n### Step 2: Run a normal flow that emits at least one heartbeat then completes\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom prefect import flow\n\n@flow\ndef heartbeat_test(duration_seconds: int = 900):\n    \"\"\"Long enough to emit multiple heartbeats with default\n    PREFECT_RUNNER_HEARTBEAT_FREQUENCY=180s, then complete normally.\"\"\"\n    time.sleep(duration_seconds)\n```\n\nDeploy and run via a Kubernetes worker. Flow runs to completion normally, emitting heartbeats every 180 seconds and then a `Completed` event.\n\n### Step 3: Observe automation firing 600s after the last heartbeat\n\nThe automation fires (incorrectly) and attempts to set the flow run to `Crashed`. The action fails because the run is already in terminal state `Completed`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"event\": \"prefect.automation.action.failed\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"reason\": \"Failed to set state: Run is already in terminal state COMPLETED.\",\n    \"action_type\": \"change-flow-run-state\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Event timeline (real example from our environment)\n\nFor flow run `fortunate-vicugna`:\n\n| Time (UTC-equivalent) | Event |\n|---|---|\n| 13:12:17 | `prefect.flow-run.heartbeat` (1st) |\n| 13:15:17 | `prefect.flow-run.heartbeat` (2nd) |\n| 13:18:17 | `prefect.flow-run.heartbeat` (3rd) |\n| 13:21:17 | `prefect.flow-run.heartbeat` (4th) |\n| 13:24:17 | `prefect.flow-run.heartbeat` (5th) |\n| 13:27:17 | `prefect.flow-run.heartbeat` (6th, last) |\n| 13:27:17 | `prefect.flow-run.Completed` |\n| 13:37:22 | `prefect.automation.triggered` (Zombie -> Crashed) |\n| 13:37:22 | `prefect.automation.action.failed` (Run already in terminal state COMPLETED) |\n\nThe `prefect.flow-run.Completed` event was emitted at the same instant as the last heartbeat (13:27:17) \u2014 well within the 600-second `within` window. The trigger should have considered the `expect: prefect.flow-run.*` clause satisfied and not fired. Instead, the automation fired exactly 600 seconds after the last heartbeat as if no follow-up event was ever received.\n\nThe `Completed` event is visible both in the UI Event Feed and via the API:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST \"$PREFECT_API_URL/events/filter\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $PREFECT_API_KEY\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"filter\": {\n      \"resource\": {\"id\": [\"prefect.flow-run.<run-id>\"]},\n      \"event\": {\"name\": [\"prefect.flow-run.Completed\"]}\n    },\n    \"limit\": 5\n  }'\n```\n\nSo the event is in the event log; it just isn't reaching the automation trigger evaluation pipeline.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nThe trigger's `expect: prefect.flow-run.*` clause should be satisfied by any `prefect.flow-run.*` event arriving within the `within` window after the `after` event. `prefect.flow-run.Completed` should qualify, preventing the Proactive trigger from firing on healthy flows.\n\n## Actual behavior\n\n`prefect.flow-run.Completed` (and possibly other state-change events) reach the event log but not the automation trigger evaluation pipeline. The trigger fires after the `within` window expires, even though qualifying events were emitted.\n\n## Impact\n\nThe zombie-detection pattern documented at <https://docs.prefect.io/v3/advanced/detect-zombie-flows> is unreliable. Every healthy flow run that heartbeats then completes will trigger the automation. The action gracefully fails for terminal states, but there is a race window where the automation could incorrectly mark a still-completing flow as `Crashed`, and the failed-action events generate significant noise in the automation feed.\n\n## Environment notes\n\n- Self-hosted Prefect server running on Kubernetes\n- Kubernetes worker submitting flows as Jobs\n- Default `PREFECT_RUNNER_HEARTBEAT_FREQUENCY=180` (heartbeats every 3 minutes)\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.7.0\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.11.15\nGit commit:           85c59e0e\nBuilt:                Wed, May 06, 2026 03:04 PM\nOS/Arch:              linux/x86_64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          ephemeral\nPydantic version:     2.13.4\nServer:\n  Database:           postgresql\n  PostgreSQL version: 15.2\nIntegrations:\n  prefect-redis:      0.2.11\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_\n", "hints_text": "Thanks for the great investigation @matthewc97! Could you share the full granularity of the event timestamps? I want to makes sure the `prefect.flow-run.Completed` did in fact come after the last `prefect.flow-run.heartbeat` event. If it didn't, that suggests the need for a client-side change.\n\nLong term, I'd like to move zombie flow detection into the server-side orchestration rules so that this automation isn't necessary. That's the best solution for this issue, but maybe there's a small enhancement or fix that can\u00a0help in the short term. \nHi @desertaxle, here's the full timeline for the flow run:\n\n**Resource events (events from this flow run):**\n\n```\n2026-05-13T05:12:10.456140Z  prefect.flow-run.Scheduled\n2026-05-13T05:12:11.075506Z  prefect.flow-run.Pending\n2026-05-13T05:12:11.163644Z  prefect.flow-run.Submitting\n2026-05-13T05:12:11.349026Z  prefect.flow-run.InfrastructurePending\n2026-05-13T05:12:14.377273Z  prefect.flow-run.pull-step.executed\n2026-05-13T05:12:17.601241Z  prefect.flow-run.Running\n2026-05-13T05:12:17.666320Z  prefect.flow-run.heartbeat\n2026-05-13T05:15:17.681665Z  prefect.flow-run.heartbeat\n2026-05-13T05:18:17.696198Z  prefect.flow-run.heartbeat\n2026-05-13T05:21:17.711027Z  prefect.flow-run.heartbeat\n2026-05-13T05:24:17.726463Z  prefect.flow-run.heartbeat\n2026-05-13T05:27:17.716889Z  prefect.flow-run.Completed       \u2190 Flow completes\n2026-05-13T05:27:17.741008Z  prefect.flow-run.heartbeat       \u2190 24ms AFTER Completed\n```\n\n**Related events (worker, K8s pod, automation):**\n\n```\n2026-05-13T05:12:11.135650Z  prefect.worker.submitted-flow-run\n2026-05-13T05:12:11.237297Z  prefect.worker.executed-flow-run\n2026-05-13T05:12:11.367449Z  prefect.kubernetes.pod.pending\n2026-05-13T05:12:12.623602Z  prefect.kubernetes.pod.running\n2026-05-13T05:27:26.836589Z  prefect.kubernetes.pod.succeeded\n2026-05-13T05:37:22.079977Z  prefect.automation.action.triggered\n2026-05-13T05:37:22.197935Z  prefect.automation.action.failed\n```\n\nLooks like a heartbeat was emitted ~24ms after the flow entered the terminal `Completed` state. Could that post-completion heartbeat be re-arming the Proactive trigger?\n@desertaxle \n\nI had the same issue as @matthewc97, which I fixed by being explicit about which type of event I was expecting:\n```json\n\"expect\": [\n    \"prefect.flow-run.Cancelled\",\n    \"prefect.flow-run.Failed\",\n    \"prefect.flow-run.heartbeat\",\n    \"prefect.flow-run.Completed\",\n    \"prefect.flow-run.Crashed\"\n],\n```\n\nI'd rather not do that workaround, but at least it kind of works for now.\n\n---\n\nMy current problem is that I'm now seeing the same thing @matthewc97 mentioned in his latest comment, with a heartbeat happening right after Completion.\n\n```\nOccurred                    | Received                    | Type\n...\n2026-08-10T19:19:10.335494Z | 2026-08-10T19:19:10.398389Z | prefect.flow-run.heartbeat\n2026-08-10T19:22:10.367843Z | 2026-08-10T19:22:10.564925Z | prefect.flow-run.Completed\n2026-08-10T19:22:10.570763Z | 2026-08-10T19:22:10.633130Z | prefect.flow-run.heartbeat\n```\n\nThe automation fails to put the flow run into a Crashed status because it's already completed, but we added a Slack notification as an action on this automation and that has been triggering often, flagging false positives.\n\nI think this issue should be reopened (maybe with a separate one for the late heartbeat thing, you decide). Unless this was fixed internally and it's waiting for release? We're using Prefect Cloud.\n\n@desertaxle following up on this \u2014 I'm still seeing this in Prefect Cloud, so I don't think the issue is resolved from the user's perspective.\n\nIn particular, the automation is still producing false positives on normally completed flow runs, which makes the documented zombie-detection pattern unreliable. The late heartbeat after Completed also appears to reproduce the behavior described in the original report.\n\nCould you reopen #21932, or let me know if this is actually fixed internally and which release should contain the fix?\n\nI'm happy to provide additional event IDs/logs if useful.\n@felixpelletier can you share what version of `prefect` you are using? A heartbeat coming after a completed event makes me think this might be a client-side issue.\nWe're using 3.6.28. Was it fixed in a more recent version?\nI see both PRs have been closed due to being stale. Is this still getting worked on?\nThanks for fixing this!\n\nDoes the fix require a client-side update or is it fully server-side?\n@felixpelletier The fix is server-side: #23445 changed how the server counts `expect` events for proactive triggers, so clients don't need an update. We rolled the same fix out to Prefect Cloud the same day, October 19. For a self-hosted server, it's in 3.9.0 and later.\n@chrisguidry Thanks Chris.\n\nI'm still getting the same behavior as described before on Prefect Cloud.\n```\nOccurred                 | Received                 | Type\n...\n2026-11-04T22:01:05.716Z | 2026-11-04T22:01:05.822Z | prefect.flow-run.heartbeat\n2026-11-04T22:04:05.586Z | 2026-11-04T22:04:05.813Z | prefect.flow-run.Completed\n2026-11-04T22:04:05.734Z | 2026-11-04T22:04:05.837Z | prefect.flow-run.heartbeat\n```\n\nAnd the automation still triggers.\n\nWe're using Prefect 3.8.8 as our client right now (when this particular instance happened)\n\nI wouldn't consider this issue fixed yet, unfortunately.\nSorry to hear that @felixpelletier, and I may have misunderstood whether my other fix actually addresses these symptoms.  I will try to get some time soon to investigate this further.  One thing I'm noticing from your timestamps is that the client actually emitted that heartbeat later than the completed event so we may need a client-side change here as well.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T18:33:22Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23783, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23783", "issue_numbers": ["23782"], "base_commit": "7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/runner/_workspace_resolver.py b/src/prefect/runner/_workspace_resolver.py\nindex e20ec20a92f4..5dcfc5920718 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/runner/_workspace_resolver.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/runner/_workspace_resolver.py\n@@ -15,6 +15,7 @@\n from prefect.client.orchestration import get_client\n from prefect.deployments.steps.core import (\n     _PULL_STEP_SOURCE_CWD,\n+    StepExecutionError,\n     _observe_step_completion,\n     run_steps,\n )\n@@ -373,6 +374,9 @@ def _track_step_workspace(\n                     flow_run=flow_run,\n                     logger=run_logger,\n                 )\n+        except StepExecutionError as exc:\n+            run_logger.error(str(exc.__cause__ or exc))\n+            raise\n         finally:\n             _PULL_STEP_SOURCE_CWD.reset(source_cwd_token)\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/runner/test__workspace_resolver.py b/tests/runner/test__workspace_resolver.py\nindex 6ec98e0bbf28..ff8270773680 100644\n--- a/tests/runner/test__workspace_resolver.py\n+++ b/tests/runner/test__workspace_resolver.py\n@@ -1,5 +1,6 @@\n from __future__ import annotations\n \n+import logging\n import os\n import subprocess\n import sys\n@@ -895,6 +896,8 @@ async def test_pull_step_logs_are_sent_to_the_api_when_a_step_fails(\n         )\n         messages = [log.message for log in logs]\n         assert \"Executing deployment step: raise_error\" in messages\n+        error_logs = [log for log in logs if log.level == logging.ERROR]\n+        assert [log.message for log in error_logs] == [\"resolver exploded\"]\n \n \n class TestRelativePathSetWorkingDirectory:\n", "problem_statement": "Pull step failures are not visible in the Prefect UI when using the workspace resolver\n### Bug summary\n\n### Summary\n\nWhen a deployment pull step fails (e.g. `git_clone` returning exit code 128), the error is not visible in the Prefect UI flow run logs. Users see the step start but no indication of what went wrong.\n\n### Introduced in\n\nPR #22432 \u2014 \"Send workspace resolver pull step logs to the API\"\n\nThat PR moved step *progress* logs from stderr to the Prefect API, which is the right thing to do. However, step *failures* were not moved \u2014 they still only go to stderr. In ephemeral environments like Kubernetes where pods are deleted quickly, this makes the error completely invisible.\n\n### What users see in the UI\n\nThe clone operation fails silently. The flow run logs show the step starting but not why it failed:\n\n```\nRunning 1 deployment pull step(s)\nExecuting deployment step: git_clone\n```\n\nThen a secondary error from the hook runner appears, which is a red herring:\n\n```\nRunner failed to retrieve flow to execute on_crashed hooks for flow run UUID('...')\nFileNotFoundError: .../workspace.json\n```\n<img width=\"1641\" height=\"774\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1fff9868-0ccd-4ad6-9c1d-c83a4c1e5809\" />\n\nThe `workspace.json` not found error happens because workspace preparation failed and the manifest was never written. The real cause \u2014 the pull step failure \u2014 is invisible in the logs.\n\nThe only way to find out what went wrong is to go to the **Event Feed** and look for the `prefect.flow-run.pull-step.failed` event, which is not something most users would think to do.\n\n### What does work\n\n- A `prefect.flow-run.pull-step.failed` **event** is emitted correctly and visible in the Events feed\n- The `flows.py` path (`load_flow_from_flow_run`) already logs pull step failures to the API \u2014 only the workspace resolver path is affected\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nPrefect 3.9.0\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\nI would like to get git_clone error into the logs.\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-05T16:32:01Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23781, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23781", "issue_numbers": ["23780"], "base_commit": "7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/server/utilities/subscriptions.py b/src/prefect/server/utilities/subscriptions.py\nindex 63ecacfa44b0..845e98534629 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/server/utilities/subscriptions.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/server/utilities/subscriptions.py\n@@ -126,8 +126,8 @@ async def still_connected(websocket: WebSocket) -> bool:\n     \"\"\"Checks that a client websocket still seems to be connected during a period where\n     the server is expected to be sending messages.\"\"\"\n     try:\n-        await asyncio.wait_for(websocket.receive(), timeout=0.1)\n-        return True  # this should never happen, but if it does, we're still connected\n+        message = await asyncio.wait_for(websocket.receive(), timeout=0.1)\n+        return message[\"type\"] != \"websocket.disconnect\"\n     except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n         # The fact that we timed out rather than getting another kind of error\n         # here means we're still connected to our client, so we can continue to send\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/server/utilities/test_subscriptions.py b/tests/server/utilities/test_subscriptions.py\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 000000000000..58f1115c29b9\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/tests/server/utilities/test_subscriptions.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@\n+from starlette.types import Message\n+from starlette.websockets import WebSocket, WebSocketState\n+\n+from prefect.server.utilities.subscriptions import still_connected\n+\n+\n+def connected_websocket(message: Message) -> WebSocket:\n+    async def receive() -> Message:\n+        return message\n+\n+    async def send(message: Message) -> None:\n+        raise AssertionError(\"still_connected should not send\")\n+\n+    websocket = WebSocket(\n+        {\"type\": \"websocket\", \"path\": \"/\", \"headers\": []}, receive, send\n+    )\n+    websocket.client_state = WebSocketState.CONNECTED\n+    return websocket\n+\n+\n+class TestStillConnected:\n+    async def test_false_after_disconnect_message(self):\n+        websocket = connected_websocket({\"type\": \"websocket.disconnect\", \"code\": 1001})\n+\n+        assert not await still_connected(websocket)\n+\n+    async def test_true_after_client_message(self):\n+        websocket = connected_websocket({\"type\": \"websocket.receive\", \"text\": \"hi\"})\n+\n+        assert await still_connected(websocket)\n", "problem_statement": "`still_connected` returns `True` for a disconnected websocket\n### Bug summary\n\n`prefect.server.utilities.subscriptions.still_connected` returns `True` after the client disconnects.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nfrom starlette.websockets import WebSocket, WebSocketState\n\nfrom prefect.server.utilities.subscriptions import still_connected\n\n\nasync def receive():\n    return {\"type\": \"websocket.disconnect\", \"code\": 1001}\n\n\nasync def main():\n    ws = WebSocket({\"type\": \"websocket\", \"path\": \"/\", \"headers\": []}, receive, None)\n    ws.client_state = WebSocketState.CONNECTED\n    print(await still_connected(ws))  # True\n\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nPrefect 3.8.6; also on main (7d8cf20e30).\n```\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-05T16:21:10Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23778, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23778", "issue_numbers": ["23777"], "base_commit": "7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/server/events/triggers.py b/src/prefect/server/events/triggers.py\nindex 48acb47cac2c..b58646b8d1ef 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/server/events/triggers.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/server/events/triggers.py\n@@ -858,26 +858,54 @@ async def automation_changed(\n     global automation_state_snapshot\n \n     async with _automations_lock():\n-        if event in (\"automation__deleted\", \"automation__updated\"):\n+        if event == \"automation__deleted\":\n             forget_automation(automation_id)\n \n         async with automations_session() as session:\n             if event in (\"automation__created\", \"automation__updated\"):\n                 automation = await read_automation(session, automation_id)\n+\n+                # Swap the automation without awaiting so that concurrent\n+                # evaluations never see it missing while it is being re-read\n+                if event == \"automation__updated\":\n+                    forget_automation(automation_id)\n                 load_automation(automation)\n+\n             automation_state_snapshot = await read_automation_state_snapshot(session)\n \n \n @db_injector\n async def load_automations(db: PrefectDBInterface, session: AsyncSession):\n-    \"\"\"Loads all automations for the given set of accounts\"\"\"\n+    \"\"\"Replaces the loaded automations with all automations from the database\"\"\"\n     query = sa.select(db.Automation)\n \n     logger.debug(\"Loading automations\")\n \n     result = await session.execute(query)\n-    for automation in result.scalars().all():\n-        load_automation(Automation.model_validate(automation, from_attributes=True))\n+    automations = [\n+        Automation.model_validate(automation, from_attributes=True)\n+        for automation in result.scalars().all()\n+    ]\n+\n+    # Swap the loaded automations without awaiting so that concurrent evaluations\n+    # see either the previous set of automations or the new one, never an empty or\n+    # partially loaded set\n+    previous_automations = automations_by_id.copy()\n+    previous_triggers = triggers.copy()\n+    previous_triggers_by_expect = {k: v.copy() for k, v in _triggers_by_expect.items()}\n+    previous_next_proactive_runs = next_proactive_runs.copy()\n+\n+    clear_loaded_automations()\n+    try:\n+        for automation in automations:\n+            load_automation(automation)\n+    except Exception:\n+        clear_loaded_automations()\n+        automations_by_id.update(previous_automations)\n+        triggers.update(previous_triggers)\n+        _triggers_by_expect.update(previous_triggers_by_expect)\n+        next_proactive_runs.update(previous_next_proactive_runs)\n+        raise\n \n     logger.debug(\n         \"Loaded %s automations with %s triggers\", len(automations_by_id), len(triggers)\n@@ -912,24 +940,7 @@ async def reconcile_automations(force: bool = False) -> bool:\n             if not force and current_snapshot == automation_state_snapshot:\n                 return False\n \n-            previous_automations = automations_by_id.copy()\n-            previous_triggers = triggers.copy()\n-            previous_triggers_by_expect = {\n-                k: v.copy() for k, v in _triggers_by_expect.items()\n-            }\n-            previous_next_proactive_runs = next_proactive_runs.copy()\n-\n-            clear_loaded_automations()\n-\n-            try:\n-                await load_automations(session)\n-            except Exception:\n-                clear_loaded_automations()\n-                automations_by_id.update(previous_automations)\n-                triggers.update(previous_triggers)\n-                _triggers_by_expect.update(previous_triggers_by_expect)\n-                next_proactive_runs.update(previous_next_proactive_runs)\n-                raise\n+            await load_automations(session)\n \n             automation_state_snapshot = current_snapshot\n             return True\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/events/server/triggers/test_service.py b/tests/events/server/triggers/test_service.py\nindex c6db9d949e51..0481000913a6 100644\n--- a/tests/events/server/triggers/test_service.py\n+++ b/tests/events/server/triggers/test_service.py\n@@ -272,7 +272,6 @@ async def test_reconcile_automations_skips_reload_when_snapshot_matches(\n \n \n async def test_reconcile_automations_reloads_when_snapshot_changes(\n-    arachnophobia: Automation,\n     open_automations_session: mock.Mock,\n     load_automations: mock.AsyncMock,\n     read_automation_state_snapshot: mock.AsyncMock,\n@@ -280,9 +279,6 @@ async def test_reconcile_automations_reloads_when_snapshot_changes(\n     previous_snapshot = (now(\"UTC\"), 1)\n     current_snapshot = (now(\"UTC\") + timedelta(seconds=1), 2)\n     triggers.automation_state_snapshot = previous_snapshot\n-    triggers.load_automation(arachnophobia)\n-    (trigger,) = arachnophobia.triggers_of_type(EventTrigger)\n-    triggers.next_proactive_runs[trigger.id] = now(\"UTC\")\n     read_automation_state_snapshot.return_value = current_snapshot\n \n     changed = await triggers.reconcile_automations()\n@@ -290,9 +286,6 @@ async def test_reconcile_automations_reloads_when_snapshot_changes(\n     assert changed is True\n     load_automations.assert_awaited_once_with(open_automations_session)\n     assert triggers.automation_state_snapshot == current_snapshot\n-    assert triggers.automations_by_id == {}\n-    assert triggers.triggers == {}\n-    assert triggers.next_proactive_runs == {}\n \n \n async def test_only_considers_messages_with_attributes(\ndiff --git a/tests/events/server/triggers/test_service_changes.py b/tests/events/server/triggers/test_service_changes.py\nindex a7b48d78073a..beeb90e447cb 100644\n--- a/tests/events/server/triggers/test_service_changes.py\n+++ b/tests/events/server/triggers/test_service_changes.py\n@@ -1,5 +1,7 @@\n import asyncio\n+from contextlib import asynccontextmanager\n from datetime import timedelta\n+from typing import Any, AsyncGenerator\n from unittest import mock\n from uuid import uuid4\n \n@@ -15,7 +17,9 @@\n     EventTrigger,\n     Posture,\n )\n+from prefect.server.events.schemas.events import ReceivedEvent\n from prefect.settings import PREFECT_API_SERVICES_TRIGGERS_ENABLED, temporary_settings\n+from prefect.types._datetime import now\n \n pytestmark = pytest.mark.clear_db\n \n@@ -268,3 +272,93 @@ async def test_gracefully_handles_errors_during_changes(\n \n         (trigger,) = automation.triggers_of_type(EventTrigger)\n         assert trigger.id not in triggers.triggers\n+\n+\n+class HeldRead:\n+    def __init__(self) -> None:\n+        self.reading = asyncio.Event()\n+        self.release = asyncio.Event()\n+\n+\n+def hold_automations_session_read(\n+    monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch, method: str, call: int\n+) -> HeldRead:\n+    \"\"\"Makes the triggers service's `call`-th `session.<method>(...)` wait until the\n+    test releases it, as a slow database query would\"\"\"\n+    held = HeldRead()\n+    calls = 0\n+\n+    @asynccontextmanager\n+    async def automations_session() -> AsyncGenerator[AsyncSession, None]:\n+        async with automations.automations_session() as session:\n+            original = getattr(session, method)\n+\n+            async def read(*args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:\n+                nonlocal calls\n+                calls += 1\n+                if calls == call:\n+                    held.reading.set()\n+                    await held.release.wait()\n+                return await original(*args, **kwargs)\n+\n+            monkeypatch.setattr(session, method, read)\n+            yield session\n+\n+    monkeypatch.setattr(triggers, \"automations_session\", automations_session)\n+    return held\n+\n+\n+def stuff_happened_to_foo() -> ReceivedEvent:\n+    return ReceivedEvent(\n+        occurred=now(\"UTC\"),\n+        event=\"stuff.happened\",\n+        resource={\"prefect.resource.id\": \"foo\"},\n+        id=uuid4(),\n+    )\n+\n+\n+async def test_reconcile_keeps_automations_loaded_while_reading(\n+    starting_automations: None, monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch\n+):\n+    \"\"\"Regression test for https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/issues/23777\"\"\"\n+    await triggers.reconcile_automations(force=True)\n+    (expected,) = triggers.find_interested_triggers(stuff_happened_to_foo())\n+\n+    # the first read is the automation state snapshot, the second reads all\n+    # automations\n+    held = hold_automations_session_read(monkeypatch, \"execute\", call=2)\n+    reload = asyncio.create_task(triggers.reconcile_automations(force=True))\n+    try:\n+        await held.reading.wait()\n+\n+        assert triggers.find_interested_triggers(stuff_happened_to_foo()) == [expected]\n+    finally:\n+        held.release.set()\n+        assert await reload is True\n+\n+    assert triggers.find_interested_triggers(stuff_happened_to_foo()) == [expected]\n+\n+\n+async def test_automation_update_keeps_automation_loaded_while_reading(\n+    starting_automations: None, monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch\n+):\n+    \"\"\"Regression test for https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/issues/23777\"\"\"\n+    await triggers.reconcile_automations(force=True)\n+    (trigger,) = triggers.find_interested_triggers(stuff_happened_to_foo())\n+    automation_id = trigger.automation.id\n+\n+    held = hold_automations_session_read(monkeypatch, \"scalar\", call=1)\n+    update = asyncio.create_task(\n+        triggers.automation_changed(automation_id, \"automation__updated\")\n+    )\n+    try:\n+        await held.reading.wait()\n+\n+        assert automation_id in triggers.automations_by_id\n+        assert triggers.find_interested_triggers(stuff_happened_to_foo()) == [trigger]\n+    finally:\n+        held.release.set()\n+        await update\n+\n+    assert automation_id in triggers.automations_by_id\n+    assert triggers.find_interested_triggers(stuff_happened_to_foo()) == [trigger]\n", "problem_statement": "Triggers service drops events evaluated while `reconcile_automations` reloads the automation cache\n### Bug summary\n\nThe triggers service does not fire an automation for a matching event when the event is evaluated while `reconcile_automations` reloads the automation cache. The event is acknowledged and nothing is logged.\n\n[`reconcile_automations`](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/main/src/prefect/server/events/triggers.py) calls `clear_loaded_automations()` and then `await load_automations(session)`. The event consumer runs in the same event loop, and `find_interested_triggers` reads the cache without taking `_automations_lock`. While `load_automations` waits on the database, the cache is empty, so a matching event finds no triggers.\n\nSince #21089, `periodic_evaluation` calls `reconcile_automations()` every 5 seconds, and the reload runs whenever the automation snapshot has changed. For example, this happens on every automation change when PostgreSQL NOTIFY/LISTEN is not available, or when many automations change in a short time. The more automations there are, the longer the read takes.\n\n`automation_changed` has the same gap for a single automation: for `automation__updated` it calls `forget_automation` and then awaits `read_automation` before `load_automation`.\n\nThe script below holds the reload at its database read, as a slow query would, and evaluates a matching event in that moment. It replaces `triggers.act` to record firings and uses a temporary SQLite database.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nimport os\nimport tempfile\nfrom datetime import timedelta\nfrom uuid import uuid4\n\nos.environ[\"PREFECT_SERVER_DATABASE_CONNECTION_URL\"] = f\"sqlite+aiosqlite:///{tempfile.mkdtemp()}/repro.db\"\n\nfrom prefect.server.database import provide_database_interface\nfrom prefect.server.events import triggers\nfrom prefect.server.events.actions import DoNothing\nfrom prefect.server.events.models.automations import automations_session, create_automation\nfrom prefect.server.events.schemas.automations import Automation, EventTrigger, Posture\nfrom prefect.server.events.schemas.events import ReceivedEvent\nfrom prefect.types._datetime import now\n\nfired = []\n\n\nasync def record_firing(firing):\n    fired.append(firing.triggering_event.id)\n\n\ndef make_event():\n    return ReceivedEvent(occurred=now(\"UTC\"), event=\"example.happened\", resource={\"prefect.resource.id\": \"example.1\"}, id=uuid4())\n\n\nasync def main():\n    await provide_database_interface().create_db()\n    async with automations_session(begin_transaction=True) as session:\n        await create_automation(\n            session,\n            Automation(\n                name=\"example\",\n                trigger=EventTrigger(\n                    posture=Posture.Reactive,\n                    expect={\"example.happened\"},\n                    match={\"prefect.resource.id\": \"example.1\"},\n                    threshold=1,\n                    within=timedelta(0),\n                ),\n                actions=[DoNothing()],\n            ),\n        )\n    await triggers.reconcile_automations(force=True)\n    triggers.act = record_firing\n\n    event = make_event()\n    await triggers.reactive_evaluation(event)\n    print(\"fires outside a reload:\", event.id in fired)\n\n    # Hold the reload at its database read, as a slow query would.\n    reload_started, release_reload = asyncio.Event(), asyncio.Event()\n    real_load = triggers.load_automations\n\n    async def slow_load(session):\n        reload_started.set()\n        await release_reload.wait()\n        await real_load(session)\n\n    triggers.load_automations = slow_load\n    reload = asyncio.create_task(triggers.reconcile_automations(force=True))\n    await reload_started.wait()\n\n    event = make_event()\n    await triggers.reactive_evaluation(event)\n    print(\"fires during a reload: \", event.id in fired)\n\n    release_reload.set()\n    await reload\n\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nOutput with `uv run --isolated --no-project --with prefect==3.9.1 python repro.py`:\n\n```text\nfires outside a reload: True\nfires during a reload:  False\n```\n\n3.7.5 gives the same output. Without holding the reload, I also saw it happen: with 2,000 automations in SQLite, a reload once a second and an event every 10 ms, 1 of 171 events did not fire.\n\n**Expected:** an event that matches an automation fires it, whether or not a reload is running.\n\n**Possible fix:** read every automation first, then call `clear_loaded_automations()` and load them with no `await` in between, so the consumer reads either the old cache or the new one. In `automation_changed`, await `read_automation` before `forget_automation`. I tested the `reconcile_automations` change against the script above and the event fires. I can open a pull request with this change and a test in `tests/events/server/triggers/test_service.py`.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.9.1\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.12.13\nGit commit:           bd4807c3\nBuilt:                Fri, Oct 30, 2026 03:52 PM\nOS/Arch:              linux/x86_64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          ephemeral\nPydantic version:     2.14.3\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.53.1\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\nThe code is the same on `main` as of 2026-11-05.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-05T10:30:57Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23776, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23776", "issue_numbers": ["23775"], "base_commit": "7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8", "patch": "diff --git a/ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/trigger-json-input.tsx b/ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/trigger-json-input.tsx\nindex 231ddf787bc8..065eebf958ba 100644\n--- a/ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/trigger-json-input.tsx\n+++ b/ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/trigger-json-input.tsx\n@@ -48,7 +48,7 @@ export const TriggerJsonInput = ({\n \t\t<div className=\"space-y-2\">\n \t\t\t<div className=\"flex items-center justify-between\">\n \t\t\t\t<Label>Trigger Configuration</Label>\n-\t\t\t\t<Button variant=\"ghost\" size=\"sm\" onClick={formatJson}>\n+\t\t\t\t<Button type=\"button\" variant=\"ghost\" size=\"sm\" onClick={formatJson}>\n \t\t\t\t\tFormat\n \t\t\t\t</Button>\n \t\t\t</div>\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/trigger-json-input.test.tsx b/ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/trigger-json-input.test.tsx\nindex c2de7841a14c..11b459d9e148 100644\n--- a/ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/trigger-json-input.test.tsx\n+++ b/ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/trigger-json-input.test.tsx\n@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@\n import { render, screen } from \"@testing-library/react\";\n import userEvent from \"@testing-library/user-event\";\n import { useState } from \"react\";\n-import { describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\n+import { describe, expect, it, vi } from \"vitest\";\n import \"@/mocks/mock-json-input\";\n import { TriggerJsonInput } from \"./trigger-json-input\";\n \n@@ -173,6 +173,21 @@ describe(\"TriggerJsonInput\", () => {\n \t\texpect(textarea).toHaveValue(\"test\");\n \t});\n \n+\tit(\"does not submit the parent form when format button is clicked\", async () => {\n+\t\tconst user = userEvent.setup();\n+\t\tconst onSubmit = vi.fn((e: React.FormEvent) => e.preventDefault());\n+\n+\t\trender(\n+\t\t\t<form onSubmit={onSubmit}>\n+\t\t\t\t<TriggerJsonInputContainer defaultValue='{\"type\":\"event\"}' />\n+\t\t\t</form>,\n+\t\t);\n+\n+\t\tawait user.click(screen.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Format\" }));\n+\n+\t\texpect(onSubmit).not.toHaveBeenCalled();\n+\t});\n+\n \tit(\"documentation link opens in new tab\", () => {\n \t\trender(<TriggerJsonInputContainer />);\n \n", "problem_statement": "V2 UI feedback: Bug or incorrect behavior\n### UI switch feedback\n\n- From UI: V2 UI\n- To UI: V1 UI\n- Current path: /automations\n- Reason: Bug or incorrect behavior\n\n### Additional notes\nWhen clicking on \"format\" on an automation trigger, it automatically saves and closes the automation\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-05T09:20:09Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23773, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23773", "issue_numbers": ["23769"], "base_commit": "7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/flows.py b/src/prefect/flows.py\nindex 52e03aa8d74d..22588d6124b9 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/flows.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/flows.py\n@@ -3032,7 +3032,12 @@ def load_flow_from_entrypoint(\n         # If the flow has dependencies that are not installed in the current\n         # environment, fallback to loading the flow via AST parsing.\n         if use_placeholder_flow:\n-            flow: Optional[Flow[P, Any]] = safe_load_flow_from_entrypoint(entrypoint)\n+            try:\n+                flow: Optional[Flow[P, Any]] = safe_load_flow_from_entrypoint(\n+                    entrypoint\n+                )\n+            except Exception:\n+                flow = None\n             if flow is None:\n                 raise\n         else:\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/test_flows.py b/tests/test_flows.py\nindex ac165de589d9..f17b78afd57a 100644\n--- a/tests/test_flows.py\n+++ b/tests/test_flows.py\n@@ -3088,6 +3088,94 @@ def test_load_flow_from_entrypoint_with_and_without_use_placeholder_flow(\n                 f\"{fpath}:{entrypoint_without_filename}\", use_placeholder_flow=False\n             )\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"source_code\",\n+        [\n+            pytest.param(\n+                \"\"\"\n+                import not_a_module\n+                from prefect import flow\n+\n+                @flow\n+                def etl():\n+                    pass\n+\n+                etl_daily = etl.with_options(name=\"etl-daily\")\n+                \"\"\",\n+                id=\"with_options\",\n+            ),\n+            pytest.param(\n+                \"\"\"\n+                import not_a_module\n+                from prefect import flow\n+\n+                def run_etl():\n+                    pass\n+\n+                etl_daily = flow(name=\"etl-daily\")(run_etl)\n+                \"\"\",\n+                id=\"decorator-call\",\n+            ),\n+            pytest.param(\n+                \"\"\"\n+                import not_a_module\n+                from prefect import Flow, flow\n+\n+                @flow\n+                def etl():\n+                    pass\n+\n+                etl_daily: Flow = etl.with_options(name=\"etl-daily\")\n+                \"\"\",\n+                id=\"annotated-assignment\",\n+            ),\n+            pytest.param(\n+                \"\"\"\n+                import not_a_module\n+                from prefect import flow as etl_daily\n+                \"\"\",\n+                id=\"re-export\",\n+            ),\n+        ],\n+    )\n+    def test_load_flow_from_entrypoint_raises_script_error_for_non_def_entrypoint(\n+        self, tmp_path: Path, source_code: str\n+    ):\n+        fpath = tmp_path / \"f.py\"\n+        fpath.write_text(dedent(source_code))\n+\n+        with pytest.raises(ScriptError) as exc_info:\n+            load_flow_from_entrypoint(f\"{fpath}:etl_daily\")\n+\n+        assert isinstance(exc_info.value.user_exc, ModuleNotFoundError)\n+\n+    def test_load_flow_from_entrypoint_raises_script_error_for_import_time_error(\n+        self, tmp_path: Path, monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch\n+    ):\n+        monkeypatch.delenv(\"PREFECT_TEST_UNSET_ENV_VAR\", raising=False)\n+        fpath = tmp_path / \"f.py\"\n+        fpath.write_text(\n+            dedent(\n+                \"\"\"\n+                import os\n+                from prefect import flow\n+\n+                SECRET = os.environ[\"PREFECT_TEST_UNSET_ENV_VAR\"]\n+\n+                @flow\n+                def etl():\n+                    pass\n+\n+                etl_daily = etl.with_options(name=\"etl-daily\")\n+                \"\"\"\n+            )\n+        )\n+\n+        with pytest.raises(ScriptError) as exc_info:\n+            load_flow_from_entrypoint(f\"{fpath}:etl_daily\")\n+\n+        assert isinstance(exc_info.value.user_exc, KeyError)\n+\n     @pytest.mark.skip(reason=\"Fails with new engine, passed on old engine\")\n     async def test_handling_script_with_unprotected_call_in_flow_script(\n         self, tmp_path, caplog, prefect_client\n", "problem_statement": "`prefect deploy` hides import errors behind `Could not find object` for non-`def` entrypoints\n### Bug summary\n\nWhen the module behind an entrypoint fails to import where `prefect deploy` runs (a missing dependency, an env var read at import time, ...), Prefect falls back to finding the flow in the source code. That works for flows defined with `def`. For any other entrypoint, like `etl_daily = etl.with_options(...)`, the fallback raises `ValueError: Could not find object 'etl_daily' in 'flows.py'`, the CLI prints only that message, and the real error is lost.\n\nWe hit this in CI: our dbt deployments point to `.with_options()` variants of one flow, and the deploy step didn't have `prefect-dbt` installed. Every deployment failed with `Could not find object 'flow_...'`, so it took a while to see that the problem was the missing package and not the entrypoint.\n\nMinimal repro, with a local server running (`prefect server start`):\n\n`flows.py`:\n\n```python\nimport some_dependency_not_installed  # e.g. a package that is only in the flow's image\n\nfrom prefect import flow\n\n\n@flow\ndef etl():\n    pass\n\n\netl_daily = etl.with_options(name=\"etl-daily\", retries=2)\n```\n\n`prefect.yaml`:\n\n```yaml\ndeployments:\n  - name: daily\n    entrypoint: flows.py:etl_daily\n    work_pool:\n      name: local\n  - name: plain\n    entrypoint: flows.py:etl\n    work_pool:\n      name: local\n```\n\n```bash\nprefect work-pool create local --type process\nprefect deploy --name daily\nprefect deploy --name plain\n```\n\nOutput (trimmed):\n\n```\n$ prefect deploy --name daily\nCould not find object 'etl_daily' in 'flows.py'\n\n$ prefect deploy --name plain\n\u2502 Deployment 'etl/plain' successfully created with id                          \u2502\n\u2502 '7b8ed71d-99bb-41b4-96b9-6666199e8205'.                                      \u2502\n```\n\n`plain` is created with the placeholder flow, as intended. Expected for `daily`: the import error, which is what the runtime raises for the same entrypoint: `ScriptError: Script at 'flows.py' encountered an exception: ModuleNotFoundError(\"No module named 'some_dependency_not_installed'\")`.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.9.1\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.12.16\nGit commit:           bd4807c3\nBuilt:                Fri, Oct 30, 2026 03:52 PM\nOS/Arch:              linux/x86_64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          server\nPydantic version:     2.14.2\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.46.1\n```\n\nSame on `main` at 7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8. Also on 2.20.0 and every 3.x release I tried; up to 3.4.0 the message reads `Could not find flow 'etl_daily' in 'flows.py'`.\n\n### Additional context\n\nThe same happens for other entrypoint shapes:\n- `etl_daily = flow(name=\"etl-daily\")(run_etl)`;\n- annotated assignments (`etl_daily: Flow = etl.with_options(...)`);\n- re-exports (`from .impl import etl as etl_daily`).\n\nIt's not only missing packages: an `ImportError` or a `KeyError` from `os.environ[...]` at import time gives the same `Could not find object`. `Flow.from_source(source=..., entrypoint=\"flows.py:etl_daily\")` raises the same `ValueError`. With `use_placeholder_flow=False`, as at runtime, the real `ScriptError` is raised, so only deploy-time loading is affected.\n\nWhere it happens:\n- `load_flow_from_entrypoint` falls back to `safe_load_flow_from_entrypoint` on `ScriptError`, and re-raises the original error only when the fallback returns `None`: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8/src/prefect/flows.py#L3031-L3039\n- the fallback starts with `_entrypoint_definition_and_source`, which raises `ValueError` when the target isn't a top-level `def` or class: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8/src/prefect/flows.py#L3418 and https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8/src/prefect/flows.py#L3721\n- `prefect deploy` turns any `ValueError` into its message: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_commands.py#L491-L492\n\nProposed fix: in `load_flow_from_entrypoint`, treat an exception from the fallback like a `None` result and re-raise the original `ScriptError`. `safe_load_flow_from_entrypoint` keeps raising `ValueError` for a missing object, which `test_flow_not_found` relies on. With that change every case above reports the real error, `def` entrypoints still load as placeholders, and the existing entrypoint tests in `tests/test_flows.py` pass. I plan to open a pull request for this; happy to adjust the approach.\n\nWhile checking this, I found that a script that fails to import stays in `sys.modules`, which makes the next error in the same process misleading too (e.g. with `prefect deploy --all`). It's a separate fix, so I opened #23770 for it.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-05T00:15:21Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23772, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23772", "issue_numbers": ["23770"], "base_commit": "7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/utilities/importtools.py b/src/prefect/utilities/importtools.py\nindex d4dda15fe49f..93e644c607fe 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/utilities/importtools.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/utilities/importtools.py\n@@ -117,13 +117,21 @@ def load_script_as_module(path: str) -> ModuleType:\n         assert spec.loader is not None\n \n     module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n-    sys.modules[module_name] = module\n \n     try:\n         with _get_sys_path_lock():\n             sys.path.insert(0, working_directory)\n             sys.path.insert(0, parent_path)\n-            spec.loader.exec_module(module)\n+            sys.modules[module_name] = module\n+            try:\n+                spec.loader.exec_module(module)\n+            except BaseException:\n+                # Match the import system: drop a module whose execution failed so\n+                # later imports of the same name re-run it instead of getting a\n+                # half-run module\n+                if sys.modules.get(module_name) is module:\n+                    del sys.modules[module_name]\n+                raise\n     except Exception as exc:\n         raise ScriptError(user_exc=exc, path=path) from exc\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/utilities/test_importtools.py b/tests/utilities/test_importtools.py\nindex 3d1a069deded..d2026c66d1bd 100644\n--- a/tests/utilities/test_importtools.py\n+++ b/tests/utilities/test_importtools.py\n@@ -443,6 +443,72 @@ def test_load_script_as_module_does_not_leak_sys_modules_on_repeated_loads(\n     )\n \n \n+def test_load_script_as_module_removes_failed_script_from_sys_modules(\n+    tmp_path: Path,\n+):\n+    helpers = tmp_path / \"failing_helpers_mod.py\"\n+    helpers.write_text(\n+        \"import some_dependency_not_installed\\n\\n\\ndef shared():\\n    pass\\n\"\n+    )\n+    flows = tmp_path / \"flows_importing_failing_helpers.py\"\n+    flows.write_text(\"from failing_helpers_mod import shared\\n\")\n+\n+    before = set(sys.modules)\n+    try:\n+        with pytest.raises(ScriptError) as exc_info:\n+            load_script_as_module(str(helpers))\n+        assert isinstance(exc_info.value.user_exc, ModuleNotFoundError)\n+        assert \"failing_helpers_mod\" not in sys.modules\n+\n+        # A script importing the failed one should see the original error, not\n+        # an `ImportError` from a half-run module left in `sys.modules`\n+        with pytest.raises(ScriptError) as exc_info:\n+            load_script_as_module(str(flows))\n+        assert isinstance(exc_info.value.user_exc, ModuleNotFoundError)\n+        assert \"some_dependency_not_installed\" in str(exc_info.value.user_exc)\n+        assert \"failing_helpers_mod\" not in sys.modules\n+    finally:\n+        for k in set(sys.modules) - before:\n+            sys.modules.pop(k, None)\n+\n+\n+def test_load_script_as_module_removes_failed_script_with_fallback_name(\n+    tmp_path: Path,\n+):\n+    dir_a = tmp_path / \"a\"\n+    dir_b = tmp_path / \"b\"\n+    dir_a.mkdir()\n+    dir_b.mkdir()\n+    (dir_a / \"dup_failing_script.py\").write_text(\"x = 1\\n\")\n+    (dir_b / \"dup_failing_script.py\").write_text(\"raise ValueError('boom')\\n\")\n+\n+    before = set(sys.modules)\n+    try:\n+        loaded = load_script_as_module(str(dir_a / \"dup_failing_script.py\"))\n+        assert sys.modules[\"dup_failing_script\"] is loaded\n+\n+        with pytest.raises(ScriptError):\n+            load_script_as_module(str(dir_b / \"dup_failing_script.py\"))\n+\n+        # The successfully loaded module keeps its entry; the failed one leaves none\n+        assert set(sys.modules) - before == {\"dup_failing_script\"}\n+        assert sys.modules[\"dup_failing_script\"] is loaded\n+    finally:\n+        for k in set(sys.modules) - before:\n+            sys.modules.pop(k, None)\n+\n+\n+def test_load_script_as_module_removes_script_that_raises_base_exception(\n+    tmp_path: Path,\n+):\n+    script = tmp_path / \"exiting_script_mod.py\"\n+    script.write_text(\"import sys\\n\\nsys.exit(3)\\n\")\n+\n+    with pytest.raises(SystemExit):\n+        load_script_as_module(str(script))\n+    assert \"exiting_script_mod\" not in sys.modules\n+\n+\n def test_concurrent_script_loading(tmpdir: Path):\n     \"\"\"Test that loading multiple scripts concurrently is thread-safe.\n \n", "problem_statement": "`load_script_as_module` leaves a script that failed to run in `sys.modules`\n### Bug summary\n\n`load_script_as_module` registers the script in `sys.modules` under the file name before running it, and doesn't remove it when the script raises. Python's own import system removes a module whose execution failed. Here the half-run module stays, so the next `import` of that name in the same process gets it, and the error changes from the original exception to `ImportError: cannot import name ...` (or `MissingFlowError: ... not found` for a module-path entrypoint).\n\nIt shows up when several entrypoints are loaded in one process, like `prefect deploy --all` or a script that deploys several flows with `Flow.from_source`: once one script fails to import, the scripts that import it fail with an error that points to the wrong place. I found it while checking #23769.\n\nMinimal repro, no server needed:\n\n`helpers.py`:\n\n```python\nimport some_dependency_not_installed\n\n\ndef shared():\n    pass\n```\n\n`flows.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom helpers import shared\n\nfrom prefect import flow\n\n\n@flow\ndef etl():\n    shared()\n```\n\n`repro.py`:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\nfrom prefect.exceptions import ScriptError\nfrom prefect.utilities.importtools import load_script_as_module\n\nfor path in (\"helpers.py\", \"flows.py\"):\n    try:\n        load_script_as_module(path)\n    except ScriptError as exc:\n        print(f\"{path}: {exc.user_exc!r}\")\n    print(f\"  'helpers' in sys.modules: {'helpers' in sys.modules}\")\n```\n\nOutput of `python repro.py`:\n\n```\nhelpers.py: ModuleNotFoundError(\"No module named 'some_dependency_not_installed'\")\n  'helpers' in sys.modules: True\nflows.py: ImportError(\"cannot import name 'shared' from 'helpers' (.../helpers.py)\")\n  'helpers' in sys.modules: True\n```\n\nExpected: `flows.py` fails with the same `ModuleNotFoundError`, and `helpers` is no longer in `sys.modules` after the first failure.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.9.1\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.12.16\nGit commit:           bd4807c3\nBuilt:                Fri, Oct 30, 2026 03:52 PM\nOS/Arch:              linux/x86_64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          server\nPydantic version:     2.14.2\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.46.1\n```\n\nSame on `main` at 7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8, and since 3.1.9.\n\n### Additional context\n\nUp to 3.1.8 the loader used a fixed `__prefect_loader__` name and popped it in a `finally`. #16458 (3.1.9) switched to a per-file name to make `from_source` safe to use concurrently and dropped the cleanup. Keeping the entry makes sense for a script that loaded, but not for one that failed: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8/src/prefect/utilities/importtools.py#L119-L128\n\nProposed fix: in the `except` branch, remove the entry before raising `ScriptError`, only if it's still the module this call created (`if sys.modules.get(module_name) is module: del sys.modules[module_name]`). Successful loads keep their entry as today.\n\nWith that change:\n- the repro reports the `ModuleNotFoundError` both times;\n- together with the fix for #23769, `prefect deploy --all` reports the real error for every deployment that depends on the broken script;\n- `tests/utilities/test_importtools.py` and the entrypoint tests in `tests/test_flows.py` pass.\n\nI plan to open a pull request for this; happy to adjust the approach.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-05T00:14:17Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23771, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23771", "issue_numbers": ["23768"], "base_commit": "7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_config.py b/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_config.py\nindex be9cdeefd5eb..6732646c09cd 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_config.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_config.py\n@@ -381,30 +381,28 @@ def _parse_name_from_pattern(\n     name_pattern = re.escape(name_pattern).replace(r\"\\*\", \".*\")\n \n     if \"/\" in name_pattern:\n-        flow_name, deploy_name = name_pattern.split(\"/\", 1)\n-        flow_name = (\n-            re.compile(flow_name.replace(\"*\", \".*\"))\n-            if \"*\" in flow_name\n-            else re.compile(flow_name)\n-        )\n-        deploy_name = (\n-            re.compile(deploy_name.replace(\"*\", \".*\"))\n-            if \"*\" in deploy_name\n-            else re.compile(deploy_name)\n-        )\n+        flow_pattern, deploy_pattern = name_pattern.split(\"/\", 1)\n+        flow_name = re.compile(flow_pattern)\n+        deploy_name = re.compile(deploy_pattern)\n     else:\n         flow_name = None\n-        deploy_name = re.compile(name_pattern.replace(\"*\", \".*\"))\n+        deploy_name = re.compile(name_pattern)\n \n     for deploy_config in deploy_configs:\n         if not deploy_config.get(\"entrypoint\"):\n             continue\n-        entrypoint = deploy_config.get(\"entrypoint\").split(\":\")[-1].replace(\"_\", \"-\")\n+        flow_func_name = deploy_config.get(\"entrypoint\").split(\":\")[-1]\n         deployment_name = deploy_config.get(\"name\")\n-        flow_match = flow_name.fullmatch(entrypoint) if flow_name else True\n+        flow_match = (\n+            flow_name.fullmatch(flow_func_name.replace(\"_\", \"-\")) if flow_name else True\n+        )\n         deploy_match = deploy_name.fullmatch(deployment_name)\n         if flow_match and deploy_match:\n-            parsed_names.append(deployment_name)\n+            # Keep the flow qualifier so deployments with the same name in other\n+            # flows aren't selected too\n+            parsed_names.append(\n+                f\"{flow_func_name}/{deployment_name}\" if flow_name else deployment_name\n+            )\n \n     return parsed_names\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/cli/test_deploy.py b/tests/cli/test_deploy.py\nindex 36a76e56542b..b2294b6ebbd4 100644\n--- a/tests/cli/test_deploy.py\n+++ b/tests/cli/test_deploy.py\n@@ -20,6 +20,10 @@\n \n import prefect\n from prefect.blocks.system import Secret\n+from prefect.cli.deploy._config import (\n+    _filter_matching_deploy_config,\n+    _parse_name_from_pattern,\n+)\n from prefect.cli.deploy._storage import _PullStepStorage\n from prefect.cli.deploy._triggers import (\n     _create_deployment_triggers,\n@@ -4598,6 +4602,101 @@ async def test_pattern_deploy_multiple_existing_deployments(\n                 \"An important name/dont-deploy-me\"\n             )\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"name_pattern\", [\"my-flow*/release\", \"my-flow/rel*\", \"*my-flow*/*release*\"]\n+    )\n+    def test_pattern_with_flow_keeps_flow_qualifier(self, name_pattern: str):\n+        deploy_configs = [\n+            {\"name\": \"release\", \"entrypoint\": \"flows.py:my_flow\"},\n+            {\"name\": \"release\", \"entrypoint\": \"flows.py:other_flow\"},\n+        ]\n+\n+        parsed_names = _parse_name_from_pattern(deploy_configs, name_pattern)\n+\n+        assert parsed_names == [\"my_flow/release\"]\n+        assert _filter_matching_deploy_config(parsed_names[0], deploy_configs) == [\n+            deploy_configs[0]\n+        ]\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"deploy_name\",\n+        [\n+            \"test-name*\",\n+            \"*test-name*\",\n+            (\"*test-name\", \"*test-name-backfill\"),\n+            \"my-flow/test-name*\",\n+            (\"my-flow*/test-name\", \"*my-flow/test-name-backfill\"),\n+            \"*my-flow*/*test-name*\",\n+        ],\n+    )\n+    async def test_pattern_deploy_wildcard_matches_empty_string(\n+        self,\n+        deploy_name: str | tuple[str, ...],\n+        project_dir: Path,\n+        prefect_client: PrefectClient,\n+        work_pool: WorkPool,\n+    ):\n+        prefect_file = Path(\"prefect.yaml\")\n+        with prefect_file.open(mode=\"r\") as f:\n+            contents = yaml.safe_load(f)\n+\n+        contents[\"deployments\"] = [\n+            {\n+                \"name\": \"test-name\",\n+                \"entrypoint\": \"./flows/hello.py:my_flow\",\n+                \"work_pool\": {\"name\": work_pool.name},\n+            },\n+            {\n+                \"name\": \"test-name-backfill\",\n+                \"entrypoint\": \"./flows/hello.py:my_flow\",\n+                \"work_pool\": {\"name\": work_pool.name},\n+            },\n+            {\n+                \"name\": \"dont-deploy-me\",\n+                \"entrypoint\": \"./flows/hello.py:my_flow\",\n+                \"work_pool\": {\"name\": work_pool.name},\n+            },\n+        ]\n+\n+        with prefect_file.open(mode=\"w\") as f:\n+            yaml.safe_dump(contents, f)\n+\n+        if isinstance(deploy_name, tuple):\n+            deploy_command = \"deploy \" + \" \".join(\n+                [f\"-n '{name}'\" for name in deploy_name]\n+            )\n+        else:\n+            deploy_command = f\"deploy -n '{deploy_name}'\"\n+\n+        await run_sync_in_worker_thread(\n+            invoke_and_assert,\n+            command=deploy_command,\n+            expected_code=0,\n+            expected_output_contains=[\n+                \"Deploying flows with selected deployment configurations...\",\n+                \"An important name/test-name'\",\n+                \"An important name/test-name-backfill\",\n+            ],\n+            expected_output_does_not_contain=[\n+                \"An important name/dont-deploy-me\",\n+            ],\n+        )\n+\n+        deployment = await prefect_client.read_deployment_by_name(\n+            \"An important name/test-name\"\n+        )\n+        assert deployment.work_pool_name == work_pool.name\n+\n+        backfill_deployment = await prefect_client.read_deployment_by_name(\n+            \"An important name/test-name-backfill\"\n+        )\n+        assert backfill_deployment.work_pool_name == work_pool.name\n+\n+        with pytest.raises(ObjectNotFound):\n+            await prefect_client.read_deployment_by_name(\n+                \"An important name/dont-deploy-me\"\n+            )\n+\n     @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n         \"deploy_name\",\n         [\n", "problem_statement": "`prefect deploy --name` patterns need at least one character where the `*` is\n### Bug summary\n\nIn a `prefect deploy --name` pattern, `*` only matches one or more characters, never an empty string. So `etl*` doesn't select the `etl` deployment, and `*etl*` selects nothing when the name starts or ends with `etl`. When a pattern selects nothing, the CLI then says that no name was given.\n\nWe ran into this with `prefect deploy --name \"*people_overview*\" --prefect-file prefect.dev.yaml`: it failed with the \"no name was given\" error, and since nothing pointed at the pattern, we stopped using `--name`.\n\nMinimal repro, with a local server running (`prefect server start`):\n\n`flows.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom prefect import flow\n\n\n@flow\ndef etl():\n    pass\n```\n\n`prefect.yaml`:\n\n```yaml\ndeployments:\n  - name: etl\n    entrypoint: flows.py:etl\n    work_pool:\n      name: local\n  - name: etl-backfill\n    entrypoint: flows.py:etl\n    work_pool:\n      name: local\n```\n\n```bash\nprefect work-pool create local --type process\nprefect deploy --name \"etl*\"\nprefect deploy --name \"*etl*\"\n```\n\nOutput (trimmed):\n\n```\n$ prefect deploy --name \"etl*\"\n\u2502 Deployment 'etl/etl-backfill' successfully created with id                   \u2502\n\u2502 '863b5b64-fa0c-4dc7-8969-65bc62b0f241'.                                      \u2502\n\n$ prefect deploy --name \"*etl*\"\nDiscovered one or more deployment configurations, but no name was given. Please\nspecify the name of at least one deployment to create or update.\n```\n\nExpected: both commands create `etl/etl` and `etl/etl-backfill`.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.9.1\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.12.16\nGit commit:           bd4807c3\nBuilt:                Fri, Oct 30, 2026 03:52 PM\nOS/Arch:              linux/x86_64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          server\nPydantic version:     2.14.2\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.46.1\n```\n\nSame on `main` at 7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8. I also checked 2.20.0, 3.0.0 to 3.4.0 and 3.6.22 to 3.9.1, and they all behave the same.\n\n### Additional context\n\n`_parse_name_from_pattern` converts `*` twice: first with `re.escape(name_pattern).replace(r\"\\*\", \".*\")`, then with `.replace(\"*\", \".*\")` on the already converted pattern, so each `*` becomes `..*`: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_config.py#L381-L397\n\nI tried 22 patterns against 7 deployment names. The 6 that select the wrong deployments are all cases where a `*` should match an empty string (`name*`, `*name`, `*name*`, `flow/name*`). Patterns like `dbt-*`, `*-2`, `my*deployment` or `flow/*` work because there is always at least one character to match. That's also why the pattern tests in `TestDeployPattern` don't catch it: they use names like `test-name-*` against `test-name-1`: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8/tests/cli/test_deploy.py#L4516\n\nWhen a pattern selects nothing, the result depends on the context:\n- with more than one deployment in non-interactive mode, the \"no name was given\" error above: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_config.py#L496-L508\n- in interactive mode, the deployment picker opens as if no name was passed\n- with a single deployment in `prefect.yaml`, that deployment is created anyway, e.g. `prefect deploy --name \"*nothing*\"`: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/7d8cf20e30b8d22e4fdc99bef3a99050ad0d7cf8/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_config.py#L491-L494\n\nProposed fix: compile the converted pattern as is, without the second replacement, and add tests where `*` matches an empty string at the start, at the end, and on both sides of `/`. With that change all 22 patterns select the expected deployments, and the existing pattern tests still pass. A clearer error for a pattern that matches nothing (e.g. `No deployment configurations match the pattern '*nothing*'.`) would also help, but 13 existing tests expect the current messages, so I'd keep it out of this fix unless you want it. I plan to open a pull request for this; happy to adjust the approach.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-05T00:13:58Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23763, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23763", "issue_numbers": ["23759"], "base_commit": "5c0fbaacaad18ea6d8147e9fe4246ab7a93d23a9", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/cli/_utilities.py b/src/prefect/cli/_utilities.py\nindex bd3463fc17e1..d944142881ec 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/cli/_utilities.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/cli/_utilities.py\n@@ -15,8 +15,15 @@\n \n \n def exit_with_error(message: str | Exception, code: int = 1, **kwargs: Any) -> NoReturn:\n-    \"\"\"Print a stylized error message and exit with a non-zero code.\"\"\"\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Print a stylized error message and exit with a non-zero code.\n+\n+    Rich markup is disabled by default because messages often include user\n+    input or server error text; pass `markup=True` for messages with trusted\n+    markup and escape any interpolated values.\n+    \"\"\"\n     kwargs.setdefault(\"style\", \"red\")\n+    kwargs.setdefault(\"markup\", False)\n     _cli.console.print(message, **kwargs)\n     raise SystemExit(code)\n \ndiff --git a/src/prefect/cli/config.py b/src/prefect/cli/config.py\nindex 28236751d37a..0da17ab619dc 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/cli/config.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/cli/config.py\n@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@\n import cyclopts\n import orjson\n from dotenv import dotenv_values\n+from rich.markup import escape\n \n import prefect.cli._app as _cli\n import prefect.context\n@@ -71,9 +72,9 @@ def set_(\n             for error in problem.errors():\n                 help_message += (\n                     f\"[bold red]Validation error(s) for setting[/bold red] \"\n-                    f\"[blue]{setting.name}[/blue]\\n\\n - {error['msg']}\\n\\n\"\n+                    f\"[blue]{escape(setting.name)}[/blue]\\n\\n - {escape(error['msg'])}\\n\\n\"\n                 )\n-        exit_with_error(help_message)\n+        exit_with_error(help_message, markup=True)\n \n     for setting, value in parsed_settings.items():\n         _cli.console.print(f\"Set {setting!r} to {value!r}.\")\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_actions.py b/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_actions.py\nindex e2cb2fa52577..37cb309f5888 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_actions.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_actions.py\n@@ -234,7 +234,8 @@ async def _generate_default_pull_action(\n                 exit_with_error(\n                     \"Your flow code must be copied into your Docker image to run\"\n                     \" your deployment.\\nTo do so, you can copy this line into your\"\n-                    \" Dockerfile: [yellow]COPY . /opt/prefect/[/yellow]\"\n+                    \" Dockerfile: [yellow]COPY . /opt/prefect/[/yellow]\",\n+                    markup=True,\n                 )\n             return await _generate_pull_step_for_build_docker_image(\n                 console, deploy_config, auto=False\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_commands.py b/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_commands.py\nindex 6160c538bdf7..4f9e61e52d61 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_commands.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_commands.py\n@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@\n from typing import Annotated, Any\n \n import cyclopts\n+from rich.markup import escape\n from rich.prompt import Prompt\n from rich.table import Table\n \n@@ -134,8 +135,9 @@ async def init(\n     except ValueError as exc:\n         if \"Unknown recipe\" in str(exc):\n             exit_with_error(\n-                f\"Unknown recipe {recipe!r} provided - run\"\n-                \" [yellow]`prefect init`[/yellow] to see all available recipes.\"\n+                f\"Unknown recipe {escape(repr(recipe))} provided - run\"\n+                \" [yellow]`prefect init`[/yellow] to see all available recipes.\",\n+                markup=True,\n             )\n         else:\n             raise\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_core.py b/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_core.py\nindex 9437bc0df862..d8839367bce0 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_core.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_core.py\n@@ -108,7 +108,7 @@ async def _run_single_deploy(\n         if not is_interactive():\n             raise ValueError(\n                 \"An entrypoint must be provided:\\n\\n\"\n-                \" \\t[yellow]prefect deploy path/to/file.py:flow_function\\n\\n\"\n+                \" \\tprefect deploy path/to/file.py:flow_function\\n\\n\"\n                 \"You can also provide an entrypoint in a prefect.yaml file.\"\n             )\n         deploy_config[\"entrypoint\"] = await prompt_entrypoint(console)\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/infrastructure/provisioners/coiled.py b/src/prefect/infrastructure/provisioners/coiled.py\nindex 0c8a028b2f83..01ee610cc0a3 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/infrastructure/provisioners/coiled.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/infrastructure/provisioners/coiled.py\n@@ -215,7 +215,7 @@ async def provision(\n                 else:\n                     raise RuntimeError(\n                         \"Coiled credentials not found. Please create a new token by\"\n-                        \" running [blue]coiled login[/] and try again.\"\n+                        \" running `coiled login` and try again.\"\n                     )\n \n             # Create the credentials block\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/infrastructure/provisioners/modal.py b/src/prefect/infrastructure/provisioners/modal.py\nindex 170f4b99e814..51c1ce76822c 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/infrastructure/provisioners/modal.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/infrastructure/provisioners/modal.py\n@@ -220,7 +220,7 @@ async def provision(\n                 else:\n                     raise RuntimeError(\n                         \"Modal credentials not found. Please create a new token by\"\n-                        \" running [blue]modal token new[/] and try again.\"\n+                        \" running `modal token new` and try again.\"\n                     )\n \n             # Create the credentials block\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/cli/test_config.py b/tests/cli/test_config.py\nindex 927790cc1409..b67fa5da7046 100644\n--- a/tests/cli/test_config.py\n+++ b/tests/cli/test_config.py\n@@ -340,6 +340,18 @@ def test_unset_with_unknown_setting():\n     )\n \n \n+def test_unset_with_unknown_setting_containing_markup():\n+    save_profiles(ProfilesCollection([Profile(name=\"foo\", settings={})], active=None))\n+\n+    invoke_and_assert(\n+        [\"--profile\", \"foo\", \"config\", \"unset\", \"foo[/x]\"],\n+        expected_output=\"\"\"\n+            Unknown setting name 'foo[/x]'.\n+            \"\"\",\n+        expected_code=1,\n+    )\n+\n+\n def test_unset_with_setting_not_in_profile():\n     save_profiles(\n         ProfilesCollection(\ndiff --git a/tests/cli/test_utilities.py b/tests/cli/test_utilities.py\nindex 15266c046bfe..fff7ec5f245d 100644\n--- a/tests/cli/test_utilities.py\n+++ b/tests/cli/test_utilities.py\n@@ -8,7 +8,7 @@\n \n import prefect.cli\n import prefect.cli._app as _cli\n-from prefect.cli._utilities import print_verbatim\n+from prefect.cli._utilities import exit_with_error, print_verbatim\n \n CLI_SOURCE_DIR = Path(prefect.cli.__file__).parent\n \n@@ -45,6 +45,29 @@ def test_print_verbatim_preserves_json_output(\n     }\n \n \n+@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+    \"message\",\n+    [\"Unknown setting name 'foo[/x]'.\", \"[bold]literal[/bold]\"],\n+)\n+def test_exit_with_error_prints_message_literally(\n+    captured_console: io.StringIO, message: str\n+):\n+    with pytest.raises(SystemExit) as exc_info:\n+        exit_with_error(message, soft_wrap=True)\n+\n+    assert exc_info.value.code == 1\n+    assert captured_console.getvalue() == message + \"\\n\"\n+\n+\n+def test_exit_with_error_renders_markup_when_requested(\n+    captured_console: io.StringIO,\n+):\n+    with pytest.raises(SystemExit):\n+        exit_with_error(\"[bold]styled[/bold]\", markup=True)\n+\n+    assert captured_console.getvalue() == \"styled\\n\"\n+\n+\n def _is_serialized_output(node: ast.expr) -> bool:\n     if isinstance(node, (ast.Constant, ast.JoinedStr)):\n         return False\n", "problem_statement": "exit_with_error crashes with MarkupError when the message contains a closing bracket tag\n### Bug summary\n\n`exit_with_error` prints its message as Rich markup. If the message contains user input or server error text with something like `[/x]`, the CLI shows a `MarkupError` traceback instead of the error.\n\n```\nprefect config unset \"foo[/x]\"\n```\n\nThis gives a `MarkupError` traceback. I expected `Unknown setting name 'foo[/x]'.`\n\nThe message goes straight to `console.print` with markup on:\nhttps://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/1d50f74d953cabd5ebed03f06d8e779d1c1fad19/src/prefect/cli/_utilities.py#L17-L21\n\nThe `config unset` call site:\nhttps://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/1d50f74d953cabd5ebed03f06d8e779d1c1fad19/src/prefect/cli/config.py#L126-L128\n\nIt takes unusual input, but about 126 call sites put setting names, flow run names, or exception messages into this string (counted at acc006a), and the real error is lost when it happens.\n\nA likely fix is `kwargs.setdefault(\"markup\", False)` in `exit_with_error`, as long as no caller relies on markup in its message.\n\n### Version info\n\n```\nVersion:              3.9.1\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.12.12\nGit commit:           bd4807c3\nBuilt:                Fri, Oct 30, 2026 03:52 PM\nOS/Arch:              darwin/arm64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          ephemeral\nPydantic version:     2.14.2\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.50.4\n```\n\nThe code is the same on main at 1d50f74.\n\n### Additional context\n\n#13903, #23529 and #23691 reported other paths with the same cause. `exit_with_error` still prints with markup on.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-04T21:02:08Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23762, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23762", "issue_numbers": ["23758"], "base_commit": "5c0fbaacaad18ea6d8147e9fe4246ab7a93d23a9", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/cli/block.py b/src/prefect/cli/block.py\nindex e4dde826b473..837510984d8f 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/cli/block.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/cli/block.py\n@@ -78,7 +78,7 @@ def _display_block_type(block_type: BlockType) -> Table:\n         \"Description\",\n         (\n             block_type.description.splitlines()[0].partition(\".\")[0]\n-            if block_type.description is not None\n+            if block_type.description\n             else \"\"\n         ),\n         end_section=True,\n@@ -520,7 +520,7 @@ async def list_types(\n                 str(blocktype.slug),\n                 (\n                     str(blocktype.description.splitlines()[0].partition(\".\")[0])\n-                    if blocktype.description is not None\n+                    if blocktype.description\n                     else \"\"\n                 ),\n                 f\"prefect block create {blocktype.slug}\",\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/cli/test_block.py b/tests/cli/test_block.py\nindex 181215ede1a5..7fd3f4cebec8 100644\n--- a/tests/cli/test_block.py\n+++ b/tests/cli/test_block.py\n@@ -554,6 +554,52 @@ def test_inspecting_a_block_type_invalid_output_format():\n     )\n \n \n+@pytest.fixture\n+async def empty_description_block_schema(session) -> schemas.core.BlockSchema:\n+    block_type = await models.block_types.create_block_type(\n+        session,\n+        block_type=schemas.core.BlockType(\n+            name=\"Empty Description\",\n+            slug=\"empty-description\",\n+            description=\"\",\n+        ),\n+    )\n+    block_schema = await models.block_schemas.create_block_schema(\n+        session,\n+        block_schema=schemas.actions.BlockSchemaCreate(\n+            block_type_id=block_type.id,\n+            fields={\n+                \"title\": \"EmptyDescription\",\n+                \"type\": \"object\",\n+                \"properties\": {\"message\": {\"title\": \"Message\", \"type\": \"string\"}},\n+                \"required\": [\"message\"],\n+            },\n+        ),\n+    )\n+    await session.commit()\n+    return block_schema\n+\n+\n+def test_inspecting_a_block_type_with_empty_description(\n+    empty_description_block_schema: schemas.core.BlockSchema,\n+):\n+    invoke_and_assert(\n+        [\"block\", \"type\", \"inspect\", \"empty-description\"],\n+        expected_code=0,\n+        expected_output_contains=[\"Empty Description\", \"Description\", \"message\"],\n+    )\n+\n+\n+def test_listing_block_types_with_empty_description(\n+    empty_description_block_schema: schemas.core.BlockSchema,\n+):\n+    invoke_and_assert(\n+        [\"block\", \"type\", \"ls\"],\n+        expected_code=0,\n+        expected_output_contains=[\"empty-description\"],\n+    )\n+\n+\n async def test_deleting_a_block_type(tmp_path, prefect_client):\n     test_file_path = tmp_path / \"test.py\"\n \n", "problem_statement": "prefect block type inspect crashes with IndexError on an empty description\n### Bug summary\n\n`prefect block type inspect` raises `IndexError` when the block type's description is an empty string. The guard only checks for `None`, and `\"\".splitlines()` is an empty list.\n\n```python\nblock_type.description.splitlines()[0].partition(\".\")[0]\nif block_type.description is not None\nelse \"\"\n```\n\nhttps://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/1d50f74d953cabd5ebed03f06d8e779d1c1fad19/src/prefect/cli/block.py#L79-L83\n\nIt should show an empty description, as it does for `None`.\n\nTo reproduce:\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom uuid import uuid4\nfrom prefect.cli.block import _display_block_type\nfrom prefect.client.schemas.objects import BlockType\n\nnow = datetime.now(timezone.utc)\n_display_block_type(BlockType(id=uuid4(), created=now, updated=now, name=\"demo\", slug=\"demo\", description=\"\"))\n# IndexError: list index out of range\n```\n\nWith `description=None` it works. I built the block type directly and did not register one on a server.\n\n`prefect block type ls` has the same guard, which I read but did not test:\nhttps://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/1d50f74d953cabd5ebed03f06d8e779d1c1fad19/src/prefect/cli/block.py#L522-L524\n\nChanging both guards to a truth test (`if block_type.description`) fixes it.\n\n### Version info\n\n```\nVersion:              3.9.1\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.12.12\nGit commit:           bd4807c3\nBuilt:                Fri, Oct 30, 2026 03:52 PM\nOS/Arch:              darwin/arm64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          ephemeral\nPydantic version:     2.14.2\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.50.4\n```\n\nThe code is the same on main at 1d50f74.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-04T21:01:07Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23757, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23757", "issue_numbers": ["23754"], "base_commit": "4bf151ad49b564d2ac2b008c78642d7207a2dd64", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/server/events/models/automation_held_events.py b/src/prefect/server/events/models/automation_held_events.py\nindex d09a987dc0bf..8b536bfb5232 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/server/events/models/automation_held_events.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/server/events/models/automation_held_events.py\n@@ -229,9 +229,16 @@ async def sweep_held_events(\n         db.AutomationBucket.bucketing_key == db.AutomationHeldEvent.bucketing_key,\n         db.AutomationBucket.end > now,\n     )\n-    await session.execute(\n-        sa.delete(db.AutomationHeldEvent).where(\n+    # Waiting on a row another sweep or `hold_event` has locked can deadlock, so\n+    # the sweep skips it and a later sweep deletes it.\n+    expired = (\n+        sa.select(db.AutomationHeldEvent.id)\n+        .where(\n             db.AutomationHeldEvent.updated < now - HELD_EXPECT_EVENTS_RETENTION,\n             ~open_bucket,\n         )\n+        .with_for_update(skip_locked=True)\n+    )\n+    await session.execute(\n+        sa.delete(db.AutomationHeldEvent).where(db.AutomationHeldEvent.id.in_(expired))\n     )\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/events/server/models/test_automation_held_events.py b/tests/events/server/models/test_automation_held_events.py\nindex 9c047722341d..3d8b70bf0f72 100644\n--- a/tests/events/server/models/test_automation_held_events.py\n+++ b/tests/events/server/models/test_automation_held_events.py\n@@ -1,5 +1,6 @@\n \"\"\"Held `expect` events stay bounded in count and lifetime, whatever the volume.\"\"\"\n \n+import asyncio\n from collections.abc import Callable, Iterable\n from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone\n from uuid import UUID, uuid4\n@@ -333,6 +334,41 @@ async def test_held_events_outlast_the_retention_while_their_bucket_is_open(\n     assert bool(await held_occurrences(db, automations_session)) is kept\n \n \n+async def test_sweep_skips_held_events_locked_by_another_transaction(\n+    db: PrefectDBInterface,\n+    automations_session: AsyncSession,\n+    trigger: EventTrigger,\n+):\n+    \"\"\"The sweep skips a held event another transaction has locked instead of\n+    waiting on it, and a later sweep deletes it.\"\"\"\n+    if db.dialect.name != \"postgresql\":\n+        pytest.skip(\"Row-level locking is PostgreSQL-only\")\n+\n+    locked, unlocked = walked(now(\"UTC\")), walked(now(\"UTC\"))\n+    await automation_held_events.hold_event(automations_session, trigger, KEY, locked)\n+    await automation_held_events.hold_event(\n+        automations_session, trigger, (\"groundhog\",), unlocked\n+    )\n+    await automations_session.commit()\n+    swept_at = now(\"UTC\") + HELD_EXPECT_EVENTS_RETENTION + timedelta(seconds=1)\n+\n+    async def sweep() -> None:\n+        async with db.session_context(begin_transaction=True) as session:\n+            await automation_held_events.sweep_held_events(session, swept_at)\n+\n+    async with db.session_context(begin_transaction=True) as other:\n+        await other.execute(\n+            sa.select(db.AutomationHeldEvent)\n+            .where(db.AutomationHeldEvent.event_id == locked.id)\n+            .with_for_update()\n+        )\n+        await asyncio.wait_for(sweep(), timeout=5)\n+\n+    assert await held_event_ids(db, automations_session) == {locked.id}\n+    await sweep()\n+    assert await held_event_ids(db, automations_session) == set()\n+\n+\n LONG_WITHIN = timedelta(hours=1)\n \n \n", "problem_statement": "`sweep_held_events` deadlocks with multiple triggers replicas\n### Bug summary\n\nEvery triggers replica runs `sweep_held_events` every 5 seconds. It deletes all expired held events in one `DELETE`, locking rows in no fixed order. When it overlaps another replica's sweep or `hold_event`, PostgreSQL can deadlock:\n\n```\nError sweeping held events\nasyncpg.exceptions.DeadlockDetectedError: deadlock detected\n[SQL: DELETE FROM automation_held_event WHERE automation_held_event.updated < $1 AND NOT (EXISTS (SELECT * FROM automation_bucket WHERE ...)) RETURNING automation_held_event.id]\n```\n\nThe next sweep retries, so nothing breaks, but it logs an error.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nSkip rows another transaction has locked:\n\n```python\ndue = (\n    sa.select(db.AutomationHeldEvent.id)\n    .where(db.AutomationHeldEvent.updated < now - HELD_EXPECT_EVENTS_RETENTION, ~open_bucket)\n    .with_for_update(skip_locked=True)\n)\nawait session.execute(\n    sa.delete(db.AutomationHeldEvent).where(db.AutomationHeldEvent.id.in_(due))\n)\n```\n\n### Version info\n\nPrefect 3.9.1 and `main`, PostgreSQL.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-04T19:51:45Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23756, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23756", "issue_numbers": ["23755"], "base_commit": "4bf151ad49b564d2ac2b008c78642d7207a2dd64", "patch": "diff --git a/src/integrations/prefect-azure/prefect_azure/workers/container_instance.py b/src/integrations/prefect-azure/prefect_azure/workers/container_instance.py\nindex edd6ec66289f..fce22e4fa3d8 100644\n--- a/src/integrations/prefect-azure/prefect_azure/workers/container_instance.py\n+++ b/src/integrations/prefect-azure/prefect_azure/workers/container_instance.py\n@@ -651,6 +651,10 @@ def _wait_for_task_container_start(\n         \"\"\"\n         Wait for the result of group and container creation.\n \n+        The container is considered started as soon as either the ARM deployment\n+        succeeds or the container group reports that it has been provisioned or\n+        that its container is running, whichever is observed first.\n+\n         Args:\n             creation_status_poller: Poller returned by the Azure SDK.\n \n@@ -666,17 +670,32 @@ def _wait_for_task_container_start(\n         timeout = configuration.task_start_timeout_seconds\n \n         while not creation_status_poller.done():\n+            container_group = self._get_container_group_if_started(\n+                client, configuration, container_group_name\n+            )\n+            if container_group is not None:\n+                return container_group\n+\n             elapsed_time = time.time() - t0\n \n             if timeout and elapsed_time > timeout:\n                 raise RuntimeError(\n                     (\n-                        f\"Timed out after {elapsed_time}s while watching waiting for \"\n+                        f\"Timed out after {elapsed_time}s while waiting for \"\n                         \"container start.\"\n                     )\n                 )\n             time.sleep(configuration.task_watch_poll_interval)\n \n+        # Check the container group once more before trusting the deployment\n+        # result, so that a deployment failure (e.g. in another resource of a\n+        # custom ARM template) doesn't cause a running container to be deleted.\n+        container_group = self._get_container_group_if_started(\n+            client, configuration, container_group_name\n+        )\n+        if container_group is not None:\n+            return container_group\n+\n         deployment = creation_status_poller.result()\n \n         provisioning_succeeded = (\n@@ -691,6 +710,38 @@ def _wait_for_task_container_start(\n         else:\n             return None\n \n+    def _get_container_group_if_started(\n+        self,\n+        client: ContainerInstanceManagementClient,\n+        configuration: AzureContainerJobConfiguration,\n+        container_group_name: str,\n+    ) -> Optional[ContainerGroup]:\n+        \"\"\"\n+        Reads the container group directly from ACI and returns it if it has\n+        been provisioned or its container has started, independently of the\n+        state of the ARM deployment that created it.\n+\n+        Returns:\n+            The `ContainerGroup` if it has started, otherwise None.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        try:\n+            container_group = self._get_container_group(\n+                client, configuration.resource_group_name, container_group_name\n+            )\n+        except ResourceNotFoundError:\n+            # The ARM deployment has not created the container group yet.\n+            return None\n+        except HttpResponseError as exc:\n+            self._logger.warning(\n+                f\"{self._log_prefix}: Unable to read the status of container group \"\n+                f\"{container_group_name!r} while waiting for it to start: {exc}\"\n+            )\n+            return None\n+\n+        if self._provisioning_succeeded(container_group):\n+            return container_group\n+        return None\n+\n     async def _provision_container_group(\n         self,\n         aci_client: ContainerInstanceManagementClient,\n@@ -721,6 +772,7 @@ async def _provision_container_group(\n             resource_group_name=configuration.resource_group_name,\n             deployment_name=f\"prefect-{container_group_name}\",\n             parameters=deployment,\n+            polling_interval=configuration.task_watch_poll_interval,\n         )\n \n         created_container_group = await run_sync_in_worker_thread(\n@@ -1020,19 +1072,34 @@ def _provisioning_succeeded(container_group: Union[ContainerGroup, None]) -> boo\n         \"\"\"\n         Determines whether ACI container group provisioning was successful.\n \n+        A container group whose container is already running (or has already\n+        run to completion) is treated as successfully provisioned, even if\n+        the group's provisioning state has not yet been updated to reflect it.\n+\n         Args:\n             container_group: a container group returned by the Azure SDK.\n \n         Returns:\n             True if provisioning was successful, False otherwise.\n         \"\"\"\n-        if not container_group:\n+        if (\n+            not container_group\n+            or not container_group.containers\n+            or len(container_group.containers) != 1\n+        ):\n             return False\n \n-        return (\n+        if (\n             container_group.provisioning_state\n             == ContainerGroupProvisioningState.SUCCEEDED\n-            and len(container_group.containers) == 1\n+        ):\n+            return True\n+\n+        instance_view = container_group.containers[0].instance_view\n+        current_state = instance_view.current_state if instance_view else None\n+        return current_state is not None and current_state.state in (\n+            ContainerRunState.RUNNING,\n+            ContainerRunState.TERMINATED,\n         )\n \n     @staticmethod\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/src/integrations/prefect-azure/tests/test_aci_worker.py b/src/integrations/prefect-azure/tests/test_aci_worker.py\nindex 5022840820dd..fe6df4208959 100644\n--- a/src/integrations/prefect-azure/tests/test_aci_worker.py\n+++ b/src/integrations/prefect-azure/tests/test_aci_worker.py\n@@ -329,6 +329,9 @@ async def test_worker_container_client_creation(\n     # test the call to the client constructors to ensure the block is calling them\n     # with the correct information.\n     mock_container_client_constructor = Mock()\n+    mock_container_client_constructor.return_value.container_groups.get.side_effect = (\n+        ResourceNotFoundError(message=\"not found\")\n+    )\n     monkeypatch.setattr(\n         prefect_azure.credentials,\n         \"ContainerInstanceManagementClient\",\n@@ -385,6 +388,9 @@ async def test_worker_credentials_are_used(\n     monkeypatch.setattr(\n         prefect_azure.credentials, \"ClientSecretCredential\", mock_credential\n     )\n+    mock_aci_client.container_groups.get.side_effect = ResourceNotFoundError(\n+        message=\"not found\"\n+    )\n \n     async with AzureContainerWorker(work_pool_name=\"test_pool\") as aci_worker:\n         # Using mock Azure clients to avoid making real calls to Azure\n@@ -467,6 +473,9 @@ async def test_worker_uses_entrypoint_correctly_in_template(\n ):\n     mock_deployment_call = Mock()\n     mock_resource_client.deployments.begin_create_or_update = mock_deployment_call\n+    mock_aci_client.container_groups.get.side_effect = ResourceNotFoundError(\n+        message=\"not found\"\n+    )\n \n     job_overrides = {\n         \"entrypoint\": entrypoint,\n@@ -499,14 +508,16 @@ async def test_delete_after_group_creation_failure(\n     mock_prefect_client,\n ):\n     # if provisioning failed, the container group should be deleted\n-    mock_container_group = Mock()\n-    mock_container_group.provisioning_state.return_value = (\n-        ContainerGroupProvisioningState.FAILED\n+    failed_container_group = create_mock_container_group(\n+        state=\"Waiting\", exit_code=None\n     )\n+    failed_container_group.provisioning_state = ContainerGroupProvisioningState.FAILED\n \n     async with AzureContainerWorker(work_pool_name=\"test_pool\") as aci_worker:\n         monkeypatch.setattr(\n-            aci_worker, \"_wait_for_task_container_start\", mock_container_group\n+            aci_worker,\n+            \"_wait_for_task_container_start\",\n+            Mock(return_value=failed_container_group),\n         )\n \n         # We expect the worker to raise an exception when creating the container group\n@@ -650,6 +661,13 @@ async def test_provisioning_timeout_throws_exception(\n     mock_resource_client.deployments.begin_create_or_update.side_effect = (\n         lambda *args, **kwargs: mock_poller\n     )\n+    # the container group exists but its container never leaves `Waiting`\n+    waiting_container_group = create_mock_container_group(\n+        state=\"Waiting\", exit_code=None\n+    )\n+    waiting_container_group.provisioning_state = \"Creating\"\n+    mock_aci_client.container_groups.get.side_effect = None\n+    mock_aci_client.container_groups.get.return_value = waiting_container_group\n \n     # avoid delaying test runs\n     job_configuration.task_watch_poll_interval = 0.09\n@@ -661,6 +679,171 @@ async def test_provisioning_timeout_throws_exception(\n         with pytest.raises(RuntimeError, match=\"Timed out after\"):\n             await aci_worker.run(worker_flow_run, job_configuration)\n \n+    mock_aci_client.container_groups.begin_delete.assert_called_once()\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+    \"provisioning_state, container_state\",\n+    [\n+        # the container is running while the ARM deployment is still in progress\n+        (\"Creating\", \"Running\"),\n+        # the container group has been provisioned before the ARM deployment\n+        # reports success\n+        (ContainerGroupProvisioningState.SUCCEEDED, \"Waiting\"),\n+    ],\n+)\n+async def test_container_start_detected_before_deployment_completes(\n+    mock_prefect_client,\n+    worker_flow_run,\n+    job_configuration,\n+    mock_aci_client,\n+    mock_resource_client,\n+    completed_worker_container_group,\n+    provisioning_state,\n+    container_state,\n+):\n+    # the ARM deployment never completes, so the worker must detect the start\n+    # by reading the container group directly\n+    mock_poller = Mock()\n+    mock_poller.done.return_value = False\n+    mock_resource_client.deployments.begin_create_or_update.side_effect = (\n+        lambda *args, **kwargs: mock_poller\n+    )\n+\n+    started_container_group = create_mock_container_group(\n+        state=container_state, exit_code=None\n+    )\n+    started_container_group.provisioning_state = provisioning_state\n+    mock_aci_client.container_groups.get.side_effect = [\n+        ResourceNotFoundError(message=\"not created yet\"),\n+        started_container_group,\n+        completed_worker_container_group,\n+    ]\n+\n+    job_configuration.task_watch_poll_interval = 0.01\n+    job_configuration.task_start_timeout_seconds = 60\n+\n+    task_status = Mock(spec=TaskStatus)\n+    async with AzureContainerWorker(work_pool_name=\"test_pool\") as aci_worker:\n+        result = await aci_worker.run(\n+            worker_flow_run, job_configuration, task_status=task_status\n+        )\n+\n+    task_status.started.assert_called_once()\n+    assert result.status_code == 0\n+    mock_poller.result.assert_not_called()\n+    assert mock_aci_client.container_groups.get.call_count == 3\n+\n+\n+async def test_running_container_not_deleted_when_deployment_fails(\n+    mock_prefect_client,\n+    worker_flow_run,\n+    job_configuration,\n+    mock_aci_client,\n+    mock_resource_client,\n+    running_worker_container_group,\n+    completed_worker_container_group,\n+):\n+    # the ARM deployment reports failure (e.g. another resource in a custom\n+    # template failed), but the task container is already running\n+    mock_poller = Mock()\n+    mock_poller.done.return_value = True\n+    mock_poller.result.return_value.properties.provisioning_state = (\n+        ContainerGroupProvisioningState.FAILED\n+    )\n+    mock_resource_client.deployments.begin_create_or_update.side_effect = (\n+        lambda *args, **kwargs: mock_poller\n+    )\n+    running_worker_container_group.provisioning_state = \"Creating\"\n+    mock_aci_client.container_groups.get.side_effect = [\n+        running_worker_container_group,\n+        completed_worker_container_group,\n+    ]\n+\n+    async with AzureContainerWorker(work_pool_name=\"test_pool\") as aci_worker:\n+        result = await aci_worker.run(worker_flow_run, job_configuration)\n+\n+    assert result.status_code == 0\n+    mock_poller.result.assert_not_called()\n+\n+\n+async def test_container_start_wait_tolerates_status_read_errors(\n+    mock_prefect_client,\n+    worker_flow_run,\n+    job_configuration,\n+    mock_aci_client,\n+    mock_resource_client,\n+    running_worker_container_group,\n+    completed_worker_container_group,\n+):\n+    mock_poller = Mock()\n+    mock_poller.done.return_value = False\n+    mock_resource_client.deployments.begin_create_or_update.side_effect = (\n+        lambda *args, **kwargs: mock_poller\n+    )\n+    mock_aci_client.container_groups.get.side_effect = [\n+        HttpResponseError(message=\"forbidden\", response=Mock(status_code=403)),\n+        running_worker_container_group,\n+        completed_worker_container_group,\n+    ]\n+\n+    job_configuration.task_watch_poll_interval = 0.01\n+    job_configuration.task_start_timeout_seconds = 60\n+\n+    async with AzureContainerWorker(work_pool_name=\"test_pool\") as aci_worker:\n+        result = await aci_worker.run(worker_flow_run, job_configuration)\n+\n+    assert result.status_code == 0\n+\n+\n+async def test_deployment_polling_interval_matches_task_watch_poll_interval(\n+    mock_prefect_client,\n+    worker_flow_run,\n+    job_configuration,\n+    mock_aci_client,\n+    mock_resource_client,\n+    completed_worker_container_group,\n+    monkeypatch,\n+):\n+    job_configuration.task_watch_poll_interval = 0.5\n+\n+    async with AzureContainerWorker(work_pool_name=\"test_pool\") as aci_worker:\n+        monkeypatch.setattr(\n+            aci_worker,\n+            \"_wait_for_task_container_start\",\n+            Mock(return_value=completed_worker_container_group),\n+        )\n+        await aci_worker.run(worker_flow_run, job_configuration)\n+\n+    call_kwargs = mock_resource_client.deployments.begin_create_or_update.call_args\n+    assert call_kwargs.kwargs[\"polling_interval\"] == 0.5\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+    \"provisioning_state, container_state, expected\",\n+    [\n+        (ContainerGroupProvisioningState.SUCCEEDED, \"Running\", True),\n+        (ContainerGroupProvisioningState.SUCCEEDED, \"Waiting\", True),\n+        (\"Creating\", \"Running\", True),\n+        (\"Creating\", \"Terminated\", True),\n+        (\"Creating\", \"Waiting\", False),\n+        (ContainerGroupProvisioningState.FAILED, \"Waiting\", False),\n+    ],\n+)\n+def test_provisioning_succeeded(provisioning_state, container_state, expected):\n+    container_group = create_mock_container_group(state=container_state, exit_code=None)\n+    container_group.provisioning_state = provisioning_state\n+\n+    assert AzureContainerWorker._provisioning_succeeded(container_group) is expected\n+\n+\n+def test_provisioning_succeeded_without_instance_view():\n+    container_group = create_mock_container_group(state=\"Waiting\", exit_code=None)\n+    container_group.provisioning_state = \"Creating\"\n+    container_group.containers[0].instance_view = None\n+\n+    assert AzureContainerWorker._provisioning_succeeded(container_group) is False\n+\n \n async def test_watch_for_container_termination(\n     mock_prefect_client,\n@@ -979,6 +1162,9 @@ async def test_provisioning_container_group(\n     mock_deployments.begin_create_or_update = mock_provisioning_call\n \n     monkeypatch.setattr(mock_resource_client, \"deployments\", mock_deployments)\n+    mock_aci_client.container_groups.get.side_effect = ResourceNotFoundError(\n+        message=\"not found\"\n+    )\n \n     async with AzureContainerWorker(work_pool_name=\"test_pool\") as aci_worker:\n         # We want to ensure that the provisioning call is made; we don't care about\n", "problem_statement": "ACI worker: start timeout is measured on the ARM LRO poller (30 s cadence) and deletes container groups that are already running\n### Bug summary\n\n### Bug summary\n\n`AzureContainerWorker._wait_for_task_container_start` decides that a container failed to start by\nwatching only the ARM deployment `LROPoller` returned by `resource_client.deployments.begin_create_or_update`.\nTwo consequences, both observed in production on Azure Container Instances (eastus2) over Oct 25 to Nov 4, 2026,\non a pool creating ~500 container groups per day:\n\n1. **The timeout fires against a 30-second poller.** `begin_create_or_update` is called without a polling\n   interval, so the Azure SDK polls ARM every 30 s (visible in the Activity Log as `operationStatuses/read`\n   every 30 s). The worker checks `creation_status_poller.done()` every `task_watch_poll_interval` (5 s)\n   and raises at exactly `task_start_timeout_seconds`. Up to 30 s of the budget is lost to poller\n   granularity. In 5 runs the ARM deployment had already reached `Succeeded` 1 to 19 seconds before the\n   worker raised and deleted the group:\n\n   | ARM deployment Succeeded | worker `containerGroups/delete` | margin |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | 12:04:37 | 12:04:56 | 19 s |\n   | 14:24:40 | 14:24:53 | 13 s |\n   | 22:10:37 | 22:10:40 | 3 s |\n   | 04:06:46.9 | 04:06:48.3 | 1.4 s |\n   | 00:34:47 (container group), 00:34:56 (deployment) | 00:34:55 | 7 s |\n\n2. **A running container is killed.** On timeout, `run()`'s `finally` branch calls\n   `_wait_for_container_group_deletion` without reading the container group's instance view or the flow\n   run state. In 2 runs the container had been `Running` for 2 min and 3.5 min (flow run in `Running`,\n   logs flowing, dbt executing) while the ARM operation stayed `Running` on Azure's side; the worker\n   deleted the group and reported the flow run as\n   `Crashed: Failed to submit flow run to infrastructure: RuntimeError: Timed out after 300.0s while watching waiting for container start.`\n   Example timeline (UTC): `containerGroups/write` Started 02:36:40 \u2192 ACI event `Started container`\n   02:37:53 \u2192 flow run Running 02:38:07 \u2192 ARM `operationStatuses` still `Running` at 02:41:12 \u2192\n   worker delete 02:41:40 \u2192 ARM write `Canceled` 02:41:49.\n\nBecause the flow run was already `Running`, the run is not retried by anything that keys on\n`run_count == 0`, and the message blames submission although the infrastructure was up.\n\nThe root trigger is slow ACI provisioning (image pulls of 3.5 to 4 min), but the worker turns a\nslow start into a lost run. Raising `task_start_timeout_seconds` only moves the race.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n- The start wait should treat the container being up as \"started\": poll the container group's\n  `instance_view` (or the flow run state) in the loop, and stop waiting once the container is `Running`,\n  regardless of the ARM LRO.\n- The poller's cadence should match the worker's: pass `polling_interval=configuration.task_watch_poll_interval`\n  (or an explicit `ARMPolling`) to `begin_create_or_update`, or drop the LRO and poll the group directly.\n- On timeout, do not delete a container group whose container is `Running`; hand it to\n  `_watch_task_and_get_exit_code` instead (or at minimum make deletion conditional on the container not\n  having started). This matches the Kubernetes worker, where `pod_watch_timeout_seconds` only covers the\n  pod reaching `Running`.\n\n### Reproduction\n\nHard to force on demand because it depends on ACI provisioning time. Deterministic variant: set\n`task_start_timeout_seconds` to a value a few seconds below the typical provisioning time of the region\n(for us ~90 s is normal, so set 85) and run a long flow; the worker deletes groups whose container has\nalready started and whose ARM deployment succeeds within the next poll interval.\n\nCode (unchanged on `main` as of Nov 4, 2026, `src/integrations/prefect-azure/prefect_azure/workers/container_instance.py`):\n\n```python\nwhile not creation_status_poller.done():\n    elapsed_time = time.time() - t0\n    if timeout and elapsed_time > timeout:\n        raise RuntimeError(\n            f\"Timed out after {elapsed_time}s while watching waiting for container start.\"\n        )\n    time.sleep(configuration.task_watch_poll_interval)\n\ncreation_status_poller = await run_sync_in_worker_thread(\n    resource_client.deployments.begin_create_or_update,\n    resource_group_name=configuration.resource_group_name,\n    deployment_name=f\"prefect-{container_group_name}\",\n    parameters=deployment,\n)\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nWorker runs in a self-built image (Prefect 3.8.4 OSS fork, Python 3.12, self-hosted server). Pinned by\nthe image's uv.lock:\n\n- prefect 3.8.4 (fork of PrefectHQ/prefect 3.8.4; `prefect_azure` is the unmodified PyPI package)\n- prefect-azure 0.4.11 \u2014 `prefect_azure/workers/container_instance.py` is identical in 0.4.11, 0.4.13 and current `main`\n- azure-core 1.38.0\n- azure-mgmt-resource 24.0.0\n- azure-mgmt-containerinstance 10.1.0\n- azure-mgmt-core 1.6.0\n- azure-identity 1.25.1\n- httpx 0.28.1, pydantic 2.13.4\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\nAdditional context\n\n- Observed on 8 runs out of 222 start timeouts in 11 days; the other timeouts are genuine ACI placement\n  failures (Failed to pull image ... CriContainerActivator is getting initialized and not ready for use)\n  and are being raised with Microsoft separately.\n- Evidence comes from the Azure Activity Log of the resource group (deployments/write,\n  containerGroups/write, operationStatuses/read, containerGroups/delete with timestamps) and from\n  ContainerEvent_CL in Log Analytics (Started container events).\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-04T19:44:01Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23749, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23749", "issue_numbers": ["23748"], "base_commit": "60d9ea54535ee10be2e7481fd1d930815c87b649", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/task_runners.py b/src/prefect/task_runners.py\nindex 8f340f25d3ff..6fc3d5c05c86 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/task_runners.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/task_runners.py\n@@ -387,7 +387,7 @@ def submit(\n \n         self._warn_if_nested_submit_would_deadlock(task)\n \n-        from prefect.context import FlowRunContext\n+        from prefect.context import FlowRunContext, SyncClientContext\n         from prefect.task_engine import run_task_async, run_task_sync\n \n         task_run_id = uuid7()\n@@ -427,6 +427,15 @@ def submit(\n                 run_task_sync,\n                 **submit_kwargs,\n             )\n+\n+        # The submitted task runs with a copy of this context and reuses its sync\n+        # client, which may be owned by a sync task run that returns (and closes\n+        # the client) before the submitted task finishes, e.g. a sync task in an\n+        # async flow. Keep the client open until the submitted task is done.\n+        if (client_ctx := SyncClientContext.get()) and not client_ctx.client._closed:\n+            sync_client = client_ctx.client.__enter__()\n+            future.add_done_callback(lambda _: sync_client.__exit__(None, None, None))\n+\n         with self._submission_lock:\n             self._active_submission_count += 1\n         future.add_done_callback(self._on_executor_future_done)\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/test_task_runners.py b/tests/test_task_runners.py\nindex bd1aa8b68b8b..a239dfe86e9f 100644\n--- a/tests/test_task_runners.py\n+++ b/tests/test_task_runners.py\n@@ -8,8 +8,8 @@\n \n import pytest\n \n-from prefect.client.orchestration import PrefectClient\n-from prefect.context import TagsContext, tags\n+from prefect.client.orchestration import PrefectClient, get_client\n+from prefect.context import SyncClientContext, TagsContext, tags\n from prefect.events import emit_event\n from prefect.events.schemas.events import Event\n from prefect.filesystems import LocalFileSystem\n@@ -300,6 +300,55 @@ def test_flow():\n \n         assert test_flow().result() == 0\n \n+    def test_submitted_task_keeps_submitting_context_sync_client_open(self):\n+        release = threading.Event()\n+\n+        @task\n+        def uses_client() -> int:\n+            assert release.wait(timeout=10)\n+            return get_client(sync_client=True).hello().status_code\n+\n+        with ThreadPoolTaskRunner() as runner:\n+            with SyncClientContext() as client_ctx:\n+                future = runner.submit(uses_client, {})\n+            release.set()\n+            assert future.result() == 200\n+\n+        assert client_ctx.client._closed\n+\n+    async def test_tasks_submitted_from_sync_task_in_async_flow_outlive_parent(\n+        self,\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        Regression test for https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/issues/23748.\n+\n+        A sync task in an async flow runs with its own sync client, which is\n+        closed when the task returns. Tasks it submitted must still be able to\n+        use that client after the parent has returned.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        parent_returned = threading.Event()\n+\n+        @task\n+        def upstream() -> int:\n+            assert parent_returned.wait(timeout=10)\n+            return 2\n+\n+        @task\n+        def downstream(x: int) -> int:\n+            return x + 2\n+\n+        @task\n+        def parent() -> PrefectFuture[int]:\n+            return downstream.submit(x=upstream.submit())\n+\n+        @flow(task_runner=ThreadPoolTaskRunner(max_workers=1))\n+        async def test_flow() -> int:\n+            future = parent()\n+            parent_returned.set()\n+            return future.result()\n+\n+        assert await test_flow() == 4\n+\n     def test_warns_on_nested_submit_when_pool_is_saturated(\n         self, caplog: pytest.LogCaptureFixture\n     ):\n", "problem_statement": "Tasks submitted from a sync task inside an async flow can fail with \"client has been closed\" after the parent task returns\n### Bug summary\n\nWhen a sync task runs inside an async flow and submits other tasks to the `ThreadPoolTaskRunner`, those child tasks can fail with `RuntimeError: Cannot send a request, as the client has been closed.` if they make an API call after the parent task has returned. It shows up most clearly when the parent task returns the futures of the tasks it submitted.\n\nThe same code works when the flow is sync, or when the parent task is async.\n\n#### Reproduction\n\n1. Save the following as `repro.py`. A single worker and a short sleep make the timing deterministic; without them it fails intermittently.\n\n   ```python\n   import asyncio\n   import time\n\n   from prefect import flow, task\n   from prefect.task_runners import ThreadPoolTaskRunner\n\n\n   @task\n   def add_two(x: int) -> int:\n       return x + 2\n\n\n   @task\n   def return_two() -> int:\n       time.sleep(2)\n       return 2\n\n\n   @task\n   def get_future():\n       two = return_two.submit()\n       return add_two.submit(x=two)\n\n\n   @flow(task_runner=ThreadPoolTaskRunner(max_workers=1))\n   async def async_flow():\n       return get_future().result()\n\n\n   @flow(task_runner=ThreadPoolTaskRunner(max_workers=1))\n   def sync_flow():\n       return get_future().result()\n\n\n   if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n       print(sync_flow())\n       print(asyncio.run(async_flow()))\n   ```\n\n2. Run `python repro.py`.\n\n3. `sync_flow` returns `4`. `async_flow` fails:\n\n   ```\n   RuntimeError: Cannot send a request, as the client has been closed.\n   ```\n\n   The failing call is `add_two` reading the state of its upstream future while its task run is being created (`_infer_parent_task_runs` \u2192 `PrefectConcurrentFuture.state` \u2192 `read_task_run`).\n\n#### Related variations\n\nAll in an async flow with the same setup, unless noted:\n\n| Variation | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Sync task called directly, returns a future of a dependent child (above) | Fails, `client has been closed` |\n| Sync task returns a future of an independent child that calls `Variable.get()` after a short delay | Fails, `The client cannot be started again after closing` |\n| Sync task submitted with `.submit()` from the flow, returns a future | Fails, `client has been closed` |\n| Sync task submits children and returns nothing | Parent completes; the dependent child never reports a run |\n| Plain function (not a task) submits and returns futures | Works |\n| Sync subflow submits and returns a future | Works |\n| Sync task returns a future in a **sync** flow | Works |\n| **Async** task returns a future in an async flow | Works |\n\n#### Additional context\n\nAs far as I can tell, this comes from the sync/async split. A sync task can't use the async client the async flow is running on, so it gets its own sync client for the duration of the task. Child tasks submitted from it run in the thread pool with a copy of the parent's context, which still points at that client, and the client is closed when the parent task returns. In a sync flow the client belongs to the flow run and outlives the task, so the same code works.\n\nThe resulting error is hard to trace back to the cause. It's intermittent without the forced timing above, it surfaces in a different task from the one that triggered it, and nothing indicates that the sync/async combination is the problem.\n\n### Version info\n\n```\nVersion:             3.9.0 (also reproduced on 3.7.8)\nPython version:      3.13\nTask runner:         ThreadPoolTaskRunner\nServer type:         ephemeral\n```\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-04T16:08:34Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23747, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23747", "issue_numbers": ["23745"], "base_commit": "60d9ea54535ee10be2e7481fd1d930815c87b649", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/docker/docker_image.py b/src/prefect/docker/docker_image.py\nindex 2c7da000baf6..6e93d5e227d2 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/docker/docker_image.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/docker/docker_image.py\n@@ -116,7 +116,7 @@ def _build_with_docker_py(\n         build_kwargs[\"stream_progress_to\"] = self.stream_progress_to\n \n         if self.dockerfile == \"auto\":\n-            with generate_default_dockerfile():\n+            with generate_default_dockerfile(context=Path(build_kwargs[\"context\"])):\n                 build_image(**build_kwargs)\n         else:\n             build_kwargs[\"dockerfile\"] = self.dockerfile\n@@ -134,7 +134,7 @@ def _build_with_buildx(\n         build_kwargs[\"stream_progress_to\"] = self.stream_progress_to\n \n         if self.dockerfile == \"auto\":\n-            with generate_default_dockerfile():\n+            with generate_default_dockerfile(context=Path(context)):\n                 buildx_build_image(\n                     context=context,\n                     push=push,\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/utilities/dockerutils.py b/src/prefect/utilities/dockerutils.py\nindex ee2b8f275822..138530faa98b 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/utilities/dockerutils.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/utilities/dockerutils.py\n@@ -661,8 +661,7 @@ def generate_default_dockerfile(context: Optional[Path] = None):\n         - context: The context to use for the Dockerfile. Defaults to\n             the current working directory.\n     \"\"\"\n-    if not context:\n-        context = Path.cwd()\n+    context = context.resolve() if context else Path.cwd()\n     lines: list[str] = []\n     base_image = get_prefect_image_name()\n     lines.append(f\"FROM {base_image}\")\n@@ -678,8 +677,7 @@ def generate_default_dockerfile(context: Optional[Path] = None):\n     temp_dockerfile = context / \"Dockerfile\"\n     if Path(temp_dockerfile).exists():\n         raise RuntimeError(\n-            \"Failed to generate Dockerfile. Dockerfile already exists in the\"\n-            \" current directory.\"\n+            f\"Failed to generate Dockerfile. Dockerfile already exists in {context}.\"\n         )\n \n     with Path(temp_dockerfile).open(\"w\") as f:\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/docker/test_docker_image.py b/tests/docker/test_docker_image.py\nindex ca65c14d622d..b3ba378d8c08 100644\n--- a/tests/docker/test_docker_image.py\n+++ b/tests/docker/test_docker_image.py\n@@ -2,6 +2,9 @@\n \n import io\n import sys\n+from collections.abc import Generator\n+from pathlib import Path\n+from typing import Any, Literal\n from unittest.mock import MagicMock, patch\n \n import pytest\n@@ -233,3 +236,173 @@ def test_docker_py_push_still_works(self, mock_docker_client: MagicMock):\n         image.push()\n \n         mock_client.api.push.assert_called_once()\n+\n+\n+class TestDockerImageAutoDockerfileContext:\n+    @pytest.fixture\n+    def project(self, tmp_path: Path, monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch) -> Path:\n+        monkeypatch.chdir(tmp_path)\n+        project = tmp_path / \"project\"\n+        project.mkdir()\n+        (project / \"requirements.txt\").write_text(\"httpx\\n\")\n+        return project\n+\n+    @pytest.fixture\n+    def mock_build_image(self) -> Generator[MagicMock, None, None]:\n+        with patch(\"prefect.docker.docker_image.build_image\") as mock:\n+            yield mock\n+\n+    @pytest.fixture\n+    def mock_buildx_build_image(self) -> Generator[MagicMock, None, None]:\n+        with (\n+            patch(\"prefect.docker.docker_image._ensure_buildx_extra\"),\n+            patch(\"prefect._internal.buildx.buildx_build_image\") as mock,\n+        ):\n+            yield mock\n+\n+    @staticmethod\n+    def _capture_dockerfile(mock: MagicMock) -> dict[str, Any]:\n+        captured: dict[str, Any] = {}\n+\n+        def inspect_build(**kwargs: Any) -> None:\n+            dockerfile = Path(kwargs[\"context\"]) / \"Dockerfile\"\n+            captured[\"context\"] = kwargs[\"context\"]\n+            captured[\"dockerfile\"] = (\n+                dockerfile.read_text() if dockerfile.exists() else None\n+            )\n+            captured[\"cwd_dockerfile_exists\"] = (Path.cwd() / \"Dockerfile\").exists()\n+\n+        mock.side_effect = inspect_build\n+        return captured\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"as_str\", [False, True])\n+    def test_docker_py_generates_dockerfile_in_context(\n+        self, project: Path, mock_build_image: MagicMock, as_str: bool\n+    ):\n+        captured = self._capture_dockerfile(mock_build_image)\n+        context = str(project) if as_str else project\n+\n+        DockerImage(name=\"test-image\", tag=\"latest\", context=context).build()\n+\n+        assert captured[\"context\"] == context\n+        assert captured[\"dockerfile\"] is not None\n+        assert (\n+            \"COPY requirements.txt /opt/prefect/project/requirements.txt\"\n+            in (captured[\"dockerfile\"])\n+        )\n+        assert \"WORKDIR /opt/prefect/project/\" in captured[\"dockerfile\"]\n+        assert not captured[\"cwd_dockerfile_exists\"]\n+        assert not (project / \"Dockerfile\").exists()\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"as_str\", [False, True])\n+    def test_buildx_generates_dockerfile_in_context(\n+        self, project: Path, mock_buildx_build_image: MagicMock, as_str: bool\n+    ):\n+        captured = self._capture_dockerfile(mock_buildx_build_image)\n+        context = str(project) if as_str else project\n+\n+        DockerImage(\n+            name=\"test-image\", tag=\"latest\", build_backend=\"buildx\", context=context\n+        ).build()\n+\n+        assert captured[\"context\"] == context\n+        assert captured[\"dockerfile\"] is not None\n+        assert (\n+            \"COPY requirements.txt /opt/prefect/project/requirements.txt\"\n+            in (captured[\"dockerfile\"])\n+        )\n+        assert \"WORKDIR /opt/prefect/project/\" in captured[\"dockerfile\"]\n+        assert not captured[\"cwd_dockerfile_exists\"]\n+        assert not (project / \"Dockerfile\").exists()\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"build_backend\", [\"docker-py\", \"buildx\"])\n+    def test_existing_dockerfile_in_cwd_does_not_block_context_build(\n+        self,\n+        project: Path,\n+        mock_build_image: MagicMock,\n+        mock_buildx_build_image: MagicMock,\n+        build_backend: Literal[\"docker-py\", \"buildx\"],\n+    ):\n+        cwd_dockerfile = Path.cwd() / \"Dockerfile\"\n+        cwd_dockerfile.write_text(\"FROM scratch\\n\")\n+\n+        DockerImage(\n+            name=\"test-image\",\n+            tag=\"latest\",\n+            build_backend=build_backend,\n+            context=project,\n+        ).build()\n+\n+        assert cwd_dockerfile.read_text() == \"FROM scratch\\n\"\n+        assert not (project / \"Dockerfile\").exists()\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"build_backend\", [\"docker-py\", \"buildx\"])\n+    def test_generated_dockerfile_removed_when_build_fails(\n+        self,\n+        project: Path,\n+        mock_build_image: MagicMock,\n+        mock_buildx_build_image: MagicMock,\n+        build_backend: Literal[\"docker-py\", \"buildx\"],\n+    ):\n+        mock_build_image.side_effect = RuntimeError(\"boom\")\n+        mock_buildx_build_image.side_effect = RuntimeError(\"boom\")\n+\n+        with pytest.raises(RuntimeError, match=\"boom\"):\n+            DockerImage(\n+                name=\"test-image\",\n+                tag=\"latest\",\n+                build_backend=build_backend,\n+                context=project,\n+            ).build()\n+\n+        assert not (project / \"Dockerfile\").exists()\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"build_backend\", [\"docker-py\", \"buildx\"])\n+    def test_defaults_to_cwd_without_context(\n+        self,\n+        project: Path,\n+        mock_build_image: MagicMock,\n+        mock_buildx_build_image: MagicMock,\n+        build_backend: Literal[\"docker-py\", \"buildx\"],\n+    ):\n+        mock = (\n+            mock_build_image\n+            if build_backend == \"docker-py\"\n+            else mock_buildx_build_image\n+        )\n+        captured = self._capture_dockerfile(mock)\n+        cwd = Path.cwd()\n+\n+        DockerImage(\n+            name=\"test-image\", tag=\"latest\", build_backend=build_backend\n+        ).build()\n+\n+        assert captured[\"context\"] == cwd\n+        assert captured[\"dockerfile\"] is not None\n+        assert f\"WORKDIR /opt/prefect/{cwd.name}/\" in captured[\"dockerfile\"]\n+        assert not (cwd / \"Dockerfile\").exists()\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"build_backend\", [\"docker-py\", \"buildx\"])\n+    def test_relative_context_uses_resolved_directory_name(\n+        self,\n+        project: Path,\n+        mock_build_image: MagicMock,\n+        mock_buildx_build_image: MagicMock,\n+        build_backend: Literal[\"docker-py\", \"buildx\"],\n+        monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch,\n+    ):\n+        monkeypatch.chdir(project)\n+        mock = (\n+            mock_build_image\n+            if build_backend == \"docker-py\"\n+            else mock_buildx_build_image\n+        )\n+        captured = self._capture_dockerfile(mock)\n+\n+        DockerImage(\n+            name=\"test-image\", tag=\"latest\", build_backend=build_backend, context=\".\"\n+        ).build()\n+\n+        assert captured[\"dockerfile\"] is not None\n+        assert \"WORKDIR /opt/prefect/project/\" in captured[\"dockerfile\"]\n+        assert not (project / \"Dockerfile\").exists()\n", "problem_statement": "Automatic Dockerfile ignores DockerImage build context\n### Bug summary\n\n`DockerImage(context=project_dir, dockerfile=\"auto\")` forwards the selected context to the builder but generates its temporary Dockerfile in the current working directory. The selected context therefore has no generated Dockerfile. An unrelated Dockerfile in the current directory instead causes a \"Dockerfile already exists\" error.\n\nThis remains after #17535 fixed context forwarding for #17534. Both current docker-py and buildx paths call `generate_default_dockerfile()` without the selected context.\n\n### Reproduction\n\nNo Docker daemon is needed. This checks the real generated file at the external build boundary:\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom tempfile import TemporaryDirectory\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\nfrom prefect.docker import DockerImage\n\noriginal_cwd = Path.cwd()\ntry:\n    with TemporaryDirectory() as directory:\n        root = Path(directory)\n        project = root / \"project\"\n        project.mkdir()\n        os.chdir(root)\n\n        def inspect_build(**kwargs):\n            print(\"context:\", Path(kwargs[\"context\"]).name)\n            print(\"Dockerfile in context:\", (project / \"Dockerfile\").exists())\n            print(\"Dockerfile in cwd:\", (root / \"Dockerfile\").exists())\n\n        with patch(\"prefect.docker.docker_image.build_image\", side_effect=inspect_build):\n            DockerImage(name=\"example\", tag=\"latest\", context=project).build()\nfinally:\n    os.chdir(original_cwd)\n```\n\nActual: `context: project`, `Dockerfile in context: False`, `Dockerfile in cwd: True`.\n\nExpected: the automatic Dockerfile should be created inside the selected context, use that directory's requirements and be removed after the build.\n\n### Version and scope\n\nReproduced on main `60d9ea54535ee10be2e7481fd1d930815c87b649`, Prefect `3.9.0+152.g60d9ea5453`, Python 3.12.14, Linux. Six focused regression cases fail on unchanged source across the two backends; both default-context controls pass. Filesystem and generation are real; only the external Docker build call is mocked. No live Docker build or deployment is claimed.\n\nI plan to pass the selected context into the existing generator in both backends and cover cleanup and default-context compatibility. Prepared and independently reproduced with Codex.\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-04T15:52:05Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23741, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23741", "issue_numbers": ["19090"], "base_commit": "60d9ea54535ee10be2e7481fd1d930815c87b649", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/server/api/server.py b/src/prefect/server/api/server.py\nindex 246fb4b89e81..03e6a9cfa93c 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/server/api/server.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/server/api/server.py\n@@ -367,9 +367,12 @@ def is_client_retryable_exception(exc: Exception) -> bool:\n         exc,\n         (\n             sqlalchemy.exc.DBAPIError,\n+            sqlalchemy.exc.TimeoutError,\n+            TimeoutError,\n             asyncpg.exceptions.QueryCanceledError,\n             asyncpg.exceptions.ConnectionDoesNotExistError,\n             asyncpg.exceptions.CannotConnectNowError,\n+            asyncpg.exceptions.PostgresConnectionError,\n             sqlalchemy.exc.InvalidRequestError,\n             sqlalchemy.orm.exc.DetachedInstanceError,\n         ),\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/server/api/test_server.py b/tests/server/api/test_server.py\nindex 815f6d5d6498..17ab03e74979 100644\n--- a/tests/server/api/test_server.py\n+++ b/tests/server/api/test_server.py\n@@ -360,6 +360,11 @@ async def raise_invalid_data_error():\n \n def test_non_data_database_errors_remain_retryable():\n     assert is_client_retryable_exception(_asyncpg_dbapi_error(\"57014\"))\n+    assert is_client_retryable_exception(TimeoutError(\"asyncpg timeout\"))\n+    assert is_client_retryable_exception(sa.exc.TimeoutError(\"queue pool timeout\"))\n+    assert is_client_retryable_exception(\n+        asyncpg.exceptions.PostgresConnectionError(\"connection failure\")\n+    )\n \n \n def _write_fake_ui_bundle(directory: pathlib.Path, marker: str) -> pathlib.Path:\ndiff --git a/tests/server/orchestration/test_rules.py b/tests/server/orchestration/test_rules.py\nindex a8a81d3f10bf..eaab46915084 100644\n--- a/tests/server/orchestration/test_rules.py\n+++ b/tests/server/orchestration/test_rules.py\n@@ -1603,6 +1603,34 @@ async def cleanup(self, initial_state, validated_state, context):\n             == \"Error validating state: RuntimeError('One time error!')\"\n         )\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"exc\",\n+        [\n+            TimeoutError(\"asyncpg query timeout\"),\n+            sqlalchemy.exc.TimeoutError(\"queue pool timeout\"),\n+        ],\n+    )\n+    async def test_context_state_validation_encounters_timeout_exception(\n+        self, session, run_type, initialize_orchestration, exc\n+    ):\n+        initial_state_type = states.StateType.PENDING\n+        proposed_state_type = states.StateType.RUNNING\n+        intended_transition = (initial_state_type, proposed_state_type)\n+\n+        ctx = await initialize_orchestration(session, run_type, *intended_transition)\n+\n+        object.__setattr__(\n+            ctx.session,\n+            \"flush\",\n+            AsyncMock(side_effect=exc),\n+        )\n+\n+        with pytest.raises((TimeoutError, sqlalchemy.exc.TimeoutError)):\n+            async with contextlib.AsyncExitStack():\n+                await ctx.validate_proposed_state()\n+\n+        assert ctx.proposed_state is None\n+\n     async def test_context_state_validation_encounters_sqlite_busy_exception(\n         self, session, run_type, initialize_orchestration\n     ):\n", "problem_statement": "flow run finishing with state running\n### Bug summary\n\nwe're running a large number of flow runs simultaneously in our OSS prefect infra (backed by postgres), and when DB load is high, we see runs finishing with the message \"Finished in state Running()\", and the flow gets stuck in the running state.\n\nAround the same time, we frequently see exception in our prefect server:\n```\n\"Encountered error during state validation\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/prefect/server/orchestration/rules.py\", line 274, in validate_proposed_state\n    await self._validate_proposed_state()\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/prefect/server/orchestration/rules.py\", line 329, in _validate_proposed_state\n    await self.session.flush()\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/ext/asyncio/session.py\", line 801, in flush\n    await greenlet_spawn(self.sync_session.flush, objects=objects)\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/util/_concurrency_py3k.py\", line 203, in greenlet_spawn\n    result = context.switch(value)\n             ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/orm/session.py\", line 4345, in flush\n    self._flush(objects)\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/orm/session.py\", line 4480, in _flush\n    with util.safe_reraise():\n         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/util/langhelpers.py\", line 224, in __exit__\n    raise exc_value.with_traceback(exc_tb)\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/orm/session.py\", line 4441, in _flush\n    flush_context.execute()\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/orm/unitofwork.py\", line 463, in execute\n    n.execute_aggregate(self, set_)\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/orm/unitofwork.py\", line 759, in execute_aggregate\n    persistence.save_obj(\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/orm/persistence.py\", line 85, in save_obj\n    _emit_update_statements(\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/orm/persistence.py\", line 885, in _emit_update_statements\n    c = connection.execute(\n        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/engine/base.py\", line 1419, in execute\n    return meth(\n           ^^^^^\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/sql/elements.py\", line 526, in _execute_on_connection\n    return connection._execute_clauseelement(\n           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/engine/base.py\", line 1641, in _execute_clauseelement\n    ret = self._execute_context(\n          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/engine/base.py\", line 1846, in _execute_context\n    return self._exec_single_context(\n           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/engine/base.py\", line 1986, in _exec_single_context\n    self._handle_dbapi_exception(\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/engine/base.py\", line 2358, in _handle_dbapi_exception\n    raise exc_info[1].with_traceback(exc_info[2])\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/engine/base.py\", line 1967, in _exec_single_context\n    self.dialect.do_execute(\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/engine/default.py\", line 951, in do_execute\n    cursor.execute(statement, parameters)\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/dialects/postgresql/asyncpg.py\", line 580, in execute\n    self._adapt_connection.await_(\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/util/_concurrency_py3k.py\", line 132, in await_only\n    return current.parent.switch(awaitable)  # type: ignore[no-any-return,attr-defined] # noqa: E501\n           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/util/_concurrency_py3k.py\", line 196, in greenlet_spawn\n    value = await result\n            ^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/dialects/postgresql/asyncpg.py\", line 558, in _prepare_and_execute\n    self._handle_exception(error)\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/dialects/postgresql/asyncpg.py\", line 508, in _handle_exception\n    self._adapt_connection._handle_exception(error)\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/dialects/postgresql/asyncpg.py\", line 794, in _handle_exception\n    raise error\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/sqlalchemy/dialects/postgresql/asyncpg.py\", line 545, in _prepare_and_execute\n    self._rows = deque(await prepared_stmt.fetch(*parameters))\n                       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/asyncpg/prepared_stmt.py\", line 176, in fetch\n    data = await self.__bind_execute(args, 0, timeout)\n           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/asyncpg/prepared_stmt.py\", line 267, in __bind_execute\n    data, status, _ = await self.__do_execute(\n                      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/asyncpg/prepared_stmt.py\", line 256, in __do_execute\n    return await executor(protocol)\n           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"asyncpg/protocol/protocol.pyx\", line 206, in bind_execute\nTimeoutError\"\n```\nfrom looking at `rules.py`, it looks like we swallow the exception on the server side, which means the client won't retry to submit a state change to the DB, and doesn't throw an error. It seems we should consider a `TimeoutError` as a retryable SQL error and accordingly return a 503 error\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:             3.4.13\nAPI version:         0.8.4\nPython version:      3.12.11\nGit commit:          2b0ea9be\nBuilt:               Thu, Aug 14, 2025 09:13 PM\nOS/Arch:             linux/aarch64\nProfile:             ephemeral\nServer type:         ephemeral\nPydantic version:    2.11.7\nServer:\n  Database:          postgresql\nIntegrations:\n  prefect-docker:    0.6.3\n  prefect-gcp:       0.6.6\n  prefect-kubernetes: 0.6.1\n  prefect-redis:     0.2.4\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-04T05:43:03Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23739, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23739", "issue_numbers": ["23592"], "base_commit": "60d9ea54535ee10be2e7481fd1d930815c87b649", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/task_engine.py b/src/prefect/task_engine.py\nindex 05f143ec7a6a..ce9cb7eb3165 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/task_engine.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/task_engine.py\n@@ -228,7 +228,21 @@ def _create_task_run_locally(\n \n \n class TaskRunTimeoutError(TimeoutError):\n-    \"\"\"Raised when a task run exceeds its timeout.\"\"\"\n+    \"\"\"Raised when a task exceeds its configured timeout.\"\"\"\n+\n+\n+class _GeneratorFinalizerError(Exception):\n+    \"\"\"Internal: a generator task's finalizer raised while the consumer closed it.\n+\n+    Wrapping the error lets the transaction contexts unwind with an exception\n+    (running rollback instead of committing a staged transaction) while the run\n+    contexts skip their retry handling; the wrapped error is unwrapped and\n+    raised to the consumer before the generator engine returns.\n+    \"\"\"\n+\n+    def __init__(self, cause: BaseException) -> None:\n+        super().__init__(cause)\n+        self.cause = cause\n \n \n @dataclass\n@@ -1058,6 +1072,10 @@ def run_context(self):\n                     yield self\n             except TimeoutError as exc:\n                 self.handle_timeout(exc)\n+            except _GeneratorFinalizerError:\n+                # A generator task's finalizer failed while the consumer closed\n+                # it; not a retryable task failure, so re-raise unchanged.\n+                raise\n             except Exception as exc:\n                 self.handle_exception(exc)\n \n@@ -1707,6 +1725,10 @@ async def run_context(self):\n                     yield self\n             except TimeoutError as exc:\n                 await self.handle_timeout(exc)\n+            except _GeneratorFinalizerError:\n+                # A generator task's finalizer failed while the consumer closed\n+                # it; not a retryable task failure, so re-raise unchanged.\n+                raise\n             except Exception as exc:\n                 await self.handle_exception(exc)\n \n@@ -1811,43 +1833,60 @@ def run_generator_task_sync(\n         context=context,\n     )\n \n-    with engine.start(task_run_id=task_run_id, dependencies=dependencies):\n-        while engine.is_running():\n-            engine.wait_until_ready()\n-            with (\n-                engine.asset_context(),\n-                engine.run_context(),\n-                engine.transaction_context() as txn,\n-            ):\n-                # TODO: generators should default to commit_mode=OFF\n-                # because they are dynamic by definition\n-                # for now we just prevent this branch explicitly\n-                if False and txn.is_committed():\n-                    txn.read()\n-                else:\n-                    call_args, call_kwargs = parameters_to_args_kwargs(\n-                        task.fn, engine.parameters or {}\n-                    )\n-                    gen = task.fn(*call_args, **call_kwargs)\n-                    try:\n-                        while True:\n-                            gen_result = next(gen)\n-                            # link the current state to the result for dependency tracking\n-                            #\n-                            # TODO: this could grow the task_run_result\n-                            # dictionary in an unbounded way, so finding a\n-                            # way to periodically clean it up (using\n-                            # weakrefs or similar) would be good\n-                            link_state_to_task_run_result(engine.state, gen_result)\n-                            raise_if_flow_run_suspension_requested()\n-                            yield gen_result\n-                    except StopIteration as exc:\n-                        engine.handle_success(exc.value, transaction=txn)\n-                    except GeneratorExit as exc:\n-                        engine.handle_success(None, transaction=txn)\n-                        gen.throw(exc)\n-            raise_if_flow_run_suspension_requested()\n-\n+    try:\n+        with engine.start(task_run_id=task_run_id, dependencies=dependencies):\n+            while engine.is_running():\n+                engine.wait_until_ready()\n+                with (\n+                    engine.asset_context(),\n+                    engine.run_context(),\n+                    engine.transaction_context() as txn,\n+                ):\n+                    # TODO: generators should default to commit_mode=OFF\n+                    # because they are dynamic by definition\n+                    # for now we just prevent this branch explicitly\n+                    if False and txn.is_committed():\n+                        txn.read()\n+                    else:\n+                        call_args, call_kwargs = parameters_to_args_kwargs(\n+                            task.fn, engine.parameters or {}\n+                        )\n+                        gen = task.fn(*call_args, **call_kwargs)\n+                        try:\n+                            while True:\n+                                gen_result = next(gen)\n+                                # link the current state to the result for dependency tracking\n+                                #\n+                                # TODO: this could grow the task_run_result\n+                                # dictionary in an unbounded way, so finding a\n+                                # way to periodically clean it up (using\n+                                # weakrefs or similar) would be good\n+                                link_state_to_task_run_result(engine.state, gen_result)\n+                                raise_if_flow_run_suspension_requested()\n+                                yield gen_result\n+                        except StopIteration as exc:\n+                            engine.handle_success(exc.value, transaction=txn)\n+                        except GeneratorExit as exc:\n+                            engine.handle_success(None, transaction=txn)\n+                            try:\n+                                gen.throw(exc)\n+                            except StopIteration:\n+                                # the generator suppressed GeneratorExit and\n+                                # returned; treat that as a successful close\n+                                pass\n+                            except Exception as close_exc:\n+                                raise _GeneratorFinalizerError(close_exc) from close_exc\n+                            else:\n+                                # the generator yielded while handling\n+                                # GeneratorExit; a plain generator's close\n+                                # rejects that\n+                                raise _GeneratorFinalizerError(\n+                                    RuntimeError(\"generator ignored GeneratorExit\")\n+                                )\n+                raise_if_flow_run_suspension_requested()\n+\n+    except _GeneratorFinalizerError as wrapper:\n+        raise wrapper.cause\n     return engine.result()\n \n \n@@ -1871,43 +1910,70 @@ async def run_generator_task_async(\n         context=context,\n     )\n \n-    async with engine.start(task_run_id=task_run_id, dependencies=dependencies):\n-        while engine.is_running():\n-            await engine.wait_until_ready()\n-            async with (\n-                engine.asset_context(),\n-                engine.run_context(),\n-                engine.transaction_context() as txn,\n-            ):\n-                # TODO: generators should default to commit_mode=OFF\n-                # because they are dynamic by definition\n-                # for now we just prevent this branch explicitly\n-                if False and txn.is_committed():\n-                    txn.read()\n-                else:\n-                    call_args, call_kwargs = parameters_to_args_kwargs(\n-                        task.fn, engine.parameters or {}\n-                    )\n-                    gen = task.fn(*call_args, **call_kwargs)\n-                    try:\n-                        while True:\n-                            # can't use anext in Python < 3.10\n-                            gen_result = await gen.__anext__()\n-                            # link the current state to the result for dependency tracking\n-                            #\n-                            # TODO: this could grow the task_run_result\n-                            # dictionary in an unbounded way, so finding a\n-                            # way to periodically clean it up (using\n-                            # weakrefs or similar) would be good\n-                            link_state_to_task_run_result(engine.state, gen_result)\n-                            raise_if_flow_run_suspension_requested()\n-                            yield gen_result\n-                    except (StopAsyncIteration, GeneratorExit) as exc:\n-                        await engine.handle_success(None, transaction=txn)\n-                        if isinstance(exc, GeneratorExit):\n-                            gen.throw(exc)\n-            raise_if_flow_run_suspension_requested()\n-\n+    try:\n+        async with engine.start(task_run_id=task_run_id, dependencies=dependencies):\n+            while engine.is_running():\n+                await engine.wait_until_ready()\n+                async with (\n+                    engine.asset_context(),\n+                    engine.run_context(),\n+                    engine.transaction_context() as txn,\n+                ):\n+                    # TODO: generators should default to commit_mode=OFF\n+                    # because they are dynamic by definition\n+                    # for now we just prevent this branch explicitly\n+                    if False and txn.is_committed():\n+                        txn.read()\n+                    else:\n+                        call_args, call_kwargs = parameters_to_args_kwargs(\n+                            task.fn, engine.parameters or {}\n+                        )\n+                        gen = task.fn(*call_args, **call_kwargs)\n+                        try:\n+                            while True:\n+                                # can't use anext in Python < 3.10\n+                                gen_result = await gen.__anext__()\n+                                # link the current state to the result for dependency tracking\n+                                #\n+                                # TODO: this could grow the task_run_result\n+                                # dictionary in an unbounded way, so finding a\n+                                # way to periodically clean it up (using\n+                                # weakrefs or similar) would be good\n+                                link_state_to_task_run_result(engine.state, gen_result)\n+                                raise_if_flow_run_suspension_requested()\n+                                yield gen_result\n+                        except (StopAsyncIteration, GeneratorExit) as exc:\n+                            await engine.handle_success(None, transaction=txn)\n+                            if isinstance(exc, GeneratorExit):\n+                                try:\n+                                    await gen.athrow(exc)\n+                                except StopAsyncIteration:\n+                                    # the generator suppressed GeneratorExit and\n+                                    # returned; treat that as a successful close\n+                                    pass\n+                                except Exception as close_exc:\n+                                    # The consumer is closing the generator and\n+                                    # its finalizer raised. Wrap the error so\n+                                    # the transaction rolls back and the run\n+                                    # contexts do not schedule a retry; the\n+                                    # consumer sees the original error from\n+                                    # aclose(), like a plain async generator.\n+                                    raise _GeneratorFinalizerError(close_exc) from (\n+                                        close_exc\n+                                    )\n+                                else:\n+                                    # the generator yielded while handling\n+                                    # GeneratorExit; a plain async generator's\n+                                    # aclose rejects that\n+                                    raise _GeneratorFinalizerError(\n+                                        RuntimeError(\n+                                            \"async generator ignored GeneratorExit\"\n+                                        )\n+                                    )\n+                raise_if_flow_run_suspension_requested()\n+\n+    except _GeneratorFinalizerError as wrapper:\n+        raise wrapper.cause\n     # async generators can't return, but we can raise failures here\n     if engine.state.is_failed():\n         await engine.result()\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/test_task_engine.py b/tests/test_task_engine.py\nindex 48f5fbb38b74..9e96ac0b8bd8 100644\n--- a/tests/test_task_engine.py\n+++ b/tests/test_task_engine.py\n@@ -3003,6 +3003,246 @@ def g():\n             pass\n         assert values == [1, 2]\n \n+    async def test_generator_finalizer_failure_on_close_does_not_restart(\n+        self, prefect_client: PrefectClient\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        A finalizer that raises while the consumer closes the generator must not\n+        restart the task body (regression test for #23592).\n+        \"\"\"\n+\n+        @task(retries=1)\n+        async def generator():\n+            starts.append(\"start\")\n+            try:\n+                yield 1\n+                yield 2\n+            finally:\n+                cleanups.append(TaskRunContext.get() is not None)\n+                raise ValueError(\"cleanup failed\")\n+\n+        starts = []\n+        cleanups = []\n+\n+        gen = generator()\n+        assert await anext(gen) == 1\n+        try:\n+            with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"^cleanup failed$\"):\n+                await gen.aclose()\n+        finally:\n+            # Disposal only; the observations above are from the first close.\n+            try:\n+                await gen.aclose()\n+            except (RuntimeError, StopAsyncIteration):\n+                pass\n+\n+        assert starts == [\"start\"]\n+        assert cleanups == [True]\n+\n+    async def test_generator_clean_close_with_retries_runs_cleanup_once(\n+        self, prefect_client: PrefectClient\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        Early closure with retries enabled runs successful cleanup exactly once\n+        without restarting the task body.\n+        \"\"\"\n+\n+        @task(retries=1)\n+        async def generator():\n+            starts.append(\"start\")\n+            try:\n+                yield 1\n+                yield 2\n+            finally:\n+                cleanups.append(TaskRunContext.get() is not None)\n+\n+        starts = []\n+        cleanups = []\n+\n+        gen = generator()\n+        assert await anext(gen) == 1\n+        await gen.aclose()\n+\n+        assert starts == [\"start\"]\n+        assert cleanups == [True]\n+\n+    async def test_generator_suppressed_exit_on_close_closes_cleanly(\n+        self, prefect_client: PrefectClient\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        A generator that suppresses GeneratorExit (e.g. `return` in its finally\n+        block) closes cleanly, like an undecorated async generator.\n+        \"\"\"\n+\n+        @task(retries=1)\n+        async def generator():\n+            starts.append(\"start\")\n+            try:\n+                yield 1\n+            except GeneratorExit:\n+                # suppress the close and fall off the end\n+                cleanups.append(TaskRunContext.get() is not None)\n+\n+        starts = []\n+        cleanups = []\n+\n+        gen = generator()\n+        assert await anext(gen) == 1\n+        await gen.aclose()\n+\n+        assert starts == [\"start\"]\n+        assert cleanups == [True]\n+\n+    async def test_sync_generator_finalizer_failure_on_close_does_not_restart(\n+        self, prefect_client: PrefectClient\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        Sync mirror of the finalizer-failure regression test for #23592.\n+        \"\"\"\n+\n+        @task(retries=1)\n+        def generator():\n+            starts.append(\"start\")\n+            try:\n+                yield 1\n+                yield 2\n+            finally:\n+                cleanups.append(TaskRunContext.get() is not None)\n+                raise ValueError(\"cleanup failed\")\n+\n+        starts = []\n+        cleanups = []\n+\n+        gen = generator()\n+        assert next(gen) == 1\n+        try:\n+            with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"^cleanup failed$\"):\n+                gen.close()\n+        finally:\n+            try:\n+                gen.close()\n+            except (RuntimeError, StopIteration):\n+                pass\n+\n+        assert starts == [\"start\"]\n+        assert cleanups == [True]\n+\n+    async def test_sync_generator_clean_close_with_retries_runs_cleanup_once(\n+        self, prefect_client: PrefectClient\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        Sync mirror of the clean early-close test.\n+        \"\"\"\n+\n+        @task(retries=1)\n+        def generator():\n+            starts.append(\"start\")\n+            try:\n+                yield 1\n+                yield 2\n+            finally:\n+                cleanups.append(TaskRunContext.get() is not None)\n+\n+        starts = []\n+        cleanups = []\n+\n+        gen = generator()\n+        assert next(gen) == 1\n+        gen.close()\n+\n+        assert starts == [\"start\"]\n+        assert cleanups == [True]\n+\n+    async def test_generator_yields_during_close_raises(\n+        self, prefect_client: PrefectClient\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        A generator that yields while handling GeneratorExit makes aclose raise\n+        RuntimeError, matching plain async generator semantics.\n+        \"\"\"\n+\n+        @task(retries=1)\n+        async def generator():\n+            starts.append(\"start\")\n+            try:\n+                yield 1\n+            finally:\n+                yield 99\n+\n+        starts = []\n+\n+        gen = generator()\n+        assert await anext(gen) == 1\n+        with pytest.raises(\n+            RuntimeError, match=\"^async generator ignored GeneratorExit$\"\n+        ):\n+            await gen.aclose()\n+\n+        assert starts == [\"start\"]\n+\n+    async def test_sync_generator_yields_during_close_raises(\n+        self, prefect_client: PrefectClient\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        Sync mirror: yielding while handling GeneratorExit makes close raise.\n+        \"\"\"\n+\n+        @task(retries=1)\n+        def generator():\n+            starts.append(\"start\")\n+            try:\n+                yield 1\n+            finally:\n+                yield 99\n+\n+        starts = []\n+\n+        gen = generator()\n+        assert next(gen) == 1\n+        with pytest.raises(RuntimeError, match=\"^generator ignored GeneratorExit$\"):\n+            gen.close()\n+\n+        assert starts == [\"start\"]\n+\n+    async def test_generator_finalizer_failure_on_close_rolls_back_transaction(\n+        self, prefect_client: PrefectClient\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        A finalizer failure while closing the generator must roll the staged\n+        transaction back rather than committing it.\n+        \"\"\"\n+\n+        events = []\n+\n+        @task(retries=1)\n+        async def generator():\n+            events.append(\"start\")\n+            try:\n+                yield 1\n+            finally:\n+                raise ValueError(\"cleanup failed\")\n+\n+        @generator.on_commit\n+        def commit(txn):\n+            events.append(\"commit\")\n+\n+        @generator.on_rollback\n+        def rollback(txn):\n+            events.append(\"rollback\")\n+\n+        gen = generator()\n+        assert await anext(gen) == 1\n+        try:\n+            with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"^cleanup failed$\"):\n+                await gen.aclose()\n+        finally:\n+            try:\n+                await gen.aclose()\n+            except (RuntimeError, StopAsyncIteration):\n+                pass\n+\n+        assert events == [\"start\", \"rollback\"]\n+\n     def test_generators_can_be_yielded_without_being_consumed(self):\n         CONSUMED = []\n \n", "problem_statement": "Async generator task retries during early close when finalizer fails\n## Bug summary\n\nSeparate follow-up to the [maintainer request on #23375](https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/pull/23375#discussion_r4367696088), not a competing fix for that PR.\n\nWith an async-generator task, `retries=1`, and a finalizer that raises, closing the generator after its first item restarts the task body. The first `aclose()` raises `RuntimeError: async generator ignored GeneratorExit`. This can repeat side effects at the start of a task even though its consumer is explicitly closing it.\n\nI reproduced the following in the repository test harness on Python 3.12, against PR head `b0becfad0d429a5211586a3f21ae4aebb7b9a8a2`. Save this as a test under `tests/` so the repository's isolated API fixtures apply:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom prefect import task\nfrom prefect.client.orchestration import PrefectClient\nfrom prefect.context import TaskRunContext\n\nasync def test_task_finalizer_retry_on_close(prefect_client: PrefectClient):\n    starts = []\n    cleanups = []\n\n    @task(retries=1)\n    async def generator():\n        starts.append(\"start\")\n        try:\n            yield 1\n            yield 2\n        finally:\n            cleanups.append(TaskRunContext.get() is not None)\n            raise ValueError(\"cleanup failed\")\n\n    gen = generator()\n    assert await anext(gen) == 1\n    try:\n        with pytest.raises(RuntimeError, match=\"^async generator ignored GeneratorExit$\"):\n            await gen.aclose()\n        # Characterizes the bug, not the desired behavior:\n        assert starts == [\"start\", \"start\"]\n        assert cleanups == [True]\n    finally:\n        # Disposal only; observations above are from the first close.\n        try:\n            await gen.aclose()\n        except (RuntimeError, StopAsyncIteration):\n            pass\n```\n\nRelevant observed output before disposal:\n\n```text\nTask run failed with exception: ValueError('cleanup failed') - Retry 1/1 will start immediately\nRuntimeError: async generator ignored GeneratorExit\nstarts=['start', 'start']; cleanups=[True]\n```\n\nExpected scope: explicitly closing the task generator should not start its body again. I am not proposing transaction/rollback changes or choosing final-state semantics here.\n\n## Version info and verification boundaries\n\n- PR head: `b0becfad0d429a5211586a3f21ae4aebb7b9a8a2`; Linux, Python 3.12; repository SQLite/API fixtures.\n- Local characterization plus the two successful-cleanup retry controls: **3 passed**. The characterization intentionally expects the buggy outcome; this is not a passing fix or clean teardown. The subsequent disposal also emitted async-generator/context cleanup errors.\n- Substituting only `task_engine.py` from base `e9bc08239af12f2bbfccab7a8e15b9f0a0ec6865` also reproduced two starts and the same first-close RuntimeError. On base, the retry is triggered by the existing `AttributeError: 'async_generator' object has no attribute 'throw'`, and the first-close cleanup list is empty. This is not evidence that base executed the same finalizer path as the PR.\n- Current main `21a1f025480406046668edf22705234ce6b1c31f` has the same `run_generator_task_async` function as that tested base version; I have not run the full current-main checkout.\n- No production fix, full suite, remote CI for new tests, final task-state guarantee, or transaction-semantics claim.\n\nAI assistance: Codex prepared and executed these reproductions and independently reviewed the characterization and evidence. No production code change is proposed in this report.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-04T03:39:14Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23738, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23738", "issue_numbers": ["23737"], "base_commit": "60d9ea54535ee10be2e7481fd1d930815c87b649", "patch": "diff --git a/ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-string-format-datetime.tsx b/ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-string-format-datetime.tsx\nindex 18b0946e117b..f24e2ab2e9b7 100644\n--- a/ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-string-format-datetime.tsx\n+++ b/ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-string-format-datetime.tsx\n@@ -3,8 +3,9 @@ import { formatInTimeZone } from \"date-fns-tz\";\n import { useState } from \"react\";\n import { DateTimePicker } from \"@/components/ui/date-time-picker\";\n import { TimezoneSelect } from \"@/components/ui/timezone-select\";\n+import { getLocalTimezone } from \"@/utils\";\n \n-const localTimezone = Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone;\n+const localTimezone = getLocalTimezone();\n \n type SchemaFormInputStringFormatDateTimeProps = {\n \tvalue: string | undefined;\ndiff --git a/ui-v2/src/components/ui/timezone-select/timezone-select.tsx b/ui-v2/src/components/ui/timezone-select/timezone-select.tsx\nindex 9d0d17472207..d4a98c9a2429 100644\n--- a/ui-v2/src/components/ui/timezone-select/timezone-select.tsx\n+++ b/ui-v2/src/components/ui/timezone-select/timezone-select.tsx\n@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@ import {\n \tDropdownMenuLabel,\n \tDropdownMenuSeparator,\n } from \"@/components/ui/dropdown-menu\";\n+import { getLocalTimezone } from \"@/utils\";\n \n function getTimezoneLabel(value: string): string {\n \treturn value.replaceAll(\"/\", \" / \").replaceAll(\"_\", \" \");\n@@ -49,7 +50,7 @@ function normalizeTimezone(\n \treturn isUTCAlias(timezone) ? \"UTC\" : timezone;\n }\n \n-const localTimezone = Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone;\n+const localTimezone = getLocalTimezone();\n const normalizedLocalTimezone = normalizeTimezone(localTimezone);\n \n const SUGGESTED_TIMEZONES = [\ndiff --git a/ui-v2/src/utils/date.ts b/ui-v2/src/utils/date.ts\nindex 0df41b427d88..82ed6bbaa1cd 100644\n--- a/ui-v2/src/utils/date.ts\n+++ b/ui-v2/src/utils/date.ts\n@@ -61,3 +61,15 @@ export function formatDateTimeRelative(\n \n \treturn `in ${formatted}`;\n }\n+\n+/**\n+ * Returns the browser's IANA time zone, or \"UTC\" when the browser doesn't\n+ * report one.\n+ */\n+export function getLocalTimezone(): string {\n+\ttry {\n+\t\treturn Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone || \"UTC\";\n+\t} catch {\n+\t\treturn \"UTC\";\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/ui-v2/src/utils/index.ts b/ui-v2/src/utils/index.ts\nindex 1a8b1a2b2649..20b7a9bb9af2 100644\n--- a/ui-v2/src/utils/index.ts\n+++ b/ui-v2/src/utils/index.ts\n@@ -1,6 +1,6 @@\n export { calculateBucketSize } from \"./calculate-bucket-size\";\n export { cn } from \"./css\";\n-export { formatDate, formatDateTimeRelative } from \"./date\";\n+export { formatDate, formatDateTimeRelative, getLocalTimezone } from \"./date\";\n export {\n \tintervalToSeconds,\n \tsecondsToApproximateString,\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-string-format-datetime.test.tsx b/ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-string-format-datetime.test.tsx\nindex 5ccaf4e29e52..8846c623add3 100644\n--- a/ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-string-format-datetime.test.tsx\n+++ b/ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-string-format-datetime.test.tsx\n@@ -1,7 +1,15 @@\n import { render, screen } from \"@testing-library/react\";\n import userEvent from \"@testing-library/user-event\";\n-import { mockPointerEvents } from \"@tests/utils/browser\";\n-import { beforeAll, describe, expect, test, vi } from \"vitest\";\n+import { mockBrowserTimezone, mockPointerEvents } from \"@tests/utils/browser\";\n+import {\n+\tafterEach,\n+\tbeforeAll,\n+\tbeforeEach,\n+\tdescribe,\n+\texpect,\n+\ttest,\n+\tvi,\n+} from \"vitest\";\n import { SchemaFormInputStringFormatDateTime } from \"./schema-form-input-string-format-datetime\";\n \n describe(\"SchemaFormInputStringFormatDateTime\", () => {\n@@ -130,3 +138,28 @@ describe(\"SchemaFormInputStringFormatDateTime\", () => {\n \t\texpect(dateButton).toHaveAttribute(\"id\", \"my-custom-id\");\n \t});\n });\n+\n+describe(\"SchemaFormInputStringFormatDateTime when the browser reports no time zone\", () => {\n+\tbeforeAll(mockPointerEvents);\n+\n+\tbeforeEach(() => {\n+\t\tmockBrowserTimezone(undefined);\n+\t\tvi.resetModules();\n+\t});\n+\n+\tafterEach(() => {\n+\t\tvi.restoreAllMocks();\n+\t});\n+\n+\ttest(\"defaults the timezone to UTC\", async () => {\n+\t\tconst { SchemaFormInputStringFormatDateTime: Input } = await import(\n+\t\t\t\"./schema-form-input-string-format-datetime\"\n+\t\t);\n+\n+\t\trender(<Input value={undefined} onValueChange={vi.fn()} id=\"test-id\" />);\n+\n+\t\texpect(\n+\t\t\tscreen.getByRole(\"button\", { name: /select timezone/i }),\n+\t\t).toHaveTextContent(\"UTC\");\n+\t});\n+});\ndiff --git a/ui-v2/src/components/ui/timezone-select/timezone-select.test.tsx b/ui-v2/src/components/ui/timezone-select/timezone-select.test.tsx\nindex 0907a24dd02b..75eb42c4e955 100644\n--- a/ui-v2/src/components/ui/timezone-select/timezone-select.test.tsx\n+++ b/ui-v2/src/components/ui/timezone-select/timezone-select.test.tsx\n@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@\n import { render, screen } from \"@testing-library/react\";\n import userEvent from \"@testing-library/user-event\";\n-import { mockPointerEvents } from \"@tests/utils/browser\";\n-import { expect, test, vi } from \"vitest\";\n+import { mockBrowserTimezone, mockPointerEvents } from \"@tests/utils/browser\";\n+import { afterEach, beforeEach, describe, expect, test, vi } from \"vitest\";\n \n import { TimezoneSelect } from \"./timezone-select\";\n \n@@ -80,3 +80,33 @@ test(\"TimezoneSelect selects the canonical UTC option\", async () => {\n \n \texpect(mockOnSelectFn).toBeCalledWith(\"UTC\");\n });\n+\n+describe(\"when the browser reports no time zone\", () => {\n+\tbeforeEach(() => {\n+\t\tmockBrowserTimezone(undefined);\n+\t\tvi.resetModules();\n+\t});\n+\n+\tafterEach(() => {\n+\t\tvi.restoreAllMocks();\n+\t});\n+\n+\ttest(\"TimezoneSelect renders with UTC as the only suggested timezone\", async () => {\n+\t\tmockPointerEvents();\n+\t\tconst user = userEvent.setup();\n+\t\tconst { TimezoneSelect: TimezoneSelectWithoutBrowserTimezone } =\n+\t\t\tawait import(\"./timezone-select\");\n+\n+\t\trender(\n+\t\t\t<TimezoneSelectWithoutBrowserTimezone\n+\t\t\t\tonSelect={vi.fn()}\n+\t\t\t\tselectedValue=\"\"\n+\t\t\t/>,\n+\t\t);\n+\n+\t\tawait user.click(screen.getByLabelText(/select timezone/i));\n+\n+\t\texpect(screen.getByText(/suggested timezones/i)).toBeVisible();\n+\t\texpect(screen.getAllByRole(\"option\", { name: /^utc$/i })).toHaveLength(1);\n+\t});\n+});\ndiff --git a/ui-v2/src/utils/date.test.ts b/ui-v2/src/utils/date.test.ts\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 000000000000..7884b6f23038\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/ui-v2/src/utils/date.test.ts\n@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@\n+import { mockBrowserTimezone } from \"@tests/utils/browser\";\n+import { afterEach, describe, expect, test, vi } from \"vitest\";\n+import { getLocalTimezone } from \"./date\";\n+\n+describe(\"getLocalTimezone\", () => {\n+\tafterEach(() => {\n+\t\tvi.restoreAllMocks();\n+\t});\n+\n+\ttest(\"returns the browser time zone\", () => {\n+\t\tmockBrowserTimezone(\"America/Chicago\");\n+\n+\t\texpect(getLocalTimezone()).toBe(\"America/Chicago\");\n+\t});\n+\n+\ttest(\"falls back to UTC when the browser reports no time zone\", () => {\n+\t\tmockBrowserTimezone(undefined);\n+\n+\t\texpect(getLocalTimezone()).toBe(\"UTC\");\n+\t});\n+\n+\ttest(\"falls back to UTC when resolving the time zone throws\", () => {\n+\t\tvi.spyOn(\n+\t\t\tIntl.DateTimeFormat.prototype,\n+\t\t\t\"resolvedOptions\",\n+\t\t).mockImplementation(() => {\n+\t\t\tthrow new RangeError(\"Invalid time zone\");\n+\t\t});\n+\n+\t\texpect(getLocalTimezone()).toBe(\"UTC\");\n+\t});\n+});\ndiff --git a/ui-v2/tests/utils/browser.ts b/ui-v2/tests/utils/browser.ts\nindex 68ff39018ad6..f0f9af54d04a 100644\n--- a/ui-v2/tests/utils/browser.ts\n+++ b/ui-v2/tests/utils/browser.ts\n@@ -45,3 +45,14 @@ export const mockInMemoryLocalStorage = () => {\n \t\t}\n \t};\n };\n+\n+/**\n+ * Stubs the time zone reported by `Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions()`.\n+ * Call `vi.restoreAllMocks()` afterwards to restore it.\n+ */\n+export const mockBrowserTimezone = (timeZone: string | undefined) => {\n+\tconst options = new Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions();\n+\treturn vi\n+\t\t.spyOn(Intl.DateTimeFormat.prototype, \"resolvedOptions\")\n+\t\t.mockReturnValue(Object.assign(options, { timeZone }));\n+};\n", "problem_statement": "UI v2 crashes with `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'replaceAll')` when the browser reports no time zone\n\n### Bug summary\n\nWhen `Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone` returns `undefined` in the browser, UI v2 throws while loading the `schema-form-*.js` chunk, and pages that use schema forms fail to render.\n\nObserved in Thorium 138.0.7204.303 (Official Build) (64-bit), a Chromium-based browser. The browser console shows this error 104 times, which looks like React retrying the render:\n\n```\nvendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8 TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'replaceAll')\n    at Ga (schema-form-DbvvOFl5.js:5:60418)\n        at schema-form-DbvvOFl5.js:5:60786\nec @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\nrc @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\no.componentDidCatch.e.callback @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\nio @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\nao @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\nJc @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\nll @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\nCl @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\n... (ll / Cl pair repeated 49 more times)\nll @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\nBu @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\nRu @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\nvu @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\n_u @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\nld @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:8\nae @ vendor-react-Cef_YSQF.js:1\n```\n\nIn the shipped 3.9.0 bundle, `schema-form-DbvvOFl5.js:5:60418` is `function Ga(e){return e.replaceAll(...)`, and `:5:60786` is the module-level `{label:Ga(Ya),value:Xa}`, where `Ya = Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone`.\n\nThe time zone select builds its option list at the top level of the module (`ui-v2/src/components/ui/timezone-select/timezone-select.tsx`) and passes the browser time zone straight to `getTimezoneLabel`, which calls `value.replaceAll(...)`:\n\n```ts\nfunction getTimezoneLabel(value: string): string {\n\treturn value.replaceAll(\"/\", \" / \").replaceAll(\"_\", \" \");\n}\n```\n\n`ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-string-format-datetime.tsx` also reads `resolvedOptions().timeZone` without a fallback.\n\n**Minimal reproduction.** No browser is needed. This runs the time zone select's module-level code from the shipped 3.9.0 bundle, with `resolvedOptions()` stubbed to return no time zone:\n\n```bash\nf=$(python -c \"import prefect,glob;print(glob.glob(str(prefect.__ui_v2_static_path__)+'/assets/schema-form-*.js')[0])\")\ngrep -o 'function Ga(e){.*,\\$a=\\[\\.\\.\\.Za,\\.\\.\\.Qa\\]' \"$f\" > /tmp/tzsnip.js\nnode -e '\nconst src = require(\"fs\").readFileSync(\"/tmp/tzsnip.js\", \"utf8\");\nconst o = Intl.DateTimeFormat.prototype.resolvedOptions;\nIntl.DateTimeFormat.prototype.resolvedOptions = function () { return { ...o.call(this), timeZone: undefined }; };\nnew Function(src + \";return $a.length\")();'\n```\n\n(The minified identifiers `Ga`/`Za`/`Qa`/`$a` are specific to the 3.9.0 build.)\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nTypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'replaceAll')\n    at Ga (eval at <anonymous> ([eval]:4:1), <anonymous>:3:25)\n    at eval (eval at <anonymous> ([eval]:4:1), <anonymous>:3:393)\n    at [eval]:4:38\n    at Script.runInThisContext (node:vm:129:12)\n    at Object.runInThisContext (node:vm:313:38)\n    at node:internal/process/execution:83:21\n    at [eval]-wrapper:6:24\n```\n\nWithout the stub, the same snippet runs without error.\n\nExpected: the UI falls back to UTC (or omits the browser time zone option) instead of crashing.\n\n### Additional context\n\n- Not a duplicate of #19148 (wrong time zone shown) or #23676 (offset applied twice). Neither involves a missing browser time zone.\n- Possible fix: a shared helper such as `Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone || \"UTC\"` (wrapped in `try`/`catch`), used in both components, plus a Vitest case that stubs `resolvedOptions()` to return `timeZone: undefined` before importing the module.\n- Possible workaround (not verified): setting `PREFECT_SERVER_UI_V2_ENABLED=false` makes the V1 UI the default for browsers without a saved preference.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.9.0\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.11.1\nGit commit:           1ef3e516\nBuilt:                Wed, Oct 21, 2026 03:29 AM\nOS/Arch:              linux/x86_64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          server\nPydantic version:     2.14.1\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.53.2\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-03T23:06:13Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23726, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23726", "issue_numbers": ["23724"], "base_commit": "1d50f74d953cabd5ebed03f06d8e779d1c1fad19", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/locking/filesystem.py b/src/prefect/locking/filesystem.py\nindex 53ea2637ac2a..19d25f1dcb86 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/locking/filesystem.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/locking/filesystem.py\n@@ -203,6 +203,7 @@ def release_lock(self, key: str, holder: str) -> None:\n             # elapsed or it was released during a task retry) \u2014 including\n             # between the checks above \u2014 so releasing it is a no-op, matching\n             # RedisLockManager.\n+            self._locks.pop(key, None)\n             return\n         raise ValueError(f\"No lock held by {holder} for transaction with key {key}\")\n \ndiff --git a/src/prefect/locking/protocol.py b/src/prefect/locking/protocol.py\nindex 3505eeec00dd..f461a657e9c6 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/locking/protocol.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/locking/protocol.py\n@@ -61,10 +61,16 @@ def release_lock(self, key: str, holder: str) -> None:\n         \"\"\"\n         Releases the lock on the corresponding transaction record.\n \n+        If the lock no longer exists (e.g. it expired or was already released),\n+        this method is a no-op.\n+\n         Args:\n             key: Unique identifier for the transaction record.\n             holder: Unique identifier for the holder of the lock. Must match the\n                 holder provided when acquiring the lock.\n+\n+        Raises:\n+            ValueError: If the lock is held by a different holder.\n         \"\"\"\n         ...\n \n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/test_locking.py b/tests/test_locking.py\nindex 52837ebdae7b..310326656829 100644\n--- a/tests/test_locking.py\n+++ b/tests/test_locking.py\n@@ -116,6 +116,24 @@ def test_raises_if_releasing_with_wrong_holder(self):\n         ):\n             manager.release_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder2\")\n \n+    def test_release_never_acquired_lock_is_noop(self):\n+        key = str(uuid4())\n+        manager = MemoryLockManager()\n+        manager.release_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder1\")\n+        assert not manager.is_locked(key=key)\n+\n+    def test_raises_if_releasing_expired_lock_reacquired_by_other_holder(self):\n+        key = str(uuid4())\n+        manager = MemoryLockManager()\n+        assert manager.acquire_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder1\", hold_timeout=0.1)\n+        assert manager.acquire_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder2\", acquire_timeout=5)\n+        with pytest.raises(\n+            ValueError, match=f\"No lock held by holder1 for transaction with key {key}\"\n+        ):\n+            manager.release_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder1\")\n+        assert manager.is_lock_holder(key, holder=\"holder2\")\n+        manager.release_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder2\")\n+\n     def test_release_lock_after_hold_timeout_expiry(self):\n         key = str(uuid4())\n         manager = MemoryLockManager()\n@@ -439,6 +457,33 @@ def test_raises_if_releasing_with_wrong_holder(self, store):\n         ):\n             store.release_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder2\")\n \n+    def test_release_expired_lock_is_noop(\n+        self, store: FileSystemLockManager, clock: _Clock\n+    ):\n+        key = str(uuid4())\n+        assert store.acquire_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder1\", hold_timeout=10)\n+        clock.advance(11)\n+        store.release_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder1\")\n+        assert not store.is_locked(key=key)\n+\n+    def test_release_never_acquired_lock_is_noop(self, store: FileSystemLockManager):\n+        key = str(uuid4())\n+        store.release_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder1\")\n+        assert not store.is_locked(key=key)\n+\n+    def test_raises_if_releasing_expired_lock_reacquired_by_other_holder(\n+        self, store: FileSystemLockManager, clock: _Clock\n+    ):\n+        key = str(uuid4())\n+        assert store.acquire_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder1\", hold_timeout=10)\n+        clock.advance(11)\n+        assert store.acquire_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder2\")\n+        with pytest.raises(\n+            ValueError, match=f\"No lock held by holder1 for transaction with key {key}\"\n+        ):\n+            store.release_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder1\")\n+        assert store.is_lock_holder(key, holder=\"holder2\")\n+\n     def test_is_lock_holder(self, store):\n         key = str(uuid4())\n         assert not store.is_lock_holder(key, holder=\"holder1\")\n", "problem_statement": "MemoryLockManager and FileSystemLockManager raise on release_lock after a lock expires; RedisLockManager does not\n## Description\n\n`release_lock` on a lock whose hold timeout has elapsed raises `ValueError`, while `RedisLockManager.release_lock` deliberately treats a vanished lock as already released (its docstring: _\"If the lock doesn't exist in Redis at all, this method will succeed even if the holder ID doesn't match\"_ \u2014 to handle locks released during a task retry).\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom prefect.locking.memory import MemoryLockManager\n\nmanager = MemoryLockManager()\nmanager.acquire_lock(\"k\", holder=\"h1\", hold_timeout=0.1)\ntime.sleep(0.2)  # hold timeout elapses; the entry is dropped\nmanager.release_lock(\"k\", holder=\"h1\")\n# ValueError: No lock held by h1 for transaction with key k\n```\n\n`FileSystemLockManager` behaves the same way (`No lock for transaction with key ...`).\n\n## Impact\n\n`Transaction.commit`/`rollback` call `store.release_lock` unguarded (`src/prefect/transactions.py`, including in a `finally:` block), so a SERIALIZABLE transaction whose lock expires mid-flight fails its own teardown \u2014 and in the `finally` path the `ValueError` masks the original exception.\n\nSince `LockManager` implementations are interchangeable behind the same protocol, the memory and filesystem managers should match the redis semantics: releasing a lock that no longer exists is a no-op; a live lock held by a different holder still raises.\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-03T14:51:27Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23725, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23725", "issue_numbers": ["23724"], "base_commit": "bf7c9eaf37e248ed255e4c45c8f06258f8ddd903", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/locking/filesystem.py b/src/prefect/locking/filesystem.py\nindex 40a06a36fe78..53ea2637ac2a 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/locking/filesystem.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/locking/filesystem.py\n@@ -194,13 +194,17 @@ async def aacquire_lock(\n \n     def release_lock(self, key: str, holder: str) -> None:\n         lock_path = self._lock_path_for_key(key)\n-        if not self.is_locked(key):\n-            raise ValueError(f\"No lock for transaction with key {key}\")\n         if self.is_lock_holder(key, holder):\n             Path(lock_path).unlink(missing_ok=True)\n             self._locks.pop(key, None)\n-        else:\n-            raise ValueError(f\"No lock held by {holder} for transaction with key {key}\")\n+            return\n+        if not self.is_locked(key):\n+            # The lock was already released or expired (e.g. its hold timeout\n+            # elapsed or it was released during a task retry) \u2014 including\n+            # between the checks above \u2014 so releasing it is a no-op, matching\n+            # RedisLockManager.\n+            return\n+        raise ValueError(f\"No lock held by {holder} for transaction with key {key}\")\n \n     def is_locked(self, key: str, use_cache: bool = False) -> bool:\n         if (lock_info := self._get_lock_info(key, use_cache=use_cache)) is None:\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/locking/memory.py b/src/prefect/locking/memory.py\nindex e454c473515d..7b472fd5be83 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/locking/memory.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/locking/memory.py\n@@ -180,13 +180,18 @@ async def aacquire_lock(\n \n     def release_lock(self, key: str, holder: str) -> None:\n         with self._locks_dict_lock:\n-            if key in self._locks and self._locks[key][\"holder\"] == holder:\n-                if (\n-                    expiration_timer := self._locks[key][\"expiration_timer\"]\n-                ) is not None:\n+            lock_info = self._locks.get(key)\n+            if lock_info is not None and lock_info[\"holder\"] == holder:\n+                if (expiration_timer := lock_info[\"expiration_timer\"]) is not None:\n                     expiration_timer.cancel()\n-                self._locks[key][\"lock\"].release()\n+                if lock_info[\"lock\"].locked():\n+                    lock_info[\"lock\"].release()\n                 del self._locks[key]\n+            elif lock_info is None or not lock_info[\"lock\"].locked():\n+                # The lock was already released or expired (e.g. its hold\n+                # timeout elapsed or it was released during a task retry);\n+                # releasing it is a no-op, matching RedisLockManager.\n+                return\n             else:\n                 raise ValueError(\n                     f\"No lock held by {holder} for transaction with key {key}\"\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/test_locking.py b/tests/test_locking.py\nindex 7fba9fa5db5e..52837ebdae7b 100644\n--- a/tests/test_locking.py\n+++ b/tests/test_locking.py\n@@ -116,6 +116,21 @@ def test_raises_if_releasing_with_wrong_holder(self):\n         ):\n             manager.release_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder2\")\n \n+    def test_release_lock_after_hold_timeout_expiry(self):\n+        key = str(uuid4())\n+        manager = MemoryLockManager()\n+        assert manager.acquire_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder1\")\n+        assert manager.is_locked(key=key)\n+        # trigger the expiration timer's callback synchronously (what a\n+        # hold_timeout would fire asynchronously) so the test has no timing\n+        # dependence on the scheduler\n+        manager._expire_lock(key)\n+        assert not manager.is_locked(key=key)\n+        # releasing a lock that has expired is a no-op, not an error, matching\n+        # RedisLockManager.release_lock\n+        manager.release_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder1\")\n+        assert not manager.is_locked(key=key)\n+\n     def test_is_lock_holder(self):\n         key = str(uuid4())\n         manager = MemoryLockManager()\n@@ -404,6 +419,17 @@ def test_acquire_lock_when_previously_holder_timed_out(self, store, clock: _Cloc\n         store.release_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder2\")\n         assert not store.is_locked(key=key)\n \n+    def test_release_lock_after_hold_timeout_expiry(self, store, clock: _Clock):\n+        key = str(uuid4())\n+        assert store.acquire_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder1\", hold_timeout=10)\n+        assert store.is_locked(key=key)\n+        clock.advance(11)\n+        assert not store.is_locked(key=key)\n+        # releasing a lock that has expired is a no-op, not an error, matching\n+        # RedisLockManager.release_lock\n+        store.release_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder1\")\n+        assert not store.is_locked(key=key)\n+\n     def test_raises_if_releasing_with_wrong_holder(self, store):\n         key = str(uuid4())\n         assert store.acquire_lock(key=key, holder=\"holder1\")\n", "problem_statement": "MemoryLockManager and FileSystemLockManager raise on release_lock after a lock expires; RedisLockManager does not\n## Description\n\n`release_lock` on a lock whose hold timeout has elapsed raises `ValueError`, while `RedisLockManager.release_lock` deliberately treats a vanished lock as already released (its docstring: _\"If the lock doesn't exist in Redis at all, this method will succeed even if the holder ID doesn't match\"_ \u2014 to handle locks released during a task retry).\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom prefect.locking.memory import MemoryLockManager\n\nmanager = MemoryLockManager()\nmanager.acquire_lock(\"k\", holder=\"h1\", hold_timeout=0.1)\ntime.sleep(0.2)  # hold timeout elapses; the entry is dropped\nmanager.release_lock(\"k\", holder=\"h1\")\n# ValueError: No lock held by h1 for transaction with key k\n```\n\n`FileSystemLockManager` behaves the same way (`No lock for transaction with key ...`).\n\n## Impact\n\n`Transaction.commit`/`rollback` call `store.release_lock` unguarded (`src/prefect/transactions.py`, including in a `finally:` block), so a SERIALIZABLE transaction whose lock expires mid-flight fails its own teardown \u2014 and in the `finally` path the `ValueError` masks the original exception.\n\nSince `LockManager` implementations are interchangeable behind the same protocol, the memory and filesystem managers should match the redis semantics: releasing a lock that no longer exists is a no-op; a live lock held by a different holder still raises.\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-03T14:46:14Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23719, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23719", "issue_numbers": ["23718"], "base_commit": "bf7c9eaf37e248ed255e4c45c8f06258f8ddd903", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/server/api/deployments.py b/src/prefect/server/api/deployments.py\nindex 0b653f8860c0..4176a728f8d7 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/server/api/deployments.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/server/api/deployments.py\n@@ -440,6 +440,9 @@ async def update_deployment(\n         # Phase 2: Apply the actual updates. Renamed schedules use\n         # their row ID (slug is now NULL); others use slug as before.\n         for schedule in schedules_to_patch:\n+            # A schedule sent with the deployment carries its full overrides, and\n+            # `prefect deploy` leaves `parameters` out when there are none\n+            schedule = schedule.model_copy(update={\"parameters\": schedule.parameters})\n             if schedule.replaces:\n                 await models.deployments.update_deployment_schedule(\n                     session=session,\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/server/models/deployments.py b/src/prefect/server/models/deployments.py\nindex a89451374a65..e519ed4c27ae 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/server/models/deployments.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/server/models/deployments.py\n@@ -1201,6 +1201,10 @@ async def update_deployment_schedule(\n     \"\"\"\n     # Exclude 'replaces' as it's a deploy-time directive, not a persisted field\n     update_values = schedule.model_dump(exclude_none=True, exclude={\"replaces\"})\n+    # `parameters` defaults to `{}`, so only write it when the caller sent it;\n+    # otherwise a partial update like `{\"active\": false}` clears the overrides\n+    if \"parameters\" not in schedule.model_fields_set:\n+        update_values.pop(\"parameters\", None)\n     if deployment_schedule_id:\n         result = await session.execute(\n             sa.update(db.DeploymentSchedule)\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/cli/deployment/test_deployment_cli.py b/tests/cli/deployment/test_deployment_cli.py\nindex 6d80a1d33096..e48c8cf5171e 100644\n--- a/tests/cli/deployment/test_deployment_cli.py\n+++ b/tests/cli/deployment/test_deployment_cli.py\n@@ -90,6 +90,18 @@ async def flojo_deployment(\n     return await prefect_client.read_deployment(create_flojo_deployment)\n \n \n+@pytest.fixture\n+async def flojo_deployment_with_schedule_parameters(\n+    flojo_deployment: DeploymentResponse, prefect_client: PrefectClient\n+) -> DeploymentResponse:\n+    await prefect_client.update_deployment_schedule(\n+        flojo_deployment.id,\n+        flojo_deployment.schedules[0].id,\n+        parameters={\"foo\": \"baz\"},\n+    )\n+    return await prefect_client.read_deployment(flojo_deployment.id)\n+\n+\n def test_list_schedules(flojo_deployment: DeploymentResponse):\n     create_commands = [\n         \"deployment\",\n@@ -638,6 +650,32 @@ def test_pausing_and_resuming_schedules_with_schedule_pause(\n             expected_output_contains=[\"'active': True\"],\n         )\n \n+    def test_pausing_and_resuming_schedule_keeps_its_parameters(\n+        self, flojo_deployment_with_schedule_parameters: DeploymentResponse\n+    ):\n+        schedule_id = str(flojo_deployment_with_schedule_parameters.schedules[0].id)\n+\n+        for command in (\"pause\", \"resume\"):\n+            invoke_and_assert(\n+                [\n+                    \"deployment\",\n+                    \"schedule\",\n+                    command,\n+                    \"rence-griffith/test-deployment\",\n+                    schedule_id,\n+                ],\n+                expected_code=0,\n+            )\n+\n+            invoke_and_assert(\n+                [\n+                    \"deployment\",\n+                    \"inspect\",\n+                    \"rence-griffith/test-deployment\",\n+                ],\n+                expected_output_contains=[\"'parameters': {'foo': 'baz'}\"],\n+            )\n+\n     @pytest.mark.usefixtures(\"create_flojo_deployment\")\n     def test_schedule_pause_deployment_not_found_raises(\n         self, flojo_deployment: DeploymentResponse\ndiff --git a/tests/server/models/test_deployments.py b/tests/server/models/test_deployments.py\nindex 2d928992c9ba..4e502ccea6be 100644\n--- a/tests/server/models/test_deployments.py\n+++ b/tests/server/models/test_deployments.py\n@@ -1963,6 +1963,69 @@ async def test_can_update_schedule(\n \n         assert the_one.active is False\n \n+    async def test_update_schedule_keeps_parameters_it_does_not_set(\n+        self,\n+        session: AsyncSession,\n+        deployment,\n+        deployment_schedules: List[schemas.core.DeploymentSchedule],\n+    ):\n+        assert await models.deployments.update_deployment_schedule(\n+            session=session,\n+            deployment_id=deployment.id,\n+            deployment_schedule_id=deployment_schedules[0].id,\n+            schedule=schemas.actions.DeploymentScheduleUpdate(parameters={\"x\": 1}),\n+        )\n+\n+        assert await models.deployments.update_deployment_schedule(\n+            session=session,\n+            deployment_id=deployment.id,\n+            deployment_schedule_id=deployment_schedules[0].id,\n+            schedule=schemas.actions.DeploymentScheduleUpdate(active=False),\n+        )\n+\n+        schedules = await models.deployments.read_deployment_schedules(\n+            session=session,\n+            deployment_id=deployment.id,\n+        )\n+\n+        the_one = next(\n+            schedule\n+            for schedule in schedules\n+            if schedule.id == deployment_schedules[0].id\n+        )\n+\n+        assert the_one.active is False\n+        assert the_one.parameters == {\"x\": 1}\n+\n+    async def test_update_schedule_with_empty_parameters_clears_them(\n+        self,\n+        session: AsyncSession,\n+        deployment,\n+        deployment_schedules: List[schemas.core.DeploymentSchedule],\n+    ):\n+        for parameters in ({\"x\": 1}, {}):\n+            assert await models.deployments.update_deployment_schedule(\n+                session=session,\n+                deployment_id=deployment.id,\n+                deployment_schedule_id=deployment_schedules[0].id,\n+                schedule=schemas.actions.DeploymentScheduleUpdate(\n+                    parameters=parameters\n+                ),\n+            )\n+\n+        schedules = await models.deployments.read_deployment_schedules(\n+            session=session,\n+            deployment_id=deployment.id,\n+        )\n+\n+        the_one = next(\n+            schedule\n+            for schedule in schedules\n+            if schedule.id == deployment_schedules[0].id\n+        )\n+\n+        assert the_one.parameters == {}\n+\n     async def test_cannot_update_schedule_incorrect_deployment_id(\n         self,\n         session: AsyncSession,\ndiff --git a/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployment_schedules.py b/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployment_schedules.py\nindex b4cf2e2b82d2..0329cc60c584 100644\n--- a/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployment_schedules.py\n+++ b/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployment_schedules.py\n@@ -283,6 +283,56 @@ async def test_can_update_schedules_for_deployment(\n         assert the_schedule.active is False\n         assert the_schedule.slug == \"new-slug\"\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"update\",\n+        [\n+            {\"active\": False},\n+            {\"schedule\": {\"interval\": 3600}},\n+            {\"slug\": \"new-slug\"},\n+            {\"max_scheduled_runs\": 5},\n+            {},\n+        ],\n+        ids=[\"active\", \"schedule\", \"slug\", \"max_scheduled_runs\", \"empty\"],\n+    )\n+    async def test_update_keeps_parameters_it_does_not_set(\n+        self,\n+        get_server_session: AsyncSessionGetter,\n+        client: AsyncClient,\n+        deployment_with_schedules,\n+        schedules_url: Callable[..., str],\n+        schedule_to_update: schemas.core.DeploymentSchedule,\n+        update: dict,\n+    ):\n+        async with get_server_session() as session:\n+            await models.deployments.update_deployment_schedule(\n+                session=session,\n+                deployment_id=deployment_with_schedules.id,\n+                deployment_schedule_id=schedule_to_update.id,\n+                schedule=schemas.actions.DeploymentScheduleUpdate(\n+                    parameters={\"foo\": \"bar\"}\n+                ),\n+            )\n+            await session.commit()\n+\n+        url = schedules_url(\n+            deployment_with_schedules.id, schedule_id=schedule_to_update.id\n+        )\n+        response = await client.patch(url, json=update)\n+\n+        assert response.status_code == status.HTTP_204_NO_CONTENT\n+\n+        async with get_server_session() as session:\n+            schedules = await models.deployments.read_deployment_schedules(\n+                session=session,\n+                deployment_id=deployment_with_schedules.id,\n+            )\n+\n+        the_schedule = next(\n+            schedule for schedule in schedules if schedule.id == schedule_to_update.id\n+        )\n+\n+        assert the_schedule.parameters == {\"foo\": \"bar\"}\n+\n     async def test_404_non_existent_deployment(\n         self,\n         client: AsyncClient,\ndiff --git a/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployments.py b/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployments.py\nindex d3b7bd422669..b09cdc9c83ad 100644\n--- a/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployments.py\n+++ b/tests/server/orchestration/api/test_deployments.py\n@@ -2334,6 +2334,62 @@ async def test_update_deployment_with_multiple_schedules_and_existing_slugs(\n         }\n         assert expected_interval_active_and_slug == interval_active_and_slug\n \n+    async def test_update_deployment_with_existing_slug_clears_parameters_left_out(\n+        self,\n+        client,\n+        flow,\n+    ):\n+        \"\"\"\n+        A schedule sent with a deployment update carries its full parameter\n+        overrides, so leaving `parameters` out clears them. `prefect deploy` sends\n+        schedules this way after the overrides are removed from `prefect.yaml`.\n+        \"\"\"\n+        schedule = schemas.schedules.IntervalSchedule(\n+            interval=datetime.timedelta(days=1)\n+        )\n+\n+        data = DeploymentCreate(  # type: ignore\n+            name=\"My Deployment\",\n+            flow_id=flow.id,\n+            schedules=[\n+                schemas.actions.DeploymentScheduleCreate(\n+                    schedule=schedule,\n+                    parameters={\"foo\": \"bar\"},\n+                    slug=\"test-schedule\",\n+                ),\n+            ],\n+            enforce_parameter_schema=False,\n+        ).model_dump(mode=\"json\")\n+        response = await client.post(\n+            \"/deployments/\",\n+            json=data,\n+        )\n+        assert response.status_code == 201\n+        assert response.json()[\"schedules\"][0][\"parameters\"] == {\"foo\": \"bar\"}\n+\n+        deployment_id = response.json()[\"id\"]\n+\n+        update_data = schemas.actions.DeploymentUpdate(\n+            schedules=[\n+                schemas.actions.DeploymentScheduleUpdate(\n+                    schedule=schedule,\n+                    slug=\"test-schedule\",\n+                ),\n+            ],\n+        ).model_dump(mode=\"json\", exclude_unset=True)\n+\n+        response = await client.patch(\n+            f\"/deployments/{deployment_id}\",\n+            json=update_data,\n+        )\n+        assert response.status_code == 204\n+\n+        response = await client.get(\n+            f\"/deployments/{deployment_id}\",\n+        )\n+\n+        assert response.json()[\"schedules\"][0][\"parameters\"] == {}\n+\n     async def test_update_schedule_without_slug_and_specifying_active_defaults_to_true(\n         self,\n         client,\n", "problem_statement": "Pausing or resuming a deployment schedule clears its parameter overrides\n### Bug summary\n\nUpdating a deployment schedule without `parameters` clears the schedule's parameter overrides. `prefect deployment schedule pause` and `resume` only send `{\"active\": ...}`, so pausing a schedule is enough to lose them, and the runs scheduled after `resume` don't get the overridden parameters. Updating only the cron, the slug or `max_scheduled_runs` does the same.\n\nWe hit this in production: a schedule was changed by hand, and its next run was created without the schedule's `comm_type` override and crashed with `ParameterBindError: Error binding parameters for function 'order_communication': missing a required argument: 'comm_type'.`\n\nMinimal repro, no server needed (on 3.9.1 it prints `{}` after the pause):\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nfrom prefect import flow\nfrom prefect.client.orchestration import get_client\nfrom prefect.client.schemas.actions import DeploymentScheduleCreate\nfrom prefect.client.schemas.schedules import CronSchedule\n\n\n@flow\ndef report(region: str):\n    print(region)\n\n\nasync def main():\n    async with get_client() as client:\n        flow_id = await client.create_flow(report)\n        deployment_id = await client.create_deployment(\n            flow_id=flow_id,\n            name=\"repro\",\n            schedules=[\n                DeploymentScheduleCreate(\n                    schedule=CronSchedule(cron=\"0 10 * * 1-5\"),\n                    parameters={\"region\": \"us\"},\n                )\n            ],\n        )\n        schedule = (await client.read_deployment(deployment_id)).schedules[0]\n        print(\"before:\", schedule.parameters)\n\n        # the call `prefect deployment schedule pause` makes\n        await client.update_deployment_schedule(deployment_id, schedule.id, active=False)\n        schedule = (await client.read_deployment(deployment_id)).schedules[0]\n        print(\"after pause:\", schedule.parameters)\n\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nbefore: {'region': 'us'}\nafter pause: {}\n```\n\nExpected: `after pause: {'region': 'us'}`, since the update only sets `active`.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.9.1\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.13.15\nGit commit:           bd4807c3\nBuilt:                Fri, Oct 30, 2026 03:52 PM\nOS/Arch:              win32/AMD64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          ephemeral\nPydantic version:     2.14.2\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.50.4\n```\n\nSame on `main` (b0de3acf7d). It goes back to 3.2.0, when schedules got `parameters`.\n\n### Additional context\n\nI think the root cause is a default mismatch between the client and server models. On the server, `DeploymentScheduleUpdate.parameters` defaults to `{}`:\nhttps://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/b0de3acf7d14303d1f1eafb1cc2ee84c7f0375b9/src/prefect/server/schemas/actions.py#L155-L157\n\nand `update_deployment_schedule` writes `model_dump(exclude_none=True)`, so the `{}` goes into every partial update, while the other fields default to `None` and are left alone:\nhttps://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/b0de3acf7d14303d1f1eafb1cc2ee84c7f0375b9/src/prefect/server/models/deployments.py#L1203\n\nThe client model defaults `parameters` to `None` and only sends what was set:\nhttps://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/b0de3acf7d14303d1f1eafb1cc2ee84c7f0375b9/src/prefect/client/schemas/actions.py#L211-L214\n\nSo any client that sends a partial update clears the overrides:\n- `prefect deployment schedule pause/resume`, `--all` included: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/b0de3acf7d14303d1f1eafb1cc2ee84c7f0375b9/src/prefect/cli/deployment.py#L1041-L1043\n- the schedule toggle in the V2 UI: https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/b0de3acf7d14303d1f1eafb1cc2ee84c7f0375b9/ui-v2/src/components/deployments/deployment-schedules/schedule-toggle-switch.tsx#L29-L30\n\nThis looks like the server side of #17982 and #17380, which were fixed in the V1 UI by sending the current parameters along with the update.\n\nProposed fix: only write `parameters` in `update_deployment_schedule` when the caller sent them (`model_fields_set`), so `{\"parameters\": {}}` still clears them. One catch: `prefect deploy` leaves `parameters` out of a schedule that has none:\nhttps://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/b0de3acf7d14303d1f1eafb1cc2ee84c7f0375b9/src/prefect/cli/deploy/_schedules.py#L87-L88\n\nand updating a deployment with schedules by slug relies on the `{}` default to clear overrides removed from `prefect.yaml`, so that path should mark `parameters` as set. I have it working with tests and will open the PR right after this. Making `parameters` optional in the server model, like the client, also works but changes the OpenAPI schema; happy to switch if you prefer that.\n\n", "hints_text": "The fix is ready in https://github.com/CassianoE/prefect/commit/968ffee202b66f2daaddf0973527cdb277d449f8 (branch `fix-schedule-update-keeps-parameters`, with tests), but GitHub isn't letting me open the pull request right now (`does not have the correct permissions to execute CreatePullRequest`). Is there a limit on open pull requests per contributor? I'll open it as soon as I can.\n", "created_at": "2026-11-02T22:18:14Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23715, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23715", "issue_numbers": ["23714"], "base_commit": "0820e108e46743a49c83cc672b3f3900a52cee18", "patch": "diff --git a/docs/v3/concepts/states.mdx b/docs/v3/concepts/states.mdx\nindex a382c49b33ca..307ae8b04fb8 100644\n--- a/docs/v3/concepts/states.mdx\n+++ b/docs/v3/concepts/states.mdx\n@@ -280,6 +280,11 @@ def my_flow_state_hook(flow: Flow, run: FlowRun, state: State) -> None:\n     ...\n ```\n \n+A flow state hook gets the flow, the flow run, and the state by name when its parameters are named `flow`, `flow_run` (or `run`), and `state`, in any order and including keyword-only parameters.\n+Any other flow state hook gets them as three positional arguments, whatever its parameter names.\n+If a hook accepts neither form, Prefect doesn't call it and logs an error.\n+Task state hooks get the task, the task run, and the state as three positional arguments.\n+\n State change hooks are versatile, allowing you to specify multiple state change hooks for the same state transition,\n or to use the same state change hook for different transitions.\n \ndiff --git a/src/prefect/_internal/engine.py b/src/prefect/_internal/engine.py\nindex 624e44845ee3..c13d5ba7d920 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/_internal/engine.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/_internal/engine.py\n@@ -1,5 +1,6 @@\n \"\"\"Internal engine utilities\"\"\"\n \n+import inspect\n from collections.abc import Callable\n from functools import partial\n from typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Any, Union\n@@ -9,6 +10,9 @@\n from prefect.flows import Flow\n from prefect.tasks import Task, TaskRunNameCallbackWithParameters\n \n+if TYPE_CHECKING:\n+    from prefect.client.schemas.objects import FlowRun, State\n+\n \n def dynamic_key_for_task_run(\n     context: FlowRunContext, task: \"Task[..., Any]\", stable: bool = True\n@@ -93,3 +97,58 @@ def get_hook_name(hook: Callable[..., Any]) -> str:\n             hook.func.__name__ if isinstance(hook, partial) else hook.__class__.__name__\n         )\n     )\n+\n+\n+def resolve_flow_hook_arguments(\n+    hook: Callable[..., Any],\n+    flow: \"Flow[..., Any]\",\n+    flow_run: \"FlowRun\",\n+    state: \"State\",\n+) -> tuple[tuple[Any, ...], dict[str, Any]]:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Return the positional and keyword arguments to call a flow state hook with,\n+    checking its signature without calling it.\n+\n+    A hook that names its parameters `flow`, `flow_run` (or `run`) and `state`\n+    gets them by name, in any order and including keyword-only parameters.\n+    Otherwise, a hook that takes three positional arguments gets them in that\n+    order, whatever their names, and a hook that only takes keyword arguments\n+    gets them by name.\n+\n+    Raises:\n+        TypeError: if the hook accepts none of these forms.\n+    \"\"\"\n+    try:\n+        signature = inspect.signature(hook)\n+    except (TypeError, ValueError):\n+        return (flow, flow_run, state), {}\n+\n+    def binds(*args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> bool:\n+        try:\n+            signature.bind(*args, **kwargs)\n+        except TypeError:\n+            return False\n+        return True\n+\n+    named = (inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD, inspect.Parameter.KEYWORD_ONLY)\n+    for flow_run_name in (\"flow_run\", \"run\"):\n+        kwargs = {\"flow\": flow, flow_run_name: flow_run, \"state\": state}\n+        declared = all(\n+            name in signature.parameters and signature.parameters[name].kind in named\n+            for name in kwargs\n+        )\n+        if declared and binds(**kwargs):\n+            return (), kwargs\n+\n+    if binds(flow, flow_run, state):\n+        return (flow, flow_run, state), {}\n+\n+    kwargs = {\"flow\": flow, \"flow_run\": flow_run, \"state\": state}\n+    if binds(**kwargs):\n+        return (), kwargs\n+\n+    raise TypeError(\n+        f\"Hook {get_hook_name(hook)!r} must accept the flow, the flow run and the\"\n+        \" state as three positional arguments or as parameters named `flow`,\"\n+        \" `flow_run` (or `run`) and `state`\"\n+    )\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/flow_engine.py b/src/prefect/flow_engine.py\nindex d0ca875ec015..fc0131411c14 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/flow_engine.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/flow_engine.py\n@@ -59,7 +59,11 @@\n     get_intent,\n     report_engine_outcome,\n )\n-from prefect._internal.engine import get_hook_name, resolve_custom_flow_run_name\n+from prefect._internal.engine import (\n+    get_hook_name,\n+    resolve_custom_flow_run_name,\n+    resolve_flow_hook_arguments,\n+)\n from prefect._internal.metrics import RunMetrics\n from prefect.client.orchestration import PrefectClient, SyncPrefectClient, get_client\n from prefect.client.schemas import FlowRun, TaskRun\n@@ -1072,7 +1076,8 @@ def call_hooks(self, state: Optional[State] = None) -> None:\n                     f\"Running hook {hook_name!r} in response to entering state\"\n                     f\" {state.name!r}\"\n                 )\n-                result = hook(flow, flow_run, state)\n+                args, kwargs = resolve_flow_hook_arguments(hook, flow, flow_run, state)\n+                result = hook(*args, **kwargs)\n                 if asyncio.iscoroutine(result):\n                     run_coro_as_sync(result)\n             except Exception:\n@@ -1803,7 +1808,8 @@ async def call_hooks(self, state: Optional[State] = None) -> None:\n                     f\"Running hook {hook_name!r} in response to entering state\"\n                     f\" {state.name!r}\"\n                 )\n-                result = hook(flow, flow_run, state)\n+                args, kwargs = resolve_flow_hook_arguments(hook, flow, flow_run, state)\n+                result = hook(*args, **kwargs)\n                 if asyncio.iscoroutine(result):\n                     await result\n             except Exception:\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/flows.py b/src/prefect/flows.py\nindex 72e90aa3b018..646dac9ef1a9 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/flows.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/flows.py\n@@ -121,15 +121,22 @@\n class FlowStateHook(Protocol, Generic[P, R]):\n     \"\"\"\n     A callable that is invoked when a flow enters a given state.\n+\n+    It receives the flow, the flow run, and the state as its first three\n+    parameters, whatever their names. A hook whose parameters are named `flow`,\n+    `flow_run` (or `run`), and `state` receives them by name instead.\n     \"\"\"\n \n     __name__: str\n \n     def __call__(\n-        self, flow: Flow[P, R], flow_run: FlowRun, state: State\n+        self, flow: Flow[P, R], flow_run: FlowRun, state: State, /\n     ) -> Awaitable[None] | None: ...\n \n \n+_FlowStateHookT = TypeVar(\"_FlowStateHookT\", bound=\"FlowStateHook[..., Any]\")\n+\n+\n if TYPE_CHECKING:\n     import logging\n \n@@ -1004,23 +1011,23 @@ def my_other_flow(name):\n                 _sla=_sla,\n             )\n \n-    def on_completion(self, fn: FlowStateHook[P, R]) -> FlowStateHook[P, R]:\n+    def on_completion(self, fn: _FlowStateHookT) -> _FlowStateHookT:\n         self.on_completion_hooks.append(fn)\n         return fn\n \n-    def on_cancellation(self, fn: FlowStateHook[P, R]) -> FlowStateHook[P, R]:\n+    def on_cancellation(self, fn: _FlowStateHookT) -> _FlowStateHookT:\n         self.on_cancellation_hooks.append(fn)\n         return fn\n \n-    def on_crashed(self, fn: FlowStateHook[P, R]) -> FlowStateHook[P, R]:\n+    def on_crashed(self, fn: _FlowStateHookT) -> _FlowStateHookT:\n         self.on_crashed_hooks.append(fn)\n         return fn\n \n-    def on_running(self, fn: FlowStateHook[P, R]) -> FlowStateHook[P, R]:\n+    def on_running(self, fn: _FlowStateHookT) -> _FlowStateHookT:\n         self.on_running_hooks.append(fn)\n         return fn\n \n-    def on_failure(self, fn: FlowStateHook[P, R]) -> FlowStateHook[P, R]:\n+    def on_failure(self, fn: _FlowStateHookT) -> _FlowStateHookT:\n         self.on_failure_hooks.append(fn)\n         return fn\n \ndiff --git a/src/prefect/runner/_hook_runner.py b/src/prefect/runner/_hook_runner.py\nindex cecd6d7c4709..1cef4fef72c5 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/runner/_hook_runner.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/runner/_hook_runner.py\n@@ -3,7 +3,7 @@\n from typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Any, Awaitable, Callable, Protocol\n \n from prefect._internal.concurrency.api import create_call, from_async\n-from prefect._internal.engine import get_hook_name\n+from prefect._internal.engine import get_hook_name, resolve_flow_hook_arguments\n from prefect.logging.loggers import flow_run_logger\n from prefect.utilities.asyncutils import is_async_fn\n \n@@ -35,12 +35,11 @@ async def _run_hooks(\n                 hook_name,\n                 state.name,\n             )\n+            args, kwargs = resolve_flow_hook_arguments(hook, flow, flow_run, state)\n             if is_async_fn(hook):\n-                await hook(flow=flow, flow_run=flow_run, state=state)\n+                await hook(*args, **kwargs)\n             else:\n-                await from_async.call_in_new_thread(\n-                    create_call(hook, flow=flow, flow_run=flow_run, state=state)\n-                )\n+                await from_async.call_in_new_thread(create_call(hook, *args, **kwargs))\n         except Exception:\n             logger.error(\n                 \"An error was encountered while running hook %r\",\ndiff --git a/src/prefect/runner/_workspace_runtime_bootstrap.py b/src/prefect/runner/_workspace_runtime_bootstrap.py\nindex 95d0594a7235..500d5837a75f 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/runner/_workspace_runtime_bootstrap.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/runner/_workspace_runtime_bootstrap.py\n@@ -109,6 +109,52 @@ async def _load_flow(manifest: dict[str, Any]) -> Flow[Any, Any]:\n         return await _run_sync(load_function_and_convert_to_flow, entrypoint)\n \n \n+def _flow_hook_arguments(\n+    hook: Callable[..., Any],\n+    flow: Flow[Any, Any],\n+    flow_run: FlowRun,\n+    state: State,\n+) -> tuple[tuple[Any, ...], dict[str, Any]]:\n+    # Same rule as `prefect._internal.engine.resolve_flow_hook_arguments`. This\n+    # module runs with the flow's runtime, which may have an older Prefect\n+    # without that function, so it keeps its own copy.\n+    try:\n+        signature = inspect.signature(hook)\n+    except (TypeError, ValueError):\n+        return (flow, flow_run, state), {}\n+\n+    def binds(*args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> bool:\n+        try:\n+            signature.bind(*args, **kwargs)\n+        except TypeError:\n+            return False\n+        return True\n+\n+    named = (inspect.Parameter.POSITIONAL_OR_KEYWORD, inspect.Parameter.KEYWORD_ONLY)\n+    for flow_run_name in (\"flow_run\", \"run\"):\n+        kwargs = {\"flow\": flow, flow_run_name: flow_run, \"state\": state}\n+        declared = all(\n+            name in signature.parameters and signature.parameters[name].kind in named\n+            for name in kwargs\n+        )\n+        if declared and binds(**kwargs):\n+            return (), kwargs\n+\n+    if binds(flow, flow_run, state):\n+        return (flow, flow_run, state), {}\n+\n+    kwargs = {\"flow\": flow, \"flow_run\": flow_run, \"state\": state}\n+    if binds(**kwargs):\n+        return (), kwargs\n+\n+    hook_name = getattr(hook, \"__name__\", type(hook).__name__)\n+    raise TypeError(\n+        f\"Hook {hook_name!r} must accept the flow, the flow run and the state as\"\n+        \" three positional arguments or as parameters named `flow`, `flow_run`\"\n+        \" (or `run`) and `state`\"\n+    )\n+\n+\n async def _run_hooks(\n     hooks: list[Callable[..., Any]],\n     flow_run: FlowRun,\n@@ -121,14 +167,8 @@ async def _run_hooks(\n     for hook in hooks:\n         hook_name = getattr(hook, \"__name__\", type(hook).__name__)\n         try:\n-            result = await _run_sync(\n-                functools.partial(\n-                    hook,\n-                    flow=flow,\n-                    flow_run=flow_run,\n-                    state=state,\n-                )\n-            )\n+            args, kwargs = _flow_hook_arguments(hook, flow, flow_run, state)\n+            result = await _run_sync(functools.partial(hook, *args, **kwargs))\n             if inspect.isawaitable(result):\n                 await result\n         except Exception:\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/runner/test__hook_runner.py b/tests/runner/test__hook_runner.py\nindex 5765a6f72632..2f54a32b8fc8 100644\n--- a/tests/runner/test__hook_runner.py\n+++ b/tests/runner/test__hook_runner.py\n@@ -56,7 +56,7 @@ async def test_run_hooks_async_hook_is_awaited(self):\n \n         await _run_hooks([async_hook], flow_run, flow, state)\n \n-        async_hook.assert_awaited_once_with(flow=flow, flow_run=flow_run, state=state)\n+        async_hook.assert_awaited_once_with(flow, flow_run, state)\n \n     async def test_run_hooks_sync_hook_runs_in_thread(self):\n         captured: dict[str, object] = {}\n@@ -76,6 +76,82 @@ def my_sync_hook(flow, flow_run, state):\n         assert captured[\"flow_run\"] is flow_run\n         assert captured[\"state\"] is state\n \n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"signature\",\n+        [\n+            \"alternate_names\",\n+            \"documented_run_name\",\n+            \"keyword_only\",\n+            \"reordered_names\",\n+            \"async_keyword_only\",\n+        ],\n+    )\n+    async def test_run_hooks_passes_flow_flow_run_and_state_to_each_signature(\n+        self, signature\n+    ):\n+        received: list[tuple[object, object, object]] = []\n+\n+        def alternate_names(flow_, run, state_):\n+            received.append((flow_, run, state_))\n+\n+        def documented_run_name(flow, run, state):\n+            received.append((flow, run, state))\n+\n+        def keyword_only(*, flow, flow_run, state):\n+            received.append((flow, flow_run, state))\n+\n+        def reordered_names(state, flow, flow_run):\n+            received.append((flow, flow_run, state))\n+\n+        async def async_keyword_only(*, flow, flow_run, state):\n+            received.append((flow, flow_run, state))\n+\n+        hooks = {\n+            hook.__name__: hook\n+            for hook in (\n+                alternate_names,\n+                documented_run_name,\n+                keyword_only,\n+                reordered_names,\n+                async_keyword_only,\n+            )\n+        }\n+        hook = hooks[signature]\n+        flow_run = _make_flow_run()\n+        flow = _make_flow()\n+        state = _make_state(name=\"Crashed\")\n+\n+        await _run_hooks([hook], flow_run, flow, state)\n+\n+        assert received == [(flow, flow_run, state)]\n+\n+    async def test_run_hooks_runs_a_hook_that_raises_type_error_once(self, caplog):\n+        calls: list[object] = []\n+\n+        def hook(flow, flow_run, state):\n+            calls.append(state)\n+            raise TypeError(\"raised inside the hook\")\n+\n+        with caplog.at_level(logging.ERROR, logger=\"prefect.flow_runs\"):\n+            await _run_hooks([hook], _make_flow_run(), _make_flow(), _make_state())\n+\n+        assert len(calls) == 1\n+        assert \"raised inside the hook\" in caplog.text\n+\n+    async def test_run_hooks_does_not_call_a_hook_with_an_incompatible_signature(\n+        self, caplog\n+    ):\n+        calls: list[object] = []\n+\n+        def hook(flow, flow_run):\n+            calls.append(flow_run)\n+\n+        with caplog.at_level(logging.ERROR, logger=\"prefect.flow_runs\"):\n+            await _run_hooks([hook], _make_flow_run(), _make_flow(), _make_state())\n+\n+        assert calls == []\n+        assert \"must accept the flow, the flow run and the state\" in caplog.text\n+\n     async def test_run_hooks_logs_hook_name_on_start(self, caplog):\n         flow_run = _make_flow_run()\n         flow = _make_flow()\n@@ -103,7 +179,7 @@ async def test_run_hooks_hook_failure_logs_error_and_continues(self, caplog):\n             await _run_hooks([failing_hook, second_hook], flow_run, flow, state)\n \n         assert \"bad_hook\" in caplog.text\n-        second_hook.assert_awaited_once_with(flow=flow, flow_run=flow_run, state=state)\n+        second_hook.assert_awaited_once_with(flow, flow_run, state)\n \n     async def test_run_hooks_successful_hook_logs_finished(self, caplog):\n         flow_run = _make_flow_run()\n@@ -141,7 +217,7 @@ async def test_run_cancellation_hooks_resolves_and_runs(self):\n         await runner.run_cancellation_hooks(flow_run, state)\n \n         resolve_flow.assert_awaited_once_with(flow_run)\n-        async_hook.assert_awaited_once_with(flow=flow, flow_run=flow_run, state=state)\n+        async_hook.assert_awaited_once_with(flow, flow_run, state)\n \n     async def test_run_cancellation_hooks_resolution_failure_logs_warning(self, caplog):\n         resolve_flow = AsyncMock(side_effect=ValueError(\"cannot resolve\"))\n@@ -189,7 +265,7 @@ async def test_run_crashed_hooks_resolves_and_runs(self):\n         await runner.run_crashed_hooks(flow_run, state)\n \n         resolve_flow.assert_awaited_once_with(flow_run)\n-        async_hook.assert_awaited_once_with(flow=flow, flow_run=flow_run, state=state)\n+        async_hook.assert_awaited_once_with(flow, flow_run, state)\n \n     async def test_run_crashed_hooks_resolution_failure_logs_warning(self, caplog):\n         resolve_flow = AsyncMock(side_effect=ValueError(\"cannot resolve\"))\n@@ -238,7 +314,7 @@ async def resolve_flow(fr):\n             await runner.run_crashed_hooks(flow_run, state)\n \n         mock_load.assert_awaited_once_with(flow_run, storage_base_path=\"/tmp\")\n-        crashed_hook.assert_awaited_once_with(flow=flow, flow_run=flow_run, state=state)\n+        crashed_hook.assert_awaited_once_with(flow, flow_run, state)\n \n     async def test_explicit_client_positional_arg_breaks_crashed_hooks(self, caplog):\n         \"\"\"Demonstrate the original bug: passing a client as the first positional\n@@ -310,7 +386,7 @@ async def resolve_flow(fr):\n             await runner.run_cancellation_hooks(flow_run, state)\n \n         mock_load.assert_awaited_once_with(flow_run=flow_run)\n-        cancel_hook.assert_awaited_once_with(flow=flow, flow_run=flow_run, state=state)\n+        cancel_hook.assert_awaited_once_with(flow, flow_run, state)\n \n     async def test_explicit_client_positional_arg_causes_misrouted_flow_run(\n         self, caplog\ndiff --git a/tests/runner/test__workspace_runtime_bootstrap.py b/tests/runner/test__workspace_runtime_bootstrap.py\nindex fea38f211ab1..c44a143ccefd 100644\n--- a/tests/runner/test__workspace_runtime_bootstrap.py\n+++ b/tests/runner/test__workspace_runtime_bootstrap.py\n@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@\n from __future__ import annotations\n \n import importlib.metadata\n+import logging\n import os\n import sys\n from pathlib import Path\n@@ -129,3 +130,87 @@ def assert_legacy_context(*_args: object, **_kwargs: object) -> None:\n     run_module.assert_called_once_with(\"prefect.engine\", run_name=\"__main__\")\n     assert _workspace_runtime_bootstrap.sys.argv is original_argv\n     assert os.environ[\"PREFECT__FLOW_ENTRYPOINT\"] == \"flows.py:original\"\n+\n+\n+@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+    \"signature\",\n+    [\n+        \"alternate_names\",\n+        \"documented_run_name\",\n+        \"keyword_only\",\n+        \"reordered_names\",\n+        \"async_keyword_only\",\n+    ],\n+)\n+async def test_run_hooks_passes_flow_flow_run_and_state_to_each_signature(\n+    signature: str,\n+):\n+    received: list[tuple[object, object, object]] = []\n+\n+    def alternate_names(flow_, run, state_):\n+        received.append((flow_, run, state_))\n+\n+    def documented_run_name(flow, run, state):\n+        received.append((flow, run, state))\n+\n+    def keyword_only(*, flow, flow_run, state):\n+        received.append((flow, flow_run, state))\n+\n+    def reordered_names(state, flow, flow_run):\n+        received.append((flow, flow_run, state))\n+\n+    async def async_keyword_only(*, flow, flow_run, state):\n+        received.append((flow, flow_run, state))\n+\n+    hooks = {\n+        hook.__name__: hook\n+        for hook in (\n+            alternate_names,\n+            documented_run_name,\n+            keyword_only,\n+            reordered_names,\n+            async_keyword_only,\n+        )\n+    }\n+    flow_run, flow, state = MagicMock(), MagicMock(), MagicMock()\n+\n+    await _workspace_runtime_bootstrap._run_hooks(\n+        [hooks[signature]], flow_run, flow, state\n+    )\n+\n+    assert received == [(flow, flow_run, state)]\n+\n+\n+async def test_run_hooks_runs_a_hook_that_raises_type_error_once(\n+    caplog: pytest.LogCaptureFixture,\n+):\n+    calls: list[object] = []\n+\n+    def hook(flow, flow_run, state):\n+        calls.append(state)\n+        raise TypeError(\"raised inside the hook\")\n+\n+    with caplog.at_level(logging.ERROR, logger=\"prefect.flow_runs\"):\n+        await _workspace_runtime_bootstrap._run_hooks(\n+            [hook], MagicMock(), MagicMock(), MagicMock()\n+        )\n+\n+    assert len(calls) == 1\n+    assert \"raised inside the hook\" in caplog.text\n+\n+\n+async def test_run_hooks_does_not_call_a_hook_with_an_incompatible_signature(\n+    caplog: pytest.LogCaptureFixture,\n+):\n+    calls: list[object] = []\n+\n+    def hook(flow, flow_run):\n+        calls.append(flow_run)\n+\n+    with caplog.at_level(logging.ERROR, logger=\"prefect.flow_runs\"):\n+        await _workspace_runtime_bootstrap._run_hooks(\n+            [hook], MagicMock(), MagicMock(), MagicMock()\n+        )\n+\n+    assert calls == []\n+    assert \"must accept the flow, the flow run and the state\" in caplog.text\ndiff --git a/tests/runner/test_runner.py b/tests/runner/test_runner.py\nindex b0be7466b8ee..355d9334740d 100644\n--- a/tests/runner/test_runner.py\n+++ b/tests/runner/test_runner.py\n@@ -1048,23 +1048,30 @@ def cancel_flow(sleep_time: int = 100):\n         )\n \n     @pytest.mark.usefixtures(\"use_hosted_api_server\")\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"flow_param, flow_run_param\",\n+        [(\"flow\", \"flow_run\"), (\"flow_\", \"run\")],\n+        ids=[\"documented-names\", \"other-names\"],\n+    )\n     async def test_runner_runs_on_crashed_hooks_for_remotely_stored_flows(\n         self,\n         prefect_client: PrefectClient,\n         caplog: pytest.LogCaptureFixture,\n         in_temporary_runner_directory: None,\n         temp_storage: MockStorage,\n+        flow_param: str,\n+        flow_run_param: str,\n     ):\n         runner = Runner(pause_on_shutdown=False)\n         temp_storage.code = dedent(\n-            \"\"\"\\\n+            f\"\"\"\\\n         import os\n \n         from prefect import flow\n         from prefect.logging.loggers import flow_run_logger\n \n-        def on_crashed(flow, flow_run, state):\n-            logger = flow_run_logger(flow_run, flow)\n+        def on_crashed({flow_param}, {flow_run_param}, state):\n+            logger = flow_run_logger({flow_run_param}, {flow_param})\n             logger.info(\"This flow crashed!\")\n \n         @flow(on_crashed=[on_crashed], log_prints=True)\ndiff --git a/tests/test_flows.py b/tests/test_flows.py\nindex 867fc0515708..ac165de589d9 100644\n--- a/tests/test_flows.py\n+++ b/tests/test_flows.py\n@@ -58,6 +58,7 @@\n from prefect.filesystems import LocalFileSystem\n from prefect.flows import (\n     Flow,\n+    FlowStateHook,\n     load_flow_arguments_from_entrypoint,\n     load_flow_from_entrypoint,\n     load_flow_from_flow_run,\n@@ -3407,6 +3408,138 @@ def foo_flow():\n         assert data == dict(name=\"hook\", state=state, kwargs={\"foo\": 42})\n \n \n+def _hooks_by_signature(received: list[tuple[Any, Any, Any]]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n+    def alternate_names(flow_, run, state_):\n+        received.append((flow_, run, state_))\n+\n+    def documented_run_name(flow, run, state):\n+        received.append((flow, run, state))\n+\n+    def keyword_only(*, flow, flow_run, state):\n+        received.append((flow, flow_run, state))\n+\n+    def reordered_names(state, flow, flow_run):\n+        received.append((flow, flow_run, state))\n+\n+    async def async_keyword_only(*, flow, flow_run, state):\n+        received.append((flow, flow_run, state))\n+\n+    return {\n+        hook.__name__: hook\n+        for hook in (\n+            alternate_names,\n+            documented_run_name,\n+            keyword_only,\n+            reordered_names,\n+            async_keyword_only,\n+        )\n+    }\n+\n+\n+HOOK_SIGNATURES = [\n+    \"alternate_names\",\n+    \"documented_run_name\",\n+    \"keyword_only\",\n+    \"reordered_names\",\n+    \"async_keyword_only\",\n+]\n+\n+\n+class TestFlowHookSignatures:\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"signature\", HOOK_SIGNATURES)\n+    def test_hook_receives_flow_flow_run_and_state(self, signature: str):\n+        received: list[tuple[Any, Any, Any]] = []\n+\n+        @flow(on_completion=[_hooks_by_signature(received)[signature]])\n+        def foo_flow():\n+            pass\n+\n+        state = foo_flow(return_state=True)\n+\n+        assert len(received) == 1\n+        hook_flow, hook_flow_run, hook_state = received[0]\n+        assert hook_flow is foo_flow\n+        assert hook_flow_run.id == state.state_details.flow_run_id\n+        assert hook_state == state\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"signature\", HOOK_SIGNATURES)\n+    async def test_hook_receives_flow_flow_run_and_state_in_async_flow(\n+        self, signature: str\n+    ):\n+        received: list[tuple[Any, Any, Any]] = []\n+\n+        @flow(on_completion=[_hooks_by_signature(received)[signature]])\n+        async def foo_flow():\n+            pass\n+\n+        state = await foo_flow(return_state=True)\n+\n+        assert len(received) == 1\n+        hook_flow, hook_flow_run, hook_state = received[0]\n+        assert hook_flow is foo_flow\n+        assert hook_flow_run.id == state.state_details.flow_run_id\n+        assert hook_state == state\n+\n+    def test_hook_that_raises_type_error_runs_once(self, caplog):\n+        calls: list[Any] = []\n+\n+        def hook(flow, flow_run, state):\n+            calls.append(state)\n+            raise TypeError(\"raised inside the hook\")\n+\n+        @flow(on_completion=[hook])\n+        def foo_flow():\n+            pass\n+\n+        foo_flow()\n+\n+        assert len(calls) == 1\n+        assert \"raised inside the hook\" in caplog.text\n+\n+    def test_hook_with_incompatible_signature_is_not_called(self, caplog):\n+        calls: list[Any] = []\n+\n+        def hook(flow, flow_run):\n+            calls.append(flow_run)\n+\n+        @flow(on_completion=[hook])\n+        def foo_flow():\n+            pass\n+\n+        foo_flow()\n+\n+        assert calls == []\n+        assert \"must accept the flow, the flow run and the state\" in caplog.text\n+\n+    @pytest.mark.parametrize(\n+        \"base\",\n+        [FlowStateHook, FlowStateHook[..., None]],\n+        ids=[\"protocol\", \"parametrized-protocol\"],\n+    )\n+    def test_flow_state_hook_subclass_receives_flow_flow_run_and_state(self, base: Any):\n+        received: list[tuple[Any, Any, Any]] = []\n+\n+        class Hook(base):\n+            def __call__(self, flow, flow_run, state):\n+                received.append((flow, flow_run, state))\n+\n+        hook = Hook()\n+\n+        @flow\n+        def foo_flow():\n+            pass\n+\n+        assert foo_flow.on_completion(hook) is hook\n+\n+        state = foo_flow(return_state=True)\n+\n+        assert len(received) == 1\n+        hook_flow, hook_flow_run, hook_state = received[0]\n+        assert hook_flow is foo_flow\n+        assert hook_flow_run.id == state.state_details.flow_run_id\n+        assert hook_state == state\n+\n+\n class TestFlowHooksOnCompletion:\n     def test_noniterable_hook_raises(self):\n         def completion_hook():\ndiff --git a/tests/typesafety/test_flows.yml b/tests/typesafety/test_flows.yml\nindex 40b9bc685dc9..5e2e13817d04 100644\n--- a/tests/typesafety/test_flows.yml\n+++ b/tests/typesafety/test_flows.yml\n@@ -83,3 +83,78 @@\n     main:7: note:     def __call__.*\n     main:7: note:     def __call__.*\n     main:7: note:     def __call__.*\n+\n+- case: prefect_flow_state_hook_parameter_names\n+  checkers: [mypy, pyright, ty]\n+  should_pass: true\n+  main: |\n+    from typing import Any\n+    from prefect import flow\n+    from prefect.client.schemas.objects import FlowRun\n+    from prefect.flows import Flow\n+    from prefect.states import State\n+\n+    def named(flow: Flow[..., Any], flow_run: FlowRun, state: State) -> None: ...\n+    def documented_run_name(flow: Flow[..., Any], run: FlowRun, state: State) -> None: ...\n+    def other_names(f: Flow[..., Any], r: FlowRun, s: State) -> None: ...\n+    async def async_named(flow: Flow[..., Any], flow_run: FlowRun, state: State) -> None: ...\n+\n+    @flow(on_completion=[named, documented_run_name, other_names, async_named])\n+    def my_flow() -> None: ...\n+\n+    @my_flow.on_failure\n+    def decorated(flow: Flow[..., Any], flow_run: FlowRun, state: State) -> None: ...\n+\n+    def call_decorated(f: Flow[..., Any], r: FlowRun, s: State) -> None:\n+        decorated(f, r, s)\n+        decorated(flow=f, flow_run=r, state=s)\n+\n+- case: prefect_flow_state_hook_subclass\n+  checkers: [mypy, pyright, ty]\n+  should_pass: true\n+  main: |\n+    from typing import Any\n+    from prefect import flow\n+    from prefect.flows import FlowStateHook\n+\n+    class Notify(FlowStateHook):\n+        __name__ = \"notify\"\n+\n+        def __call__(self, flow: Any, flow_run: Any, state: Any) -> None: ...\n+\n+    class NotifyOnFailure(FlowStateHook[..., None]):\n+        __name__ = \"notify_on_failure\"\n+\n+        def __call__(self, flow: Any, flow_run: Any, state: Any) -> None: ...\n+\n+    @flow(on_completion=[Notify()])\n+    def my_flow() -> None: ...\n+\n+    my_flow.on_failure(NotifyOnFailure())\n+\n+- case: prefect_flow_state_hook_list\n+  checkers: [mypy, pyright, ty]\n+  should_pass: true\n+  main: |\n+    from typing import Any\n+    from prefect import flow\n+    from prefect.flows import Flow, FlowStateHook\n+\n+    def build(hooks: list[FlowStateHook[..., Any]]) -> Flow[[], None]:\n+        @flow(on_completion=hooks)\n+        def my_flow() -> None: ...\n+\n+        return my_flow.with_options(on_failure=hooks)\n+\n+- case: prefect_flow_state_hook_wrong_parameter_count\n+  checkers: [mypy, pyright, ty]\n+  main: |\n+    from typing import Any\n+    from prefect import flow\n+    from prefect.client.schemas.objects import FlowRun\n+    from prefect.flows import Flow\n+\n+    def too_few(flow: Flow[..., Any], flow_run: FlowRun) -> None: ...\n+\n+    @flow(on_completion=[too_few])  # E:\n+    def my_flow() -> None: ...\n", "problem_statement": "Runner calls `on_crashed` hooks by keyword, so a hook that works for `on_failure` fails on crash\n### Bug summary\n\nThe flow engine calls state hooks positionally (`hook(flow, flow_run, state)`), but the runner calls `on_crashed` and `on_cancellation` hooks by keyword (`hook(flow=..., flow_run=..., state=...)`). So a hook whose parameters have other names works for `on_failure`, but when the run crashes the call raises `TypeError`, the runner only logs it, and the hook never runs.\n\nWe hit this in production with a process worker: a notification hook declared as `def failure_hook(flow_, run, state)` was attached to both `on_failure` and `on_crashed`. Failures sent the alert, crashes didn't, and the only trace was an ERROR line in the worker log.\n\nMinimal repro with `.serve()` against a local server:\n\n```python\nimport os\n\nfrom prefect import flow\n\n\ndef notify(flow_, run, state):\n    print(f\"notify() ran for state {state.name!r}\", flush=True)\n\n\n@flow(on_failure=[notify], on_crashed=[notify])\ndef hook_names(crash: bool = False):\n    if crash:\n        os._exit(1)  # the flow run process dies, so the runner marks the run Crashed\n    raise ValueError(\"this flow run fails\")  # the engine marks the run Failed\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    hook_names.serve(name=\"repro\")\n```\n\nWith `python flow.py` running:\n\n```bash\nprefect deployment run 'hook-names/repro'\nprefect deployment run 'hook-names/repro' -p crash=true\n```\n\nOutput of the `serve` process:\n\n```\nINFO    | Flow run 'xanthic-mamba' - Running hook 'notify' in response to entering state 'Failed'\nnotify() ran for state 'Failed'\nINFO    | Flow run 'xanthic-mamba' - Hook 'notify' finished running successfully\nERROR   | prefect.runner.flow_run_executor - Process exited with status code: 1. Process exited with a general error.\nINFO    | Flow run 'sensible-sheep' - Running hook 'notify' in response to entering state 'Crashed'\nERROR   | Flow run 'sensible-sheep' - An error was encountered while running hook 'notify'\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \".../site-packages/prefect/runner/_hook_runner.py\", line 41, in _run_hooks\n    await from_async.call_in_new_thread(\n        create_call(hook, flow=flow, flow_run=flow_run, state=state)\n    )\n  File \".../site-packages/prefect/_internal/concurrency/calls.py\", line 374, in aresult\n    return await asyncio.wrap_future(self.future)\n           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \".../site-packages/prefect/_internal/concurrency/calls.py\", line 399, in _run_sync\n    result = self.fn(*self.args, **self.kwargs)\nTypeError: notify() got an unexpected keyword argument 'flow'. Did you mean 'flow_'?\n```\n\nExpected: `notify()` runs for the crashed run too, like it does for the failed one.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.9.1\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.13.15\nGit commit:           bd4807c3\nBuilt:                Fri, Oct 30, 2026 03:52 PM\nOS/Arch:              win32/AMD64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          ephemeral\nPydantic version:     2.14.2\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.50.4\n```\n\nSame on `main`. The production case was a Linux process worker on 3.8.6, and the two calling conventions go back to 3.0.0.\n\n### Additional context\n\nThe engine calls hooks positionally:\n- https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/55b3bb8c11d7a2bfa290ac24a1e5d27b7cddcb2a/src/prefect/flow_engine.py#L1075\n- https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/55b3bb8c11d7a2bfa290ac24a1e5d27b7cddcb2a/src/prefect/flow_engine.py#L1806\n\nThe runner calls them by keyword, for served runs (the traceback above) and for worker runs:\n- https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/55b3bb8c11d7a2bfa290ac24a1e5d27b7cddcb2a/src/prefect/runner/_hook_runner.py#L38-L43\n- https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/55b3bb8c11d7a2bfa290ac24a1e5d27b7cddcb2a/src/prefect/runner/_workspace_runtime_bootstrap.py#L124-L131\n\n`FlowStateHook` names the parameters `flow, flow_run, state` (https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/55b3bb8c11d7a2bfa290ac24a1e5d27b7cddcb2a/src/prefect/flows.py#L121-L130), but nothing checks hook signatures, and the engine accepts any names.\n\nProposed fix: make the runner call hooks positionally, like the engine, in both places. I have it working with tests and will open the PR right after this. If you'd rather validate hook signatures when the flow is defined, as suggested in the review of #9715, happy to switch.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-02T18:22:37Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23712, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23712", "issue_numbers": ["23711"], "base_commit": "55b3bb8c11d7a2bfa290ac24a1e5d27b7cddcb2a", "patch": "diff --git a/src/prefect/runner/storage.py b/src/prefect/runner/storage.py\nindex cdc287960317..17895935096b 100644\n--- a/src/prefect/runner/storage.py\n+++ b/src/prefect/runner/storage.py\n@@ -459,12 +459,27 @@ async def _pull_code_locked(self) -> None:\n             # read-only attribute first to avoid `[WinError 5] Access is denied`.\n             _clear_read_only_attributes(git_dir)\n \n-            # Sparsely checkout the repository if directories are specified and the repo is not in sparse-checkout mode already\n-            if self._directories and not await self.is_sparsely_checked_out():\n+            # Make the checkout match the configured directories. The destination\n+            # can be reused by runs whose `directories` differ (a redeploy that\n+            # changed the list, or deployments sharing a working directory), so\n+            # an existing sparse checkout only grows: narrowing it would remove\n+            # files that another run may still be using.\n+            is_sparse = await self.is_sparsely_checked_out()\n+            if self._directories:\n                 await run_process(\n-                    [\"git\", \"sparse-checkout\", \"set\", \"--\", *self._directories],\n+                    [\n+                        \"git\",\n+                        \"sparse-checkout\",\n+                        \"add\" if is_sparse else \"set\",\n+                        \"--\",\n+                        *self._directories,\n+                    ],\n                     cwd=self.destination,\n                 )\n+            elif is_sparse:\n+                await run_process(\n+                    [\"git\", \"sparse-checkout\", \"disable\"], cwd=self.destination\n+                )\n \n             self._logger.debug(\"Pulling latest changes from origin/%s\", self._branch)\n             # Update the existing repository\n", "test_patch": "diff --git a/tests/runner/test_storage.py b/tests/runner/test_storage.py\nindex 287209e13449..d512d3dad1fb 100644\n--- a/tests/runner/test_storage.py\n+++ b/tests/runner/test_storage.py\n@@ -612,6 +612,10 @@ async def test_pull_code_with_commit_sha(\n                 [\"git\", \"config\", \"--get\", \"remote.origin.url\"],\n                 cwd=str(Path.cwd() / \"repo\"),\n             ),\n+            call(\n+                [\"git\", \"config\", \"--get\", \"core.sparseCheckout\"],\n+                cwd=Path.cwd() / \"repo\",\n+            ),\n             call(\n                 [\"git\", \"fetch\", \"origin\", \"--unshallow\", \"1234567890\"],\n                 cwd=Path.cwd() / \"repo\",\n@@ -650,12 +654,16 @@ async def test_pull_code_with_commit_sha_when_current_commit(\n \n         await repo.pull_code()\n \n-        # Should only check the remote URL since the commit is already checked out\n+        # Should only inspect the checkout since the commit is already checked out\n         expected_calls = [\n             call(\n                 [\"git\", \"config\", \"--get\", \"remote.origin.url\"],\n                 cwd=str(Path.cwd() / \"repo\"),\n             ),\n+            call(\n+                [\"git\", \"config\", \"--get\", \"core.sparseCheckout\"],\n+                cwd=Path.cwd() / \"repo\",\n+            ),\n         ]\n \n         mock_run_process.assert_has_awaits(expected_calls)\n@@ -1999,6 +2007,109 @@ async def test_pull_code_updates_submodules_in_place(\n         assert in_use.exists(), \"pull_code deleted and re-cloned the checkout\"\n \n \n+class TestGitRepositorySparseCheckout:\n+    \"\"\"\n+    An existing sparse checkout must follow the configured `directories`: a\n+    destination can be reused after a redeploy that changed the list, or by\n+    deployments with different `directories` sharing a working directory.\n+    \"\"\"\n+\n+    @pytest.fixture\n+    def remote(self, tmp_path: Path, monkeypatch: pytest.MonkeyPatch) -> Path:\n+        monkeypatch.setenv(\"GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL\", os.devnull)\n+        monkeypatch.setenv(\"GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM\", \"1\")\n+        for role in (\"AUTHOR\", \"COMMITTER\"):\n+            monkeypatch.setenv(f\"GIT_{role}_NAME\", \"test\")\n+            monkeypatch.setenv(f\"GIT_{role}_EMAIL\", \"test@example.com\")\n+\n+        remote = tmp_path / \"remote.git\"\n+        work = tmp_path / \"work\"\n+        _git(\"init\", \"--bare\", str(remote), cwd=tmp_path)\n+        _git(\"clone\", str(remote), str(work), cwd=tmp_path)\n+        _git(\"symbolic-ref\", \"HEAD\", \"refs/heads/main\", cwd=work)\n+        for directory in (\"flows_a\", \"flows_b\"):\n+            (work / directory).mkdir()\n+            (work / directory / \"flow.py\").write_text(f\"NAME = {directory!r}\\n\")\n+        _git(\"add\", \".\", cwd=work)\n+        _git(\"commit\", \"-m\", \"v1\", cwd=work)\n+        _git(\"push\", \"origin\", \"main\", cwd=work)\n+        return remote\n+\n+    async def _pull(\n+        self,\n+        remote: Path,\n+        tmp_path: Path,\n+        directories: list[str] | None,\n+        commit_sha: str | None = None,\n+    ) -> GitRepository:\n+        repo = GitRepository(\n+            url=remote.as_uri(),\n+            branch=None if commit_sha else \"main\",\n+            commit_sha=commit_sha,\n+            directories=directories,\n+        )\n+        repo.set_base_path(tmp_path / \"storage\")\n+        await repo.pull_code()\n+        return repo\n+\n+    async def test_pull_code_checks_out_directories_added_to_existing_sparse_checkout(\n+        self, remote: Path, tmp_path: Path\n+    ):\n+        await self._pull(remote, tmp_path, [\"flows_a\"])\n+\n+        repo = await self._pull(remote, tmp_path, [\"flows_a\", \"flows_b\"])\n+\n+        assert (repo.destination / \"flows_a\" / \"flow.py\").exists()\n+        assert (repo.destination / \"flows_b\" / \"flow.py\").exists()\n+\n+    async def test_pull_code_checks_out_directory_added_in_new_remote_commit(\n+        self, remote: Path, tmp_path: Path\n+    ):\n+        await self._pull(remote, tmp_path, [\"flows_a\"])\n+        work = tmp_path / \"work\"\n+        (work / \"flows_c\").mkdir()\n+        (work / \"flows_c\" / \"flow.py\").write_text(\"NAME = 'flows_c'\\n\")\n+        _git(\"add\", \".\", cwd=work)\n+        _git(\"commit\", \"-m\", \"add flows_c\", cwd=work)\n+        _git(\"push\", \"origin\", \"main\", cwd=work)\n+\n+        repo = await self._pull(remote, tmp_path, [\"flows_a\", \"flows_c\"])\n+\n+        assert (repo.destination / \"flows_c\" / \"flow.py\").exists()\n+\n+    async def test_pull_code_checks_out_directories_added_at_pinned_commit(\n+        self, remote: Path, tmp_path: Path\n+    ):\n+        sha = _git(\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\", cwd=tmp_path / \"work\")\n+        await self._pull(remote, tmp_path, [\"flows_a\"], commit_sha=sha)\n+\n+        repo = await self._pull(remote, tmp_path, [\"flows_b\"], commit_sha=sha)\n+\n+        assert (repo.destination / \"flows_b\" / \"flow.py\").exists()\n+\n+    async def test_pull_code_keeps_directories_checked_out_for_other_runs(\n+        self, remote: Path, tmp_path: Path\n+    ):\n+        first = await self._pull(remote, tmp_path, [\"flows_a\"])\n+        # Stands in for a flow run that is using the first set of directories\n+        in_use = first.destination / \"flows_a\" / \"flow.py\"\n+\n+        second = await self._pull(remote, tmp_path, [\"flows_b\"])\n+\n+        assert (second.destination / \"flows_b\" / \"flow.py\").exists()\n+        assert in_use.exists(), \"pull_code removed a directory another run uses\"\n+\n+    async def test_pull_code_checks_out_whole_repository_when_directories_are_removed(\n+        self, remote: Path, tmp_path: Path\n+    ):\n+        await self._pull(remote, tmp_path, [\"flows_a\"])\n+\n+        repo = await self._pull(remote, tmp_path, None)\n+\n+        assert (repo.destination / \"flows_a\" / \"flow.py\").exists()\n+        assert (repo.destination / \"flows_b\" / \"flow.py\").exists()\n+\n+\n class TestRemoteStorageErrorHints:\n     \"\"\"Tests for _get_remote_storage_error_hint pattern matching.\"\"\"\n \n", "problem_statement": "`GitRepository.pull_code()` ignores changes to `directories` once the checkout is sparse\n### Bug summary\n\n`GitRepository.pull_code()` only runs `git sparse-checkout set` when the destination isn't a sparse checkout yet. Once it is, the `directories` from the first clone stick: a directory added later is never checked out, and removing `directories` leaves the checkout sparse.\n\nI ran into this while going back over `pull_code()` after #23351. It shows up whenever the destination is reused, like a `git_clone` pull step after `set_working_directory`: after a redeploy that adds a directory, or when deployments of the same repo with different `directories` share the working directory, the files the run needs aren't in the checkout.\n\nMinimal repro, no server needed (on 3.9.1 the second and third pulls still list only `flows_a`):\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nimport os\nimport subprocess\nimport tempfile\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfrom prefect.runner.storage import GitRepository\n\nos.environ |= {\"GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL\": os.devnull, \"GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM\": \"1\"}\nos.environ |= {f\"GIT_{r}_{f}\": v for r in (\"AUTHOR\", \"COMMITTER\") for f, v in ((\"NAME\", \"a\"), (\"EMAIL\", \"a@a\"))}\n\n\ndef git(*args, cwd):\n    subprocess.run([\"git\", *args], cwd=cwd, check=True, capture_output=True)\n\n\nasync def pull(url, base, directories):\n    storage = GitRepository(url=url, branch=\"main\", directories=directories)\n    storage.set_base_path(base)\n    await storage.pull_code()\n    return sorted(path.parent.name for path in storage.destination.glob(\"*/flow.py\"))\n\n\nasync def main():\n    root = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp())\n    git(\"init\", \"--bare\", \"remote.git\", cwd=root)\n    git(\"clone\", \"remote.git\", \"work\", cwd=root)\n    work = root / \"work\"\n    for name in (\"flows_a\", \"flows_b\"):\n        (work / name).mkdir()\n        (work / name / \"flow.py\").write_text(f\"# {name}\\n\")\n    git(\"add\", \".\", cwd=work)\n    git(\"commit\", \"-m\", \"init\", cwd=work)\n    git(\"push\", \"origin\", \"HEAD:main\", cwd=work)\n\n    url, base = (root / \"remote.git\").as_uri(), root / \"storage\"\n    print(\"directories=['flows_a']:\", await pull(url, base, [\"flows_a\"]))\n    print(\"directories=['flows_a', 'flows_b']:\", await pull(url, base, [\"flows_a\", \"flows_b\"]))\n    print(\"directories=None:\", await pull(url, base, None))\n\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\ndirectories=['flows_a']: ['flows_a']\ndirectories=['flows_a', 'flows_b']: ['flows_a']\ndirectories=None: ['flows_a']\n```\n\nExpected: `['flows_a', 'flows_b']` after the second and the third pull.\n\n### Version info\n\n```Text\nVersion:              3.9.1\nAPI version:          0.8.4\nPython version:       3.12.16\nGit commit:           bd4807c3\nBuilt:                Fri, Oct 30, 2026 03:52 PM\nOS/Arch:              linux/x86_64\nProfile:              ephemeral\nServer type:          ephemeral\nPydantic version:     2.14.2\nServer:\n  Database:           sqlite\n  SQLite version:     3.46.1\n```\n\nSame on `main` at da9d9dc15f647f10135a8ff79ce93c4bdb2dbf6a.\n\n### Additional context\n\nThe check is here, and it's been there since sparse checkout support landed in #15824 (3.1.11): https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefect/blob/da9d9dc15f647f10135a8ff79ce93c4bdb2dbf6a/src/prefect/runner/storage.py#L462-L467\n\nProposed fix: on an existing sparse checkout, add the configured directories with `git sparse-checkout add`, and run `git sparse-checkout disable` when `directories` is no longer set. I'd keep it grow-only instead of running `set` on every pull, because runs with different `directories` can share the destination, and narrowing it would remove files another run may still be importing from (the same reason the re-clone was a problem in #16241). I have it working with real-git tests and will open the PR right after this; happy to adjust the approach.\n\n", "hints_text": "", "created_at": "2026-11-02T17:53:03Z"}
{"repo": "PrefectHQ/prefect", "pull_number": 23710, "instance_id": "PrefectHQ__prefect-23710", "issue_numbers": ["23709"], "base_commit": "e136714944ab7f793307d7685e306b2383f2f310", "patch": "diff --git a/ui/package-lock.json b/ui/package-lock.json\nindex 93dec505755d..0964e8c84334 100644\n--- a/ui/package-lock.json\n+++ b/ui/package-lock.json\n@@ -30,6 +30,7 @@\n         \"ts-node\": \"10.9.2\",\n         \"typescript\": \"^5.9.2\",\n         \"vite\": \"^8.3.4\",\n+        \"vitest\": \"5.0.0\",\n         \"vue-tsc\": \"^3.1.2\"\n       }\n     },\n@@ -352,9 +353,10 @@\n       }\n     },\n     \"node_modules/@jridgewell/sourcemap-codec\": {\n-      \"version\": \"1.5.5\",\n-      \"resolved\": \"https://registry.npmjs.org/@jridgewell/sourcemap-codec/-/sourcemap-codec-1.5.5.tgz\",\n-      \"integrity\": \"sha512-cYQ9310grqxueWbl+WuIUIaiUaDcj7WOq5fVhEljNVgRfOUhY9fy2zTvfoqWsnebh8Sl70VScFbICvJnLKB0Og==\"\n+      \"version\": \"1.6.0\",\n+      \"resolved\": \"https://registry.npmjs.org/@jridgewell/sourcemap-codec/-/sourcemap-codec-1.6.0.tgz\",\n+      \"integrity\": \"sha512-T7jf+5zgsZHwNJ4lvQ7/aezbyk0nNX+zJVWpmHA7VYsEx7a7qr5Rg5IbtJFqkgze5Y2sruq1RUY8Q837Od7iFw==\",\n+      \"license\": \"MIT\"\n     },\n     \"node_modules/@jridgewell/trace-mapping\": {\n       \"version\": \"0.3.9\",\n@@ -1480,18 +1482,43 @@\n       \"integrity\": \"sha512-yOlFc+7UtL/89t2ZhjPvvB/DeAr3r+Dq58IgzsFkOAvVC6NMJXmCGjbptdXdR9qsX7pKcTL+s87FtYREi2dEEQ==\",\n       \"devOptional\": true\n     },\n+    \"node_modules/@types/chai\": {\n+      \"version\": \"5.2.3\",\n+      \"resolved\": \"https://registry.npmjs.org/@types/chai/-/chai-5.2.3.tgz\",\n+      \"integrity\": \"sha512-Mw558oeA9fFbv65/y4mHtXDs9bPnFMZAL/jxdPFUpOHHIXX91mcgEHbS5Lahr+pwZFR8A7GQleRWeI6cGFC2UA==\",\n+      \"dev\": true,\n+      \"license\": \"MIT\",\n+      \"dependencies\": {\n+        \"@types/deep-eql\": \"*\",\n+        \"assertion-error\": \"^2.0.1\"\n+      }\n+    },\n     \"node_modules/@types/css-font-loading-module